// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.591-dev (Dev. TWO FABRICATIONS ON TROY NOEL'S LEAD, BOTH SHIPPED AS FACT. Lead 2073356549, Community Honda Baytown, 8/27, rescanned on v9.7.589. (1) THE HANG-UP COUNT. The close-override read "Customer has hung up on 11 calls. Phone is not working." Troy's record carries exactly ONE hang-up note -- 08/27 2:52 PM, "By: Kaylee Guzman | hung up during screening", logged by our own agent during dialer screening, not by the customer. The 11 was sbTotal: texts + calls + emails, every outreach attempt on the lead. sbHungUp was only ever a BOOLEAN (.test() -- did the phrase appear at all) and sbTotal was silently standing in for a count nobody computed. Worse, it scales with our own progress: the SAME lead read "6" on v9.7.585 and "11" on v9.7.589 because the v9.7.589 transcript fix handed the tally more text to count, so every future context improvement inflates the fabrication further. And "Phone is not working" was asserted flat, with nothing behind it -- a hang-up says somebody ended a call, never that the line is broken. FIX: _lpHangUpCount() counts the note lines that actually carry a hang-up phrase; sbHungUp is now derived from it (identical truth value); the override reports N hang-ups out of sbTotal attempts, and explicitly forbids telling the customer their phone is broken or stating why any call ended. The SITUATION BRIEF line got the same treatment. (2) THE OWNERSHIP CLAIM. "YOUR LAST SUBSTANTIVE MESSAGE TO THIS CUSTOMER" was titled that unconditionally, and on this lead it quoted Yvonne Ortega's 8/27 email while Kaylee Guzman was the signing agent -- 34 lines above the rule that says do not claim ownership of messages you did not send. Not an edge case: Troy's lead passed System -> Samantha Lopez -> Kaylee Guzman inside three minutes, so at Community rooftops the newest substantive outbound very often is not the signer's. VinSolutions already carries the author IN the note body ("By: <name>" on calls/emails/notes, "Sent by: <name>" on texts), so _lpOutboundAuthor() recovers it with no new scrape plumbing. When the author is not the signer the heading names them and a following line bans "my last message"/"I reached out"/"I sent you". _lpSameAgent() compares first+last token and returns TRUE when either side is unknown -- an unrecoverable author must not become a manufactured "someone else wrote this", which would be the same class of fabrication pointing the other way. New [LP ANCHOR AUTHOR DIAG] prints author, signer and the verdict. NOTE ON WHAT 589 ALREADY FIXED, unchanged here: the arc-state contradiction is gone ("1 inbound / 7 outbound" now agrees with itself) and the populated transcript let the model see "By: Nyriel Benton"/"By: Yvonne Ortega" and write "we've tried reaching you" rather than claiming the messages -- a fix by evidence, not by rule, which is why the mislabel still had to be fixed here. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Pairs: DEV v9.7.591-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.591.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.590 (Dev. THE TAPBACK GUARD COULD NEVER REACH THE TAPBACK. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. WHEN A CUSTOMER TAPS 'LOVE' on an iPhone message, the SMS gateway delivers it as an INBOUND message from her whose body is OUR OWN TEXT, quoted back. She authored one word: the verb. Everything in the quotes is ours. THE BUG: the guard for this was anchored ^\s* — the reaction had to be the very first thing in the string. A real VinSolutions note never is. Verified against the 8/27 DOM dump rather than assumed: the content element opens with routing headers in their own <div>s, so innerText yields 'Received from: (832) 459-3726' / 'Received by: Rotaxlyn Hudson' / 'Loved “Great I got you down for 3pm on Saturday ...”' and the verb sits on line THREE. The guard read position zero, saw 'R', and gave up. It has therefore almost certainly never fired on live traffic since v9.7.567 — the third guard this week found waiting for a string shape production does not produce, after the v9.7.586 on-premise check (anchored past these same headers) and the value-fact resolver (waiting on a command nobody types). A guard that cannot match production is not distinguishable from no guard at all. WHAT IT COST, on Keisha Burgess (lead 2074168344, 8/27 3:48 PM): the scheduler read 'Saturday' out of OUR OWN sentence and logged 'day name IS customer-authored' with cutBy '(nothing cut)' and customerLen 259 === rawLen 259. Harmless there, because Saturday was genuinely her day from an earlier message. The general case is not harmless: a tapback is the LEAST specific reply a person can send and this made it the most specific. Text a customer 'How about Monday at 10?', have them tap Love, and LP would hard-lock a day and time they never chose — and the same applies to any price, vehicle or commitment we put in a message they merely react to. THE FIX: allow the CRM routing headers to precede the reaction, and require the verb at a LINE START. The line-start requirement is the half that matters as much as the headers: searching anywhere would make 'I loved “Top Gun”, anyway about the car' delete a real customer message, trading a false positive for a false negative — and silently emptying customer text is precisely the class v9.7.589 just fixed. Zero headers still matches, so the original bare-reaction shape is untouched. TWO FIXES NOW COMPOSE, worth stating because the outcome depends on both: with the reaction yielding no customer text, the v9.7.588 scan-depth change carries the scheduler PAST that note to the message where she actually wrote 'I'm still coming Saturday'. Before v9.7.588 the loop would have stopped there and found nothing at all. VERIFIED 22/22 in a new tapback-anchor suite plus all 46 suites green (1,489 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite EXECUTES the shipped helper against the note shape taken from the real dump. NON-VACUOUS: run against v9.7.589 it fails 13 ways, while the 9 that pass there are the false-positive guards and the bare-reaction shape — behaviours that were already correct and are asserted unchanged. Asserted: Keisha's real note is recognised as a reaction and leaves NOTHING customer-authored, so 'Saturday' is unreachable as something she wrote; the verb is still reported, because that IS her signal; all seven reaction verbs match behind headers, in both Received and Sent header shapes, with straight and smart quotes; a bare reaction with no headers is unchanged; 'I loved “Top Gun”' is NOT a reaction and that message survives intact; a mid-sentence verb is not a reaction; a verb with no quote is not a reaction; a reaction that follows real customer prose does not swallow the whole note; and the quoted-reply markers this helper already handled still cut correctly. ONE THING THIS CHANGES IN THE DATA: the day-lock observer has been recording DISAGREE-REGEX-ONLY on leads like this one, which reads as 'comprehension missed what the regex caught'. On this lead it was the reverse — comprehension correctly found no customer-authored day because there is none in reach, and the regex was reading our own words. Those counts should be re-read before comprehension is judged on them. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a lead whose ONLY day/time evidence was a tapback will now find no constraint there and fall through to whatever the customer actually wrote, or to none. That is the intended direction — a constraint we cannot source is worse than no constraint — and the scan-depth change makes the fall-through land on real evidence rather than on nothing. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the helper is INSIDE inlineScraper alongside its callers. Builds on v9.7.589.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.589 (Dev. A SAME-DAY LEAD LOST ITS ENTIRE TRANSCRIPT. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. THE BUG: three transcript filters compared a DATE-TRUNCATED line stamp against a TIME-PRECISE cutoff. A line reads "[08/27/2026 12:36 PM]" -- the time is right there -- but the capture group stopped at the date, so every line collapsed to MIDNIGHT of its day. transcriptCutoffMs is time-precise: for Keisha Burgess (lead 2074168344) it came from her "Lead received" note at 08/27 11:12 AM minus a 1-hour buffer, so 10:12 AM. Midnight < 10:12 AM, and EVERY note from that day was dropped -- all 32 -- leaving the delivered prompt carrying an empty transcript between its fences. THE RULE IT PRODUCED, stated plainly: every note dated the SAME CALENDAR DAY as the lead-received timestamp was discarded, unless the lead arrived before 1:00 AM. On a multi-day lead that costs one day and hides. On a SAME-DAY lead it costs the whole conversation -- the hottest traffic there is. Andrea Pardon's transcript was full (notes 8/22-8/26, receipt 8/21 9:05 PM) while Keisha's was empty: same code, same day, opposite outcomes, which is exactly why this survived. Her own "Lead received" line was dropped too, and nobody noticed that either. THE FIX IS TWO-PART, because either half alone leaves the same hole: (1) capture the time already present in the line so both sides compare at full precision -- a note genuinely from 10 AM before an 11:12 AM receipt still drops CORRECTLY, which is the prior-cycle exclusion this filter exists for; (2) when a line carries NO time, compare against the START OF THE CUTOFF'S DAY, because otherwise a date-only line is still killed by a same-day cutoff -- the identical bug in a smaller costume, and precisely how it was missed the first time. Written ONCE as _lpLineIsBeforeCutoff and called from all three sites, which were byte-identical copies; fixing them separately would have left the next reader three chances to re-diverge. A FOURTH SITE WAS FOUND AND DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE: the 180-day concern-scan filter captures the time with the date and was always correct. It is asserted untouched, as a standing counter-example. THE DIAGNOSTIC THAT MISLED THREE INVESTIGATION PASSES IS ALSO FIXED. [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] printed 'transcript fences NOT found' whenever ctx_full came back unchanged -- an INFERENCE, not an observation, and wrong on the lead that mattered. Keisha's context HAD both fences; the body between them was empty because the cutoff had deleted every line. An empty transcript and an unfenced one need different fixes, so they now print different lines, and the empty case says outright to look at the cutoff rather than the fences. VERIFIED 20/20 in a new transcript-cutoff suite plus all 45 suites green (1,467 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite EXECUTES the shipped helper. NON-VACUOUS: run against v9.7.588 it fails 12 ways, including all six of Keisha's real lines -- and the harness carries a PRE-FIX FALLBACK that reproduces the old comparison from the OLD FILE'S OWN regex, lifted verbatim, because without it the suite threw on load and printed nothing, which reads exactly like 'the suite does not catch the bug'. That vacuous-check-of-a-vacuous-check has now been hit twice in this repo and is guarded here explicitly. Asserted: all six same-day lines survive; the 11:23 AM note eleven minutes past the receipt survives; the 'Lead received' line stops deleting itself; a 9:15 AM prior-cycle note on the SAME day still drops; a months-old note still drops; the boundary is the cutoff instant (10:11 out, 10:13 in); Andrea's three later-day lines are unchanged; a date-only line on the cutoff day survives while one from the day before does not; undated and unparseable lines are never dropped on time grounds; a zero cutoff drops nothing; single-digit and 2-digit-year stamps parse; and an indented entry is still recognised. THREE OVER-BROAD ASSERTIONS WERE WRITTEN AND CORRECTED while building this, all the same shape: expressing 'the bad pattern is gone' as a REGEX OVER SOURCE caught innocent code twice -- the 180-day filter, then the Created-field parse, which is legitimately date-only because it reads a date FIELD rather than a timestamped line -- and the post-mortem comments quoted the broken line so a raw-source scan found its own explanation. Matching a regex with a regex is how that keeps happening; the final assertion counts a LITERAL anchored pattern, and the executable assertions are the real coverage. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: same-day leads now carry their transcript, which is a large content change to those prompts -- the model will see the conversation it was previously blind to. Multi-day leads gain back only the notes from their own receipt day. The prior-cycle exclusion is unchanged in every direction that was working. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the helper is INSIDE inlineScraper alongside its callers. Builds on v9.7.588.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.588 (Dev. A TWO-CHARACTER REPLY ERASED A DAY, A TIME, AND THE REASON SHE COULD COME. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. THE INCIDENT, AND IT ARRIVED AS A CONTROLLED PAIR: Keisha Burgess (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2074168344, 8/27), two grabs eight minutes apart on the same lead with opposite results. At 12:56 note[0] read '...I'm still coming Saturday and what your name so I can ask for you' and the prompt carried 'CUSTOMER NAMED A SPECIFIC DAY -- THIS IS A HARD CONSTRAINT' plus a NOT-TODAY block. At 13:04 she had sent '3pm'; note[0] carried no day name, the scheduler's loop broke, and the prompt swapped the Saturday lock for 'SUGGESTED APPOINTMENT TIMES: 3:15 PM today / 4:00 PM today'. The draft survived on luck rather than design -- the model still said Saturday because the MOST RECENT CUSTOMER MESSAGES list carries it as entry two -- but nothing in the guardrails was holding it. THE CAUSE: the loop walks notes NEWEST-FIRST and broke at the first INBOUND note regardless of what it contained, instead of at the first note carrying a CONSTRAINT. ESTABLISHED AGAINST THE DOM DUMP RATHER THAN ARGUED, after TWO WRONG DIAGNOSES that are recorded because the second one was self-inflicted. First reading -- 'it stops at the first inbound note' -- was right but unproven. Second reading -- 'noteEls only had one element' -- was WRONG, and came from an awk scan for /\bbreak\b/ finding nothing: \b in awk ERE is a BACKSPACE, not a word boundary, so the break was invisible to the very check meant to find it. The dump settled it: the notes list holds 32 items, 16 Inbound and 11 Outbound, matching the relationship reading exactly. noteEls was never short; the loop simply stopped. WHAT SAT BELOW THE BREAK, all inbound, none read: [2] '...I'm still coming Saturday...', [6] '...Sometime around 2:30 pm', [7] 'I'll will uber there'. The last is not a scheduling detail -- the 11:22 call note records 'she doesn't have a vehicle so he can't come in', and she had solved that herself where LP could not see it. THE FIX is one condition: break once a CONSTRAINT is found rather than once an inbound note is seen. Newest-first ordering keeps override semantics for free -- the first constraint found is the most recent one stated, so a later 'actually Monday works' still beats an earlier Saturday -- and every assignment in the loop was already guarded on !customerScheduleConstraint, so scanning on cannot overwrite a fresher constraint with a staler one. It also stops starving the day-lock probe: schedCustomerNotes is pushed inside this loop, so breaking at note[0] handed the comprehension reader ONE note (notesRead:1 in the 12:56 capture) out of sixteen customer messages. New [LP SCHED SCAN DIAG] reports notes available, window scanned, customer notes read and the constraint found -- including the empty case -- so a short read is visible next time instead of silent. VERIFIED 12/12 in a new sched-scan-depth suite plus all 44 suites green (1,447 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite drives the SHIPPED loop with Keisha's REAL note order transcribed from the dump, and slices _lpCustomerAuthoredPart out of the shipped file rather than stubbing it -- a hand-written stand-in for the attribution helper would quietly re-open the v9.7.560 quoted-reply hole. NON-VACUOUS: against v9.7.587 it fails 6 ways including the incident assertion itself, while the 12:56 shape passes on BOTH builds, proving the path that worked is untouched. Asserted: the 13:04 shape now locks Saturday; the constraint found is the DAY one rather than a time preference scraped off '3pm'; a NEWER Monday overrides an older Saturday and the Saturday does not leak alongside it; a constraint in note[0] still short-circuits at one note, so this is not 'always scan everything'; a lead with no constraint still finds none rather than inventing one; and outbound notes are still skipped, because an agent naming a day is not the customer naming one. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: leads whose newest inbound message carries no scheduling content will now surface a constraint stated slightly earlier in the thread, where before they surfaced none. That is the intended change and it is the safer direction -- a stale day is visible and correctable, a silently dropped one produced same-day times for a customer who had named Saturday. STILL OPEN and NOT addressed here: the delivered CONVERSATION TRANSCRIPT renders empty on this lead despite 32 CRM entries, and [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] reports 'transcript fences NOT found' on every grab. That is upstream of this fix and wants its own investigation. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the change is INSIDE inlineScraper and logs via _lpD. Builds on v9.7.587.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.587 (Dev. HALF A FIX TO A CONTRADICTION IS A NEW CONTRADICTION. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. v9.7.586 fixed the arc block's 'never replied' claim by reading lastInboundAgeDays off relationshipSignals instead of the merged scrape -- and passed totalInbound while leaving totalOutbound behind. The exchange line renders on `if (inb || outb)`, and `parseFloat(x) || 0` turns an ABSENT count into a confident zero, so supplying only one half flipped a silently-dropped line into a published falsehood. Keisha Burgess's rescan (lead 2074168344, 8/27 12:56 PM) shows it exactly: 'Total exchange: 15 inbound / 10 outbound' from the relationship reading at line 247, and 'Exchange so far: 15 inbound / 0 outbound' from the arc block at line 410, in the same delivered prompt. Before v9.7.586 both counts were zero so the line never rendered at all; the fix is what made it visible and wrong. FIXED IN BOTH PLACES, deliberately. The call site now passes totalOutbound alongside totalInbound. And the RENDERER now gates on whether each number was SUPPLIED rather than on whether it is truthy -- absence stays silent, a genuine zero still prints. The plumbing fix alone would have left the same trap armed for the next caller, which is the whole reason the rule belongs at the render site. WHAT THE RESCAN CONFIRMS ABOUT v9.7.586, on the same lead an hour later: the on-premise false positive is GONE -- no [LP ON-PREMISE DIAG] line and no 'CUSTOMER IS AT THE STORE RIGHT NOW' block in the delivered prompt, where the 12:41 PM run had both; and the arc block now reads 'exchange:15in/0out | sinceReply:0d' where it read '0in/0out | never replied'. The day-lock detector also fired correctly and honestly on this run -- [LP SCHED DAY DIAG] verdict 'LOCKED -- day name IS customer-authored' on 'coming Saturday', and [LP FACT DETAIL] day-lock AGREE-FIRED with the quote verified in note 1 -- so the delivered message offered two Saturday times and nothing else, which is what the customer asked for. VERIFIED 30/30 in arc-state (4 new) plus all 43 suites green (1,435 assertions), dev===comm on every case. NON-VACUOUS: run against v9.7.586 the new assertion fails, naming the regression by the lead it came from. Asserted: both halves known renders the line; inbound known with outbound ABSENT renders NO zero; neither known stays absent exactly as before; and a genuine zero-outbound lead is still reportable when the zero is KNOWN rather than inferred. STILL OPEN, FOUND IN THIS RESCAN AND NOT FIXED HERE: the delivered CONVERSATION TRANSCRIPT section is EMPTY on this lead despite 30 CRM entries, and the CONVERSATION ARC lists only the 2 general notes. The model is working from the MOST RECENT CUSTOMER MESSAGES summary and the call note alone, which is the most likely reason the draft asks Keisha to confirm she is still shopping for a K5 -- a question her own thread answers repeatedly. [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] reports 'transcript fences NOT found' and [LP SCAFFOLD-LEAK DIAG] truncates 1262 chars on both runs. That is a scrape/merge gap, it predates these builds, and it wants its own investigation rather than a guess appended to this one. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing. One prompt line either gains its correct second number or stays absent; no other behaviour changes. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.586.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.586 (Dev. LP TOLD A CUSTOMER WITH NO CAR THAT SHE WAS STANDING IN OUR LOT, AND THE SAME PROMPT SAID SHE HAD NEVER REPLIED. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. TWO INDEPENDENT DEFECTS on one lead -- Keisha Burgess, Community Kia Baytown, lead 2074168344, 8/27. (1) THE ON-PREMISE FALSE POSITIVE, FIFTH OF ITS CLASS AND THE FIRST ON A CUSTOMER'S OWN WORDS. Her inbound text read 'May have to print them out here is my ID also'. The place token matched 'out here' -- the 'out' of 'print them OUT' plus the 'here' of 'HERE is my ID', two different phrases adjacent by accident -- and the subject token matched the 'is' of 'here is'. Same clause, so the v9.7.533 same-clause rule could not help: the tokens genuinely ARE adjacent, they just belong to different units of meaning. The prompt then carried the 'CUSTOMER IS AT THE STORE RIGHT NOW -- THIS OUTRANKS EVERY OTHER DIRECTIVE' block and LP wrote 'I see you're here now. Someone is coming right out -- where on the lot are you?' to a customer who had written, nine minutes earlier, 'I don't have a car so the one time I do come I hope to leave with a vehicle.' THE STRUCTURAL FINDING: _opOurOwnOutbound excludes notes OPENING with our own send headers (left message / sent to: / sent by: / subject:), so it catches agent messages -- but an inbound customer text opens 'Received from:', which is not in that list. A detector built to read AGENT OBSERVATIONS was reading CUSTOMER SPEECH, where 'here is my ID' is ordinary English for 'attached'. Third-person tokens (is/was/said/she's) describe someone ELSE being present; on the customer's own text they describe nothing. FIXED IN TWO PLACES because either alone leaves a hole: the phrasal-verb object ('them/it/those/him/her/us out') is blanked before the place test, exactly as the inventory and budget idioms already were; and authorship now governs the qualifier -- an agent note keeps the third-person observation rule unchanged, while a CUSTOMER text must carry a FIRST-PERSON presence claim. Inbound is NOT excluded outright, because 'I'm outside' from the customer is the strongest on-premise signal there is; it simply has to say THEY are here rather than merely use a word that can mean here. New [LP ON-PREMISE REJECT] logs a suppressed candidate WITH the reason, so a future miss is visible instead of silent. (2) 'THE CUSTOMER HAS NEVER REPLIED TO ANYTHING ON THIS LEAD' WAS ASSERTED ON EVERY LEAD SINCE v9.7.578, INCLUDING THIS ONE, WHERE SHE HAD SENT 10 MESSAGES. The same prompt carried 'Customer replied today. Total exchange: 10 inbound / 8 outbound' 150 lines above it. TWO CAUSES. The call site read lastScrapedData.lastInboundAgeDays and lastScrapedData.consecutiveOutboundNoReply; BOTH live on data.relationshipSignals (built ~11050, consumed as s.lastInboundAgeDays ~14784) and NEITHER is ever set on the merged scrape -- so daysSinceReply was null on every generation for eight builds. And the line fired on `since === null` alone, turning an unavailable field into a positive claim about a real customer: the manufactured-directive class, inside the block written to prevent it. Now reads the signals object, and the claim requires POSITIVE evidence -- a known inbound count of zero. Unknown says nothing, which is this section's whole posture ('Observed facts, each with the evidence it came from'). Andrea Pardon's prompt carried the same false line the day before and it went unnoticed because for her it was true. VERIFIED 21/21 in a new on-premise-authorship suite plus 5 new arc-state assertions, all 43 suites green (1,431 assertions), dev===comm on every case. Both suites EXECUTE the shipped code; the on-premise one drives Keisha's VERBATIM captured text. NON-VACUOUS: against v9.7.585 the on-premise suite fails 10 ways and arc-state 2. Asserted: Keisha's real text no longer reads as on-premise; five genuine customer presence claims ('I'm outside', 'im here in the lot', ...) still fire; five genuine agent observations still fire unchanged; all four earlier false positives stay suppressed; and the SAME third-person sentence fires as an agent note and NOT as a customer text, which is the authorship rule stated as a single assertion. AN EXISTING ASSERTION HAD PINNED THE BUG AS CORRECT and had to be rewritten: arc-state required the call site to read lastScrapedData.consecutiveOutboundNoReply -- a REAL field name off the WRONG OBJECT, which a source scan cannot distinguish from a right one, and which stayed green for eight builds. That is the third time this month a test has confirmed a defect verbatim. ONE TEST ERROR OF MY OWN: the reject-log assertion was pointed at Keisha's text, which fix (A) defuses before the authorship branch runs, so it asserted a log that correctly never fired; re-pointed at the case fix (B) actually handles. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: an on-premise reading now needs an observer. A customer who writes only 'here' without claiming presence no longer triggers the at-the-store override -- that is the intended trade, and the reject log makes any real miss countable. Agent notes are byte-identical in behaviour. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the detector change is INSIDE inlineScraper and therefore logs via _lpD, not console.log. Builds on v9.7.585.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.585 (Dev. PHASE 2, VALUE FACT: MEASURE FIRST. NOTHING IS SURFACED AND NO BEHAVIOUR CHANGES. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. The brief asked whether LP can auto-source the VALUE FACT instead of the agent typing one, and scoped itself INVESTIGATE-FIRST. Two findings say that question cannot be answered yet, and both are checkable rather than argued. (1) THE RESOLVER HAS NEVER RUN. The v9.7.231 auto-resolver already exists, gated on /VALUE FACT|NURTURE FACT/ tested against the agent's LP commands. The LIVE VinSolutions task list tells agents to type [LP: value]. Run the SHIPPED regex against that exact string and it is FALSE. Across every generation ever captured -- 712 carrying no command at all and 5 carrying ["value"]/["curiosity"] -- not one could have fired it. So the MANUAL path is as dead as the automatic one: nobody has been typing what the code waits for, which inverts the brief's premise that agents supply this by hand today. (2) THERE IS NO DENOMINATOR. Nothing anywhere counts how often each fact is PRESENT on a lead, so 'is this worth building' has no data behind it -- equity on 4% of leads is not worth a build and on 40% plainly is, and nobody knows which. Attempts to measure it from captured prompts returned counts that were matching the PROMPT'S OWN INSTRUCTION TEXT rather than lead data -- the self-matching trap, hit three separate times during this investigation and discarded each time rather than reported. The measurement has to come from live traffic. WHAT THE SIX ACTUALLY ARE, and why only one is a real candidate: store incentives already auto-source (zero agentLPCommands references in that block); the loyalty/owned vehicle already auto-sources (fires on `if (d.ownedVehicle)`); unit position already auto-sources at the AGED INVENTORY / FRESH ARRIVAL block, so the copy inside the gate is REDUNDANT with a path that already runs; conditionalOffer is READ twice and ASSIGNED NOWHERE in the entire build, a dead branch that cannot fire; finance (Virtual Retailing numbers) is blocked by POLICY rather than plumbing, because the OTD / PAYMENT DISCIPLINE block in this same prompt forbids volunteering a monthly payment or financing number unless the customer asked for it first, and auto-surfacing VR numbers on a value touch does exactly that -- the Phase-5-vs-STALLED contradiction again, and a policy call rather than a code one; leaving EQUITY as the single clean candidate, since it asserts no number. THE GATE IS DELIBERATELY NOT WIDENED. Making it accept [LP: value] looks like a one-line correction and would immediately begin volunteering the customer's payment numbers into outbound messages. That is recorded in place so the next person hits the reason before the fix. WHAT SHIPPED: one [LP VALUE FACT DIAG] line, UNCONDITIONAL, outside the gate, on every generation -- reporting the gate state (NO-COMMAND / COMMAND-PRESENT-BUT-NO-MATCH[...] / MATCH / STALE-COMMAND), per-fact presence for finance, equity, offer and unit position, and whether the resolver ran. Same posture as [LP CADENCE DIAG] and [LP STALLED PHASE DIAG]: observe first, decide on data. PRESENCE ONLY, NEVER VALUES -- a VR payment is the customer's financial data and logs get exported, so the line reports which fields exist and never what is in them; asserted by scanning the emitted line for every figure on a fully-populated lead and requiring zero hits. VERIFIED 22/22 in a new value-fact-diag suite plus all 42 suites green (1,406 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite EXECUTES the observer out of the shipped file. THE ASSERTION THE WHOLE BUILD RESTS ON is that it adds NOTHING to the prompt on any input -- a rich lead, an empty lead, a stale command, a matching command, and a throwing scrape all leave vehicleExtras at length 0. Also asserted: exactly ONE line per generation rather than one per fact; absent facts read 'no' rather than being omitted, because an omitted field cannot be counted; daysOnLot of 0 is a real reading via typeof rather than truthiness; and a throw inside the observer is REPORTED rather than swallowed into a quiet 'nothing available', which is how the v9.7.576 off-franchise gate hid itself. ONE TEST ERROR RECORDED: an assertion field was named `ran` while testing for the string 'DID NOT RUN', so the expectation got written inverted and a CORRECT diagnostic read as a failure -- renamed didNotRun. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing. No prompt text changes, no gate changes, no customer-facing behaviour changes; one console line is added per generation. The decision this unblocks -- whether to auto-source equity, and what to do about the OTD conflict -- needs a day of that line first. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the observer is outside inlineScraper (6463-11440) and therefore uses console.log rather than _lpD. Builds on v9.7.584.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.584 (Dev. THE REGENERATE BUTTON RETURNED THE DRAFT IT WAS ASKED TO REPLACE. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. THE DEFECT: the proxy's edge cache is keyed on SHA-256(centralDate + systemText + userText). A plain Regenerate sets no directive and changes no lead state, so both texts are BYTE-IDENTICAL to the draft the agent is asking to replace -- same key, same entry, same draft, back in ~100ms. A regen on an unchanged lead could not return anything different. Structural, not occasional. THE CHIP PATH ALREADY HANDLED THIS and its own comment says why -- 'Chip clicks always bypass edge cache -- agent explicitly wants fresh output' -- and that was the ONLY assignment of the bypass flag in the entire build. A chip and a regen are the same request, 'this draft is wrong, give me another'; only one was honoured. WHY IT READ AS 'REGEN DOES NOTHING' RATHER THAN AN ODDITY: EDGE_CACHE_TTL is 120s and the key carries 'CURRENT TIME: <h>:<mm> Central', so an entry is reachable only inside the same clock minute -- which is exactly when a person reads a draft and clicks Regenerate. MEASURED 8/26: all five abandoned sessions returned byte-identical text or nothing, and all six that got a genuine change were kept -- 11 of 11, no exceptions -- with the one pure-regen row (12:46 PM, lead 2071842343, no chips) in exactly this shape. Median latency across all requests was 207ms against a PRIMARY call average of 3230ms, so most traffic never reached the model. THE FIX is one term: _isRegenSession joins the bypass condition. Verified in scope first -- both it and the bypass live in generateAll, 471 lines apart -- because an out-of-scope identifier has taken this system down twice this week. COST, STATED: regens stop being free. At the measured 0.5% regen rate that is ~13 extra model calls a day and ~3.5s instead of ~100ms. Paying 3.5s for a different draft is the point of the button; 100ms for the same one is not. New [LP EDGE BYPASS DIAG] reports the decision AND which leg caused it (regen / chip / which lead-state flag), and reports the cache-eligible case too, so the bypass ratio is countable rather than inferred. COLD STARTS INVESTIGATED AND CLOSED AS NOT-OURS, recorded here so it is not re-opened from scratch: buildSystemPrompt was extracted and executed across bdc, sales, director, manager and concierge and all five stores, and every run produced ONE identical cacheable head -- 18,220 chars, ~4,555 tokens, comfortably over the 1,024-token minimum, sha 36f712fe57a3 in every case. The breakpoint is applied, the prefix is universal, and CACHE_TTL is '30m' because that is the only value the 5.6 family documents -- there is no knob. At 713 primary calls across ~9 hours (one every ~45s) a 30-minute TTL cannot be the binding constraint, so 48% cold is provider-side routing, not our configuration. The hourly data agrees: cold RATE tracks concurrency (4.3% at 08:xx/255 reqs vs 16.2% at 13:xx/475 reqs), which is the backend fan-out signature. What IS ours and is left undone deliberately: 19,741 chars of instruction-shaped text sits in the USER prompt BELOW the boundary -- more than the 18,220 above it -- and some of it is conditional, so only the unconditional subset could move up without fragmenting the one shared prefix into variants. Establishing that subset is an investigation, not an edit, and is not attempted here. VERIFIED 18/18 in a new edge-bypass suite plus all 41 suites green (1,384 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite EXECUTES the shipped bypass decision rather than scanning for it. NON-VACUOUS: run against v9.7.583 it fails 5 ways, while all nine lead-state legs still pass there -- proving the regen term is what changed and the pre-existing legs are untouched. Asserted: a regen bypasses; a first pass on the same lead does not; a chip still bypasses; chip+regen is an OR not a double; every one of the nine state legs still fires on a non-regen; and the diag names the leg rather than just the outcome. ONE HARNESS ERROR RECORDED: the first END anchor was the NEW diag line, which does not exist in v9.7.583, so the non-vacuity run could not load the old build and printed NOTHING -- which reads exactly like 'the suite does not catch the bug'. A vacuous check of a vacuous check, the same shape as the v9.7.575 old573.js mistake. Re-anchored on a comment present in both. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: regens get SLOWER and start costing a model call. First-pass drafts, chips and every lead-state bypass are byte-identical. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.583.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.583 (Dev. THE PROMPT TOLD THE MODEL TO ASSUME THE CUSTOMER HAD ALREADY BOUGHT, AND LEFT THE BLANK FOR IT TO FILL. Extension only; proxy v7.68 and reporter v1.21 unchanged and NOT redeployed. THE INCIDENT: Andrea Pardon (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2071842343, 8/26 12:51 PM). The delivered voicemail read "I'm glad you found your 2025 Toyota Camry" and referred her to a sister store. Her entire arc is a 2019 Jeep Cherokee trade appraisal and a Kia Sorento; she has never replied to anything and has never named a Toyota. FRAME BLEED WAS ELIMINATED AGAINST THE ARTIFACTS RATHER THAN ARGUED AWAY, and that mattered because it was the obvious suspect -- the Julian/Juan phantom-Corolla shape. The delivered prompt was pulled and COUNTED: 'camry' 0, 'toyota' 0, 'sister' 0, 'referral' 0 across all 66,627 chars; only 'Sorento', 12 times. [LP PIVOT SCOPE DIAG] reported scopeCandidates:[] with fullCtxCandidates:[Sorento] correctly EXCLUDED BY SCOPE as non-customer speech, so the SISTER-BRAND PIVOT block never fired. [LP LEAD-GRID DIAG] reported leads on this customer record: 1, so no donor lead exists. The vehicle was invented and nothing upstream supplied it. IT WAS NOT INVENTED UNPROMPTED, AND THAT IS THE ACTUAL DEFECT. The stalled ladder's top rung was named 'PHASE 5 -- ASSUMPTION CLOSE' and instructed: 'Gently assume they moved on: "I am guessing you found something already -- did you end up going with something similar?"' That directs the model to state an outcome the system cannot observe, and leaves the object of the sentence empty. A RE-GRAB OF THE SAME LEAD hours later rendered the SAME directive safely -- 'did you end up finding something already?' -- so both runs obeyed and only one had somewhere to put a car. A directive whose safety depends on the model choosing to stay vague is not safe, least of all inside a prompt that says 'Specific beats generic every time' and names vagueness as 'the trap'. AND IT CONTRADICTED A RULE SHIPPING ~270 LINES ABOVE IT: the STALLED flag block already bans this exact guess -- "Do NOT guess at their personal circumstances or motivations -- 'you found another offer,' 'something changed on your end,' 'timing,' 'still comparing,' a life event, or any menu of such guesses to pick from are all presumptuous." Phase 5 instructed the thing Phase 5's own flag text forbids; this resolves the contradiction toward the rule. THE FIX: the rung is renamed 'PHASE 5 -- GRACEFUL CLOSE-OUT' -- the NAME matters because it is pushed into the prompt verbatim one block below, so 'ASSUMPTION CLOSE' was an instruction to the model, not a label for us -- and the approach now concedes without asserting: acknowledge the silence, ask ONE question that lets the CUSTOMER say where this stands, assert nothing about what they did or why, and name no vehicle, dealership or outcome they have not stated themselves. The concession SPIRIT is kept: still the softest rung, still zero pressure, still an easy out. WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED: the TRIGGER. Andrea reached the top rung on a FOUR-DAY-OLD lead because stalledTouches is a raw count of 'Outbound Text Message' plus 'Email reply to prospect' strings with nothing checking it against the calendar -- and the cadence engine, looking at the same shape, STOOD DOWN ('[LP CADENCE DIAG] REFUSED -- 3 prior touch(es) implies day-13 but the lead is only 4d old ... no touch is safer than the wrong one') while this ladder consumed the count without question. Re-thresholding would change messaging for every stalled lead in the fleet (280 signals on 8/26 alone) with no measurement behind it, so new [LP STALLED PHASE DIAG] REPORTS the disagreement on every generation -- phase, touch split, lead age, and a flag when the top rung lands on a lead under 7 days -- and changes nothing. One day of that line makes the threshold decision data-driven instead of one-lead-driven. VERIFIED 17/17 in a new stalled-phase suite plus all 40 suites green (1,366 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite EXECUTES the shipped ladder rather than scanning it, because this repo has twice this week shipped a live outage past assertions that only proved a string was present in a file. NON-VACUOUS: run against v9.7.582 it fails 11 ways. Asserted: no rung from 0 to 20 touches instructs an assumption about what the customer did or why; no rung NAME contains 'ASSUMPTION'; Phase 5 still concedes, still offers the out, still asks exactly one question, and forbids naming a vehicle; rungs 1-4 are byte-identical and the 4-to-5 boundary has not moved; the phase is IDENTICAL with and without the young-lead flag, proving the diag is observation only; and the STALLED rule this rung used to contradict is still shipping. TWO HARNESS ERRORS RECORDED BECAUSE THEY ARE THE SAME SHAPE AS THE BUG. (1) The first slice started at `var stalledTexts` -- two lines BELOW where ctx_stalled is derived -- so the extracted block ran with ctx_stalled undefined: the out-of-scope class this very fix is a post-mortem for, reproduced inside its own harness. (2) The blanket 'no rung says this' assertion matched the NEW directive's own PROHIBITION, because banning a phrasing requires quoting it; that is the self-matching trap in live prompt text rather than in a comment, where stripComments cannot reach it, so the scan now drops prohibition sentences first. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: leads at 5+ unanswered touches get a different final message -- a clean close-out ask instead of an assumed-departure ask. Some operators value the assumptive close as a technique; the question form of it was ALSO banned by the STALLED rule above, so restoring it is a deliberate decision about that rule, not a revert of this one. Rungs 1-4 and every non-stalled lead are byte-identical. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.582.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.582-dev (DEV. THE COMMERCIAL BUILD HAS NOT SENT `detector` SINCE v9.7.566, AND EVERY LAYER BELOW IT HID THAT. Pairs with proxy v7.67; reporter v1.21 unchanged. THE DIVERGENCE. _lpSendCommitComprehension in the COMMERCIAL build omitted three fields the dev build has sent since Phase 3 shipped: detector, authoritative, sourceUsed. The v9.7.566 hunk missed the mirror and nothing has compared the two payloads since. Investigated because the 8/25 report's BY EXTENSION VERSION table (added in reporter v1.21 for exactly this question) showed every commercial build posting verbal-commit ONLY -- 806 rows -- while all 8 day-lock and all 10 off-franchise rows came from a single machine running dev. The operator's read was deployment lag; it was not. The fleet was running the build it was supposed to be running. The artefact was wrong. WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE, AND THIS IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: TWO SILENT DEFAULTS IN SERIES WITH THE DIAGNOSTIC BELOW BOTH. A commercial row arrived with NO detector. The proxy's own fallback wrote detector:'verbal-commit' onto it BEFORE storage, as a real stored value. The reporter then read a legitimate detector and its 'Defaulted' column -- which I added two days ago FOR THIS EXACT QUESTION -- correctly reported 0, because by the time it saw the row the substitution had already happened one layer up. A fix aimed at a symptom sat beneath the cause. Same shape as v7.60's compKind clamp: a fallback that makes broken data look like real data. THE DISTORTION IT CAUSED, stated plainly: commercial day-lock and off-franchise verdicts have been counted as verbal-commit results for three weeks. The READINGS were genuine -- nothing was fabricated and no quote was invented -- but they were attributed to the wrong detector, which means the per-detector table has been describing one detector's traffic while carrying three detectors' rows. The 96.9% agreement figure is not wrong; the per-detector split beneath it is. WHY NO SUITE CAUGHT IT, and it is a structural gap rather than a missing case: every harness in this repo slices ONE SPAN out of BOTH files and asserts the spans agree -- which they did. The missing keys were in a span nobody compared. dev===comm has been asserted thirty-nine suites deep on everything except whether the two files send the same fields. New assertion compares the two payloads' KEY SETS directly, deliberately on keys rather than values, because a build that stops sending a field is the failure mode here. Run against the v9.7.581 commercial build it fails and NAMES the three fields. PROXY v7.67 MAKES THE DEFAULT AUDIBLE. The detector fallback is lifted out of an inline expression into a named _ccDetector that LOGS which case it hit -- no detector field at all, or an unrecognised one -- and says outright that a modern build doing this is a bug. The fallback itself stays, because pre-v9.7.566 rows genuinely are verbal-commit by construction; what changes is that erasing a field is no longer silent. VERIFIED 72/72 in commit-persistence plus all 39 suites green (1,342 assertions), dev===comm on every case and now on the payload itself. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing. What changes is the DATA -- from the next commercial deploy, day-lock and off-franchise rows will start arriving attributed correctly, so the per-detector table will shift and the three detectors' volumes will separate for the first time. Rows already in KV are not rewritten and cannot be; historical per-detector splits stay wrong and should be read as verbal-commit-plus-unknown rather than corrected after the fact. DEPLOY THE PROXY FIRST so the default is audible before the fleet updates. node --check clean on both builds and the proxy; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.581-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.581-dev (DEV. A LEAD SOURCE IS NOT A SPOUSE. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.21 unchanged. INCIDENT -- PETER GORDON (Audi Lafayette, lead 2068934775, 8/25). Lead source: 'Audi Partner Lead - Audi Partner Lead (Internet)'. The concern scanner tested a BARE \\bpartner\\b against text that included that source string, matched the word PARTNER inside a CRM FIELD, and pushed 'SPOUSE/PARTNER INVOLVED: Customer mentioned needing to involve their spouse or partner' into the prompt. Peter has no spouse and never mentioned one anywhere in 43 notes. The operator's diagnosis was exactly right. WHY THIS ONE IS WORSE THAN THE OTHERS: IT COMPOUNDS. Two agents wrote the invented fact TO HIM -- 'Since you mentioned involving your spouse' (8/18) and 'especially with your spouse involved' (8/20). Once those went out they became TRANSCRIPT, so every later generation re-matched on LP'S OWN WORDS and the claim reinforced itself. Jeri's Yahoo header and Fidel's past-tense Saturday were single-shot errors; this one ratchets, and the longer it runs the more 'evidence' it accumulates for itself. TWO FAULTS, BOTH FIXED. (1) LEAD-SOURCE METADATA WAS BEING SCANNED AS SPEECH. It is a CRM field, not something a person said. The source string is now stripped from the scan text BEFORE any concern detector runs, so this cannot resurface in a different detector. (2) A BARE NOUN IS NOT A RELATIONSHIP CLAIM. A real mention is POSSESSED ('my wife', 'her partner') or is an act of consulting someone ('run it by my husband', 'talk to my wife'). The bare-noun alternative is removed entirely, and the old loose 'talk (to|with) (my|the)' -- which matched 'talk to the dealership' -- is anchored to an actual person. A SECOND OVER-MATCH THE SUITE CAUGHT: 'our partner program' still fired, because 'partner' is the one noun in this set that is ALSO A BUSINESS TERM and the possessive form does not disambiguate it. 'my wife' is never a business phrase; 'our partner' routinely is, and the very string that caused this incident is in that register. A negative lookahead now excludes program/network/portal/site/dealer/store/lead/team/group. A WIDER SURFACE, NAMED RATHER THAN CHANGED BLIND: ELEVEN concern detectors read allTranscriptText and only TWO read customerOnlyText, which sits defined one line away. That is not uniformly wrong -- an agent CALL NOTE recording 'customer said he has bad credit' is legitimate evidence and lives in a [NOTE], so switching all eleven would lose real signal. The defect here was the bare noun and the metadata, and that is what is fixed. The scoping question is real and is recorded for its own pass rather than resolved by guesswork. [LP SPOUSE DIAG] now logs the matched string AND sixty characters of surrounding context whenever the directive fires, so the next bad match is one log line rather than a page dump -- the same discipline as [LP SCHED SOURCE DIAG] and [LP OFF-FRANCHISE GATE]. VERIFIED 17/17 in a new spouse-attribution suite plus all 39 suites green (1,337 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The SHIPPED expressions are evaluated, not copied. Peter's real source string is asserted silent in four phrasings; the OLD bare-noun rule is asserted to have MATCHED it, so the test is not vacuous; LP's own fabricated sentence is asserted to no longer re-fire the loop; six genuine mentions are asserted to still fire; and four business/neutral phrasings are asserted silent. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: the spouse directive fires less often. A missed genuine mention costs a message that does not invite both people -- recoverable. A false one tells a customer we remember a conversation that never happened, and then teaches itself that it did. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.580-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.580-dev (DEV. THE SITUATION READ FINALLY REACHES A PROMPT. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.21 unchanged. TWO STACKED BUGS, BOTH MINE, AND THE SECOND WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL THE FIRST WAS CLEARED. v9.7.578 shipped the WHERE THIS RELATIONSHIP ACTUALLY STANDS block and it threw 'm is not defined' on every generation -- I referenced m.inboundCount and m.outboundCount, neither of which exists anywhere in the file. v9.7.579 corrected the field names, and log140 then showed the block COMPUTING correctly ('[LP ARC STATE DIAG] ... rendered:3 fact(s)') and STILL failing one line later with 'Assignment to constant variable.' -- because userPrompt is declared const and the block appends to it. Fixed to let. The feature has existed for two builds and has never once reached a model. WHY THE SUITE MISSED BOTH: every assertion in arc-state.test.js exercised _lpBuildArcState and _lpRenderArcState DIRECTLY, and both work -- the log proves it. NOTHING EXERCISED THE SITE THAT CALLS THEM. That is the same lesson as the v7.64 proxy ReferenceError and the v1.20 reporter orphan, for the third time: a suite that proves the parts exist does not prove the thing runs. New assertions pin the WIRING -- the binding the block appends to must be mutable, the voicemail prompt's own const is asserted untouched, the append is guarded, and the fields read must be ones that exist rather than the two v9.7.578 invented. Run against v9.7.579 the suite now fails. VERIFIED 22/22 in arc-state plus all 38 suites green (1,320 assertions), dev===comm. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: prompts get roughly six lines longer and now carry the spent-angle inventory for the first time. That is the v9.7.578 intent finally taking effect, so the next capture is the first one where its behaviour can actually be judged. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.579-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.579-dev (DEV. A DAY THE CUSTOMER ALREADY SPENT IS NOT A DAY THEY ARE AVAILABLE -- PLUS MY OWN v9.7.578 FEATURE HAD NEVER RUN. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.21 unchanged. INCIDENT -- FIDEL (Community Toyota Baytown, 8/24). His own inbound text, verbatim: 'Hey Joseph yes I talked to my wife and we love the truck we actually stopped by Saturday around 8:30pm but it was too late I was able to show her which one'. He CAME. On Saturday. After closing. He showed his wife the truck through the glass and is now asking to finish the numbers on a 2020 Tacoma trade. LP read the word Saturday and injected 'CUSTOMER NAMED A SPECIFIC DAY -- THIS IS A HARD CONSTRAINT: Customer said Saturday is when they are available. LOCK IN Saturday - do NOT offer any other day... Do NOT try to pull them in sooner.' The model obeyed it exactly and offered 10:00 AM or 1:00 PM Saturday August 29 -- FIVE DAYS OUT, to a customer who was ready that day. THIS IS THE JERI SHAPE WITH A NEW CAUSE. Jeri's Monday came from a Yahoo timestamp and Hayden's Ram came from the letters inside 'Timeframe' -- in both the text was not the customer's. HERE THE WORDS ARE GENUINELY HIS AND THE DAY IS GENUINELY A DAY. The error is TENSE, which nothing checked. 'stopped by Saturday' is a report of a visit that happened, not a statement of when he can come. COMPREHENSION MADE THE SAME MISTAKE -- the row is AGREE-FIRED, both readers firing on 'Saturday'. So the rule ships in BOTH: a deterministic past-visit guard in the regex path and the same rule written into the day-lock probe prompt. A guard in one reader would have left the other free to make the identical call, which is the whole reason Phase 3 runs two readers. THE GUARD'S FIRST VERSION WAS WORSE THAN THE BUG, and the existing suite caught it in one run: matching verb STEMS meant 'come' matched both 'came by Saturday' (past) and 'I'll come by Monday' (future), so two genuine locks went silent. SUPPRESSING A REAL DAY-LOCK IS WORSE THAN THE FALSE ONE THIS FIXES -- a customer who named a day and gets ignored is a worse outcome than one pushed to the wrong day. The verb list is now INFLECTED PAST FORMS ONLY (stopped, came, swung, went, drove, showed up, was/were there, got there), every alternative is word-boundary anchored so 'was' cannot match inside 'always', and the day must sit within twenty characters of the verb in either order. AND THE OTHER HALF OF THIS BUILD IS MINE FROM THIS MORNING: v9.7.578's situation-read block HAD NEVER RENDERED, NOT ONCE. It read `m.inboundCount` and `m.outboundCount` -- `m` does not exist at that scope and NEITHER FIELD EXISTS ANYWHERE IN THE FILE. I invented both. Every generation since it shipped logged '[LP ARC STATE DIAG] threw, no facts added: m is not defined' and added nothing to the prompt. The feature's own try/catch is the only reason it was visible at all, which is the diagnostic doing the job the feature could not. The real fields are sig.lastInboundAgeDays and sig.consecutiveOutboundNoReply. There is NO exported inbound/outbound total, so that line is DROPPED rather than sourced from a guess -- emitting a fact I cannot trace is precisely what that block exists not to do. A SEPARATE PRE-EXISTING GAP, FOUND AND VERIFIED NOT TO BE MINE: 'I am free Sunday if you are open' produces no lock, and does not on the build from BEFORE this guard either. The base future-day regex has never recognised 'I am free <day>' as availability. Recorded in-suite so it is not later mistaken for a regression from this change, and so it can be picked up on its own terms. A KNOWN LIMIT, NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: a message carrying BOTH a past visit and a future day ('we stopped by Saturday but can come Wednesday instead') suppresses both. The guard is deliberately conservative there. The cost is a missed lock on a rare compound message; the alternative is what happened to Fidel. Under-firing is the safe side of THIS trade and the suite asserts the behaviour so it stays a choice. VERIFIED 63/63 in the sched-attribution suite plus all 38 suites green (1,316 assertions), dev===comm on every case. Fidel's real message is asserted to produce no lock and no constraint at all; five past-visit shapes are asserted suppressed; four genuine availability statements are asserted to STILL lock; Jeri and every prior day-lock case still pass unchanged. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: fewer day-locks. A day-lock is a hard constraint that removes the model's judgement, so firing it less often is the conservative direction -- and the four genuine-availability assertions are the floor that stops it going too far. The v9.7.578 situation read now actually renders for the first time, which DOES change prompts: that was the intent of that build and it has simply not been true until now. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.578-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.578-dev (DEV. THE TAG IS GONE. LP READS THE ARC AND STATES WHAT IS TRUE; THE MODEL DECIDES THE MESSAGE. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.21 unchanged. WHAT v9.7.577 GOT WRONG, and it is worth naming because the fix is the opposite of the mistake: it computed which cadence TAG applied and fed '[LP: value]' to the model. That removed the agent's paste but KEPT THE TAG AS THE MECHANISM -- and the tag is the workaround, a one-word directive standing in for a situation nobody had described. Every serious incident this month was a layer manufacturing a directive and the model correctly obeying it: 'LOCK IN Monday' from a Yahoo timestamp, 'were you looking for a Ram?' from the letters inside 'Timeframe', a condolence from the words 'is passing'. Automating the tag would have automated exactly that shape. WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD: a WHERE THIS RELATIONSHIP ACTUALLY STANDS block of OBSERVATIONS -- lead age, the inbound/outbound split, how long since the customer replied and how many unanswered sends since, where the 90-day schedule has arrived, WHICH ANGLES LP'S OWN PRIOR MESSAGES HAVE ALREADY SPENT, and which have not been raised at all. It contains no instruction and no imperative; a test asserts that. The system prompt already carries the judgement rules and is tuned -- this supplies the facts those rules operate on, which is precisely what the tag was standing in for. EVERY CLAIM CARRIES ITS EVIDENCE, and that is the safety property. An angle is never asserted as a judgement ('you used the trade angle') but as a quotation ('trade (08/12, your words: \"appraisal\")'). The model can therefore DISAGREE with a bad reading instead of inheriting it -- a label cannot be argued with, a quote can. Angles are matched by literal phrase presence in LP'S OWN OUTBOUND ONLY, never in customer text, so a customer asking about their trade can never register as us having raised it; asserted. THE 90-DAY TASK LIST KEEPS ITS JOB and loses the other one: it says WHEN to touch and on which channel, and its [LP: ...] annotations are ignored. The cadence position computed in v9.7.577 survives as ONE OBSERVED FACT ('the day-41 touch of the 90-day sequence') which says where the schedule arrived and does not say what to write. _lpRenderCadenceTouch is now a dead stub returning '', so tag injection cannot come back by accident; asserted. A REAL DEFECT THE SUITE CAUGHT: the price angle matched a bare dollar amount, so '$500 down' registered as BOTH payment and price -- every finance message would have reported a price angle it never used, and the model would have avoided a lever that was still available. A dollar sign cannot distinguish the two; the words can. Bare-amount matching removed. AND ONE TEST FAULT OF MINE, recorded because it reads exactly like the bug it was hunting: the no-imperatives assertion flagged the FACTUAL sentence 'the customer has never replied to anything on this lead' on the word 'never'. The block was clean; the test was wrong. It now matches verb-initial commands and modal instructions rather than adverbs. HONEST LIMIT, DISCLOSED IN THE BLOCK ITSELF: the v9.7.570 outboundSends carrier holds the 8 most recent sends, so an angle used earlier than those will not appear. Rather than implying the history is complete, the block says so in its own words when the cap is hit. Raising the cap doubles an already 16k-char payload and was not worth it for angles that are, by definition, stale. SHIPPED ON. LEADPRO_ARC_STATE is TRUE and this one DOES change the prompt, deliberately -- it is the feature. The failure mode is mild and visible: a wrong angle match makes the model avoid a lever that was still available, and because the evidence is printed beside it, both the model and the log can see the mistake. [LP ARC STATE DIAG] reports every count and both lists, and [LP ARC ANGLE] prints each angle with the exact words that produced it. VERIFIED 18/18 in a new arc-state suite plus all 38 suites green (1,303 assertions), dev===comm on every case. Asserted: angles carry date and verbatim evidence; the newest use of a repeated angle is the one named; customer text is never scanned; the rendered block contains no imperative; a never-replied lead is stated as fact not strategy; cadence position renders without any role word in it; the 8-send cap is disclosed; the tag renderer is a dead stub; and nothing injects a bare [LP: role] tag anywhere. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: the prompt grows by roughly six lines and the model now has an explicit inventory of spent angles, so drafts should repeat themselves less. If an angle regex over-matches, the visible cost is a lever the model leaves unused -- annoying, not dangerous, and the evidence string beside it makes the cause obvious from one log line. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the arc code is outside inlineScraper, asserted. Builds on v9.7.577-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.577-dev (DEV. PHASE 1: THE 90-DAY CADENCE POSITION IS COMPUTED, LOGGED, AND NOT YET INJECTED. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.21 unchanged. WHAT IT DOES: works out where a lead sits in the 90-day BDC cadence from data LP already has -- lead age and its own prior outbound history -- and emits the SAME role tag an agent pastes by hand today. A CORRECTION I HAVE TO LEAD WITH, because I got it wrong mid-build and it changed the design. I first built this against LeadPro_90Day_LP_Commands.md, whose 16 touches carry long prose blocks, and reported that the brief's verification gate could not be satisfied because ZERO captured prompts contained a cadence command -- dismissing the five that WERE captured (['value'] x3, ['curiosity'] x2) as mere role tags. THOSE ARE THE CADENCE COMMANDS. The live VinSolutions task list pastes exactly [LP: value], [LP: curiosity] and [LP: soft] -- nothing longer. So verification data does exist, and the .md is a design document rather than the thing that runs. THE LIVE CADENCE ALSO DIFFERS FROM THE DOCUMENT, and the live one governs: 15 LP-commanded touches at days 4, 7, 8, 13, 15, 24, 28, 33, 41, 45, 53, 64, 68, 78, 82. The doc's day-2 touch carries no LP command in the real list, and day 33 is [LP: value] in the live cadence where the doc does not mark it VALUE. The table is transcribed from the live list, and the suite holds it against that transcription rather than against the code. WHY THE ROLE TAG IS THE RIGHT OUTPUT, and it made the build much smaller: the system prompt's TOUCH ROLE section already defines value, curiosity and soft, already says a tagged touch is honoured in FULL regardless of phase, and is already tuned. Auto-selection therefore authors NOTHING NEW -- it emits the three characters of intent the agent would have typed and every downstream behaviour is unchanged. Injecting the .md prose blocks instead would have introduced a second, competing set of instructions for the same slot. RECONCILIATION, AND ITS ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT. Day-count alone is wrong whenever a lead drifts, so prior-touch-count wins when both are readable: a lead that fell behind lands on its correct NEXT touch, and one that ran ahead is not double-counted. THE FIRST VERSION CAPPED THE DISAGREEMENT SYMMETRICALLY AT THREE TOUCHES AND ITS OWN TEST CAUGHT THAT AS WRONG -- the touches cluster early (days 4, 7, 8, 13, 15 is five in a fortnight), so a lead two weeks behind is four touches behind, and a symmetric cap refuses the single case the brief most wants handled. Skew in touch-index is not skew in days. Falling behind is now never refused; running more than two touches AHEAD of what the calendar allows is, because that is what an over-counting bug looks like -- a reply to a live customer counted as a scheduled touch. PHONE CALLS ARE NOT CADENCE TOUCHES. The count reads dated [AGENT] Outbound Text Message and Email entries off the transcript and excludes call entries, which would otherwise over-advance every lead the BDC actually dials. It reads the transcript rather than the v9.7.570 outboundSends carrier, which is capped at 8 notes for the delivery matcher and would silently under-count any lead past roughly day 15. SHIPPED ADDITIVE AND OFF. LEADPRO_CADENCE_AUTO is FALSE: the position is computed and logged on every generation and injected on none, which produces the comparison sample the brief's verification gate asks for, on live traffic, with no customer-facing change. When it is armed, a manual paste still wins -- a role tag present means the human has chosen -- while an agent CONTENT command ('Need POI, how much down') does NOT suppress it, because those occupy different slots. [LP CADENCE DIAG] FIRES ON EVERY GENERATION whatever happened, popup-side, reporting lead age, prior touch count, the selected touch, the confidence, THE REASONING (day-count match vs prior-touch reconciliation vs refusal), whether a manual paste overrode it, and whether anything was injected. Every silent-fact bug this cycle was found late because the diagnostic was missing or lived frame-side; this one ships with full visibility from day one. PHASE 2 IS NOT BUILT. The VALUE FACT slot is untouched and still awaits a human. Auto-sourcing it is a separate, higher-risk question and the brief asks for an investigation before any code -- delivered separately, not here. A test asserts no auto-value-fact machinery exists anywhere in this build. VERIFIED 32/32 in a new cadence suite plus all 37 suites green (1,284 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The table is asserted against an independent transcription of the LIVE cadence; only the three real roles ever appear; the seven [LP: value] days are asserted exactly; texts and emails count and phone calls do not; two sends the same day on the same channel count once; behind reconciles and implausibly-ahead refuses; before day 4, past day 90 and all-15-sent each select nothing; a role tag suppresses and a content command does not; and the diagnostic is asserted to report all seven fields including on the select-nothing path. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing. With the flag off the only change is one log line per generation. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the cadence code is outside inlineScraper, asserted. Builds on v9.7.576-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.576-dev (DEV. OFF-FRANCHISE IS ABOUT NEW CARS, AND THE PROBE DID NOT KNOW THAT. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.21 unchanged. THE INCIDENT: 8/24 produced exactly two DISAGREE-COMPREHENSION-ONLY rows and BOTH were FALSE POSITIVES where the regex was correctly silent -- the reverse of the Gladiator day. (1) lead 2049868669, Audi Lafayette: comprehension flagged 'Hyundai' on the quote 'Is your Used 2025 Hyundai Sonata SEL listed for $23,448.00 still available?' -- the customer asking about OUR listed unit at OUR price. (2) lead 2072414751, Honda Baytown: comprehension flagged 'BMW' when the lead's own VOI IS a 2024 BMW 4 Series M440i xDrive (Pre-Owned), confirmed in stock, Stock #TA035651A. Neither customer was shopping another franchise; both were asking about a car the store has on its lot. THE PRINCIPLE, and it is the operator's framing rather than mine: A FRANCHISE CONSTRAINT IS ABOUT NEW CARS. A Honda store cannot sell, order or dealer-trade a NEW BMW. It sells USED ones off its own lot constantly, mostly trades. So 'the customer named a make we do not franchise' was never the question -- 'the customer wants a NEW car we cannot source' is. THE REGEX READER HAS HAD THIS RIGHT SINCE IT SHIPPED: `if (_ofSaidNew || (_ofUnits && _ofMatch.length === 0))` -- fire only when they said NEW near the make, or when we hold zero units of it. The COMPREHENSION probe had none of it; its prompt asks the strictly broader 'did the customer ask about a different manufacturer?', which both customers truthfully had. The model was not misreading anything. It was answering a question that is not the one this detector is for -- the manufactured-directive class, one level up. A VOI-MAKE COMPARISON WOULD HAVE FIXED ONLY ONE OF THE TWO, and this is why the obvious cheap fix was rejected: it catches the BMW (VOI make matches) and sails straight past the Hyundai, whose lead carries NO VOI AT ALL -- [LP VOI DIAG] parsed:\"\" on every frame in log138. The gate has to be inventory-and-condition aware, not VOI-aware. Asserted in-suite so it is not proposed again. THE FIX, AND IT IS AN EXTRACTION RATHER THAN A NEW IMPLEMENTATION: the two conditions the regex already applied are lifted verbatim into _lpOffFranchiseGate(make, text, dealerId, cache) -- ONE definition, asserted exactly one. The regex path now CALLS it (its behaviour is unchanged; the same two tests it always passed still pass) and the comprehension path calls it from the detector's fired() predicate, so a probe answer is recorded verbatim but only COUNTS as fired when the gate agrees. That is the _lpCustomerAuthoredPart lesson: the correct logic already existed, so make the second reader consult it rather than write a copy that can drift. The probe PROMPT also states the rule now, so the model is asked the right question in the first place rather than merely corrected afterwards -- the deterministic gate remains the guarantee. Both readers log [LP OFF-FRANCHISE GATE] with the verdict AND the reason, so a suppression is never silent. INVENTORY UNKNOWN DOES NOT FIRE, deliberately: a failed valuefact fetch must not manufacture an off-franchise directive. That is the v9.7.483 posture and the safer side of the trade -- a missed catch costs a weaker message, a false catch tells a customer we cannot sell them a car we have on the lot. THREE FAULTS FOUND WHILE BUILDING IT, all mine and all caught by tests rather than review. (1) THE SCOPE TRAP AGAIN: the first version logged via _lpD, which is declared INSIDE inlineScraper and does not exist at module scope where the regex block runs -- a ReferenceError that would have killed the prompt build, the v9.7.455/228 trap and the same shape as the v7.64 proxy crash. Now console.log, and asserted. (2) THE GATE LANDED EIGHT LINES ABOVE THE SPAN the off-franchise suite extracts, so the regex path called an undefined function and the suite's own CONTROL test went silent -- relocated inside the span and the boundary is asserted. (3) THE HELPER REACHED FOR A GLOBAL IT DID NOT NAME: it read module-scope _lpValueFactCache, while the suite wraps the guard in a function declaring its OWN local one, so the gate saw no inventory and never fired -- and its try/catch turned that into a quiet fires:false that reads exactly like the feature working. The cache is now an ARGUMENT. Production was never affected by any of the three, but the third is a real design fault and the fix is strictly better. VERIFIED 61/61 in the off-franchise suite (17 new) plus all 36 suites green (1,252 assertions), dev===comm on every case. NON-VACUOUS: run against v9.7.575 the suite fails 17 ways. Both 8/24 leads are asserted by name to stop firing; a NEW off-brand ask is asserted to fire EVEN WHEN we hold used units of that make; the Gladiator shape (off-brand, zero units) still fires; unknown inventory does not; 'new' must sit near the make so 'I am new to the area' does not trigger it; chevy/vw/benz aliases resolve; the gate is asserted defined ONCE, called by BOTH readers, handed the cache, living outside inlineScraper, and logging through console.log rather than _lpD. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: the regex path's behaviour is unchanged -- same two conditions, now in a named function -- and its two existing tests prove it. The comprehension path gets STRICTER: it will fire less often, and the rows it stops firing on are the ones that were wrong. Every authoritative flag is still OFF, so no customer-facing message changes either way. Off-franchise remains the detector to flip LAST: it now has two documented false positives where the regex was right, and its failure mode is promising a customer something we cannot deliver. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the gate is outside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own marker. Builds on v9.7.575-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.575-dev (DEV. THE REGEN REASONING ESCALATION IS REMOVED. LATENCY WINS. Extension only; proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.19 unchanged and NOT redeployed. WHAT IS GONE: v9.7.573 raised a regen's draft to reasoningEffort:'high'. The TRIGGER reasoning still holds -- a regen is the one signal on this surface that is a human decision rather than a heuristic, because a person read the draft and judged the first read wrong. The COST did not survive contact with real numbers. MEASURED, on this prompt shape (~62k chars) against gpt-5.6-luna: 'low' reaches a finished draft in about 6.4s; 'high' was observed at 13.9s, 14.4s, 15.1s and 17.1s SUCCESSFUL, plus timeouts at the 18000ms ceiling. The escalation therefore roughly TRIPLED the wait an agent feels on the one interaction where they are already unhappy, and 18000ms was never a comfortable ceiling -- a 17.1s success lands with under a second of headroom, which puts the budget in the MIDDLE of the tail rather than past it. That is why the answer was not to buy more budget again. AND NOTHING EVER ARRIVED TO PAY FOR IT. Every escalated sample captured was a FORCED test rather than natural agent behaviour, so the feedback pipeline never produced a quality comparison in either direction. The operator's read is that Luna is good enough at 'low' and that medium and high make little difference to output quality on this task -- which is consistent with the complete absence of any measured gain. Eleven extra seconds for a difference nobody can measure is the wrong trade, so the experiment is dropped rather than re-tuned to 'medium'. THE CONSTANTS ARE REMOVED, NOT SWITCHED OFF. LEADPRO_REGEN_ESCALATE and LEADPRO_REGEN_EFFORT are deleted along with the payload block. A dormant flag invites someone to flip it back without re-reading the numbers above; deleting it makes re-introduction a deliberate act. A regen now sends NO effort field at all -- byte-identical to a first-pass draft -- and takes the worker default of 'low'. The two PROBE payloads are untouched and still ask for 'none'. WHAT DELIBERATELY STAYS, and it is the important half: THE PROXY RAILS. v7.63's primary-tier-only guard and v7.64's escalated primary budget are NOT experiment scaffolding. They protect ANY caller from re-creating the v9.7.219 failure -- 'medium reasoning blew through the entire cascade, PRIMARY timing out at 10000ms, FALLBACK also timing out, leads landing on SAFE_FALLBACK after 23s' -- which happened because the elevated effort applied to EVERY tier, so the recovery tiers were reasoning hard too and could not cover the hole the primary left. With nothing escalating today those rails simply go quiet, and a quiet rail is exactly the kind that rots unnoticed, so every one is still asserted. The proxy is NOT redeployed for this build. VERIFIED 26/26 in the rebuilt regen-effort suite plus all 35 suites green (1,208 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The suite is asserted NON-VACUOUS: run against the v9.7.573 build it fails 5 ways, so a re-introduction cannot pass silently. Asserted: both constants absent; ZERO assignments of reasoningEffort onto the draft payload; the draft's generationConfig literal does not mention the field; the removal rationale carries the measured numbers so it is not re-litigated from scratch; the probes still ask for 'none' and are the ONLY place the extension names an effort at all; and separately, every proxy rail still resolves correctly -- an above-baseline effort still reaches the primary tier ONLY, no caller-named effort can slow any tier below primary, and 'none' still reaches every tier because it makes recovery faster rather than slower. THREE OF MY OWN TEST ERRORS, RECORDED BECAUSE THEY ARE THE SAME SHAPE. Two new assertions scanned raw source and matched THEIR OWN explanatory prose -- a scan for reasoningEffort hit the paragraph explaining why reasoningEffort was removed. Fixed with a shared stripComments helper rather than another one-off, since this file has now made that mistake three times. The third was worse and was in the VERIFICATION step: the first non-vacuity run pointed at a fixture named old573.js, which does not match the suite's /popup\.js$/ argument filter, so the suite exited on usage and printed zero failures -- and zero failures read as 'the test does not catch it'. A vacuous check of a vacuous check. Re-run from a correctly named path it fails 5 ways as intended. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: regens get FASTER and nothing else changes. Non-regen traffic was already byte-identical. No proxy or reporter change is needed and none should be deployed for this build. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.574-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.574-dev (DEV. NOTHING TO READ IS AN ANSWER, NOT A FAILURE. Pairs with proxy v7.66 and reporter v1.19. THE REPORT WAS CALLING THE MOST COMMON LEAD STATE A DEFECT. On 8/23, 42 of 97 comprehension rows landed under the tile 'Probe never answered', which reads as 42 broken probe calls. NOT ONE was broken. Those are leads where the customer has NEVER WRITTEN ANYTHING -- Malaka Phillips (Community Honda Lafayette, 8/23) is the type case: 1 inbound / 7 outbound, every note either an outbound text or a content-free call note ('hung up', 'No'). Zero-customer-response is one of the most common states in a BDC queue, not an edge case. WHY IT WAS NEVER A CORRECTNESS BUG, stated plainly because it is the thing that matters: a customer who has never written anything CANNOT have named a day or mentioned an off-brand vehicle. The absence of input IS a determinate 'none', reached correctly and without spending an API call -- which is why those probes settle in 0ms. The regex finds nothing on these leads either, so no directive is fabricated, and an authoritative flag being ON would produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL message. That last point also answers the sampling worry: drawing the comprehension corpus only from customer-replied leads is not a bias, because those are the ONLY leads where flipping a flag can change anything. TWO REAL DEFECTS FIXED. (1) ONE HARDCODED REASON STRING FOR THREE DETECTORS WITH THREE DIFFERENT INPUTS. The abstention returned 'no customer-authored notes to read' for all of them -- and verbal-commit does not read customer-authored text AT ALL. It reads CALL NOTES, an agent's record of what the customer said aloud, which is the entire point of that detector. On Malaka it abstained because every call note was content-free, and the log blamed a missing customer note. Each detector now carries its own emptyLabel and states what IT was missing. (2) 'NOTHING TO READ' WAS ROUTED THROUGH THE SAME _fail() AS A TIMEOUT, A BAD JSON BODY OR A MISSING ENDPOINT, so a correct answer was indistinguishable from a broken call. A new vacuous marker separates them, a new NO-CUSTOMER-TEXT delta carries it to the proxy, and NO-COMPREHENSION-VERDICT now means what its name says: the probe WAS asked and did not answer, which is a defect worth chasing. IT STAYS OUT OF THE AGREEMENT RATE, deliberately. A row where there was nothing to comprehend is not a test of comprehension, and folding it in would inflate the rate with rows that tested nothing. It is now labelled truthfully instead of being filed next to real failures. The 83% agreement figure on the 8/23 report was already computed correctly; only the exclusion's NAME was wrong. PROXY v7.66 accepts the delta and persists a structural `vacuous` flag so the reporter never has to pattern-match reason prose. REPORTER v1.19 splits the single tile in two -- 'Probe never answered' stays RED and now reads 'asked, no answer -- a defect'; a new grey 'No customer text' tile reads 'nothing to read -- correct none, excluded from both rates'. A DOUBLE-COUNT CAUGHT BEFORE IT SHIPPED: a vacuous row also carries probeOk:false, so without an explicit exclusion the SAME 42 rows would have appeared in both the red tile and the grey one. The reporter now excludes them from unverifiedProbe, accepting EITHER the structural flag or the delta so it works across the version boundary. Rows written before v9.7.574 carry neither and are deliberately NOT reclassified after the fact -- they cannot be, and they are not guessed at. AN EXISTING ASSERTION CAUGHT MY GAP, which is the reporter v1.18 lesson repeating: 'every delta the proxy accepts is classified by the reporter as exactly one of agree / disagree / neither' failed with ['NO-CUSTOMER-TEXT'] because I added a delta without placing it. It is now the FOURTH not-a-comparison delta, named in the positive list rather than left to fall through. VERIFIED 89/89 in the Phase 3 suite (6 new) plus all 35 suites green (1,209 assertions), dev===comm on every case. Asserted: a vacuous lead yields NO-CUSTOMER-TEXT with vacuous:true; a REAL probe failure still yields NO-COMPREHENSION-VERDICT with vacuous:false, so the two names now mean different things; the three emptyLabels are distinct and verbal-commit's names call notes; the vacuous path makes ZERO api calls; a vacuous row is still POSTED so the lead state stays countable; and the reporter's double-count exclusion is pinned by its exact shape -- the OLD text is deliberately not asserted, because re-pinning it would have re-pinned the bug. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing, and no rate moves. The persisted row gains one boolean; both key-set assertions were updated on purpose rather than loosened. If the proxy is deployed WITHOUT the extension, no NO-CUSTOMER-TEXT rows are produced and the report behaves exactly as it does today. If the EXTENSION ships without the proxy, the new delta is rejected by the allow-list and those rows are dropped -- so DEPLOY THE PROXY FIRST. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.573-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.573-dev (DEV. A REGEN THINKS HARDER, AND ONLY THE PRIMARY TIER DOES. Pairs with proxy v7.63; reporter v1.18 unchanged. THE TRIGGER: a regen is not a heuristic guess at difficulty -- it is DIRECT EVIDENCE that a human read the draft and judged the first reading wrong. It is the only signal on this surface that is a human decision rather than an inference, which is why it ships and a flag-count 'lead difficulty score' does not. That would be a heuristic standing in for judgment upstream of the model -- the same shape as every manufactured-directive bug this month -- and its failures would be INVISIBLE: an under-reasoned hard lead produces a slightly worse draft that an agent edits and sends, which the feedback pipeline records as a success. Note also that activeFlags is NOT the auto-detected situation set; it is the agent's manual chip set (trade, distance, credit, loyalty, stalled), so a flag-count tier would need a collection that does not exist yet and must be costed as a real build, not a freebie. PRIOR ART, AND IT NEARLY MADE THIS A BAD IDEA. v9.7.219 removed a complexity reasoning router because 'medium reasoning blew through the entire cascade -- PRIMARY timing out at 10000ms, FALLBACK also timing out, leads landing on SAFE_FALLBACK after 23s'. So 'medium' is not an untried middle rung: it has been run on this exact task and it exhausted all three tiers. But the root cause was NOT that medium is too slow for the primary tier -- it was that the elevated effort applied to EVERY tier, so the recovery tiers were reasoning hard too and could not cover the hole the primary left. An expensive draft became no draft at all. THE FIX THAT MAKES ESCALATION SURVIVABLE (proxy v7.63): an effort ABOVE the baseline is PRIMARY-TIER ONLY. A regen gets one high-effort attempt on the full primary budget; if it times out the fallback answers at 'low' and the agent still gets a real draft. The v9.7.219 failure mode is now structurally unreachable -- a slow escalation costs latency, never a SAFE_FALLBACK. An effort AT OR BELOW the baseline (the probes' 'none') still applies to every tier, because it makes the recovery tiers FASTER, not slower; asserted separately so the guard cannot swallow the probes. WHY 'high' AND NOT 'medium': this is a FIRST EXPERIMENT asking whether more reasoning helps LP's drafting task AT ALL, and regens are rare -- zero in log132's 8 generations -- so a one-rung change risks an effect too small to read against that sample. Maximum contrast answers the question cheapest: if 'high' shows nothing then 'medium' almost certainly shows nothing and the question is closed; if it helps, walk it DOWN to find the cheapest level that keeps the gain. This is explicitly NOT a claim that 'high' is the right steady state. A PREMISE WORTH CORRECTING, because it is easy to get backwards: 'medium' is not the model default. The GPT-5.4/5.6 family default is 'none', and LP's draft baseline is 'low' set by the WORKER (the popup sends no effort field for drafts). So this is a two-rung move from LP's real baseline, not a nudge to a default. WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS WRONG, STATED UP FRONT: every incident this month has been the model OVER-INFERRING -- a day-lock from a Yahoo header, a bereavement from 'is passing', an off-franchise from the letters inside 'Timeframe'. More reasoning on a task whose failures are hallucinated inference could plausibly make it WORSE. The experiment must be able to show that, so [LP REGEN EFFORT] logs the level on every regen and the outcome is readable in the existing feedback pipeline. Two constants control it: LEADPRO_REGEN_EFFORT sets the level and LEADPRO_REGEN_ESCALATE switches it off. A REAL DEFECT MY OWN SUITE CAUGHT, and it is the fourth of this shape: the first version of the v7.63 guard ran the escalation clamp BEFORE the 'tier takes no reasoning_effort' test, so the non-reasoning emergency tier (gpt-4.1-nano) would have been sent reasoning_effort:'low' -- reintroducing one layer up the exact bad-field-on-a-non-reasoning-model failure that v7.61 added TIER_EFFORTS to prevent. Order now fixed and commented; caught by a test, not by review. VERIFIED 19/19 in a new regen-effort suite plus all 34 suites green (1,172 assertions), dev===comm on every case. Asserted: the escalation is gated on _isRegenSession and nothing else; it is the ONLY assignment of reasoningEffort onto the draft payload, so a non-regen still sends no field; 'high' reaches the primary tier intact; that same 'high' does NOT reach fallback or emergency, and the note explains why and cites v9.7.219; NO effort a caller can name can slow any tier below primary (medium/high/xhigh/max all resolve to 'low' there); 'none' is NOT treated as an escalation and still reaches every tier; isEscalation is asserted to be a real comparison against the baseline rather than a hardcoded list; and the pre-v7.63 behaviour is REPRODUCED in-test to prove the guard is what changed it. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: only regens change, and only their primary-tier attempt. Worst case is a regen that takes the full primary budget, times out, and is answered by the fallback at 'low' -- slower than today, but a real draft rather than a placeholder. Non-regen traffic is byte-identical. DEPLOY THE PROXY FIRST -- against v7.62 or older there is no tier guard, so 'high' would reach the fallback tier and that is precisely the v9.7.219 configuration. node --check clean on both builds and the proxy; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.572-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.572-dev (DEV. THE DISAGREEMENTS THAT MATTER MOST HAD THE LEAST DETAIL IN THE LOG. Extension only; proxy v7.61 and reporter v1.18 unchanged. FOUND IN log132 (8/23, 8 generations on v9.7.571 with proxy v7.61 live), which is the first batch carrying real disagreement data: 24 rows, 4 disagreements, and THREE OF THE FOUR were DISAGREE-COMPREHENSION-ONLY -- the probe found a fact the regex missed, which is the single most interesting thing this programme can observe. Every one of those three produced a telemetry row and NO DIAGNOSTIC LINE AT ALL. WHY: [LP FACT DIAG] is written by _lpFactDecide, and _lpFactDecide only runs when the REGEX fired or a fenced directive existed. On a comprehension-only disagreement neither is true, so the decision function never runs and nothing in the log says WHAT the probe found. Adjudicating those three would have meant opening each lead by hand -- and adjudicating them is exactly what Phase C is. This is the same diagnostic gap fixed four times this week (v9.7.563 verbal-commit, v9.7.565 day-lock, v9.7.567 bereavement, v9.7.570 email signature), sitting in the one place that decides whether any authoritative flag ever flips. THE FIX: a new [LP FACT DETAIL] line written from _lpFlushFactTelemetry, which already runs unconditionally once per generation and already holds both readings. For every row that is not a plain AGREE-NONE it reports the comprehension kind, its VERIFIED QUOTE and which note of how many it was verified against, what the regex found, whether the detector is authoritative, and whether a decision was reached at all. AGREE-NONE stays silent so the majority case adds no noise. CONSOLE ONLY -- the quote is CRM note text and does not enter the persisted row, which still carries lengths and locators exactly as before; the row's own key set is asserted unchanged. ALSO CONFIRMED FROM log132, and worth recording because it settles two open questions. (1) THE v9.7.570 SIGNATURE FIX IS LIVE AND THE CONDITION IS COMMON, NOT RARE: 8 of 8 generations logged 'agent home store differs from lead store' -- profile store Community Toyota Baytown, lead store Community Honda Lafayette, DEALER_ID_MAP used every time. Under v9.7.569 all eight emails would have signed the wrong rooftop. The 8/22 incident was not an edge case; it is what happens whenever an agent works a lead outside their home store. (2) PROBE LATENCY UNDER reasoningEffort:'none' with proxy v7.61 deployed: 849-1289ms across seven generations plus one 3204ms cold start, against 991-1955ms at 'low' in log131. Suggestive of a real improvement, but n=8 with an outlier is not a measurement and it is recorded as a direction, not a result. THE DISAGREEMENT SHAPE SO FAR, stated with its sample size: 21 comparable rows, 4 disagreements -- verbal-commit 1 of 5, day-lock 1 of 8, off-franchise 2 of 8. Three of four are comprehension-only; the lone exception is a day-lock DISAGREE-REGEX-ONLY. Two batches now point the same direction, which is a hint and not a verdict, and the whole point of this build is that the next batch will be readable without opening leads. VERIFIED 84/84 in the Phase 3 suite (6 new) plus all 33 suites green (1,150 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The comprehension-only shape is asserted end to end -- named, quoted, note-verified, regex reported as 'found nothing', and explicitly marked 'no decision reached this generation'. AGREE-NONE is asserted to write nothing; a three-row mix is asserted to write exactly three lines; an unusable probe reports its reason; a regex that never RAN is asserted to say so rather than read as 'found nothing' (the v9.7.563 distinction); and the persisted row's key set is asserted UNCHANGED so the new line cannot have widened the payload. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing and nothing persisted. The cost is up to three extra console lines per generation on leads that produce a non-AGREE-NONE row -- on log132's traffic that would have been 9 lines across 8 generations. If that proves noisy the threshold is one condition. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.571-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.571-dev (DEV. THE PROBES NO LONGER PAY TO THINK, AND A REJECTED REASONING EFFORT IS NO LONGER INVISIBLE. Pairs with proxy v7.61; reporter v1.18 unchanged. Three changes from the GPT-5.6 API guidance, plus one harness fault the work uncovered. (1) THE PROBES ASK FOR reasoningEffort:'none'. Each fact probe asks ONE extraction question and must return a quote copied character-for-character out of a named note -- and that quote is then MECHANICALLY VERIFIED as a literal substring before it is usable. The safety net is _lpVerifyCommitQuote, not the model deliberating, so reasoning effort buys nothing and costs latency an agent feels: three probes run in parallel IN FRONT of the draft, measured at 904-1955ms on live traffic (log131). BOTH probe payloads are changed -- the Phase 3 probe and the superseded v9.7.559 observer that is still the fallback when Phase 3's verbal-commit comprehension is switched off. Leaving that one on 'low' would have made the fallback path quietly cost more than the primary for no benefit. THE DRAFT IS UNTOUCHED and keeps 'low'. (2) THE PROXY WAS SILENTLY SWAPPING ANY UNRECOGNISED EFFORT FOR 'low', and this is the THIRD instance of that shape this week. The validation was `(gen.reasoningEffort === 'low' || 'medium' || 'high') ? it : 'low'` -- a three-value allow-list written when three values existed. GPT-5.6 supports none, low, medium, high, xhigh and max, so a caller asking for 'none' got 'low' with NO error and NO log line and would reasonably believe it had shipped. Same class as v7.60's compKind clamp and v7.58's MIN_CONTENT_CHARS floor: a parameter quietly replaced by a different one. Proxy v7.61 widens the set to the model's real one, adds PER-TIER gating (an effort a tier cannot take degrades to its NEAREST supported neighbour rather than 400ing -- a 400 on the FALLBACK tier turns a recoverable primary failure into a SAFE_FALLBACK), omits the field entirely on the non-reasoning emergency tier, and LOGS EVERY SUBSTITUTION with what was asked for, what was used, and why. Silence now means nothing changed. (3) prompt_cache_retention -> prompt_cache_options.ttl, the replacement the guidance names. v7.42 correctly kept the deprecated field away from Luna, which left it split: the PRIMARY tier got NO retention hint at all while the two older tiers still got a deprecated one. Both now get the current field, ttl merged into Luna's existing prompt_cache_options rather than overwriting its mode:'explicit'. The value is '30m', the documented supported value for the 5.6 families, not the old '24h' -- an unsupported ttl risks a 400 on the primary tier. Renamed CACHE_TTL so nothing silently keeps meaning 'retention'. (4) A HARNESS FAULT FOUND WHILE TESTING (2), and it is the same lesson one layer out: safe-fallback-contract.test.js defaulted to a HARDCODED 'worker/cloudflare-worker-v7.58.js' when no proxy argument was passed, and run-all.sh had it in the extension-only loop -- so 68 assertions had been silently running against a THREE-VERSION-OLD proxy and reporting green. The default is removed (a missing proxy is now a loud failure) and the runner hands it the real one. The suite total goes 1,076 -> 1,144 purely because those 68 stopped being skipped. NOT ADOPTED, stated so nobody re-litigates it from the same doc: the RESPONSES API (its benefits are reasoning persistence, tool calling and multi-turn; LP is single-turn with no tools, so a large migration buys nothing it can use), PRO MODE (the guidance explicitly excludes routine, latency-sensitive, high-volume work, which is exactly this workload at ~1,300-1,650 requests/day), and PROGRAMMATIC TOOL CALLING (there are no tools). text.verbosity was ALREADY adopted -- VERBOSITY='low' has shipped on every GPT-5 tier since v7.41. MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, on the guidance's leaner-prompt claim: across all 22 delivered prompts in the repo the mean total prompt is 59,612 chars (~14,900 tokens) and the largest is 92,881 -- but EXACT-DUPLICATE LINES AVERAGE ONLY 594 CHARS, under 1%. So there is no dedup win here; LP's redundancy is semantic, not literal, and the doc's 41-66% figure would require its actual method (remove one group of guidance, rerun evals) rather than a quick pass. Also worth knowing: the USER prompt is 2-3.5x the system prompt, and only the system prompt is cacheable -- so the uncached half is the expensive half and is where any slimming should aim. STILL OPEN, DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED: the draft's own effort (low vs the guidance's suggested medium baseline) and the PRIMARY model (luna is the doc's 'efficient, high-volume' tier, while sol is frontier and terra balanced). Both are real questions with a real trade-off and neither should move on a doc alone -- they want an A/B against the existing feedback pipeline. VERIFIED 68/68 in the extended safe-fallback-contract suite plus all 33 suites green (1,144 assertions), dev===comm on every case. The OLD three-value validation is REPRODUCED in-test and asserted to collapse none/xhigh/max/garbage all to 'low', so the fix is demonstrably what changed it; each of those now resolves to itself on the primary tier; a tier that cannot take an effort is asserted to degrade to the nearest supported value in BOTH directions; the non-reasoning tier is asserted to omit the field; an unknown value is asserted to fall back AND to say so; and a supported effort on a supporting tier is asserted to produce NO note, so silence means nothing changed. prompt_cache_retention is asserted absent from the shipped proxy and mode:'explicit' is asserted to survive the ttl merge. ONE OF MY OWN TEST ERRORS, recorded: the first version of the 'inside generationConfig' assertion used a fixed 900-char lookback that the new comment pushed out of range, and it found only the FIRST of the two probe payloads -- which is how the second one turned out to still be on 'low'. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: if 'none' turns out to degrade probe accuracy, the quote verification will refuse the bad answers rather than let them through -- the failure mode is more PROBE-FAILED rows, not wrong verdicts, and the flags are all still off. The ttl change is the one to watch on deploy: if '30m' is rejected by any tier the primary will 400 and fall to the fallback, which would show immediately as a fallback-hit spike in the daily report. DEPLOY THE PROXY FIRST -- against an older proxy the probes' 'none' silently degrades to 'low', which is exactly today's behaviour. node --check clean on both builds and the proxy; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.570-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.570-dev (DEV. THE EMAIL SIGNED THE AGENT'S HOME STORE, AND PHASE A OF DRAFTED-vs-DELIVERED. Extension only; proxy v7.60 and reporter v1.18 unchanged. PART 1 -- THE SIGNATURE, AND GIL'S INSTINCT WAS RIGHT WITH THE MECHANISM ONE LAYER OVER. LIVE 8/22: eight emails on Community Honda Baytown and Community Kia Baytown leads were signed 'Community Toyota- Baytown'. Gil's read was 'agent info should be hard-coded in LP, this shouldn't have happened' -- and the hard-coding is already there and already correct. PHONE_DIR carries Patricia Galvan under both her names with identical, correct numbers for all three rooftops. ALL 22 DELIVERED PROMPTS IN THE REPO were checked and every EMAIL SIGNATURE block carries the RIGHT store and a phone matching its own Agent Phone line, Patricia's two included. So the data was right and the prompt was right. THE DEFECT IS IN enforceEmailPhone's STORE CHAIN, and it is a lead-scoped line resolved from an agent-scoped value. The chain was `remote dealer-config store.name -> resolvedContext.storeName -> _leadProProfile.store`. Two faults, either sufficient alone: (1) it PREFERRED THE REMOTE DEALER CONFIG over DEALER_ID_MAP, which is where 'Community Toyota- Baytown' comes from, stray hyphen and all -- that exact string appears NOWHERE in this extension, which is precisely why grepping for it found nothing and why it looked like model invention; (2) its last fallback is THE AGENT'S OWN PROFILE STORE, so any resolution miss signs Patricia's home rooftop onto whatever lead she is working. WHY THE SMS WAS NEVER WRONG ON THE SAME GENERATIONS: enforceSmsSig resolves the store from DEALER_ID_MAP -- the hard-coded map in this file -- keyed on the lead's own dealerId. The SMS is right because it is STAMPED BY CODE; the email was wrong because its signature was a request the model could ignore and a fallback chain that could answer wrongly. FIXED to mirror enforceSmsSig exactly: DEALER_ID_MAP first, keyed on THIS lead. The agent-profile fallback is REMOVED OUTRIGHT rather than demoted -- there is no lead on which the agent's home store is the right answer for the lead's signature, so keeping it lower would only make the same bug rarer and harder to find. New [LP EMAIL SIG DIAG] names the store used, its source, and flags when the agent's home store differs from the lead's. THE SAME CHAIN ONE LAYER UP is fixed too: buildSystemPrompt's storeName -- the line that states 'You are <agent> at <store>' -- had the identical fallback. LATENT rather than live (all 22 prompts are correct, so resolvedContext is winning in practice) and fixed anyway because it is the identical defect in the line that defines the agent's store identity. Its existing chain is KEPT beneath the map because buildSystemPrompt also runs on the VM path where no lead is scraped. PART 2 -- PHASE A OF DRAFTED-vs-DELIVERED, THE MATCHER AND ONLY THE MATCHER. THE GAP: Lead Pro scores itself on what it PRODUCED. The agent then edits in VinSolutions and sends. Nothing has ever compared the two -- which is why this very signature defect, on eight of twenty-one emails, read as an 89% positive rate and a 95% shipped rate. Every one counted a success because the agent copied the draft and then quietly corrected it. THE COPY BUTTON MEASURES INTENT TO USE, NOT QUALITY OF WHAT WAS USED. Both halves already existed and neither needed new plumbing: the final draft is captured by v9.7.311's draft pairs, the sent message is a CRM outbound note this file's scraper already walks. They had simply never been put side by side. PHASE A IS DELIBERATELY ONLY THE MATCHER -- no classification, no scoring, no persistence beyond a console line, flag OFF. Pairing a send to the draft that produced it is the entire risk: an agent may send something hand-written, send nothing, send hours later, or send from another tool, and A WRONG PAIRING PRODUCES CONFIDENTLY WRONG EDIT DATA -- it would report that an agent rewrote a draft they never saw. That is materially worse than no data, and it is the same shape as the v9.7.564 fabricated-agreement series that looked like a healthy corpus for four builds. So the rule is REFUSE RATHER THAN GUESS: THREE OF THE FOUR OUTCOMES ARE REFUSALS (NO-SEND, AMBIGUOUS, NO-MATCH) and that ratio is the design. Two or more candidates clearing the floor is refused outright rather than picking the closer one. The scraper carries recent real sends out with their title and timestamp (call logs and voicemails excluded at the carrier), capped at 8 notes and 2000 chars. The overlap score is a shared-rare-word measure rather than an edit distance -- deliberately explainable, so a match can be checked by eye from the log -- with sub-5-character function words excluded because every dealership message shares them and they inflate two unrelated drafts toward 1. ONE PROPERTY WORTH NAMING: signatures, phone numbers, URLs and the compliance footer are normalised out before comparison, because those are the parts LP itself rewrites deterministically. That means the 8/22 case -- body identical, store name corrected by hand -- MATCHES, which it must: otherwise the exact defect this feature exists to measure would read as 'the agent rewrote it'. Asserted directly. VERIFIED 39/39 in a new suite plus all 33 suites green (1,125 assertions), dev===comm on every case. Phase A's non-wiring is asserted five ways: the flag is off, the flag off produces zero output, the block contains no fetch and no persistence, nothing outside the block calls it, and an anti-vacuity check confirms the block is really there. Every refusal is asserted to carry a human-readable reason; both channels are asserted to report on every run, because silence on one would hide half the refusal rate; malformed notes are asserted not to throw. The signature fix is asserted by slicing enforceEmailPhone and checking the profile fallback is GONE rather than reordered, and enforceSmsSig is asserted UNCHANGED as the reference implementation. ONE FIXTURE ERROR OF MY OWN, caught and recorded: the URL-normalisation test used two sentences differing by a real word ('or call' vs 'call') and failed -- which read as a normaliser bug and was a fixture bug, the normaliser having correctly preserved a genuine difference. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: the signature change means an email on a lead whose dealerId is unknown to DEALER_ID_MAP now falls to the remote config or the VinSolutions chrome rather than the agent's profile -- strictly better, but it can now render a blank store where it previously rendered a wrong one, and blank is the honest answer. Phase A changes nothing today; its costs are 8 extra note bodies in the scrape payload and, when the flag is eventually turned on, two console lines per generation. PHASE B AND PHASE C ARE DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT and must not be until this matcher's false-match rate is known on real traffic -- if it cannot hold a low rate on live leads, the right move is to stop rather than ship a classifier on top of an untrusted pairing. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.569-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.569-dev (DEV. THREE CONTAINED FIXES FOUND BY READING THE FIRST REAL COMPREHENSION DATA. Pairs with proxy v7.60 and reporter v1.18. FIRST, THE GOOD NEWS, because it is the thing that made the rest findable: v9.7.568's telemetry flush WORKS ON LIVE TRAFFIC. log130 (8/22, v9.7.568-dev) shows 3 generations, 3 rows each, 9 rows total -- '[LP FACT TELEMETRY] posted 3 row(s) to /commit-comprehension -- verbal-commit:AGREE-NONE day-lock:AGREE-NONE off-franchise:AGREE-NONE' twice, and once with verbal-commit:NO-REGEX-VERDICT where the regex was gated off on that lead (the v9.7.563 distinction doing its job). These are the FIRST genuine comprehension rows the programme has ever produced. It also settles the open question from the 8/22 17:16 report: the 40 rows there carry no probeOk field at all, so they were written by older code and the reporter's v1.16 shape test correctly refused to count them. The data handling was right; the '100% agreement' tile is a display artefact over an empty denominator. ALSO MEASURED AND WORTH RECORDING: the three parallel probes settle in 904-1113ms, not the 4500ms worst case v9.7.566 budgeted for. Added latency in front of the draft was the single largest risk I flagged on Phase 3 and it is a non-issue at ~1s. FIX 1 -- PROXY v7.60, AND THIS ONE WOULD HAVE SILENTLY CORRUPTED THE DECISION DATA. v7.59 made /commit-comprehension per-detector but left compKind clamped to ['firm','soft','none'] -- verbal-commit's vocabulary, and the only one that existed when the clamp was written. Day-lock answers with a WEEKDAY ('Saturday'); off-franchise answers with a MAKE ('Ram'). Both fell through and were stored as 'none', which is indistinguishable from 'found nothing'. Every positive verdict those two detectors ever produced would have been recorded as a negative, and the report would have shown them as working-but-quiet rather than broken -- the worst possible failure shape for data whose only job is to decide whether a flag gets flipped. Caught before it bit: every row in log130 is AGREE-NONE, so no real answer has been lost. The clamp is now per-detector and is still a clamp: unrecognised values still collapse to 'none', a make is bounded by shape and length rather than trusted, and verbal-commit cannot smuggle a weekday through. FIX 2 -- REPORTER v1.18. The disagreement list was an EXCLUSION list -- `delta !== 'AGREE-COMMITMENT' && delta !== 'AGREE-NONE'` -- which was complete when there were four deltas and silently wrong once there were nine. The three added since (AGREE-FIRED, PROBE-FAILED, NO-COMPREHENSION-VERDICT) plus NO-REGEX-VERDICT all landed in a table headed DISAGREEMENTS; the 8/22 report listed a genuine AGREE-FIRED row as a disagreement. Now a POSITIVE list of the three deltas that ARE disagreements, so a future delta cannot fall into it. That is the whole lesson: an exclusion list is a promise that nobody will ever add a case, and somebody always does. A new assertion checks every delta the proxy accepts is classified as exactly one of agree / disagree / neither, with nothing unaccounted for. FIX 3 -- EXTENSION. The v9.7.567 [LP BEREAVEMENT DIAG] fires per FRAME, and a VinSolutions page carries six or seven frames with no transcript at all: log130 has 21 diag lines for 3 generations, 18 of them {"fired":false,"scanLen":1} from empty frames. A miss on an empty frame is not information and 18 lines of it per grab buries the three that are. The non-fire line now requires something to have been scanned. The threshold is deliberately low so a genuinely note-free LEAD frame still reports -- 'this lead had nothing to read' IS information -- and a FIRE is never suppressed, whatever the scan length. Asserted four ways. VERIFIED all 32 suites green (1,086 assertions, 10 new), dev===comm on every case. The clamp is exercised by running the SHIPPED helper rather than restating it: Saturday survives, Someday collapses, Ram survives, a 60-char blob collapses, firm survives, and verbal-commit given 'Saturday' collapses. One harness error of my own, caught and worth recording: the first version eval'd ccKind alone and threw 'CC_WEEKDAYS is not defined' -- the helper closes over its weekday list, so slicing the function without its dependency fails in a way that reads as a code fault and is not one. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing on any of the three. The clamp change means compKind values that previously all read 'none' will now carry real weekday and make strings, so any consumer keyed on the old three-value vocabulary needs to expect more -- the reporter's own by-detector table does not read compKind, so nothing in this repo is affected. The reporter change makes the DISAGREEMENTS table SMALLER, which is correct but will look like a drop if compared against yesterday's inflated count. The diagnostic change removes ~18 log lines per grab; if a future investigation wants the empty-frame lines back, the threshold is one constant. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.568-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.568-dev (DEV. THE TELEMETRY POST WAS CONDITIONAL ON THE REGEX HAVING FIRED, SO ON AN ORDINARY LEAD IT NEVER FIRED AT ALL. My regression, introduced in v9.7.566 and silent for its whole life. REPORTED: /commit-comprehension receives zero POSTs. Measured 8/22 on v9.7.567 (log129, 15:11 CT): 8 of 8 generations skipped, and three same-day reporter runs (02:18-10:10, 02:18-11:45, 02:18-14:01 CT, 578-867 requests) all read "No comprehension verdicts recorded for this date" with proxy v7.59 and extension v9.7.567 both live. The report was accurate: KV genuinely holds zero rows for the day. THE REPORTED MECHANISM IS A RED HERRING, and it matters because fixing it would have changed nothing. The line '[LP COMMIT COMPREHENSION DIAG] superseded by the Phase 3 fact probe this generation -- not dispatching a second call' is CORRECT and has never had anything to do with the POST. It suppresses a duplicate PROBE CALL for a question Phase 3 has already asked -- without it every generation makes two identical API calls and persists two conflicting rows for one lead. It is untouched. THE ACTUAL CAUSE: v9.7.566 hung the POST off _lpFactDecide, and that function has exactly TWO call sites -- inside _lpApplyFactOverrides, which returns early unless the day-lock or off-franchise directive is PRESENT in the context, and inside `if (dayCommitPre)`, which only runs when the verbal-commit REGEX ALREADY FIRED. So persistence happened only on leads where a regex had already found something, and the majority case -- regex silent, comprehension silent, AGREE-NONE, the row that establishes the baseline -- was never recorded. log127 shows the shape exactly: '[LP FACT DIAG] all probes settled in 2810ms | verbal-commit:usable day-lock:usable off-franchise:usable' and then NOT ONE per-detector decision line, because the verbal-commit regex found nothing and no fence existed. Three usable verdicts, produced, logged, and thrown away. ANSWERING THE RETROACTIVE QUESTION DIRECTLY: this is NOT new to .567. The shipped bytes of v9.7.566 and v9.7.567 are identical on this point -- one send, inside _lpFactDecide, two conditional call sites -- so EVERY comprehension row for the entire Phase 3 window is zero, not just 8/22. Worse in context: v9.7.559's observer persisted UNCONDITIONALLY on every generation with notes, so v9.7.566 replaced unconditional persistence with conditional persistence and nobody noticed because the rows it stopped writing were the ones nobody was watching. AND THE HONEST BOTTOM LINE, because it is the number Phase C rests on: combining this with v9.7.564, the disagreement dataset has NEVER contained a single real row. Before .564 the probe could not succeed at all (MIN_CONTENT_CHARS rejected every answer) so every row was a fabricated AGREE-NONE; from .566 the rows stopped being written entirely. The only window that could have produced genuine data is .564/.565 running against proxy v7.58, which was hours. That is three distinct causes in four builds, all of them mine, and the corpus is still empty. THE FIX: new _lpFlushFactTelemetry posts ONE ROW PER DETECTOR PER GENERATION, unconditionally, from the verdicts the Phase 3 probes already produced -- no second API call, exactly as the brief asked. _lpFactDecide no longer sends; it RECORDS its decision into window._lpFactDecisions and the flush reads it, so sourceUsed and authoritative still reach KV when a decision was reached, and are empty when none was -- an absence that is itself information. The flush runs at the old observer dispatch site specifically because that is AFTER buildUserPrompt, the only moment the verbal-commit regex verdict exists. The decision map and the flush token are CLEARED per generation, so a previous lead's source can never ride over into this one -- the v9.7.562/563 stale-global class, in the field that records whether comprehension changed a customer-facing directive. THREE PROPERTIES WORTH NAMING. The flush is IDEMPOTENT per generationId (a regen re-enters the generate path and must not double-post) but flushes again for a NEW generation. One detector's POST throwing cannot stop the other two. And when NO probe ran -- every observer flag off -- it posts NOTHING rather than a row asserting 'no comprehension verdict' on a question that was never asked, which would be the v9.7.564 fabrication mistake in a new costume. VERIFIED 78/78 in the Phase 3 suite (19 new) plus all 32 suites green (1,076 assertions), dev===comm on every case. THE REGRESSION THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT THIS ON THE DAY is asserted first: a generation where no regex fired still posts one row per detector, all three AGREE-NONE. Also asserted: a fired regex posts DISAGREE-REGEX-ONLY rather than silence; both readers firing is AGREE-FIRED; an unusable probe still posts as NO-COMPREHENSION-VERDICT with its reason; a regex that never RAN reports NO-REGEX-VERDICT (the v9.7.563 distinction); the row carries exactly the fifteen fields proxy v7.59 stores; _lpFactDecide contains zero sends; and the 'superseded' line is asserted still present, because removing it would reintroduce double-probing. WHAT I CANNOT VERIFY FROM HERE, stated plainly: the brief's step 3 asks for confirmation against real traffic. I can only verify the code path. What to look for after deploying: a '[LP FACT TELEMETRY] posted 3 row(s)' line popup-side on every generation, three '[COMMIT-COMP] <detector> | ...' lines proxy-side per generation, and a non-empty Commit Comprehension section on the next report. If the report is still empty with those popup lines present, the fault is between the extension and KV, not here. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: KV write volume goes from zero to THREE ROWS PER GENERATION -- roughly 2,600/day at 867 requests, on a 90-day TTL in the same namespace as feedback. That is the intended cost of having the data at all, but it is a real jump and worth watching against the v7.29/W6c precedent. No customer-facing behaviour changes: the flush is fire-and-forget, runs after the draft is rendered, and every authoritative flag is still OFF. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.567-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.567-dev (DEV. "is passing" IS NOT A BEREAVEMENT, AND THE REPORTED CAUSE WAS NOT THE CAUSE. LIVE INCIDENT, and it reached an agent's screen: Mary Pacella (Community Toyota Baytown, lead 2040382104, 8/21, log127 on v9.7.566). LP generated "Mary, I'm very sorry for your loss. I know this is a difficult time -- please accept my condolences from all of us at Community Toyota Baytown" plus an email offering to "close out your file and stop any further contact". There is no death anywhere in her 39-note history. THE REPORT ATTRIBUTED IT TO AN iMESSAGE TAPBACK -- `Loved "...you will always get my friends and family pricing."` -- tripping a supposed loved/family proximity check. THERE IS NO SUCH CHECK, and that Tapback matches none of the four bereavement rules; both facts are asserted in the new suite rather than argued. THE ACTUAL TRIGGER IS MARY'S OWN INBOUND EMAIL OF 8/4, and it is the single most engaged message on the lead: "My apologies to you timing is passing.. I need to know when is the best time to consider buying a vehicle ? I want to stay in the Toyota family, thank you for reaching out and not giving up on me". The fourth rule was /\b\w+['`]?s\s+passing\b/ -- THE APOSTROPHE WAS OPTIONAL and \w+ can match a SINGLE letter, so "**is** passing" satisfies it as \w+="i" plus a literal s. It equally matches "was passing", "this passing", "days passing", "deals passing". A pattern written for "Robert's passing" was matching the ordinary English word "is" -- so a customer saying she wants to keep buying from us was told we were sorry for her loss and offered a file closure. Confirmed by lifting the old regex out of the shipped bytes and running it against the real dump: it returns "is passing" on her email and nothing on the Tapback. WHY IT TOOK A PAGE DUMP TO FIND: [LP EXIT DIAG] named the component ('fired via hasBereavementSignal') and never the EVIDENCE. This guard writes a condolence and offers to close a customer's file -- the most irreversible directive in the system -- and it logged only that it had fired, never on what. THREE CHANGES. (1) THE POSSESSIVE RULE IS NOW TWO RULES, because the genuine phrase comes in two shapes: a real possessive REQUIRES the apostrophe and a word of 2+ letters ("Robert's passing", straight or curly), and "his/her/their passing" -- genuine grief English with no apostrophe -- is NAMED EXPLICITLY rather than swept in by an optional-apostrophe wildcard. Everything else reaching "<word> passing" is ordinary prose and no longer qualifies. (2) NEW [LP BEREAVEMENT DIAG] on EVERY evaluation, firing or not, carrying which of the four rules matched, the matched string verbatim, and 200 chars of surrounding source text. The next case is a grep. (3) THE TAPBACK GUARD IS BUILT ANYWAY -- it was not the cause here, and the changelog says so, but the shape is real and unhandled: an iPhone Tapback over the SMS gateway arrives as literal `Loved "<our own prior outbound>"`, the customer authored ONE WORD of it, and every sentiment, exit, trade and schedule scan was reading our own marketing prose as something the customer typed. Handled inside _lpCustomerAuthoredPart (same semantic as a quoted-reply header: everything after the marker is somebody else's text) AND at the transcript level, where the quoted payload is replaced by the verb alone -- the reaction IS a positive customer signal and is kept as one, rather than the line being discarded. VERIFIED 37/37 in a new suite plus all 32 suites green (1,056 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against Mary's real notes rather than a reconstruction. Her 8/4 email is asserted silent; the Tapback is asserted silent AND asserted to have been silent under the OLD rule too, so the report's mechanism is ruled out rather than assumed away; the old rule is asserted to have matched "is passing", so the test is not vacuous. THE FEATURE IS INTACT: all nine genuine shapes still fire (passed away, husband died, death in the family, funeral, obituary, lost my mother, she passed, no longer with us, deceased), both apostrophe forms of a real possessive fire, his/her/their passing fire, and Robert's real 7/25 death notification -- the incident this guard was built for in v9.7.481 -- still fires. All nine automotive false-positive idioms the v9.7.481 lookahead exists for stay silent (battery died, car died, engine died, deal died, passed on/by/up/through/along). Eight new prose shapes stay silent, including the exact "timing is passing". The Tapback guard is asserted not to over-fire on a real message that merely starts with "Liked" or quotes something mid-sentence. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a bereavement phrased ONLY as "<name>s passing" with the apostrophe genuinely omitted -- a real typo shape -- no longer fires. That is the deliberate trade: the cost of missing it is a normal follow-up message, and the cost of the false positive is a condolence and a file-closure offer sent to an active buyer. The Tapback change means a reaction no longer contributes its quoted text to ANY scan, so a lead whose only trade or scheduling evidence sat inside text we ourselves wrote loses that evidence -- which is correct, because it was never the customer's. ALSO NOTED, NOT CHANGED: the third rule still accepts bare "he"/"she" + passed behind a negative lookahead; it is tested, it did not misfire here, and narrowing it would need its own evidence. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the changed regions are inside inlineScraper and depend on nothing outside it. Builds on v9.7.566-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.566-dev (DEV. PHASE 3 -- COMPREHENSION BECOMES THE AUTHORITATIVE READER FOR THREE CONVERSATIONAL-FACT DETECTORS, AND EVERY FLAG SHIPS OFF. Pairs with proxy v7.59 and reporter v1.17. THE THESIS THE DESIGN SERVES: for conversational facts -- did the customer commit, did they name a day, did they mention an off-brand vehicle -- a regex manufacturing a short 'fact' and handing it to the model as a trust-this-over-your-own-reading directive has been the source of nearly every live incident this month. Not the model misreading the transcript. Jeri's 'LOCK IN Monday' came from a Yahoo timestamp; Hayden's 'were you looking for a Ram?' came from the letters inside 'Timeframe'. In both cases the model was handed a false fact and obeyed it correctly. The fix is not a better regex; it is letting the model read the real, verified customer text for this category, with the regex demoted to comparison and mandatory fallback. PREMISE CORRECTION, AND IT CHANGED THE TIERING. The brief scoped verbal-commit to go live broadly 'given the existing data' (~113 observer verdicts) while day-lock and off-franchise waited behind flags. THAT ASYMMETRY DOES NOT EXIST. v9.7.564 established that the proxy's MIN_CONTENT_CHARS=150 floor -- already 150 in v7.52, while the observer shipped against v7.55 -- rejected EVERY probe answer at every tier, and that the SAFE_FALLBACK returned in its place is valid JSON with no 'kind' key, which the observer read as kind:'none'. Those ~113 rows are the count of PERSISTED ROWS and essentially all of them are that fabricated series; a row could only have been genuine if the model returned a quote over ~110 characters, which the probe prompt actively discourages. Proxy v7.58 fixes the floor but shipped hours ago with no post-fix traffic yet. SO ALL THREE DETECTORS HAVE THE SAME EVIDENCE BASE, WHICH IS ZERO, and LEADPRO_VERBALCOMMIT_AUTHORITATIVE ships OFF alongside the other two. That is a deliberate deviation from the brief, flagged rather than silent, and it is the ONLY thing narrowed: everything else asked for is built, and flipping any one flag is a single boolean with the override path already wired and tested. WHAT SHIPPED. A generic _lpRunFactProbe drives all three detectors through ONE code path, so a rail cannot be implemented in one and forgotten in another. Each detector has its own probe prompt naming the failure IT has actually shipped, its own observer kill switch, and its own authoritative flag -- six separate symbols, asserted separate. Both v9.7.564 fallback nets are reused unchanged, including the one that needs no cooperation from the proxy at all. THE RAILS, ALL THREE DETECTORS. (1) FALLBACK TO REGEX IS MANDATORY on every failure: flag off, no endpoint, no notes, SAFE_FALLBACK, proxy error, non-JSON, JSON array, missing answer key, unverified quote, timeout, a fetch that never settles, a fetch that throws synchronously -- eleven modes, each asserted individually. No path can end with less signal than today. (2) A POSITIVE ANSWER WITHOUT A LITERALLY VERIFIED QUOTE IS UNUSABLE, the same bar verbal-commit has always held; a NEGATIVE answer needs no quote and that asymmetry is deliberate and asserted. (3) EVERY DECISION LOGS ITS SOURCE per detector -- [LP FACT DIAG] carries source:comprehension or source:regex-fallback, whether the detector is authoritative or observer-only, both readings, and the agreement verdict. (4) SEPARATE KILL SWITCHES, asserted independent: turning day-lock off leaves the other two making their calls. (5) THE PROBE READS THE SAME BYTES THE REGEX READ. Day-lock's text is CARRIED OUT of inlineScraper by the scraper itself rather than re-walked popup-side from data.context -- those are not the same string, and a disagreement caused by two paths reading different text is worthless data. That also satisfies the brief's 'reuse _lpCustomerAuthoredPart, do not reimplement it': it is not reimplemented, its OUTPUT is transported, and the suite asserts exactly one definition of it and that the definition sits inside inlineScraper. Off-franchise reads _lpCustomerText -- the same function whose ungated lastInboundMsg caused Hayden's incident and which v9.7.555 fixed. THE ARCHITECTURAL PROBLEM THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME, AND HOW IT IS SOLVED. An authoritative reader must produce its answer BEFORE the prompt is built; an observer never had to. So the probes are AWAITED at generate time, three in parallel behind a 4.5s timeout, which adds one probe's latency rather than three. But day-lock and off-franchise render their directives inside populateFromData at SCRAPE time, before any probe has an answer -- and rewriting assembled prompt prose by pattern-matching it is exactly the fragile thing this file gets bitten by. Both directives are therefore SENTINEL-FENCED at the point they are written, and the generate path removes or replaces a whole fenced region deterministically. The fences are stripped UNCONDITIONALLY on every path including the throw path and including an orphan with no partner -- asserted four ways -- because a stray sentinel in a customer-facing prompt would be worse than any directive this prevents. Precedent: the LP_CACHE_BREAKPOINT sentinel already in the system prompt. A REAL DEFECT FOUND WHILE WIRING IT, worth recording because it would have doubled the cost and corrupted the data: the v9.7.559 observer would have kept firing alongside the Phase 3 probe, making TWO calls per generation for the SAME verbal-commit question and persisting two conflicting rows per lead -- the disagreement series would have double-counted itself. The old dispatch is now gated on the Phase 3 flag and says so in the log. It is NOT deleted: it stays the tested fallback observer for when Phase 3's verbal-commit comprehension is switched off. PROXY v7.59 stores `detector` (clamped to the three ids; a row without one is verbal-commit, which every pre-v9.7.566 row is by construction), plus `authoritative` and `sourceUsed`, and accepts AGREE-FIRED and NO-COMPREHENSION-VERDICT. REPORTER v1.17 splits the section BY DETECTOR -- merging three different questions into one rate makes every one uninterpretable -- and separates WOULD HAVE DISAGREED from ACTUALLY CHANGED THE DIRECTIVE. With all three flags off that CHANGED column must read 0; anything else means a flag is live that should not be. VERIFIED 58/58 in a new suite plus all 31 suites green (1,019 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against the three real captures the incidents came from. Jeri: the probe is asserted to receive only 'Thank you.' with the Yahoo header absent from the MESSAGES block; with the flag ON a no-day verdict REMOVES the fabricated LOCK IN directive; with the flag OFF -- today's default -- the directive is kept byte-untouched. Hayden: the probe is told the store brand (without it the question is unanswerable) and its prompt is asserted to name the exact failure, letters inside another word. Jason: the verbal-commit walk still reads his real 22-note history. Quote verification is asserted both ways on all three: a real quote verifies, a paraphrase is refused as fabricated, and verification is whitespace-normalised and case-insensitive rather than brittle. THREE OF MY OWN ERRORS THE SUITES CAUGHT. (1) The harness seeded flag overrides into the sandbox BEFORE running the code span -- which re-declares `var LEADPRO_DAYLOCK_AUTHORITATIVE = false` and silently clobbered them, so three assertions failed in a way that looked like the override was broken when the harness was. (2) An assertion that the Yahoo header never reaches the probe scanned the WHOLE prompt, and the probe's own RULES deliberately quote a Yahoo header as an example of what to ignore -- it was matching my own prose. (3) One expectation said REMOVE in its name and KEEP in its want. Also caught, and a genuine robustness gap rather than a test artefact: the verbal-commit override read `window` unguarded, which threw in any context without it; a comprehension layer that can break the prompt build is worse than no layer, so every override path is now defensive and degrades to regex. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PER DETECTOR, SINCE THEY CARRY DIFFERENT RISK. TODAY, WITH EVERY FLAG OFF: nothing customer-facing changes on any of the three -- the regex directive ships exactly as it does now, asserted. The real costs today are THREE PARALLEL API CALLS PER GENERATION (up from one) and up to 4.5s of added latency in front of the draft when the proxy is slow. Both are the price of collecting the evidence, and each detector's switch turns its own third off. IF VERBAL-COMMIT IS FLIPPED ON: a probe that reads the notes and finds no commitment SUPPRESSES the CALL NOTE block, so a genuine commitment the regex caught and the model misses is lost -- the failure mode inverts from fabrication to omission. IF DAY-LOCK IS FLIPPED ON: a real customer-named day that the probe misses means the lead gets today/tomorrow instead of their day -- annoying, recoverable, and strictly less bad than Jeri's three-days-out lock. IF OFF-FRANCHISE IS FLIPPED ON: a genuine off-brand request the probe misses means the store promises something it structurally cannot sell, which is the Halie Bott failure and is the WORST of the three -- flip that one last and on the most evidence. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper except the schedCustomerNotes carrier, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.565-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.565-dev (DEV. A DAY NAME IN A QUOTED-REPLY HEADER IS NOT A CUSTOMER STATEMENT. Jeri Mayes (Community Honda Lafayette, lead 2065165925, 8/21) was offered times three days out on Monday 8/24 -- skipping today AND Saturday -- while the store was open with four hours of usable time left, because the prompt carried "Customer said Monday is when they are available. LOCK IN Monday - do NOT offer any other day... Do NOT try to pull them in sooner." JERI NEVER WROTE THE WORD MONDAY. Her one inbound email, in full, is: "Thank you.Sent from Yahoo Mail for iPhoneOn Monday, August 10, 2026, 3:00 PM, Kaylee Guzman kguzman@... wrote: Jeri,Your CarGurus inquiry is for the 2026 Honda Odyssey..." She wrote "Thank you." The Monday is a YAHOO QUOTED-REPLY HEADER -- a timestamp her mail client generated, naming when OUR OWN email was sent -- and the detector's `(?:until|till|on|this|next)\s+(monday|...)` alternative matched the "On Monday" inside it. WHY IT SURVIVED THIS LONG, and log123 shows it exactly: this detector had NO DIAGNOSTIC AT ALL. Log123 records the note passing the gate ([LP SCHED GATE] noteIndex:7, dir:inbound, passesGate:true) and then NOTHING -- because the one adjacent diagnostic, [LP SCHED DIAG], sits inside `if(!customerScheduleConstraint)` and is suppressed precisely when this branch fires. The failure was invisible by construction, which is why it took a full live capture to notice a hard LOCK-IN directive being fabricated. ON GIL'S SELF-INDUCED HYPOTHESIS -- THE ORIGIN IS RIGHT, THE LOOP IS NOT, AND THE CORRECTION POINTS SOMEWHERE BETTER. The origin is almost certainly LP: an [LP: value] note sits at 8/14 3:22 PM, SEVENTEEN MINUTES before the 3:39 PM "Monday appraisal for your Passport" email, and the Yahoo artifact predates both (8/10 3:02 PM) -- so the 8/14 generation had exactly the input that produces LOCK IN Monday, and an agent sent it uncaught. BUT THAT EMAIL CANNOT FEED THE DETECTOR BACK: log123 line 345 shows it gated OUT -- {"noteIndex":3,"dir":"outbound","title":"email reply to prospect","passesGate":false}. The loop reads inbound notes only. So this is not a compounding loop, it is a PERSISTENT ARTIFACT: one static string, permanently in the notes, producing the same wrong directive on every generation forever. 8/14 and 8/21 are two independent firings of one cause, not a bug feeding itself. That distinction matters for triage in the direction that argues for fixing it HARDER -- a compounding bug needs someone to catch it once; a persistent one has no catch-it-next-time path at all and will fire on this lead every single time until the code changes. THE FIX. New _lpCustomerAuthoredPart cuts an inbound note at the FIRST marker of any kind and returns only what the customer typed. Because this file has been bitten repeatedly by enumerating shapes it had already seen, the two SHAPE rules carry the weight -- "On ... wrote:" and "<an email address> ... wrote:" cover every mail client without naming one -- with client signatures, -----Original Message-----, Outlook From:/Sent: blocks, the Outlook divider, "Begin forwarded message:" and bare ">" prefixes enumerated beneath them. The cut is applied ONCE, at the top of the loop, so every detector benefits: "out of town", "camping", the arrival-time scan and the work-hours scan are all equally capable of matching a quoted agent email, and fixing only the one that produced an incident would have left the rest loaded. TWO NEW DIAGNOSTICS, per the brief. [LP SCHED DAY DIAG] reports LOCKED or REFUSED on every day match, with the day, the matched text, the SOURCE LINE it came from, which marker cut the note, and what the customer actually authored -- so the next Jeri is a grep, not an investigation. [LP SCHED SOURCE DIAG] reports a note with no customer-authored text at all. [LP SCHED DIAG] now carries rawLen/cutBy/cutAt so "saw nothing" and "was shown a quoted agent email" stop looking identical. A SECOND CONSUMER, FOUND BY THE SURVEY THE BRIEF ASKED FOR, and it is in Jeri's delivered prompt too: the personalContext scanner rendered "[geographic] Sent from Yahoo Mail for iPhoneOn Monday, August 10, 2026, 3:00 PM, Kaylee Guzman kguzman@communityhondalafayette" under PERSONAL CONTEXT THE CUSTOMER HAS SHARED. Two independent faults: it read the whole note including the quoted agent email, and its geographic pattern's bare `from` alternative needed no first-person framing at all, so "Sent from Yahoo" matched `from` + [A-Z][a-z]+. Not cosmetic -- the v9.7.220 block promotes a [geographic] entry naming a US state to an operational DISTANCE BUYER fact. Both halves fixed; the movement verbs ("driving from", "coming from") were never the problem and are untouched. MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, ACROSS THE CORPUS: the artifact appears in 3 of 22 recent captures (log103 x2, log120, plus Jeri) -- it is not a one-lead curiosity. VERIFIED 50/50 in a new suite plus all 30 suites green (960 assertions), dev===comm on every case, driving the loop sliced from the SHIPPED file against a fake DOM. Jeri's real email yields NO day lock, NO not-today flag, and a logged REFUSED line quoting the Yahoo header; the OLD behaviour is reproduced on the uncut text in the same suite so the fix is demonstrably what changed it; Kaylee's own 8/14 "Monday" subject is asserted gated out. THE FEATURE STILL WORKS: seven genuine phrasings still lock, including a real day sitting ABOVE an Apple signature and above a Gmail quote. Ten artifact shapes are refused, one of them by SHAPE ALONE with no client named. The other detectors are asserted both ways -- a quoted agent "out of town" no longer trips NOT-TODAY, a customer genuinely saying it still does. TWO OF MY OWN ERRORS THE SUITE CAUGHT, both worth recording. (1) The harness anchored on "if (dir === 'inbound' && body) {" which appears MORE THAN ONCE -- frustrationSignals opens identically and comes first -- so it silently tested the wrong block and reported empty results that read as a code failure. (2) Tightening the geographic pattern to `i'm from|i am from` DROPPED THE /i FLAG's protection: the old bare `from` matched lowercase text, my replacement was case-sensitive against a capitalised sentence start, so "I'm from Baton Rouge" stopped matching. A real regression, caught by the still-works assertion rather than by the fix's own tests. STILL OPEN, MEASURED AND DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS DIFF: inboundMsgs (popup.js ~9055) pushes raw note text with no cut, and feeds the customer-commitment detector, which emits 'CUSTOMER COMMITTED: Customer said they would come in' and 'PENDING DECISION: Customer said -'. A quoted agent "I'll check on that for you" is exactly that shape. Same class, same fix, but inboundMsgs also feeds lastInboundMsg, trivial-reply and burst detection, so it changes behaviour well outside this incident and wants its own verified pass rather than a bolt-on -- the same discipline as the Phase 1 consumer migration. node --check clean on both builds; both manifests parse; the new helper and BOTH its call sites are asserted INSIDE inlineScraper and asserted to depend on nothing outside themselves (a module-scope reference there is a ReferenceError that aborts the entire scrape -- the v9.7.455/228 trap); version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.564-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.564-dev (DEV. THE COMPREHENSION OBSERVER HAS NEVER ONCE PRODUCED A REAL READING, and every verdict it ever persisted was fabricated. Pairs with proxy v7.58 and reporter v1.16 -- DEPLOY THE PROXY FIRST. Gil reported an hour-long worker incident on 8/21 (15:04-16:02 UTC): 17 requests failed all three model tiers and returned SAFE_FALLBACK, the canned placeholder with unfilled [Agent]/[Store], and asked whether real customers received it. THEY DID NOT, AND NONE OF THE 17 WAS A CUSTOMER DRAFT. Proven, not inferred: every one carries cacheKey=lp_fd4886ea, which is promptCacheKey() of the OBSERVER PROBE's own system string, computed and matched against the shipped worker hash. In the same window the 71 real generations (cacheKey=lp_773499b2) were 71/71 OK -- 67 PRIMARY, 4 FALLBACK. The partition is perfect: 17/17 probes failed, 0/71 drafts did. The up|implicit_copy events Gil saw seconds after each SAFE_FALLBACK are causally BACKWARDS -- the probe fires because a generation just SUCCEEDED, and the copy is of that successful draft. ROOT CAUSE, AND IT IS MINE: the proxy's MIN_CONTENT_CHARS floor is 150 and applies to every response at every tier. The probe's CORRECT answer is {"kind":"none","note":null,"quote":null} -- 40 characters. A firm verdict with a 53-char quote is 89. EVERY POSSIBLE CORRECT PROBE ANSWER IS UNDER THE FLOOR, so all three tiers rejected it as "Empty response (finish=stop)", deterministically, on every probe ever sent. Not a rate limit, not a quota, not a provider issue, and NOT token starvation -- the logged tokens=300 never reached OpenAI, because spec.tokens is set on all three tiers and shadows callerMax. The models answered correctly in ~1s and the proxy threw the answer away. WORSE THAN THE FAILURE: SAFE_FALLBACK_TEXT is VALID JSON. This observer JSON.parse'd it, found no "kind" key, defaulted to kind:'none', and recorded and PERSISTED a canned customer apology as "the comprehension pass read the notes and found no commitment". All 17 rows that hour read AGREE-NONE or NO-REGEX-VERDICT and every one is fabricated. Because the 150 floor predates the observer, this is true of EVERY comprehension row ever collected -- three builds of fixing this observer (v9.7.562 empty context, v9.7.563 stale stash, this one) and the probe was dead for all of them. THREE PREMISE CORRECTIONS TO THE REPORT, all evidence-backed. (1) The draft path ALREADY refuses to render SAFE_FALLBACK -- v9.7.379/377 W4 reads candidates[0]._fallback and calls showError instead, on the main, voicemail and translate paths alike. The "agents cannot tell a placeholder from a draft" concern is false for drafts and was true only for this observer, the one consumer that never looked at the marker. (2) The 4 PRIMARY "Degenerate field content" failures on gpt-5.6-luna are NOT truncation -- the ellipsis in those log lines is DEGENERATE_SAMPLE_CHARS truncating the LOG, not the model truncating output. The email field genuinely held a nested {"subject":...} object, the exact second shape v7.45/7.46 was built for. The guard is model-agnostic, it FIRED, and FALLBACK rescued all 4; it needs no extension for Luna. (3) /feedback-to-generation requestId correlation was genuinely IMPOSSIBLE from existing data -- the proxy minted requestId per request but returned it only on the FALLBACK envelope, never on success. One missing field, now added. FIXES. PROXY v7.58 adds `responseContract`: 'draft' (the default, and the default for anything absent or unrecognised, so no caller can weaken the draft guards by omission) keeps MIN_CONTENT_CHARS and hasDegenerateField untouched; 'fact' replaces both with the floor that fits a one-fact answer -- it must parse to a non-array object with at least one key. It also refuses to serve SAFE_FALLBACK_TEXT to a non-draft contract at all, returning an explicit error envelope with no candidates, and returns _requestId on the SUCCESS envelope. EXTENSION: the probe declares responseContract:'fact', and the observer gets TWO INDEPENDENT NETS against ever reading a verdict out of a fallback again. Net one reads the _fallback marker the proxy has set since v7.30. Net two needs no cooperation from the proxy at all -- a parsed response that does not carry its own "kind" key is not a probe answer, whatever produced it -- and net two would have caught this alone, which is why it is there and not just net one. A failed probe now records PROBE-FAILED with a reason, so a dead probe is COUNTABLE instead of arriving disguised as agreement. Real verdicts carry probeOk:true; its absence on a stored row is the exact shape test for "collected while the probe was dead". Feedback rows carry workerRequestId, cleared in _lpFeedbackReset rather than left to be overwritten -- a generate that errors or aborts would otherwise stamp the next lead's row with a previous generation's id, which is the v9.7.562/563 stale-global class in a correlation field, where a wrong value is worse than a missing one. REPORTER v1.16 counts PROBE-FAILED separately, excludes it from both rates, and excludes every row without probeOk===true -- retiring the entire fabricated series on a shape test rather than a version comparison. VERIFIED 48/48 in the new safe-fallback-contract suite plus all 29 suites green (910 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against the real artifacts: the probe's own system string is asserted to hash to lp_fd4886ea using promptCacheKey sliced from the shipped worker; all four probe answer shapes are asserted under 150 chars; SAFE_FALLBACK_TEXT is asserted to parse and to yield kind:'none' under the OLD code and PROBE-FAILED under the new; the fact contract is asserted to still reject "", "{}", "[]", a bare brace, whitespace and prose while accepting all four real answer shapes; absent, unknown, malformed and hostile contract values are all asserted to fall back to 'draft'; the degenerate-field guard is asserted still to catch the nested {"subject":...} email shape and asserted NOT to be model-gated; and a non-draft contract is asserted to receive no candidates array at all. NET TWO IS ASSERTED WITHOUT THE PROXY'S HELP -- the same SAFE_FALLBACK text with the _fallback marker REMOVED is still refused -- and asserted not to over-fire on an unknown kind value or a null kind. WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE DATA, PLAINLY: the Phase 2 corpus is not thin, it is EMPTY -- zero real readings, not zero disagreements among real readings. Phase C cannot be decided on anything collected before this build and the reporter now says so rather than showing a confident 100% agreement rate built entirely out of failed probes. The third rate discontinuity in three builds, and the last one that is an artefact. ALSO MEASURED, worth knowing: the probe contributed 17 of the day's 63 edge-cache MISSes and never STOREs, so part of the elevated cache-miss number is this bug rather than traffic. STILL OPEN, unchanged: the System callmeasurement URL row still reaches the probe. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.563-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.563-dev (DEV. THE FIRST DISAGREEMENT THE PROGRAMME EVER RECORDED WAS AN ARTEFACT, AND THE BUG WAS MINE. Pairs with proxy v7.57 and reporter v1.15. Gil flagged log121's one disagreement -- Alissa (Community Honda Lafayette, Cap One Mailer), DISAGREE-REGEX-ONLY on the quote 'coming in he lives in Mississippi', twice on two grabs -- and read it as possibly a third party rather than her own commitment. The note turns out not to be hers at all. WHAT LOG121 ACTUALLY SHOWS: her two generations are BOTH marked SHOWROOM FOLLOW-UP, and NEITHER carries a [LP VERBAL COMMIT DIAG] line of any kind -- there is no FIRED line anywhere in the file. The verbal-commit block is gated `if (hasCallNoteContent && !data.isShowroomFollowUp)`, the v9.7.197 exception, so on her lead the block never ran. v9.7.558 placed the stash RESET INSIDE that same gate, 35 lines below it. When the gate is closed the reset never executes, so window._lpVerbalCommitVerdict keeps the PREVIOUS lead's value and the observer compares its reading against a verdict belonging to a different customer. The quote is a leftover from an earlier lead in the same panel session. SAME CLASS AS THE v9.7.562 WIRING BUG -- a stale global read as though it were current -- one build later, in code I wrote, and it produced a false positive rather than a false negative, which is the worse direction. TO BE PRECISE ABOUT ONE THING IN THE REPORT: 'quoteVerified:true' does not mean the regex quote was verified. That field reports the COMPREHENSION quote, and the code sets it true unconditionally when comprehension returns none -- there was nothing to verify. It read as corroboration and was vacuous. THREE FIXES. (1) THE RESET MOVES OUTSIDE THE GATE, so it runs on every generation regardless of which path the lead takes. (2) THE STASH NOW CARRIES `ran` AND `skipReason`, because 'the regex looked and found nothing' and 'the regex never looked at this lead' are different evidence and were being reported identically as 'regex:none'. A row where it never ran gets its own delta, NO-REGEX-VERDICT, and the log says 'regex:DID NOT RUN (showroom follow-up -- the v9.7.197 exception)'. Rows written before this build carry no `ran` field, so the check is `!== false` and they stay readable rather than being retroactively relabelled. (3) quoteVerified now prints 'n/a (nothing to verify)' when comprehension returned none. PROXY v7.57 accepts NO-REGEX-VERDICT and stores regexRan; REPORTER v1.15 counts those rows separately and EXCLUDES them from BOTH rates, showing 'X of Y comparable' -- folding a non-comparison into either rate would silently move the exact number Phase C is meant to decide on. WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE DATA, PLAINLY: the disagreement dataset is still empty. log121's two DISAGREE rows were the same artefact twice, so the real count of genuine disagreements observed to date is ZERO, not one. The comprehension pass has not yet been shown to catch anything the regex missed, and it has not been shown not to either. VERIFIED 33/33 in the wiring suite (13 new) plus all 28 suites green (857 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against log121's real shape: the reset is asserted to sit BEFORE the gate and to appear nowhere inside it; a showroom-followup lead and a note-free lead each clear the stash with the right skipReason; a stash pre-loaded with Alissa's exact stale quote is asserted cleared; a ran:false verdict yields NO-REGEX-VERDICT rather than AGREE-NONE; a stale FIRED with ran:false does NOT become a disagreement; and a genuine ran:true disagreement still reports DISAGREE-REGEX-ONLY. A CONTAINMENT ASSERTION NEEDED FIXING TOO, and it is worth recording: the v9.7.558 check that buildUserPrompt never READS the stash counts symbol occurrences, and my new explanatory comment NAMES the symbol -- so prose failed a check that claims to measure code. It now strips line comments before counting, which is what it always meant. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing; the verbal-commit directive itself is untouched and the showroom exception still suppresses it exactly as v9.7.197 intended. The visible change is that a meaningful share of rows will now report NO-REGEX-VERDICT instead of AGREE-NONE, so the agreement rate will move -- not because behaviour changed but because rows that were never comparisons stop being counted as agreements. Rates either side of this build are not comparable, which is the second such discontinuity in three builds and is the honest cost of finding these before the data was trusted. ALSO STILL OPEN from v9.7.562, unchanged: a System callmeasurement URL row still reaches the probe. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.562-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.562-dev (DEV. THE PHASE 2 OBSERVER WAS NEVER HANDED ANY TEXT. LIVE WIRING BUG, and Gil found it by noticing that two diagnostics in the SAME generation disagreed about whether the lead even had notes. The dispatch site passed `(lastScrapedData && lastScrapedData.context) || ''` while the regex path it exists to mirror reads `data.context` -- which buildUserPrompt is handed as `context: leadContext`. IT IS NOT STALENESS: lastScrapedData.context is never populated at all, and this file's OWN diagnostic says so in as many words -- [LP CTX-SCOPE DIAG] prints 'legacy d.context was: undefined (expected)'. The expression evaluated to '' on EVERY generation since v9.7.559 shipped. MEASURED, NOT INFERRED: log120 (8/21, running v9.7.561-dev) contains 7 generations, 7 x 'SKIPPED -- no readable notes (0 refused)', and ZERO verdicts -- while [LP VERBAL COMMIT DIAG] read real notes on 4 of them, one reporting 'note 1 of 16 on the lead (12 call, 4 general; examined 1, skipped 0)' and quoting the note it read. So the disagreement dataset this whole programme exists to collect is EMPTY, not undercounted, and every commit-comprehension row the reporter could have shown is missing for a reason that has nothing to do with the note-reading logic verified all week. WHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE DESIGN, PLAINLY: Phases 1 and 2 rested on 'both paths walk the same entries, so a disagreement can never be a boundary artefact'. That guarantee is real and it held -- but it only begins to apply once the TEXT ARRIVES, and nothing established that it did. The dispatch is the layer below the guarantee, and it was unverified. FIXED: the dispatch now passes leadContext, the same source the regex reads, with an explicit fallback chain so a future rename degrades to something rather than silently to ''. THE DIAGNOSTIC GAP THAT MADE IT INVISIBLE IS CLOSED TOO, and this is the part worth keeping: 'SKIPPED' read identically whether a lead genuinely had no notes or the wiring had handed over nothing. It is now THREE distinct outcomes -- 'NO CONTEXT TEXT REACHED THE OBSERVER -- this is a wiring fault, not a note-free lead' with the ctxSource and ctxLen, 'every note was refused (N as boilerplate or contact data)', and 'this lead genuinely carries no CRM notes' -- and the FIRED line now carries ctxSource and ctxLen as well, so the source of the text is visible on every outcome rather than reconstructable only by diffing against the regex line. THE REAL LESSON IS THE TEST GAP, and it is why this shipped past a suite that was otherwise thorough: every existing observer test injects ctxText directly. commit-comprehension.test.js and commit-persistence.test.js both call _lpRunCommitComprehension(ctx, verdict, deps) with a context they construct themselves -- they exercise the FUNCTION exhaustively and never once exercise the WIRING that decides what the function is given. A dispatch site passing an always-empty expression passed every suite in the repo. New observer-wiring.test.js slices the ACTUAL DISPATCH SITE out of the shipped file and RUNS it with the live variable shape, so what the observer receives is asserted rather than assumed. VERIFIED 20/20 in that new suite plus all 28 suites green (844 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against the real log120 capture: the dispatch now hands over text byte-identical to what the regex path reads, reports ctxSource:leadContext, and the observer reads 2 of the lead's 3 call notes -- including the 'Hung up' note the regex diagnostic named -- with the 'Left message' machine drop still correctly refused. The old always-empty expression is asserted GONE as the primary source, the empty-context case is asserted to report a wiring fault rather than a note-free lead, and the three skip reasons are asserted mutually exclusive. SURVEY, per Gil's ask about sizing the reporter's 'no data yet' gap: log120 is the only log in hand that postdates the v9.7.559 deploy, and in it the answer is total -- 7 of 7 generations, 100% of the gap is this bug rather than note-free leads. The test.pdf reporter showing 'No comprehension verdicts recorded' for 8/20 is correctly empty for a different and harmless reason: that day predates the deploy. NOTED, NOT FIXED HERE, with the evidence pinned in the suite so the next build does not rediscover it: a System-authored callmeasurement URL row is not conversation, and now that the observer actually reads notes it reaches the probe as a numbered note. Adding it to the boilerplate filter is a one-line change, but v9.7.560 made that filter SHARED with the verbal-commit regex path, so touching it would change commit-detection behaviour in a build whose entire job is a wiring fix. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: the observer now actually runs, which means the second API call v9.7.558 described as a cost is only now being paid -- expect the per-generation call count and the KV write volume to rise from zero to real on any lead with a readable note. That is the feature working, not a regression, but it is the first build where it costs anything. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; no worker or reporter change -- v7.56 and v1.14 remain current; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.561-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.561-dev (DEV. PHASE A OF THE FULL-ARC RELEVANCY PASS -- LIBRARY ONLY, DELIBERATELY NOT WIRED. The goal is broader than the last four builds: given a lead's whole history, what facts, stated preferences or context should the NEXT message account for? This build ships the READ and its VERIFICATION and stops there, per Gil's instruction to scope Phase A as its own build for review. Nothing here is dispatched, logged in the generate path, persisted, or referenced by any prompt builder -- Phase B wires it as an observer, Phase C decides promotion off real disagreement data. The NON-WIRING IS ASSERTED, five ways: buildUserPrompt and classifyScenario reference zero Phase A symbols, nothing outside the block calls the walker, the block contains no fetch and no console.log at all, and an anti-vacuity check confirms the block is really there. A later build cannot quietly wire it without this suite failing first. IT DOES NOT SUBSUME THE VERBAL-COMMIT CHECKS, and that is a decision with a reason. 'Did they name a day' is a binary with a single verified quote and four builds of scar tissue (v9.7.197, v9.7.368, v9.7.429/427, v9.7.556); the relevancy read is a multi-item interpretation with far more surface. Folding the commit question into it would put the one question that has actually shipped bad output behind a looser reader, and would break the AGREE/DISAGREE series Phases 2 and 2.5 exist to accumulate. They run ALONGSIDE, share the same walked arc so a disagreement can never be a boundary artefact, and any overlap is REPORTED via _lpRelevancyCommitOverlap rather than silently duplicated. TWO FINDINGS FROM THE REAL CAPTURES, measured across 19 delivered prompts plus the three stored contexts, and both shaped the walker. (1) THE ENTRY TAG DOES NOT ESTABLISH THE SPEAKER. Of 19 distinct [CUSTOMER]-tagged entries, FOUR were written by an agent or by System -- identifiable only from the entry's own attribution line, where 'Received from: <phone> Received by: <agent>' means the customer genuinely sent it and 'By: <name>' means someone at the store typed it. One of those four reads 'wanting to get his girlfriend a new vehicle for her birthday, coming in next thursday to meet with a sales rep' -- and the v9.7.560 commitment regex MATCHES 'coming in next thursday' inside it. A reader trusting the tag would attribute agent-written text to the customer, which is the v9.7.560 subject-attribution problem arriving at entry level. Speaker is therefore derived from the attribution line, never from the tag, and the model is not permitted to overrule it: _lpVerifyRelevancy overwrites the model's claimed speaker with the arc's, keeping the claim only as claimedSpeaker. (2) EVERY GENUINE INBOUND CARRIES THE CUSTOMER'S PHONE IN ITS HEADER -- 15 of 15. Unlike a general note, that contact data is a structural PREFIX rather than the content, so the v9.7.560 whole-entry refusal would have thrown away every customer message. The header is stripped and the body kept; the whole-entry refusal still applies to general notes, where the contact data IS the note. No arc item from any real capture carries a phone or an email, asserted. THE VERIFICATION IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. A 'what matters here' read is nothing but interpretation surface, so the constraint is tighter than the commit probe's, not looser: EVERY item must name the arc line it came from AND quote it character for character, and the quote is verified as a literal substring of that line. A free-floating summary claim cannot survive, by construction. A REAL quote on the WRONG line is reported as a distinct failure from an invented one and is NOT counted as fabrication -- those are different mistakes and only one is dishonest. Items are verified individually, so a response with one good and one invented item keeps the good one and counts the other. An empty items array is an explicitly valid answer, and the probe says so, because the cheapest way to get a fabricated relevancy item is to imply the list must be non-empty. TWO REAL BUGS THIS SUITE CAUGHT BEFORE THEY SHIPPED, both of which would have been silent. (a) VinSolutions puts the message on the SAME LINE as its header -- 'Received from: (713) 725-6721 Received by: Mario Sanchez I am sorry frist day of school...' -- and the first LP_ARC_MSG_HEADER_RE ran to end of line, swallowing all 15 real customer messages and returning an empty body for every one. The arc would have been able to read nothing the customer ever said. The header is now bounded at the agent's name, shaped so the message's own first word ('I', 'Ok', 'Hi') cannot be eaten as a surname. (b) Lead Pro's own 'ZERO-CONTACT LEAD -- APPOINTMENT ENGINE DISABLED' preamble was entering the arc as an item, because it sits above the first dated entry and so has no date -- the self-pollution class of v9.7.545/552/556 arriving by a new door. Undated entries are now skipped; both bugs have their own regression. VERIFIED 54/54 in a new suite plus all 27 suites green (823 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against the real captures and a new 25-item corpus of genuine customer-authored content (tests/fixtures/customer-arc-corpus.json) pulled from those prompts rather than written by hand. Jeffrey Best's Gubagoo chat explodes into his five real turns with the [CHAT BOT] turns never surfaced as customer speech (the v9.7.552 lesson); Jason Pellegrin's housekeeping general notes are filtered out; a real customer line about wanting to trade in verifies, and a plausible invention about the same arc does not. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing, today -- no code path calls any of it, which the suite asserts. The risks are all Phase B's to take on: a second API call per generation with a much larger prompt than the commit probe's (the arc can be 24 items), and a verification bar that will reject genuinely useful paraphrase along with fabrication, which is the deliberate trade and is exactly what the Phase B disagreement data is meant to size. Also noted rather than fixed: the arc drops one- and two-character bodies as noise, so a bare 'Ok' -- a real reply in the corpus -- is not carried; the stripper keeps it intact and only the arc filter drops it, and both halves are asserted separately so the decision is visible. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker; version and version_name both bumped. Phase B and Phase C are deliberately NOT in this build. Builds on v9.7.560-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.560-dev (DEV. BOTH COMMIT READERS NOW READ GENERAL NOTES, NOT JUST CALL NOTES. Scope expansion, pairs with proxy v7.56 and reporter v1.14. TWO FINDINGS MEASURED BEFORE ANY CODE WAS WRITTEN, and both changed the build. (1) THE TAG IS [NOTE], NOT [GENERAL NOTE]. The scraper emits '[<date>] [NOTE] General Note' and this file's own General-Note filter reads indexOf('[NOTE]') && indexOf('General Note'). A type list of '[GENERAL NOTE]' matches ZERO entries on every capture in the repo -- it would have shipped as a silent no-op and looked like a working feature. Asserted against all three fixtures. (2) 13 OF 23 REAL GENERAL NOTES -- 57% -- CONTAIN THE CUSTOMER'S PHONE OR EMAIL. A new fixture, tests/fixtures/general-notes-corpus.json, holds 23 distinct general notes pulled from 13 leads' delivered prompts. Classified: 14 are pure CRM housekeeping ('no dupes <name> <phone> <email> <address>', 'SubmitSENT NEW LEAD Dismiss Edit Assigned To:', 'Customer copied from <store> and assigned to <rep>', '*Sales rep was alerted*'), 3 are empty or an [LP: ...] remnant the context builder already stripped, and only 3 carry real contact-free content. THE MOST COMMON GENERAL NOTE IS THE DUPLICATE-CHECK NOTE AND IT IS A BLOCK OF CONTACT DATA. That makes the general-note boilerplate filter a PRIVACY REQUIREMENT rather than a noise filter: without it those notes reach the comprehension probe's model prompt, and the v9.7.559 unverified-quote path could then persist them to KV -- straight against the v9.7.489 posture that contact data never leaves the CRM. WHAT WAS BUILT. Per-type boilerplate via _lpNoteBoilerplateReason(text, kind), which returns a REASON string rather than a boolean so an over-filtered note type is visible in the log instead of silently missing. The call-note list is v9.7.556's, unchanged. The general-note rule enumerates the housekeeping shapes AND -- because this file has been bitten four builds running by enumerating only what it had already seen -- adds a SHAPE-BASED CONTACT GATE: any general note carrying a phone number or an email is refused outright. That gate earned itself immediately: the corpus contains "GIRLFRIEND'S INFO BUT DO NOT CONTACT SINCE ITS A SURPRISE <name> <phone> <email>", which is not a duplicate-check note, whose shape nobody enumerated, and which carries a THIRD PARTY's contact details. I had written the test expecting it to survive as content; the gate refused it and the suite caught my expectation. SUBJECT ATTRIBUTION IS FINALLY ADDRESSED rather than deferred a third time. v9.7.556 recorded it as residual risk on the 'coming <day>' widening -- an agent might be writing about their own callback plan -- and agent-typed general notes make that common. New _lpCommitSubject reads the words governing the matched verb and returns us / customer / unattributed. It is a NARROW VETO PLUS A REPORTED READING, deliberately not a classifier: it refuses only where the subject is unambiguously us (first person, a named dealership role, or 'told him/her'), and it reports its reading on EVERY fire so the ambiguous middle becomes countable instead of assumed. The corpus contains exactly that ambiguity -- 'spoke with manager said he will let Marvin know to give him a calll' -- where even a human cannot say whose plan 'he' is; that note is reported, not guessed at. SET EXPECTATIONS HONESTLY: the v9.7.556 commitment regex fires on ZERO of the 23 real general notes. This widening adds no detections on today's data. What it adds is coverage of a note type that CAN carry one ('Customer is rescheduling appointment for tomorrow' is in the corpus and is exactly that shape) plus the per-type disagreement data to tell whether it ever matters. BOTH DIAGS NOW REPORT NOTE TYPE, per the brief: [LP VERBAL COMMIT DIAG] carries noteType and subject on every outcome and now reports the lead's TRUE per-type totals ('note 2 of 34 on the lead (22 call, 12 general; examined 2, skipped 1)') -- an earlier draft capped first and silently turned 'of 22' into 'of 12', losing the fact that the lead HAS 22 call notes. [LP COMMIT COMPREHENSION DIAG] carries regexNoteType and compNoteType, the per-type read counts and the refusal count; proxy v7.56 stores all of it clamped to the two known tags; reporter v1.14 adds a BY NOTE TYPE table and a NOTE column on the disagreement list, with pre-v9.7.560 rows landing under 'unknown' rather than being dropped or silently counted as call notes. VERIFIED 53/53 in a new suite plus all 26 suites green (769 assertions), dev===comm on every case, against the real captures. The call-note path is asserted UNCHANGED on Jason Pellegrin's real context: his verdict still fires from the 8/19 CALL note, a call note still wins over a general note when both could fire, and the per-type walk exists precisely so a lead's general notes can never push its call notes past the cap and change an existing verdict. BEHAVIOUR CHANGE STATED RATHER THAN ABSORBED, and it is the one thing to watch: a lead whose every note is refused now SKIPS instead of producing an AGREE-NONE row. Emitting AGREE-NONE would assert the model answered 'none' when it was never asked. The cost is real -- the agreement DENOMINATOR shifts at the v9.7.559/560 boundary, so rates either side are not directly comparable. Rows carry extensionVersion and the SKIP line names how many notes were refused, so the discontinuity is visible rather than silent; on Jason that is 11 refused of the 12 examined. WHAT ELSE THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a genuine general note that happens to contain a phone number -- an agent writing 'customer will come in Friday, call him at 555-1234' -- is now refused entirely, losing a real commitment. That is the deliberate trade against putting customer contact data in a model prompt and in KV, and the refusal reason is logged so the case is countable if it turns out to be real. The subject veto can also refuse a genuine customer commitment an agent happened to write in the first person; it is narrow for that reason, and every fire reports its reading. NOTE: refused notes are refused only from the COMMIT DETECTORS and the probe -- they still reach the model through the ordinary AGENT CONTEXT path, so nothing is hidden from the draft. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.559-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.559-dev (DEV. THE PHASE 2 OBSERVER BECOMES DURABLE. Pairs with proxy v7.55 (POST /commit-comprehension) and reporter v1.13 (Commit Comprehension section). STILL NO CUSTOMER-FACING BEHAVIOUR CHANGE -- this is instrumentation, now persistent instead of ephemeral. WHY: v9.7.558 logged [LP COMMIT COMPREHENSION DIAG] to console exactly like every other diagnostic in this file, visible only in a live DevTools session and gone unless someone manually captured it. That defeated the observer's own stated purpose -- the plan is to read delta counts off LIVE traffic to decide whether the comprehension pass is ever trusted, and a console line nobody exports cannot produce a sample. EXTENSION SIDE: new _lpSendCommitComprehension follows _lpFeedbackSend exactly -- same endpoint resolution, same _lpAttachLicense, keepalive:true, .catch(function(){}), never awaited. It is called from INSIDE the observer's own resolution rather than from a second dispatch site, so it fires exactly when a verdict exists and inherits the observer's kill switch for free: LEADPRO_COMMIT_COMPREHENSION = false means the observer never runs, so nothing logs and nothing posts. No second flag, per instruction. WHAT IS PERSISTED, AND THE POSTURE IS v9.7.489's RATHER THAN A NEW ONE: the row carries CRM RECORD LOCATORS -- autoLeadId, customerId, dealerId, leadSource, store -- so a disagreement is investigated by opening the lead in VinSolutions and reading the real notes there. No customer contact data. No CRM note text: the notes stay in the CRM, exactly as feedback rows leave drafts out unless a correction happened. ONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION, narrow and stated rather than slipped in: when the comprehension quote FAILED verification, that string IS sent (scrubbed with the same _lpScrubPII the draft-pair capture uses, capped at 300 chars). It is MODEL-GENERATED and by definition does NOT appear in the lead, so opening the lead cannot recover it -- it is the one thing a QUOTE-FABRICATED row could not be audited without. A VERIFIED quote is never sent; only its length, because the note it came from is already in the CRM. WORKER v7.55: POST /commit-comprehension mirrors POST /feedback line for line -- invalid-JSON rejection, required-field check, REQUIRE_LICENSE handling with the same fleet-readiness log when it is off, a SERVER timestamp in the KV key (a client-chosen ts+id could otherwise overwrite another row -- the v7.29/W3 lesson), the same LEADPRO_LICENSES namespace and the same 90-day TTL. It additionally rejects an unrecognised delta rather than storing junk, and clamps every stored string. The /feedback handler is asserted BYTE-IDENTICAL to v7.54 -- this is purely additive. REPORTER v1.13: a Commit Comprehension section reading the commit: KV prefix with the same two-UTC-prefix + CT-date filter the feedback read uses, so a verdict recorded near CT midnight cannot appear in both days' reports. It shows agreement rate, disagreement rate, a fabricated-quote count, the per-delta breakdown with what each delta MEANS in plain words, a by-lead-source table, and a disagreement list whose LEAD column deep-links into VinSolutions via the v1.12 vinLeadUrl. The read sits in its own try/catch: instrumentation ABOUT an observer must never cost the daily report its operational content. An unrecognised delta is skipped rather than crashing the render. The section states on its face that no comprehension verdict has ever changed a customer-facing message. VERIFIED 50/50 in a new suite plus all 25 suites green (710 assertions), dev===comm on every case, and the two things that actually matter are PROVEN rather than asserted. (1) FIRE-AND-FORGET IS REAL: the observer is handed a POST that NEVER resolves, and separately one that throws synchronously, and it still resolves with its own verdict both times -- so a hung or failing proxy cannot delay or alter a draft. A rejecting fetch is swallowed; a verdict with no delta sends nothing; buildUserPrompt references the sender nowhere. (2) THE PRIVACY POSTURE IS ENFORCED, NOT DESCRIBED: the payload is built from a scraped record deliberately loaded with phone, email, name and ZIP, and the test asserts NONE of those strings appears anywhere in the body, field by field. The worker's stored meta is asserted to be exactly the five locator keys with no phone/email/name/zip/address field. THREE OF MY OWN TEST ASSERTIONS WERE WRONG AND THE SUITE CAUGHT ALL THREE, which is the value of writing them this way: the /feedback byte-identity check sliced 'from /feedback to /list-licenses' and broke the moment a new endpoint was inserted between them (now bounded by the handler's own first and last lines); the reporter harness omitted percentiles and _CT_FMT from its helper slice; and a NaN assertion scoped to the WHOLE report failed on the pre-existing Latency table reacting to this test's own deliberately thin reqs fixture -- asserting on my fixture rather than on the code under test. That one is now scoped to the commit section, with an anti-vacuity check that the section was really found. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing, and the suite fails loudly if that stops being true. The real costs are KV write volume (one row per generation that has a real call note, 90-day TTL, same namespace as feedback -- worth watching against the v7.29/W6c precedent where feedback: keys at 90-day retention were being parsed as licenses by /list-licenses; commit: keys are a NEW prefix and /list-licenses filters on its own unprefixed list, so this should not recur, but it is named here so it is checked rather than assumed) and one extra POST per verdict. The flag turns all of it off with no other change. node --check clean on both builds, the proxy and the reporter; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.558-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.558-dev (DEV. PHASE 2 OF 2 -- A COMPREHENSION PASS THAT CAN ONLY WATCH. NO CUSTOMER-FACING BEHAVIOUR CHANGES IN THIS BUILD, by design and by test. The frustration this answers is real: nearly every incident this month was the EXTRACTION layer missing a phrasing shape or a boundary, not the model failing to understand a note once it saw the right thing. But this exact block carries the counter-evidence -- v9.7.197 and v9.7.368 are two tightenings that each FOLLOWED a looser, more interpretive version fabricating a verbal commitment and shipping it to a real customer. Both failed by INTERPRETING LOOSELY, not by misreading clear text. So this is not 'trust the model instead of the regex'; it is a second reading that runs alongside and is structurally incapable of authoring anything. THREE CONTAINMENT PROPERTIES, each asserted rather than promised. (1) IT NEVER REACHES A PROMPT: buildUserPrompt and classifyScenario contain ZERO references to any symbol the pass defines, and the window stash is written exactly once and read exactly nowhere. If a later build wires it in, those assertions fail first. The regex verdict stash is written inside buildUserPrompt and read only at the observer dispatch, which the suite pins at exactly one read in the whole file. (2) ITS OUTPUT IS FALSIFIABLE, NOT A JUDGEMENT: the probe must return a quote copied character-for-character out of ONE named note, or say there is none. The quote is then VERIFIED as a literal substring of a note -- whitespace-normalised and case-insensitive, because a model reflowing a line is not the failure mode that hurt us; inventing words is. A paraphrase is rejected as QUOTE-FABRICATED and counted. That is the single biggest lever against repeating v9.7.197/368, and it is mechanical, so 'the model made something up' becomes a number rather than an argument. (3) IT IS ISOLATED FROM THE REGEX'S OWN ANSWER: the probe sees ONLY the walked call-note entries -- no Lead Pro directives, no VOI, no scenario, and specifically NOT the 'CALL NOTE -- READ BEFORE WRITING' block the regex writes when it fires. This one is load-bearing and easy to get wrong: a probe embedded in the main generation call would sit in a prompt that already states the answer, agree by construction, and produce disagreement data worth nothing. It is therefore a separate, isolated call. IT RUNS ON PHASE 1'S WALKED ENTRIES -- the same _lpWalkCrmEntries(context, {type:'[CALL NOTE]', max:6}) the regex path reads -- so both verdicts are answering a question about the SAME text and a disagreement is about comprehension, never about boundaries. That is the concrete payoff of shipping Phase 1 first. WHAT IT LOGS: [LP COMMIT COMPREHENSION DIAG] prints the two verdicts side by side with a delta -- AGREE-COMMITMENT, AGREE-NONE, DISAGREE-REGEX-ONLY, DISAGREE-COMPREHENSION-ONLY, or QUOTE-FABRICATED -- in the same family as [LP COMMAND COVERAGE DIAG]'s sms-vs-email delta. Every exit path logs, including SKIPPED (no call notes / no endpoint), UNPARSEABLE, FAILED and THREW: a probe that silently did not run would quietly bias the very count this build exists to collect. COST AND LATENCY, PLAINLY, because this is not free: it is a SECOND API call on any lead that has a real call note. It is fire-and-forget -- dispatched after the draft is already rendered and never awaited, which the suite asserts -- so it cannot delay or alter a message, and it is gated so it does not run when there are no notes to read. LEADPRO_COMMIT_COMPREHENSION = false switches it off entirely. VERIFIED 42/42 in a new suite plus all 24 suites green (660 assertions), dev===comm on every case, run against the real captures: Jason Pellegrin's 22-call-note history and the log119 line-436 lead. The fetch is injected, so every branch -- fabricated quote, wrong note number, fenced JSON, unparseable text, rejected fetch, missing candidates, unknown kind, flag off -- is exercised with no network. DISAGREE-COMPREHENSION-ONLY is asserted REACHABLE on a real phrasing shape the regex does not cover ('said he would swing past after work thursday'), because a disagreement detector that can only ever agree would be worthless. A CONTAINMENT ASSERTION CAUGHT MY OWN TEST BUG, which is the point of writing it that way: the first version sliced buildUserPrompt as 'from its declaration to classifyScenario', but classifyScenario is defined BEFORE it in the file, so the slice silently ran to end-of-file and swept in the dispatch site. It is now taken from the function's own closing brace, plus an anti-vacuity assertion that the sliced body is really the whole function. WHAT HAPPENS NEXT, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT: nothing is promoted on the strength of this build. The next step is reading the delta counts off live logs -- how often the two differ, in which direction, and on what shape of note -- and that data decides whether the comprehension pass ever becomes load-bearing. It is not a judgement call to make here. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: nothing customer-facing, and the suite is structured so that claim fails loudly if it stops being true. The real costs are money and log volume -- one extra model call per generation on a lead with call notes, and one extra diagnostic line. If either bites, the flag turns it off with no other change. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker; version and version_name both bumped. Phase 1 (v9.7.557) shipped and was verified green before this build started, per instruction. Builds on v9.7.557-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.557-dev (DEV. PHASE 1 OF 2 -- ONE SHARED CRM ENTRY WALKER. CONSOLIDATION, NOT A BUG FIX: no live incident, no customer-facing behaviour change, and every migrated consumer is verified byte-identical rather than merely suite-green. WHY: five consumers hand-rolled 'split the notes into entries and do not absorb across a boundary' and five shipped their own boundary bug -- v9.7.552, v9.7.553, v9.7.554, v9.7.555 and v9.7.556 (twice in one build). New _lpWalkCrmEntries(context, opts) splits on LP_CRM_ENTRY_SPLIT_RE (the pattern v9.7.556 proved against Jason Pellegrin's real 22-note history), trims trailing scaffold per-entry with the shared v9.7.552 LP_SCAFFOLD_LINE_RE, and returns entries in context order with an optional type filter, an optional cap applied AFTER the filter, and opts.trimText for byte-exact migration. WHAT THE INVESTIGATION CHANGED ABOUT THE SCOPE, and it is the main finding: OF THE FIVE CONSUMERS NAMED, ONLY ONE IS ACTUALLY A DATED-ENTRY WALKER. From the shipped bytes -- v9.7.552/553 pivot is ctxForPivot.split('\\n'), a LINE-level tag state machine; v9.7.554 coverage is cmdText.split(/[,;\\n]/) over an AGENT'S COMMAND STRING, which is not context at all; v9.7.555 off-franchise is new RegExp(makes.join('|')), a TOKEN boundary; only v9.7.556 verbal-commit splits dated entries. They are THREE different boundary problems that happen to share a symptom, and forcing the other four onto an entry walker would buy nothing and obscure what each actually does. The two other families are real and named for a later build: token-boundary (v9.7.554's _lpCmdTermHit and v9.7.555's make alternation are literally the same bug and could share one helper) and line-level tag state machine (v9.7.552/553's pivot opener/closer and _lpCustomerText / _lpArcHasSchedulingConstraint, which is the family with TWO shipped bugs). A SIXTH INSTANCE THE REPORT DID NOT LIST was found and IS a genuine fit: ctxEntries, the arc-bound / scaffold-leak splitter, splitting on /\\n(?=\\[\\d{2}\\/\\d{2}\\/)/ -- narrower than v9.7.556's, requiring a two-digit month AND day AND no leading whitespace. On every real capture the two agree exactly (68 entries on Jason's context either way), but the narrow one silently fails to split '[8/2/2026 ...]' or an indented entry: the same absorb-across-the-boundary class, latent rather than live, now closed. SHIPPED AS TWO SEPARATE VERIFIED STEPS, per Gil's instruction not to write and consolidate in one diff: step 1 commits the utility and its 37-assertion suite with ZERO consumers touched, so 'the utility is correct' is checkable on its own; step 2 migrates verbal-commit and ctxEntries, so 'the migration preserved behaviour' is a separate claim. MIGRATION PROOF IS BYTE-LEVEL, NOT CONTENT-LEVEL. The first migration of ctxEntries was content-identical but NOT byte-identical -- the walker trims each entry and the hand-rolled split did not, so the FINAL entry of every context lost its trailing newline. Rather than argue that delta is inert, opts.trimText was added and the site passes trimText:false; all three real captures now compare byte-for-byte entry-by-entry, and the suite asserts BOTH that they match with the flag and that exactly one entry differs without it, so the flag cannot rot into a no-op. TESTED AGAINST REAL CAPTURES ONLY: Jason Pellegrin's 68-entry / 22-call-note history, Jeffrey Best's Gubagoo chat (where the [GUBAGOO CHAT] entry must survive as ONE entry with all five [CUSTOMER] turns intact), and the Corolla context. Asserted: no entry ever contains two dated headers; order is newest-first as VinSolutions renders; the cap counts KEPT entries; an entry's own dated header is never treated as scaffold while a scaffold line below it still cuts; a date inside an entry body does not split it; and null/undefined/empty/whitespace-only inputs never throw. TWO OF MY OWN TEST EXPECTATIONS WERE WRONG AND THE SUITE CAUGHT BOTH: Jeffrey's context does carry one [CALL NOTE] (08/18 9:54 AM), and a dated header with an empty body is real content that must be KEPT rather than dropped as whitespace. All 23 suites green (618 assertions), dev===comm on every case. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: ctxEntries now splits shapes it previously ran together -- a one-digit-dated or indented entry. That is strictly more correct, but it means an arc that previously presented as one entry could now present as two, which changes what the v9.7.543 per-entry scaffold cut sees on such a lead. No capture reviewed contains that shape, which is exactly why it is latent. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker; version and version_name both bumped. PHASE 2 (comprehension-pass observer) is deliberately NOT in this build and starts only now that this one is verified. Builds on v9.7.556-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.556-dev (DEV. A CUSTOMER'S SATURDAY COMMITMENT WAS BLOCKED BY THE VOICEMAIL NOTE SITTING NEXT TO IT. LIVE INCIDENT: Jason Pellegrin (Community Honda Lafayette, dealerId 24399, lead 2043828702, 8/20). He told Chassica Vincent by phone on 8/19 at 4:10 PM -- disposition Contacted, a real conversation, not a drop -- that he would 'try to come in on sat just to see what her car is worth'. The draft was a cold 'did you end up getting a number elsewhere?' with no mention of Saturday. Gil identified the capture as the cause and was right; reproducing it against his real 20,913-char context found TWO MORE defects behind it, and fixing only the reported one leaves the lead broken. (1) THE REPORTED CAUSE: the capture was /\\[CALL NOTE\\][\\s\\S]{0,500}/ -- it started at the FIRST [CALL NOTE] tag and swept a flat 500 characters with no boundary at the next note. Context is newest-first and the newest entry on his lead is an unrelated 8/20 11:36 AM '(Machine) / Left message', so the window held BOTH notes concatenated and _isVoicemailBoilerplate tested the combined blob. log119 line 1442 printed exactly that. (2) THE SCAN ONLY EVER LOOKED AT THE FIRST CALL NOTE. Bounded but not iterated, it would have examined the 8/20 voicemail, correctly blocked on it, and NEVER REACHED the 8/19 commitment one entry below -- Jason would still have got the cold message. Of his 22 call notes only 7 are non-boilerplate and the real one is #2. The walk now skips boilerplate notes and keeps looking. (3) THE COMMITMENT REGEX MATCHED NEITHER NOTE. 'will try to come in on sat' fails `will (?:come|be|visit|stop|bring)` because of the intervening 'try to', and 'coming sat 29th 4pm' fails `coming (?:in|by|today|tomorrow)` because 'sat' is not in that list. Both notes could be extracted perfectly and still not fire. A SECOND VICTIM IN THE SAME LOG, not reported and found by reading it: line 436, a different lead, identical collision -- an unrelated '(Machine) / Left message' by Rochelle Price blocking an even more explicit '(Contacted) ... coming sat 29th 4pm' by Jolette Aguilar. Line 1195 in the same log is a genuine voicemail-only lead that must keep blocking, and does. THE FIX. Notes are split on the dated-entry boundary the context is already built with, trailing scaffold is trimmed per-note with the shared v9.7.552 LP_SCAFFOLD_LINE_RE (so a 'FOLLOW-UP: read the full transcript' block can no longer ride along on the last entry), and the boilerplate test runs against ONE note's own text. The walk is CAPPED at the newest 6 notes: the old 500-char window reached about two notes deep, so walking all 60 entries on a lead like this one would be a real widening and could surface a months-old commitment as though it were live. The cap covers a run of voicemail drops before the real conversation and stops there, and it is stated in the log rather than applied silently. If the entry shape ever changes the block degrades to the previous flat-window behaviour rather than losing the feature, same discipline as the v9.7.544 fence fallback. WIDENING THE COMMITMENT REGEX IS NOT DONE LIGHTLY -- it has three prior tightenings against it (v9.7.197, v9.7.368, v9.7.429/427), two of which followed a fabricated commitment shipping to a real customer. Two shapes were added, both from real notes in this log, both kept as the OBJECT of a commitment verb so the v9.7.429 rule still holds that a day-word never matches standalone: 'will TRY TO/AND come' and 'coming <day-name>'. Every documented false positive is re-asserted in the suite and none of them match. A NEW NEGATION GUARD ships with the widening, because a wider net over agent shorthand meets 'not coming in' and "won't be coming in" sooner: a commitment phrase preceded by not/never/won't/can't/cannot/unable to/no longer within 12 characters is refused and the refusal is logged. FOUR DIAGNOSTIC STATES INSTEAD OF TWO, all naming the single note they read and how many were walked past: FIRED, NO COMMITMENT (a real conversation that carries no commitment phrase -- previously indistinguishable from no note being read at all, which is exactly the difference between defects 1 and 2 here), NO USABLE NOTE (every note in the capped walk was boilerplate, with the counts), and REFUSED (negated). The old BLOCKED line is now reachable only on the degraded flat-window path and is labelled as such rather than left looking live. VERIFIED 36/36 IN A NEW SUITE against the real captures, sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case. There was no committed suite for this block before -- three tightenings and no regression file -- so the documented false positives from all three are now asserted rather than trusted. The before/after is run against Jason's actual context: v9.7.555 emits the BLOCKED line byte-identical to log119 line 1442 with hasVerbalCommitment false; v9.7.556 fires, quotes his exact words, and reports 'note 2 of 22 (examined 2, skipped 1 boilerplate)'. The second lead fires on 'coming sat 29th 4pm' with the FOLLOW-UP scaffold trimmed off; the voicemail-only lead still refuses; the showroom-followup exception still suppresses the whole block; and the nine shapes that already fired all still fire. TWO OF MY OWN TEST EXPECTATIONS WERE WRONG AND THE SUITE CAUGHT THEM: the negation guard as first written missed "won't BE coming in" because it required the negator immediately adjacent, and I had asserted the old BLOCKED wording for a voicemail-only lead when the correct new report is NO USABLE NOTE, which carries more (how many notes existed and how many were refused). WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: the walk now reaches up to six notes deep instead of roughly two, so a lead carrying five voicemail drops above a stale commitment from weeks ago could surface that commitment as current. The cap bounds it, the note's own date is printed in the diagnostic, and the alternative -- a customer who told us they are coming Saturday getting asked whether they bought elsewhere -- is worse. The regex widening admits 'coming <day>', which an agent might write about their OWN callback plan rather than the customer's visit; the negation guard does not catch that shape and it is a real residual risk worth watching in the FIRED lines. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; nothing added inside inlineScraper, verified against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker (dev 4532-9299, commercial 4303-8982); version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.555-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.555-dev (DEV. A CALL-METADATA LINE WAS READ AS CUSTOMER SPEECH, AND 'ram' WAS FOUND INSIDE 'timeframe'. LIVE INCIDENT: Hayden N (Community Honda Lafayette, dealerId 24399, lead 2070578764, 8/20). A CarGurus PHONE lead -- no chat transcript, no Ram in the call notes, the agent notes, the panel, or anywhere on the record -- was asked TWICE: 'Are you calling about the Civic, or were you specifically looking for a Ram?' log118 lines 1278 and 1477 both carry [LP OFF-FRANCHISE DIAG] "ram" (make ram) vs honda store | saidNew:false | inStockOfMake:0. TWO INDEPENDENT DEFECTS STACKED, and Gil identified both before reporting; each is sufficient alone to produce the incident. (1) _lpCustomerText PREPENDED d.lastInboundMsg AS CUSTOMER SPEECH WITH NO GATE, unlike every other source it reads -- the conversationBrief and context scans both require a real [CUSTOMER] tag, and lastInboundMsg required nothing. On a phone lead with no transcript that field is not the customer's words at all; it is the provider's call record. Hayden's, verbatim: 'Phone Lead from CarGurus. Caller Id: None, Duration: 6 minutes, 51 seconds Likelihood to buy: Standard Timeframe: 2 weeks. Show Less'. Nobody said that, and FIVE consumers were reading it as though someone had -- the off-franchise guard, the deal-condition and trade scans, isRemoteBuyer (which tests for 'out of state', a phrase a call record could easily carry), and the day-off scan. (2) _lpChatVehicleCandidates BUILT ITS MAKE ALTERNATION WITH NO WORD BOUNDARY on either side, so 'ram' matched inside 'Timef(ram)e'. Exactly the defect class v9.7.554 fixed one build ago in _lpCmdTermHit, where bare substring matching let 'poi' match 'appointment' -- and the short makes are a standing trap: ram (timeframe, program, dramatic), kia (Kiawah), mini (minivan), gmc, vw, benz (benzene). TWO FIXES, both layered deliberately so neither is load-bearing alone. (A) A METADATA-SHAPE GATE, NOT A LEAD-TYPE CHECK. New _lpIsSystemMetadataLine tests the STRING, not the source: an opening 'Phone/Call/Inbound/Voice Lead from <provider>', or two-or-more distinct call-record labels (caller id, duration, likelihood to buy, timeframe, disposition, lead type/source, talk time, ring time, show less). A leadSource==='phone' check would have been enumeration, and this file has been bitten by enumerating the shapes it happened to have seen in three consecutive builds -- v9.7.552 (five closers, [CHAT BOT] not among them), v9.7.553 (one opener, two tags missing), v9.7.554 (keyword tokens). A shape test rejects the NEXT provider's blob too, whatever it is called. The bar is deliberately TWO labels: one alone ('Duration: 3 minutes') is a phrase a person could plausibly type. (B) THE MAKE ALTERNATION IS NOW \\b-BOUNDED on both sides, matching the v9.7.554 treatment. NEW [LP CUSTOMER-TEXT DIAG] logs every refusal with the leadSource, convState and the rejected value verbatim -- ONCE PER DISTINCT STRING, not once per call, because _lpCustomerText runs five times a generation and a five-line-per-lead diagnostic is noise rather than instrumentation. A future case where a metadata line collides with a make name now surfaces from the log. VERIFIED 44/44 IN A NEW SUITE, sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case, against Hayden's REAL lastInboundMsg rather than a reconstruction. PROVENANCE NOTE, because it matters: the v9.7.483 changelog records 'VERIFIED 21/21' for this guard, but that harness was a one-off and NEVER landed in tests/ -- there was no committed off-franchise suite before this build. The new file reconstructs that coverage from the incidents v9.7.483 names and adds the v9.7.555 regressions on top. A TRAP CAUGHT WHILE WRITING IT, worth recording: the first version of the Hayden assertions passed an EMPTY inventory cache, and they passed against the PREVIOUS build too -- because the guard fires via `_ofSaidNew || (_ofUnits && _ofMatch.length === 0)`, so a missing cache suppresses it on its own and the test proved nothing. log118's 'inStockOfMake:0' says the cache WAS loaded, which is what made it fire. Every Hayden case now runs against a loaded, Ram-free Honda cache, plus an explicit CONTROL asserting that the SAME cache and rooftop DO fire on real prose naming a Ram -- so the silence is caused by the fix and not by the fixture. Confirmed against the previous build under that cache: v9.7.554 emits the diag line byte-identical to log118 line 1278; v9.7.555 emits nothing. The suite also asserts EACH LAYER ALONE stops the incident, that Halie Bott's verbatim inbound ('we are planning to purchase a new chevy silverado...') still fires with chevy normalised to Chevrolet and her own Dodge Durango trade still correctly not the hit, that Monique Meaux's '2022+ Ford F-150 XLT' still produces its candidate, that a genuine Ram request fires even when the word 'timeframe' sits beside it, and that every pre-existing precision guard is unchanged (house brand silent, own trade silent, agent notes cannot trigger, off-brand IN stock silent without 'new', explicit NEW fires and surfaces the real used unit, unknown rooftop silent, Kia rooftop fires on Honda). The boundary rule is tested directly against timeframe/programs/dramatic/Kiawah/minivan/benzene while Ram 1500, Kia Telluride, GMC Sierra, Honda Civic Sport and Mini Cooper all still match. All 21 suites green (545 assertions). WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a customer whose genuine first message IS terse and label-shaped -- something like 'Trade: 2019 Durango. Timeframe: 2 weeks.' typed by hand -- would now be refused as metadata and drop out of all five consumers. It takes two labels to trigger, the message still reaches the model in full through the prompt's own CUSTOMER'S INQUIRY block (nothing is hidden from the model here; only the five internal scans are affected), and the new diagnostic makes it visible if it happens. Losing a trigger on a rare hand-typed label-shaped message is the right trade against asking a Honda buyer twice about a truck we cannot sell. ALSO NOW BOUNDED, as a side effect worth naming: 'mini' no longer matches 'minivan', which was a real latent false positive at every rooftop. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; the new helpers verified absent from inlineScraper against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker (dev 4531-9298, commercial 4302-8981); version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.554-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.554-dev (DEV. A MULTI-PART AGENT LP COMMAND SURVIVED IN THE EMAIL AND LOST A PART IN THE SMS. NOT A DATA BUG and not a pipeline failure -- Gil verified the command pipeline is working correctly before reporting it, and log117 backs that up: parsed once ('[Lead Pro] LP commands found: 1'), injected three times, highest-priority framing on each. LEAD: Amber Johnson (Community Kia Baytown, dealerId 6190, lead 2070428771, 8/20). The agent wrote three distinct asks -- 'Need POI, How much money as initial investment is the most they can use, What vehicle and are they flexible.' The EMAIL asked all three: '...must-haves, or are you flexible? Also, what is the most you want to use as your initial investment, and what can you provide for proof of income?' The SMS asked two: '...must-haves, and what is the most you want to use initially?' The proof-of-income clause is what lost the length cut. THE GAP IS CHANNEL-SPECIFIC COMPLETENESS, and it is a rule that was never written. The SMS format rule said 'Same specific hook, same quality, same framing. Just written at text length' -- which tells the model to compress, and says nothing about what compression may NOT touch. An LP command is the one thing in the prompt an agent typed by hand for this specific lead, and it was competing for space with scaffold the file generates automatically. TWO CHANGES. (1) THE RULE, in two places. The shipped SMS format rule now states that writing at text length is a CUT and an agent LP command is never what gets cut -- naming what to cut INSTEAD (a comparable vehicle, an incentive mention, an appointment time, a second reason to come in, scene-setting) rather than only forbidding, so the model has somewhere to take the length from. And a new MULTI-ASK MANDATE renders inside the AGENT LP COMMAND block itself, stating the clause count outright, requiring every ask in the SMS AND the email AND the voicemail, and closing the two obvious escapes -- merging two asks into one vague question, and deferring one to 'a later message' on the assumption the email covered it, when the customer may only ever read the text. It is deliberately GATED on the command actually having more than one clause, so the common single-ask command adds zero prompt text. This is the same shape as the URL MANDATE that already sits ten lines below it ('This URL MUST appear in BOTH the SMS and the email. This is NOT optional.') -- per-channel completeness for agent-supplied content is existing precedent in this block, applied to the asks themselves. (2) THE DIAGNOSTIC, so the next one surfaces from the log instead of needing a human to notice the SMS read shorter than the email. New always-on [LP COMMAND COVERAGE DIAG] beside [LP RAW DIAG]: splits the command on commas/semicolons/newlines, drops filler words, expands the dealership shorthand an agent types but a draft writes out (POI -> proof of income, DP -> down payment, DL -> driver's license, and so on), and reports sms/email/vm coverage plus a per-clause table and an explicit 'DROPPED FROM SMS ONLY' list. On Amber it reads sms:2/3 email:3/3 and names 'Need POI' outright. IT IS A ROUGH KEYWORD CHECK AND DELIBERATELY NEVER A GATE -- paraphrase that shares no keyword will read as a miss, so an uncovered clause is a prompt to go look at the draft, not a verdict on it; the SMS-vs-EMAIL DELTA is the signal worth watching. ONE SUBTLETY THAT MATTERED: naive substring matching reports the POI clause as COVERED, because 'poi' is a substring of 'appointment' and of 'point' -- the exact word most likely to appear in a BDC text. Short tokens (<=4 chars) therefore require BOTH word boundaries while longer tokens keep a trailing-open boundary so 'initial' still matches 'initially'. Without that the diagnostic would have silently reported this very incident as fine. VERIFIED 31/31 in a new suite, sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case, run against the REAL rawSms/rawEmail strings out of log117 rather than a reconstruction: the email reads 3/3, the SMS reads 2/3, the uncovered clause is 'Need POI' specifically, and the SMS/email delta names exactly that clause. The boundary rule is tested directly ('poi' must not match 'appointment' or 'point'; 'dl' must not match 'idle'). An SMS that DOES ask for POI reads 3/3, so the fix has somewhere to land. The scan is exercised on every shape it will meet in production -- empty command, empty channel, null inputs, single-ask, trailing punctuation, regex-special characters, an embedded URL. The mandate fires on a 2-part and a 3-part command, renders NOTHING for a single-ask command or no command at all, and the pre-existing SMS format rules are asserted still intact. All 20 suites green (501 assertions). WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a genuinely long multi-part command now makes the SMS longer than the terse text this file otherwise pushes for, and on a 4-5 part command the text may read as a questionnaire. That is the deliberate trade -- an agent who wrote four asks wants four answers, and the alternative is silently dropping one -- but if it lands badly the right follow-up is prioritising within the command rather than reverting the rule. The coverage diagnostic will show whether long commands are common enough to matter. NOT CHANGED: the command still renders three times per prompt (HIGHEST PRIORITY block, AGENT INSTRUCTIONS in vehicleExtras, AGENT OVERRIDE INSTRUCTIONS last); the mandate is added to ONE of them rather than all three, since tripling the mandate text would crowd the very asks it protects. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; the new helpers verified absent from inlineScraper against the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker (dev 4485-9252, commercial 4256-8935); version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.553-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.553-dev (DEV. THE PIVOT DETECTOR'S OPENER HAD THE SAME ENUMERATION PROBLEM v9.7.552 JUST FIXED IN ITS CLOSER -- and v9.7.552 made it worse. NOT A LIVE INCIDENT: Gil found this by reading the new [LP PIVOT SCOPE DIAG] rather than from a bad draft, so no wrong output shipped. Recorded in full here so it is found once instead of rediscovered from a future log. THE GAP: custOnlyLines opened only on a literal [CUSTOMER] tag, but the scraper writes the customer's own words under THREE tags depending on how the lead arrived -- [CUSTOMER] for full turns, '[CUSTOMER CHAT SUMMARY]' once a chat-sourced lead (m-chat|chat lead|gubagoo.*chat|live chat) moves past first-touch (popup.js ~6310), and '[CUSTOMER REQUEST FROM INQUIRY]' for a lead-received note's extracted question (~6460). Neither of the latter two is the literal [CUSTOMER], so neither opened the block -- and v9.7.552's new uppercase-tag CLOSER then matched both, so the fix that bounded the runaway also actively SHUT the scan on the customer's own text. CONFIRMED LIVE ON THE SAME LEAD AS v9.7.552: Jeffrey Best (2069849624). His first-touch capture carried full [CUSTOMER] turns and the detector scanned them; the very next follow-up capture on that same lead switched to [CUSTOMER CHAT SUMMARY] and reported custLines:0. Reproduced against v9.7.552's shipped bytes: both tags return custLines:0, candidates []. WHY IT MATTERS EVEN THOUGH NOTHING WAS WRONG: the summary text still reaches the model as plain prose elsewhere in the prompt, so nothing was hidden from it. What was lost is the EXPLICIT 'VEHICLE PIVOT DETECTED' directive -- which is exactly the failure the detector was built to close. The Allen Billups incident (v9.7.537) was a real signal losing to a competing directive because nothing named it outright, and a chat-lead follow-up carrying a genuine pivot in its older history is that same shape. FIX: the opener now accepts all three tags via one regex; every closer is unchanged, so the uppercase-tag and scaffold boundaries behave exactly as v9.7.552 shipped them. New _pvOpenedBy records WHICH shape opened the block and how often, surfaced as openedBy in [LP PIVOT SCOPE DIAG] -- a fourth transcript shape will now show as 'openedBy:(nothing -- no recognised customer tag in this context)' in the log line instead of requiring another live-capture investigation. VERIFIED 37/37 in the fences-fallback suite (7 new), all sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: both new tags open and yield the right candidate; the opened-by set is reported for a mixed context and comes back empty rather than silent when nothing opens; a [GUBAGOO CHAT] tag still CLOSES a block it did not open; an agent [NOTE] naming a model still cannot open the scan; and Jeffrey's real 8,870-char context still yields zero candidates, so the wider opener does not reopen the v9.7.552 hole. All 19 suites green. OBSERVED WHILE IN HERE, NOT CHANGED: the '[=== CURRENT LEAD SUBMITTED HERE ===]' marker matches neither the tag closer (it does not start with A-Z) nor the anchored scaffold regex (it is preceded by a date), so it does not bound the pivot scan. That is arguably correct -- the detector is meant to read older history -- but it is the one recognised transcript marker the scan ignores, and it is written down here rather than changed. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.552-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.552-dev (DEV. A LEAD PRO DIRECTIVE AND A LEAD PRO SCAFFOLD LINE WERE READ BACK AS CUSTOMER EVIDENCE, and the draft told a live buyer his in-stock car had sold and offered him the car he already owns. LIVE INCIDENT: Jeffrey Best (Community Honda Baytown, dealerId 6191, lead 2069849624, 8/18). His VOI is a 2021 Kia Telluride EX, CPO, stock TG466039A, confirmed in stock and parsed straight off the panel by a clean single-panel merge ([LP VOI DIAG] frame 2246 panelText "2021 Kia Telluride EX (Certified)") -- this is NOT a split-frame repeat, as Gil said. The delivered SMS: "The Telluride has sold, but I can connect you with Community Toyota Baytown for a Highlander." Neither half is true. ROOT CAUSE, MEASURED AGAINST HIS REAL 8,882-CHAR CONTEXT, NOT A RECONSTRUCTION -- AND IT IS NOT CROSS-LEAD BLEED. The customer does have three other leads on his record (the v9.7.551 [LP LEAD-GRID DIAG] reports 4, including a Sold 2015 Toyota Highlander from 5/14), but no text from those leads reaches data.context. BOTH false signals come out of Lead Pro's OWN scaffold: (1) SOLD -- running the shipped sold regex over his context returns exactly ONE hit, at offset 5876, inside our own directive "If the newest thing we told them was that the vehicle sold, you may not now write as though it is available." That is the identical string v9.7.545 was built to stop. (2) HIGHLANDER -- the only occurrence in any customer-scoped text is our own appended line "Customer's current vehicle (confirmed from service/sales history): 2015 Toyota Highlander." It appears in zero [CUSTOMER] lines. WHY v9.7.544/545 DID NOT HOLD: both bounded their scan to the region between the 'CONVERSATION TRANSCRIPT (...)\n---' fences and fell back to the whole assembled string when the fences were absent -- and that block is emitted ONLY when convState !== 'first-touch'. A first-touch lead, and every Gubagoo chat lead (which takes the 'CHAT TRANSCRIPT (' branch at popup.js:7812 instead), never carries the fences at all. The fallback was the live path and nobody could see it, because the fallback had no outcome line of its own. WHY THE PIVOT DETECTOR ABSORBED IT: custOnlyLines opened on any [CUSTOMER] line and closed on exactly five markers -- [AGENT], [NOTE], [CALL NOTE], [BROWSE, [TFS -- plus a '---' rule. Jeffrey's arc is a Gubagoo bot chat, so it alternates [CUSTOMER] / [CHAT BOT] / [CHAT SUBJECT], and [CHAT BOT] is not one of the five. The block opened on his first chat turn and NEVER closed: 39 lines, 5,627 chars, straight to the end of the context, swallowing the entire VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS section. Fourth appearance of the absorb-until-next-recognised-marker class (v9.7.529 / v9.7.543 / v9.7.544), in the consumer those fixes did not reach. THREE CHANGES. (1) SHARED SCAFFOLD BOUNDARY: new module-scope LP_SCAFFOLD_LINE_RE + LP_CTX_ENTRY_RE + _lpStripScaffold(), one list of the section headers and directive openers Lead Pro itself writes into data.context. Used only as a CLOSER, never as evidence, so an unrecognised scaffold line degrades to today's behaviour rather than eating real conversation. (2) SOLD SCAN: the fences-not-found fallback now strips that scaffold instead of scanning it. Dated CRM entries and tagged lines are untouched, so a genuine sold vehicle detected from real prose still reports sold; the authoritative 'vehicle status: sold' marker deliberately keeps reading the FULL context, since it is our own deliberate signal and is the path that works. (3) PIVOT custOnlyLines: TWO closers instead of five markers -- ANY uppercase bracketed tag that is not [CUSTOMER] ends the block (enumerating [CHAT BOT] alone would only move the bug to the next tag), plus the shared scaffold regex. Uppercase-only, so a customer typing "[ok]" cannot close it, and the [CUSTOMER] test runs first so a dated "[08/18/2026] [CUSTOMER]" header still opens. BOTH NOW LOG THEIR OUTCOME EITHER WAY, which is the part that makes the next one findable: [LP SOLD SCAN DIAG] fires on every fallback rather than only on a hit and carries scanScope, scaffoldStripped char count, proseMatchInScope vs proseMatchInFullCtx and the suppressed match verbatim; new always-on [LP PIVOT SCOPE DIAG] carries custLines/chars, closedByTag, closedByScaffold, whether the fences were found, and scopeCandidates vs fullCtxCandidates so every model name coming from our own text is named directly. HOW OFTEN THIS FALLBACK ACTUALLY RUNS -- Gil asked, so it was counted rather than guessed, across the six logs captured since v9.7.544 (109/110/111/112/113/115): [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] 15 fences-found vs 4 not-found, but [LP SOLD SCAN DIAG] 9 transcript-only vs 10 full-context. The two disagree because the arc-bound diag is gated on conversationBrief plus a non-first-touch convState, so it goes silent on exactly the leads where the sold scan falls back -- the true fallback rate is materially higher than the arc-bound line suggested. Of those 10 fallback rows, 4 returned vehicleSold:true on prose alone with authoritativeMarker:false, across TWO distinct leads: Jeffrey (1 generation) and lead 2068821407, a 2023 Toyota Corolla on 8/17 (3 generations). The Corolla's prompt was captured too, and its 3,620-char context contains exactly one sold-regex match -- the same directive of ours. It is now a second real-capture regression. Correspondingly, 7 of the 9 transcript-only rows report proseMatchInFullCtx:true with proseMatchInTranscript:false, i.e. v9.7.545 correctly refusing a scaffold hit on the leads where the fences happened to exist. VERIFIED 30/30 in the new fences-fallback suite plus all prior suites green, every block sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case. The before/after is run against the real captures: the PREVIOUS build over Jeffrey's actual context returns vehicleSold:true and the verbatim delivered note "SISTER-BRAND PIVOT: Customer is now interested in a Highlander (Toyota)" with custOnlyLines 39 lines / 5,627 chars; this build returns false, zero candidates, and no note. Same pair on the Corolla: true -> false. Must-still-fire holds -- a genuine agent "unfortunately that unit has been sold" in a dated entry, a "no longer available" inside a Gubagoo chat, the authoritative marker with scaffold all around it, a real Sorento pivot spoken inside a Gubagoo chat, a multi-line customer message, and a lowercase "[asking again]" that must NOT close the block. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a customer message that begins with a line matching a scaffold header -- someone typing "Color: silver" or "Manager: who is it" as the first line of a continuation -- loses the rest of that entry from the pivot scope and from the fallback sold scan. The [LP PIVOT SCOPE DIAG] closedByScaffold counter makes that visible if it happens, and losing a pivot note leaves the model the full arc to read, whereas telling a live buyer his car sold declines the sale. SCOPE DISCIPLINE: the new helpers are module-scope and are NOT referenced anywhere inside inlineScraper -- verified by the file's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker (dev 4408-9175, commercial 4179-8858), zero executable hits, the only textual match being a pre-existing comment. NOT FIXED HERE, flagged and real: the fences are still not emitted on first-touch and Gubagoo leads at all. Reconciling the brief builder so every path emits one bounded transcript region is the correct end state and would retire the fallback entirely -- it changes prompt text on every first-touch lead and needs its own build and its own before/after batch. Until then the fallback is bounded rather than trusted. Also unchanged: scenarioRules still renders twice per prompt and the sold and stalled directives can still co-occur with no arbitration. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.551-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.551-dev (DEV. SPLIT-FRAME ADOPTION TOOK A VEHICLE FROM A SERVICE RECORD ON A DIFFERENT LEAD OF THE SAME CUSTOMER. LIVE INCIDENT: Juan Aguirre (Community Toyota Baytown, dealerId 6189, lead 2068821407, Amp - Buying Signals, 8/17). His lead has NO vehicle of interest -- his own row in the LeadSelector grid has an empty Vehicle cell -- and Lead Pro told him "the Corolla you asked about has sold." He never asked about one. THREE DEFECTS IN SERIES, and log113 names all three. (1) THE DONOR IS A SERVICE RECORD, AND WE ALREADY KNEW IT. The adopted frame is the Autosoft (Service) record -- Status Complete, created 4/16/24, root 'vehicleinfo' -- carrying the Corolla Juan brought in for service. PASS 1 refuses these outright and said so on this very grab: '[Lead Pro] Frame rejected -- service-record source (not a sales lead): Autosoft' at log113 line 32. But it refuses with a `continue` INSIDE ITS OWN LOOP, which removes the frame from PASS 1 and from nothing else -- it stays in `sorted`, and the split-frame block reads `sorted`. The v9.7.544 comment sitting twenty lines above the bug describes this exact thing happening on Maci Alvarado: 'an xTimeLegacy SERVICE record ... that this same grab rejected as a lead source -- rejected as a LEAD, while its vehicle still reached the merge.' That build fixed the RECEIVING side and left the donor side open. Same donor class, second incident. Service frames are now excluded as donors on BOTH the main-merge and rescue paths, using the same test PASS 1 uses plus the frame's own _isServiceFrame flag (v9.7.263), which was already on the result object and already correct. (2) 'PANEL RENDERED EMPTY' WAS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM 'NO PANEL AT ALL'. v9.7.544's discriminator required `panelText && panelText !== '(no panel element)' && rawLen > 0`. That describes Maci's frame (panelText '(New)', rawLen 5) and NOT Juan's: his panel element EXISTS -- _voiDiag reports sel_gid1 true, sel_spanClass true, sel_spanAny true -- and reads as the EMPTY STRING, so panelText is falsy and rawLen is 0. Both halves of the test failed and the guard built for precisely this situation was inert. The real discriminator is whether an ELEMENT was found, which _voiDiag records directly: panelText is the literal '(no panel element)' ONLY when there was nothing to read, so anything else -- including '' -- is positive evidence the panel rendered and this lead has no vehicle. The selector booleans are now checked as an independent second route to the same fact. (3) 'ZERO COMPETING LEAD FRAMES' NEVER MEANT 'ONE LEAD IN PLAY'. _sfCompeting counts frames carrying a DIFFERENT autoLeadId. A donor with no id at all -- which is the only kind this block will adopt from -- contributes nothing to it, so the condition reads as a population check while measuring something else entirely. The page publishes the actual lead-level population: the LeadSelector grid renders every lead on the customer record with its own id in the row class. Juan's shows FOUR (active 2068821407 with an empty vehicle cell; 1474156609 Lost/Toyota Sequoia; 1434107959 SOLD/2023 Toyota Corolla; 158542771 Lost/2015 Toyota RAV4). New scraper field _leadSelectorCount/_leadSelectorIds plus an [LP LEAD-GRID DIAG] line; with more than one lead on the record, an ID-less panel cannot be attributed to the active lead and is refused. WHY customerId IS THE WRONG FIX, and it is worth stating because it is the obvious one: the Corolla lead belongs to Julian Aguirre, Juan's 2023 co-buyer, on the SAME customer record BY DESIGN -- VinSolutions is correct to show all four leads together. GlobalCustomerID cannot separate Julian's sold Corolla lead from Juan's active one. The discriminator had to be lead-level, and now is. ON THE PROPOSED 'FIX THE DONOR'S OWN autoLeadId EXTRACTION' ANGLE: it does not apply here, and the evidence says so plainly. The donor is not a lead whose id failed to read -- it is a SERVICE record with no autoLeadId to extract. Its own Lead Info block reads Source: Autosoft (Service), Status: Complete, 'Service Sales Rep:', and it is not one of the four leads in the grid. There is nothing to fix in its id extraction; the correct answer is that it is not a lead at all, which is what fix (1) encodes. LOGGING, per the request: the ACCEPTED/REFUSED/ABSTAINED line now reports the reason and the evidence on one line -- leadsOnRecord, activePanelEmpty and serviceDonorsRejected -- so this case reads as REFUSED with cause instead of the silent ACCEPTED it produced in log113. The rescue path gains its own REFUSED line. [LP VOI DIAG][conflict] AUDITED as asked. The concern is correct and the diagnostic does compare 'same-customer frames', which cannot separate a co-buyer's lead from the active one -- the identical blind spot. It is NOT the mechanism here (it reported 'clean -- 1 model family' because by the time it ran there was only one vehicle in play, the adopted one), and it is a chip-lighting detector rather than a prompt input, so changing its semantics is a behaviour change to a separate feature. NOT touched in this build; flagged for its own, with the note that its 'clean' verdict is worth nothing on a multi-lead customer record. VERIFIED 29/29 against the block sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case, driven with Juan's seven real frames as logged. His case now adopts nothing and logs REFUSED naming the service record, the empty active panel and the four leads on the record. Each of the three fixes is shown to stop it ALONE (service donor with an absent panel and one lead; rendered-empty panel with a clean donor; four leads with a clean donor and an absent panel), so none is load-bearing by itself. xTimeLegacy and CDK donors are caught too. NOT OVER-CORRECTED, which was the explicit risk: the v9.7.383 case the block exists for -- a single-lead customer whose only panel frame genuinely failed to publish its autoLeadId -- still ADOPTS, brings its stock number, and logs ACCEPTED; an UNKNOWN lead count (no frame rendered the grid) does not block; and a frame with no _voiDiag cannot prove the panel rendered, so it does not block either. Every pre-existing guard re-verified unchanged: competing leadId blocks, dealer conflict blocks, two candidates still ABSTAIN, a text-scanned non-panel vehicle is still not a candidate, and a lead that already has a vehicle is never touched. All thirteen extension suites green (264 assertions), node --check clean both builds, both manifests parse. SEPARATELY, THE v9.7.550 PROBE REPORTED IN: [LP AMP DIAG] fired on both dashboard-bearing frames of this grab and both read blurbById:false byClass:false bannerTextInFrame:false keyInfoNodes:0. One frame reports keyInfoPanel:true -- a key-information CONTAINER exists -- with zero keyInfo-* children, which is the render-timing signature rather than the never-renders one. The banner is still not capturable at scrape time. No further action this build; the question now has live data behind it. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a genuinely VOI-less lead on a multi-lead customer record whose real vehicle lives only in an ID-less frame now stays generic instead of adopting it. That is the intended trade -- the no-VOI directive ('do NOT invent one, ask what they are looking for') is the correct output for such a lead, and the alternative is what happened to Juan. The new REFUSED line makes any such case visible in a log rather than silent. Builds on v9.7.550-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.550-dev (DEV. [LP AMP DIAG] -- INSTRUMENT ONLY, ANSWERING A REACHABILITY QUESTION RATHER THAN GUESSING AT IT. Gil asked whether the Customer Dashboard's AMP banner is captured, and if not whether it can be: #keyInfo-Amp-blurb / .amp-summary, "This customer received an AMP marketing event", visible in the CRM on Juan Aguirre (Community Toyota Baytown, dealerId 6189, lead 2068821407, Amp - Buying Signals, 8/17). (1) IS IT TARGETED TODAY? No. Zero selectors anywhere in either build reference keyInfo-Amp, amp-summary, or the banner text. The only AMP capture that exists is ampEmailSubject, a TEXT regex over Contact History for "Marketing Campaign Email (subject: ...)", which did not match on this lead. (2) CAN IT BE, TODAY? NO -- AND THIS IS THE FINDING THAT MATTERS. The element is not in the scraped DOM at all. Measured against the 8/17 capture: keyInfo-Amp 0, amp-summary 0, "AMP marketing event" 0, "Equity:" 0, "Customer Intelligence" 0, and the campaign copy ("Upgrade Your") 0 -- in a file that DOES contain CustomerDashboardTop carrying Juan's name, three phone numbers and his street address. So the dashboard frame WAS captured; the entire Key Information panel simply had not rendered into it. The single "keyInfo" string in the whole document is "#cd-customer-profile #keyInfo-Accelerate" inside a Google Tag Manager click handler -- a selector in analytics SOURCE, not rendered content, and a trap for exactly this kind of grep. A selector added today could not fire, and would be indistinguishable from a working feature. This file has now shipped that mistake three times -- v9.7.455 (a call that threw frame-side and aborted every scrape), v9.7.505/506 (two diagnostics printing to the page console where no exported log could ever see them) and v9.7.532 (verified 11/11 against reconstructed note bodies, fired 0/1 against real ones). So this build ships a PROBE, not a feature. It reports, per frame: the id hit, the class hit, whether the banner text appears anywhere in the frame, how many [id^=keyInfo-] nodes exist and their names, whether the Key Information container exists, and the banner's own text when found. It speaks only on frames that could plausibly hold it, so it cannot spam the shells. One live log now settles reachability -- and distinguishes the three outcomes that need different fixes: never renders (dead end), renders after our capture (a timing fix), or renders into a shadow root (a different traversal). (3) IF IT IS REACHABLE, HOW IT MUST BE USED -- recording the design now, deliberately NOT built: the vehicle named in that banner and in the campaign creative ("Are You Ready to Upgrade Your Toyota Corolla") is the customer's CURRENT vehicle, the trade-up subject. It is not a vehicle of interest, it must never populate d.vehicle, and it must never be eligible for the sold scan. The correct destination is the existing ownedVehicle field, which already renders as "Customer's current vehicle (confirmed from service/sales history) ... This is their CURRENT vehicle, not what they want to buy" -- a line already present and already correct on this very prompt. The payoff would be giving s.isAMP something to do: it is assigned once from /\bamp\b/i on the lead source and read in exactly one place, the s.isStandard negation. It routes nothing and adds no directive -- confirmed dead, as flagged earlier. (4) INDEPENDENCE FROM THE VOI-FINDER WORK, since it was asked directly: total, and the probe is built so that it cannot be otherwise. It writes nothing to the scraper's return object, assigns no vehicle/VOI/ownedVehicle field, and shares no selector or code path with the VOI finder. Verified by assertion, not by reading. On the substance, the 8/17 log names the bleed itself, which is worth recording here because it is unusually clean evidence: [LP VOI DIAG] frame 904 reports the ACTIVE lead's own panel as empty (parsed:"" rawLen:0 panelText:""), then [LP VOI DIAG][finder] reports its fallback hit as {"parent":"tr.ant-table-row.LeadSelector_LeadId_1434107959","path":"td","text":"2023 Toyota Corolla"} -- a table cell belonging to a DIFFERENT lead. The lead-selector grid in that dump holds four rows: the active 2068821407 (Amp - Buying Signals) with an EMPTY vehicle cell, 1474156609 (Lost, 9/12/23, Toyota Sequoia), 1434107959 (SOLD, 6/22/23, 2023 Toyota Corolla) and 158542771 (Lost, 10/26/14, 2015 Toyota RAV4). The finder walked into the second-newest row. That also explains the SOLD scenario on a lead with no vehicle: [LP SOLD SCAN DIAG] reports authoritativeMarker:false with proseMatchInTranscript:true and scanScope:full-context (fences not found), so the prose scan matched the word SOLD from the same grid. One bleed, two symptoms. Independently, ownedVehicle correctly derived "2023 Toyota Corolla" from the 2024 service text -- the right value by the right route, which is why the prompt states the Corolla as both the VOI and the current vehicle at once. NONE of that is touched by this build. VERIFIED 23/23 against the probe sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case, driven through a DOM shim: the real 8/17 shape reports blurbById:false byClass:false bannerTextInFrame:false keyInfoNodes:0 while still emitting a line (absence on a dashboard frame is the finding); the GTM analytics string does not inflate the node count; the rendered shape from the screenshot reports both routes true, lists the sibling keyInfo ids so a rename stays diagnosable, and captures the banner text verbatim; a partially rendered panel is distinguishable from total absence; text-present-but-element-absent is distinguishable, which is the shadow-DOM tell; and a bare shell or lead frame emits nothing. Inertness is asserted rather than described: inside inlineScraper, zero module-scope references, _lpD not console.log, zero vehicle-field assignments, zero writes to the result object, whole thing inside one try/catch. NOTE ON THE TEST FIXTURE: the first version put amp-summary on the div and the class probe correctly reported false -- the real markup nests it on a CHILD span. The fixture was wrong and was corrected; the probe was right. All twelve extension suites green (235 assertions), node --check clean both builds, both manifests parse. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK: nothing. No prompt text changes, no directive is added or removed, no flag is wired, and the probe returns nothing any consumer reads. The only observable difference is one additional diagnostic line per dashboard-bearing frame in the console. Builds on v9.7.549-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.549-dev (DEV. TWO FEEDBACK-CAPTURE DEFECTS, both found while investigating Konye Brown (Community Honda Lafayette, dealerId 24399, lead 2068417329, 8/15). VinSolutions shows a real outbound SMS at 8:31 AM carrying the canonical stacked signature enforceSmsSig appends -- so it was demonstrably a copied LP draft -- while the feedback rows for that morning show no copy anywhere. (A) EVERY PANEL LOAD EMITTED A PHANTOM 'incomplete' ROW. _lpFeedbackFlush opens with `if (!_lpFeedback.generationId) return;   // nothing generated yet (module-init call)` -- the guard is right, and it was being defeated by the very call it names. The module-init `_lpFeedbackReset()` minted a real generationId, so on the agent's FIRST Generate the flush saw a populated id, an empty meta, hasCopied false and no explicit rating, and POSTed a row for a session that never existed. PROVEN IN log111, not inferred: the build loads at line 9, the first '[Lead Pro] Generating' is line 213, and between them sits '[LP FEEDBACK] incomplete | no_interaction | trigger: superseded | regens: 0 | chips: none' with no prior generation anywhere in the file to account for it. Those rows carry no meta, so they land in the daily report as a phantom 'unknown' store at 0% shipped. THIS IS ALSO, AT MINIMUM, PART OF WHAT v9.7.541 MEASURED AND MISATTRIBUTED: that build saw 34 abandoned rows on 8/11 with 28 carrying no meta, read them as failed or aborted generates, and reclassified them as 'incomplete' -- correct handling of the symptom, but the cause was here and was never removed, which is why the ~15%-of-generations 'incomplete' rate stayed unexplained across three builds. _lpFeedbackReset now takes an init flag and leaves the id null on the module-init call, which is exactly what the guard has always assumed. Nothing reads generationId before a generate: _lpFeedbackSend returns early without one, the thumbs are hidden until output exists, and the copy handler bails on an empty field. (B) A COPY THE AGENT MAKES BY HAND LEFT NO SIGNAL AT ALL. The ONLY thing that could set hasCopied was the Copy button. An agent who selected the draft in the output box and pressed Ctrl+C -- or right-clicked and chose Copy -- sent a real message to a real customer and recorded nothing. That is worse than a missing counter, because both negative legs of _lpFeedbackDeriveRating are guarded on !hasCopied, so the same session then flushed as 'down' (regenerated or chipped, never copied) on the next generate: A USED DRAFT RECORDED AS A REJECTED ONE. Konye's lead is that shape. A native 'copy' listener is now bound to the three output fields; it sets the same three flags the button sets and sends copy_<pane>_manual. The button path is untouched and cannot double-fire it -- a programmatic navigator.clipboard.write raises no copy event, and the email fallback's execCommand('copy') runs against a detached div, not these fields. Deduped per pane per generation so repeated Ctrl+C on one field is one signal rather than a burst. WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. The Copy BUTTON path was audited and is correct: it sets the flags and calls _lpFeedbackSend inline, so there is no race with a later regen and no dependence on the flush -- the reported hypothesis that a copy attaches to a generationId already marked 'superseded' does not hold, because the send happens synchronously in the click handler against the live session. Which of the two paths Patricia used on this lead cannot be determined from the artifacts; (B) is the only mechanism that produces a genuinely sent draft with no copy row, so it is the candidate, but it is not proven for this specific send. READING NOTE FOR THE REPORT, worth stating because it changed the diagnosis: the Rejected Sessions table lists ONLY rows with rating 'down'. A successful copy is 'up' / 'weak_up' / 'neutral' and appears in the tiles and the Signal Type table, never in that table. So four Rejected-Sessions rows on a lead is not evidence that no copy was recorded for it -- the report has no per-lead view of successful sends at all. Reporter v1.12 adds the lead ID to rejected rows only. VERIFIED 20/20 against the state machine sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case, driven as the real call sites drive it (reset at the top of generate, regenCount incremented after, meta captured on success). Konye's exact reported sequence -- load, generate, regen, regen, regen, copy -- now yields three discarded-draft rows plus one neutral/implicit_regen_copy row and NO phantom, ending in copy_sms for the button and copy_sms_manual for a hand copy; a copied session is not re-reported as abandoned on panel close or on the next generate; a clean first-try hand copy reads up/implicit_copy and a chip-then-hand-copy reads weak_up/implicit_chip_copy; three copies of one pane are one signal, two panes are two, a new generation re-arms the dedupe, and a copy before any generation is ignored. Pre-existing behaviour held: a genuinely failed generate still reports 'incomplete', regen-then-abandon still reports 'down' on the regenerated session, chip-then-abandon still reports 'down', and an explicit thumb still wins over everything. All eleven extension suites green (212 assertions), node --check clean both builds, both manifests parse. ONE EXPECTATION CORRECTED IN TEST, NOT IN CODE: the regen counter belongs to the session a regen CREATES, not the one it replaces (the call site is `_lpFeedbackReset(); if (_isRegenSession) _lpFeedback.regenCount++;`), so the opening draft of a regen chain flushes as 'abandoned' rather than 'down'. That is correct and pre-existing; the fixtures were wrong and were fixed. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: copy volume will RISE from today, because hand copies were previously invisible. Shipped and first-try rates should rise with it and the 'incomplete' count should fall by roughly one per panel session -- so day-over-day comparisons across this deploy are not like-for-like, and a jump is the fix working rather than a change in agent behaviour. STILL OPEN, unchanged: [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] reports 'transcript fences NOT found' on live traffic (log111 line 227), so the v9.7.544/545 fence bounding is not engaging and the scaffold-leak truncation is still firing -- 1,468 chars on this lead. That needs a live capture and its own build. Builds on v9.7.548-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.548-dev (DEV. A MANUFACTURER CODE WAS READ AS A CUSTOMER'S HOME STATE. LIVE INCIDENT: Shaniya Kamakeawong (Community Honda Baytown, dealerId 6191, lead 2067899945, 8/13). She has NO address on file at all -- PageData Buyer.PostalCode is null and Buyer.State and Buyer.City are both empty strings -- so both v9.7.499/546 PageData-primary paths correctly declined and the page-text fallback ran. The first and only "two letters + five digits" string anywhere on her page is the VEHICLE's own line, "Mfr code: CK15543" -- a real GM chassis code for the 2018 Silverado. The fallback matched it, "CK" passed the /^[A-Z]{2}$/ test, and _geoOutOfState fired: a customer with no known location was told "Since you're out of state." THE v9.7.547 DIAGNOSTICS FOUND THIS IN ONE READ, which is what they were built for -- [LP ADDRESS DIAG] perFrame [{lead:2067899945, cust:1440262683, st:"CK", zip:"15543"}] followed by [LP DISTANCE DIAG] prompt customerZip:15543 | customerState:CK | _geoOutOfState:true | distanceBlockRendered:true. No reverse-engineering from the delivered prompt was needed. NOTE THE MERGE BEHAVED CORRECTLY: that frame's customerId matched the active customer, so the v9.7.547 identity gate accepted it, exactly as designed. The value was already garbage at the point of EXTRACTION, which is where this build fixes it -- the previous build closed the wrong-customer path, this one closes the wrong-kind-of-string path. TWO FIXES. (1) VALIDATE AGAINST THE ACTUAL 50 STATES + DC, not the shape. /^[A-Z]{2}$/ is a FORMAT check and every consumer of customerState was using it as though it were a validity check. New module-scope US_STATES set and _lpIsUSState(), now the gate at all seven popup-side sites: _geoOutOfState in buildUserPrompt (the one that rendered the block), _isOutOfState in classifyScenario (which feeds mayStateDistance), _dsOutOfState in populateFromData's unconditional distance rule, both merge address bundles, and both halves of the cross-frame state rescue. This alone makes Mfr codes, stock numbers, trim strings and anything else shaped like a state inert as geography, wherever it enters from. (2) EXTRACT THE ADDRESS SHAPE, NOT ANY TWO LETTERS NEAR FIVE DIGITS. The scraper now tries the real rendered form FIRST -- city, comma, state, ZIP ("Crowley, LA 70526" and "Crowley, LA, 70526", both of which VinSolutions emits) -- and only falls back to the loose pattern when that finds nothing. Both paths require a genuine US state. The loose fallback additionally walks ALL matches rather than taking the first, and skips any hit introduced by a vehicle-panel label (mfr code / stock # / vin / style / model code / odom / engine / trim), so a unit identifier cannot stand in for an address even on a page that has no address. A state that fails validation is dropped rather than passed downstream: a ZIP with no state is still a usable geo fact because the local-ZIP set is keyed on the ZIP alone, but a state that is not a state is worse than none, since _geoOutOfState reads it as a mismatch. SCOPE DISCIPLINE, EXPLICITLY: the extraction lives INSIDE inlineScraper, which chrome.scripting.executeScript serializes into the VinSolutions frame -- only that function's own body travels, so the module-scope _lpIsUSState does NOT exist there and calling it would throw a ReferenceError that aborts the whole scrape. That is the v9.7.455 incident verbatim, and the v9.7.228 comment ~250 lines above the edit says so. The scraper therefore declares its own local validator in full. Verified by the file's own marker-based check against '} // end inlineScraper' (dev lines 4321-9035, commercial 4092-8718): ZERO executable module-scope references inside the injected function, the only textual hits being this changelog's own description. VERIFIED 29/29 in a new suite plus 52/52 in the existing distance suite, all sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case. THE REGRESSION IS RUN AGAINST THE REAL ARTIFACT, not a reconstruction: the 8/13 dump's actual body innerText (2,676 chars, confirmed to contain the Mfr code line) is fed to the shipped extraction and yields state:"" zip:"" in both builds, where the v9.7.547 regex over the identical text yields state:"CK" zip:"15543". Downstream, _geoOutOfState is false and NO distance signal renders -- matching the confirmed-correct no-address leads in the same log (customerZip:(none) | customerState:(none) | s.isDistanceBuyer(final):false). Real addresses still extract in every shape VinSolutions renders, including ZIP+4, two-word cities and cities with a period or hyphen; the real address is taken even though the Mfr code appears EARLIER in the page; PageData still outranks page text when populated; a bogus PD state is refused; a bare "LA 70526" with no city still works; and a vehicle label cannot introduce a match. All 51 abbreviations accepted, lowercase normalized, "CK"/"XX"/three-letter/empty/undefined rejected. All ten extension suites green (192 assertions), node --check clean both builds, both manifests parse. HARNESS NOTE WORTH KEEPING: adding the validator above the ZIP sets broke eight distance-suite assertions, and four of them failed as a silent `false` rather than a throw -- because the distance decision wraps its geo block in try/catch, so a missing helper reads as "not out of state" instead of an error. The suite anchor was corrected; the lesson is that this particular block cannot report its own breakage. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a lead whose address renders in a form the strict pattern misses AND whose state the loose fallback then rejects now yields no state at all, rather than a wrong one. It keeps its ZIP if one is present, so the local-ZIP set still governs; it simply cannot be called out-of-state. Given the alternative was telling a customer with no address on file that they live in another state, that is the correct trade. PR and other territories are deliberately NOT in the set -- no rooftop serves them, and admitting them would only widen the surface. STILL OPEN, unchanged: [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] reports 'transcript fences NOT found' on live traffic, so the v9.7.544/545 fence bounding is not engaging and the scaffold-leak truncation is still firing; that needs a live capture and its own build. Builds on v9.7.547-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.547-dev (DEV. THE CUSTOMER'S HOME ADDRESS WAS THE ONE IDENTITY FACT THE FRAME MERGE NEVER GUARDED -- and it is the entire input to the distance decision. This supersedes the v9.7.546/544 diagnosis. That build attributed the two 8/13 incidents (Billy Broussard, Audi Lafayette 21135, Crowley LA 70526; Kevin Cordes, Community Honda Baytown 6191, Baytown TX 77521) to a 'distance' entry in activeFlags being honoured over a confirmed-local ZIP. Gil then confirmed the Distance chip was NOT clicked on either lead, which collapses that explanation: the scraper never assigns d.isDistanceBuyer anywhere in popup.js (only content.js does, and content.js results are not consumed -- the v9.7.46 note), so the auto-detect `toggleFlag('distance', true)` at the top of populateFromData is DEAD CODE, and with no chip click there is no way for 'distance' to enter activeFlags at all. That leaves exactly one path to the rendered block: `_geoOutOfState || _inStateFar`, both computed from data.customerState and data.customerZip. Two customers who are each demonstrably LOCAL to their own rooftop came out non-local on the same day, so the address itself is what arrived wrong. ROOT CAUSE, FOUND IN THE MERGE: `customerState` and `customerZip` appear in NONE of the three PASS-2 gate lists. _isIdentityField covers dealerId, store, name, agent, salesRep, manager, customerId, phone, email, vehicle, vehicleRaw, condition, stockNum, vin, priorSoldVehicle. _isConversationField covers the transcript and reply/exit state. _isLeadStateField covers trade, inventory warning, lead age and friends. The buyer's STATE and ZIP are in none of them, so they fell straight through to the generic `else if (!m[k] && d[k]) m[k]=d[k]` with no customer check whatsoever. The phone is guarded. The email is guarded -- it even got its own atomic bundle in v9.7.507. The home address, which is the sole determinant of whether we tell someone they live too far to visit, was ungated. ANY frame in the tab could supply it: a stale customer-level frame left from prior navigation, or one of the session-chrome frames that ride along on every grab (both 8/13 grabs carried two, logged as "VinSolutions Connect [SWBMOTOPR-...]"), whose document.body.innerText is the whole app shell and can contain a worklist or dashboard grid listing OTHER customers' "City, ST 00000". The scraper's extraction is a bare /\b([A-Z]{2})\s*,?\s*(\d{5})\b/ over the first 12,000 characters of that innerText, so such a frame returns a confident, well-formed address for the wrong person. One bled STATE is enough on its own: _geoOutOfState fires on a state mismatch and renders "REMOTE / OUT-OF-STATE BUYER" without ever consulting the ZIP. THE FIX, IN THREE PLACES. (1) MERGE, PASS 2: the address is now gated exactly like _isLeadStateField -- when PASS 1 authoritatively scraped the active lead, an unverified frame supplies NO address; when PASS 1 found no active frame (AI Buying Signal leads) merging still happens, and the v9.7.228 cross-frame rescue is untouched for the genuine address-renders-in-the-dashboard-frame case it was built for. (2) MERGE, BOTH PASSES: the address is now an ATOMIC BUNDLE -- whichever frame supplies the state supplies the zip -- so a state from one frame can no longer pair with a zip from another. Same shape as the v9.7.507 email bundle and the v9.7.517 equity bundle; a zip-only frame may still fill a still-empty zip, so nothing the old rule kept is lost. (3) ARBITRATION: the ZIP now OUTRANKS a state that contradicts it. Each rooftop's local set is single-state by construction (Baytown = TX ZIPs, Lafayette = LA ZIPs), so a ZIP sitting IN the store's own local set while the state reads out-of-state is not a close call -- both cannot be true and one is bled. The ZIP wins: it is PageData-primary since v9.7.499, it is the dealership's own hand-supplied ground truth, and it is rooftop-specific, while the state is the value most easily filled by a stray page-text match. The local-set lookup no longer short-circuits on _geoOutOfState (which is what prevented the ZIP from ever disagreeing), and mayStateDistance in classifyScenario carries the same veto so a bled state can no longer buy permission to name the customer's mileage back to them -- the exact behaviour v9.7.401 exists to prevent. WHAT v9.7.546/544 GOT RIGHT AND KEEPS: the local-ZIP veto in buildUserPrompt (correct backstop, and it now also catches the manual-chip case if an agent ever does click it on a local customer), the isRemoteBuyer haystack moving off context.substring(0, 1000) to _lpCustomerText (independent and real -- LP's own 10:27 AM voicemail 'Since you're not local' was re-entering as evidence four minutes later), the PageData Buyer.State graduation, and the state rescue no longer overwriting a good ZIP with ''. Note that the ZIP veto ALONE would not have saved either lead: _geoOutOfState short-circuited the local-set lookup, so a bled state beat it. That is why (3) exists. NEW [LP ADDRESS DIAG]: every frame's claimed customerState/customerZip with its leadId and customerId, the count of distinct addresses across frames, the active customerId, and what the merge kept -- plus a console.warn naming the disagreement whenever frames differ. The bleed was invisible before because the merge erases the evidence; this is the line that would have answered this in one read. [LP DISTANCE DIAG] from v9.7.546 gains a zipVetoedState field. VERIFIED 52/52 against blocks sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case. The merge branch is exercised frame-by-frame in `sorted` order against a partially-built m, as the real merge does: a chrome/stale frame with no customerId is refused once PASS 1 scraped the lead; the verified customer-dashboard frame is still accepted (the v9.7.228 case); a different customer's well-formed address is refused; stale-first-then-real still yields the real one; state and zip always originate together; a zip-only frame still fills an empty zip. The arbitration is verified narrow: TX-on-70526-at-21135 and LA-on-77521-at-6191 both stop reading out-of-state, while a genuine LA customer at Baytown (70506, not in the Baytown set) still reads out-of-state, still renders distance, and claims no veto. All nine extension suites green (163 assertions), node --check clean both builds, both manifests parse. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a lead whose address genuinely renders ONLY in an unverified frame -- no customerId on that frame, and PASS 1 did scrape the active lead -- now loses the address entirely rather than adopting it unchecked. It then has no ZIP, the local set cannot be consulted, and the lead falls back to the pre-ZIP behaviour instead of being told something false about where it lives. The [LP ADDRESS DIAG] line will show if that case is real on live traffic. NOT ADDRESSED, flagged and real: the dead `toggleFlag('distance', true)` auto-detect is left in place -- removing it is correct but it is the only wiring point if d.isDistanceBuyer is ever populated popup-side, and deleting it in the same build that re-diagnoses this flag would remove the evidence. STILL OPEN from v9.7.546: [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] reports 'transcript fences NOT found' on nearly every 8/13 lead, so the v9.7.544/545 fence bounding is not engaging on live traffic and the scaffold-leak truncation is still firing; that needs a live capture and its own build. Builds on v9.7.546-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.546-dev (DEV. LOCAL CUSTOMERS WERE BEING TOLD THEY ARE REMOTE, because the authoritative local-ZIP veto never reached the function that writes the prompt. LIVE INCIDENT: Billy Broussard (Audi Lafayette, dealerId 21135, lead 2067692473, 8/13). PageData Buyer.PostalCode is "70526" -- Crowley, LA -- and '70526' is listed in LOCAL_ZIPS_LAFAYETTE. The ZIP set is correct, the ZIP scraped correctly, and classifyScenario read it correctly: s.isDistanceBuyer has carried `(!_isLocalByZip || _explicitDistanceReq)` as a hard conjunct since v9.7.348, and the manual-chip latch below it carries the same veto. The delivered prompt still said "REMOTE / OUT-OF-STATE BUYER: Customer is NOT local" and the SMS opened "I know you're not local". TWO DEFECTS IN SERIES, both measured against the shipped bytes. (1) THE VETO WAS IN THE WRONG FUNCTION. classifyScenario decides s.isDistanceBuyer; buildUserPrompt decides what the model actually READS, and it never consulted DEALER_LOCAL_ZIPS at all -- it computed _localSetHit purely to decide the in-state-far TIER, then rendered the whole distance block off `flags.includes('distance')`, where a 'distance' entry in activeFlags was an unconditional override. So the one place the ZIP could stop the sentence from being written was the one place it was not consulted. PROVEN, NOT INFERRED: running the shipped decision block over Billy's exact values returns no distance flag from geo (_isLocalByZip true, _geoOutOfState false, _inStateFar false), which means the flag came from activeFlags and was honoured verbatim. The veto now runs in buildUserPrompt too: a ZIP confirmed in the store's own local set strips 'distance' from the rendered flags, and _manualDistance carries the same veto so the REMOTE branch cannot be forced behind it. (2) LP READ ITS OWN VOICEMAIL BACK AS PROOF THE CUSTOMER IS REMOTE. isRemoteBuyer tested /not (?:local|in the (?:local )?area|nearby)|out of state|.../ against `lastInboundMsg + context.substring(0, 1000)` -- and the first thousand characters of the arc are the AGENT CONTEXT header and our own notes. Lead Pro generated the 10:27 AM voicemail "Since you're not local, would you prefer a video walkaround", VinSolutions logged it as a call note, and four minutes later the next generation read that sentence as evidence. Once the phrase is in the CRM the lead is latched REMOTE forever, on any rooftop, whatever the ZIP says. The haystack is now _lpCustomerText(data) -- the customer's own inbound plus [CUSTOMER]-attributed transcript lines. This is the v9.7.350 decision applied to the second detector that needed it (that build scoped _lpExplicitDistanceReq to customer inbound for the identical Robby Li reason and left this one blended), and the same agent-text-pollution class as v9.7.411 / v9.7.539. TWO UPSTREAM HARDENINGS, both on the path the ZIP travels, neither observed failing on this lead but both able to produce it: (3) PageData Buyer.State GRADUATED TO PRIMARY alongside Buyer.PostalCode. The ZIP has been PD-primary since v9.7.499 while the STATE was still a bare /\b([A-Z]{2})\s*,?\s*(\d{5})\b/ match over the first 12,000 chars of body innerText -- the same fragile shape v9.7.499 replaced for the ZIP, and the half that feeds _geoOutOfState, which alone can render REMOTE. It also gates whether the cross-frame STATE RESCUE runs, so a missed state could drag a wrong ZIP in behind it. Same object, same reliability, confirmed populated ("LA") on the Broussard capture. (4) THE STATE RESCUE COULD DELETE A GOOD ZIP. `m.customerZip = _zipHitVerified` was unconditional, so a frame that supplied a STATE but no ZIP overwrote a correct PageData ZIP with '' -- and an empty ZIP means the local set can never be consulted, which is exactly how the distance decision falls through to weaker signals. It now adopts a ZIP only when the rescuing frame has one, and a new independent CROSS-FRAME ZIP RESCUE fills an empty ZIP even when the state was already present (the rescue above only ran when the STATE was missing). Same verified-first / unverified-only-when-identity-unknown rule as the state rescue it sits beside. (5) NEW [LP DISTANCE DIAG], per Gil: two lines per generation -- one in classifyScenario at the point s.isDistanceBuyer is final, one in buildUserPrompt at the point the block renders -- carrying customerZip, customerState, dealerId, _isLocalByZip, data.isDistanceBuyer (raw), _explicitDistanceReq, the manual-chip state, the veto, _geoOutOfState/_inStateFar and the rendered outcome. This flag has five prior builds against it (v9.7.348 cross-lead bleed, v9.7.401 don't-state-miles, v9.7.405 unconditional rule, v9.7.499 PD zip, this one); the next incident should be read off the log, not reverse-engineered from source. SECOND LIVE INCIDENT, SAME DAY, CONFIRMED SAME CLASS: Kevin Cordes (Community Honda Baytown, dealerId 6191, lead 2067662277, Cars.com Finance Intent, system-marked Duplicate). Buyer panel reads "Baytown, TX 77521" -- the store's own town, and '77521' is in LOCAL_ZIPS_BAYTOWN -- while his CELL is (337) 400-5105, a Lafayette LOUISIANA area code. The drafts read "Since you're out of state" and "Since you're not local". Nothing in the geo path can produce that (TX customer, TX rooftop, ZIP in the local set), so on his lead too the flag arrived via activeFlags and was honoured verbatim. There is no area-code geography anywhere in the file; the LA phone is a red herring that only misleads a human reading the panel. VERIFIED 34/34 against blocks sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: Billy's and Kevin's exact values each yield _isLocalByZip true / s.isDistanceBuyer false / no block rendered even with the chip on, and Billy's own voicemail no longer trips isRemoteBuyer; a customer who says "can you ship the vehicle to me" still overrides their local ZIP and still routes REMOTE; an LA customer at a Baytown rooftop still renders out-of-state; Alexandria 71301 still renders in-state-far and still routes to the DRIVE-IN branch (the v9.7.405 Michelle Lacour case); the chip still forces REMOTE on a non-local ZIP; a lead with no ZIP on file behaves exactly as before; and a [CUSTOMER] line deep in the transcript still trips remote while an [AGENT] line saying the same words does not. All nine extension suites green (145 assertions), node --check clean both builds, both manifests parse. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: an agent who chips Distance Buyer on a customer whose ZIP is in the store's local set no longer gets the remote treatment -- the chip is now advisory against ground truth rather than above it. If that customer is genuinely buying remotely they will have said so, and the explicit-request path still honours it; if they have not said so, the ZIP is the better evidence and this is the intended behaviour. Leads whose ONLY remote evidence is an agent note describing the customer in the third person lose the REMOTE branch -- that is the v9.7.350 decision, applied consistently. NOT ADDRESSED, flagged and real: LOGGED THROUGHOUT 8/13, on nearly every lead -- [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] transcript fences NOT found -> falling back to the full context, and the matching [LP SOLD SCAN DIAG] scanScope:full-context. The v9.7.544/545 fence-based bounding is not engaging on live traffic; the fallback is safe but it is the OLD behaviour, so the scaffold-leak truncation is still firing (1,182 chars on Billy). The fence string the builder emits and the one the reader looks for need to be reconciled against a live capture; that is its own build. Builds on v9.7.545-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.545-dev (DEV. SOLD SCENARIO FIRED ON A LEAD WITH NO VEHICLE, because the greedy sold scan matched LEAD PRO'S OWN HYPOTHETICAL. LIVE INCIDENT: Sydnee Fuselier (Community Honda Lafayette, lead 2064315759, 8/12, down-voted twice). Her lead carries NO vehicle -- "Vehicle: (none specified)" and "CRM CONFIRMS: no vehicle of interest is on file for this lead (authoritative record, not a display failure)" -- yet the prompt opened with "TASK: The specific vehicle of interest has been sold". The delivered prompt then held two UNSATISFIABLE instructions at once: the sold branch's "offer a real specific alternative, not a generic 'we have options'" against the no-VOI branch's "Do NOT reference or name any vehicle from the conversation history". The draft that shipped -- "if you decide to shop for another pre-owned vehicle, send me the year and model" -- is the ONLY output satisfying both, which is exactly why it reads hedged. The model was not ignoring the directives; it was obeying an impossible pair. ROOT CAUSE, found by running the shipped regex over the delivered prompt rather than by inspection: exactly ONE match in 45,174 characters, and it is inside a Lead Pro directive -- "If the newest thing we told them was that the vehicle sold, you may not now write as though it is available". Our own conditional, in our own scaffold, read back as an assertion of fact. Fifth instance of the self-pollution class after v9.7.538 (trade guard directive), v9.7.539 (agent copy), v9.7.543 and v9.7.544 (scaffold in the arc). NOTE ON THE REPORTED DIAGNOSIS: this was reported as two contradictory SITUATION blocks stacking -- sold + stalled -- on the v9.7.537 independent-heuristics model. That reading does not hold. Senthil Subramaniam (Community Toyota Baytown, same day, genuinely sold RAV4 XSE) carries the IDENTICAL sold + stalled pair and was not flagged, and Angie Coronado carries a different SITUATION plus stalled and was not flagged either. All four prompts reviewed carry TWO 📋 SITUATION lines, but they are the same text twice -- the known scenarioRules double-render flagged in v9.7.462, duplication rather than contradiction. What is unique to Sydnee is that her SOLD is FALSE, and the same regex over Senthil's prompt returns ZERO matches: his SOLD comes from the authoritative 'vehicle status: sold' marker, the path that works. The contradiction is sold-vs-no-vehicle, not sold-vs-stalled. TWO GUARDS. (1) INVARIANT: a lead with no vehicle cannot have a sold vehicle. This alone resolves Sydnee and is unarguable -- s.noSpecificVehicle was already being computed two lines below and never consulted here. (2) SCOPE: the greedy prose regex now reads only the conversation transcript, bounded by the same fences as the v9.7.544 arc work, so it can never see the scaffold again. The authoritative marker check deliberately KEEPS scanning the full context -- it is Lead Pro's own intentional signal rather than prose, and removing it would break Senthil. New always-on [LP SOLD SCAN DIAG] reports vehicleSold, the vehicle on the lead, whether the authoritative marker fired, and whether the prose matched in the transcript versus the full context -- so any future divergence between those last two names itself instead of hiding. VERIFIED 14/14 against the block sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm: Sydnee's exact shape is false, and stays false even with a vehicle present; Senthil's marker path still returns true, as do an agent telling the customer in the transcript and a plain "no longer available"; the invariant holds three ways including a whitespace-only vehicle; all five pre-existing suppressors (in-transit v9.7.519, loyalty, lease-mature-early, audi-all-available, live-inventory v9.7.509) are unchanged; and the no-fences fallback still scans the full context. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a genuinely sold vehicle whose only evidence is prose sitting OUTSIDE the transcript fences -- in an agent note block above them -- would no longer be detected. The new diagnostic's proseMatchInTranscript vs proseMatchInFullCtx pair makes exactly that case visible in a live log. NOT ADDRESSED, flagged and real: scenarioRules still renders twice per prompt (v9.7.462), and the sold and stalled directive blocks genuinely can co-occur -- benign where both are true, as on Senthil, but no arbitration exists between them. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.544-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.544-dev (DEV. TWO ROOT-CAUSE FIXES ON THE MACI ALVARADO LEAD, both closing classes that prior builds only cleaned up after. (A) SPLIT-FRAME VEHICLE ADOPTION PUT A FOREIGN CAR IN FRONT OF A CUSTOMER. Maci Alvarado (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2019154122, 8/12): her Vehicle Info panel carries a stock number and nothing else -- "(New)", "Style: -- None --", no year/make/model -- and VOI-4 independently reported "ABSTAINED (selected row carries no vehicle -- active lead is VOI-less)". The main-merge split-frame block then adopted "2017 KIA SOUL" from a lead:null frame and the prompt told the model to sell it: "VEHICLE ON LEAD: 2017 KIA SOUL -- confirmed in stock ... we have it here". That string appears ZERO times in her CRM dump. Gil identified why a foreign record was resident: an 08/06 "Customer Merged -- 2 customers auto-merged" note, whose merged-in side brought three years of unrelated history including an xTimeLegacy SERVICE record from 3/10/23 -- a record this same grab rejected as a lead source ("Frame rejected -- service-record source") while its vehicle still reached the merge. WHY THE BLOCK COULD NOT TELL: its entry condition (!m.vehicle) fires identically on two different situations -- (a) the panel frame for THIS lead genuinely lacks an autoLeadId, the case v9.7.383 was built for, and (b) this lead is simply VOI-less while an unrelated panel is co-resident. FIX: the discriminator was already being collected. A frame with no panel element reports _voiDiag.panelText "(no panel element)" and rawLen 0 -- nothing was read. A frame whose panel RENDERED but parsed no vehicle reports real panelText with rawLen > 0 -- Maci's read panelText:"(New)" rawLen:5 -- which is positive evidence the lead HAS no vehicle. Adoption is refused there and the lead stays generic, so the existing no-VOI directive ("do NOT invent one, ask what they are looking for") governs -- confirmed by Gil as the correct output for this lead. (B) THE ARC IS NOW BOUNDED AT THE SOURCE. conversationBrief is built as stateLabel + Total CRM entries + keySignal + commitmentBlock + concernBlock + 'CONVERSATION TRANSCRIPT (...):\n---\n' + recentHistory + '\n---\n', with the AGENT CONTEXT note block PREPENDED back afterwards -- so the string runs dated agent-context notes, then a block of Lead Pro scaffold, then dated transcript entries. ctxEntries splits on a lookahead for the next dated header, so the LAST agent-context note absorbs the entire scaffold block between the two sections. That is the actual mechanism behind every scaffold leak recorded: v9.7.529 (Daniel Boyd, FOLLOW-UP directive) and v9.7.543 (Maci Alvarado and Kahari Oford, EXIT SIGNAL directive) both cut inside the entry AFTER the fact. The arc is now built from the region between the transcript fences, which contains dated entries and nothing else by construction, so no scaffold can be absorbed at all. Nothing is lost -- the transcript section already carries [NOTE] and [CALL NOTE] entries alongside [CUSTOMER] and [AGENT] -- and the agent-context copies at the top were DUPLICATES, so each note now enters the arc once instead of twice. Falls back to the full context when either fence is missing, so a future brief-format change degrades to today's behaviour rather than emptying the arc; the v9.7.543 per-entry cut is deliberately KEPT as defence in depth on that fallback path. New [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] reports how many context chars were excluded, or that the fences were not found. VERIFIED 17/17 against both blocks sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case. (A): Maci's exact frame shape refuses, as does a fuller stub panel; the v9.7.383 target case (no panel element at all) still adopts; a frame that rendered AND supplied its own vehicle is untouched; and the guard is inert when no frame carries the active lead id or when _voiDiag is absent, so it can never refuse on absence of evidence. (B): against the real brief shape, EXIT SIGNAL, AGENT CONTEXT, Total CRM entries and the appended VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS block are all outside the bounded arc while both real messages and the in-transcript CALL NOTE survive, and the duplicated JOSE AREVALO note now appears exactly once; all three fallback shapes return the context unchanged rather than emptying it. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: (A) a lead whose panel renders non-vehicle text while its real vehicle legitimately lives in an ID-less frame would now stay generic. No such shape appears in any capture reviewed, and staying generic is recoverable -- naming the wrong car is not. (B) if a note type ever exists in the AGENT CONTEXT block but NOT in the transcript, it would drop out of the arc. Checked against this capture and they are duplicates; the [LP ARC-BOUND DIAG] char counts make a divergence visible if one appears. ALSO CORRECTED FOR THE RECORD: an earlier read of this lead flagged LEAD AGE 89 days as a wrong signal -- it is correct, the panel reads Created 5/15/26 (89d), Source Repeat Customer; and the "confirmed in stock (Stock #t6144553)" directive is legitimate, gated on stockNum presence which the panel genuinely carries. Still open on this lead and NOT addressed: 🔥 LIVE CONVERSATION and THIS IS A CLOSE appear in the same prompt, and SPOUSE/PARTNER APPROVAL fires off a husband's APR question. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.543-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.543-dev (DEV. SCAFFOLD LEAK CLOSED ON THE PATH ITSELF -- and the diagnostic that was reporting success while the leak shipped. CONFIRMED ON A GENUINE EXIT, which is the case that was missing: Maci Alvarado (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2019154122, 8/12). Her exit is real and unambiguous -- the raw CRM dump carries "Text Message Reply Received: Not needed. Already purchased another vehicle" -- and LP's close was correct and gracious. But the arc handed the model this, quoted, labelled as note content: CALL NOTE: "By: JOSE AREVALO Left message EXIT SIGNAL: customer has declined or is no longer interested ... Do NOT pivot to alternatives, do NOT offer appointment times ..." -- a THREE-MONTH-OLD voicemail stub from 5/15 with Lead Pro's own close directive welded onto it. "EXIT SIGNAL" appears ZERO times in the dump. WHY IT SURVIVED v9.7.529's FIX, and this is the important part: _scaffoldRe is an ALLOWLIST of marker strings, and EXIT SIGNAL: was never on it. The detector DID fire and logged "truncated 965 chars" -- it cut at a marker FURTHER ALONG the same entry (Total CRM entries:) and left the unrecognised EXIT SIGNAL block standing. So the log reported a successful truncation on an entry that still carried a full directive. A truncation count is not proof an entry came out clean, and that false reassurance is why this sat unnoticed across three separate logs (868, 965, 1069, 1198, 1374 chars across five leads in two days). FIX, two parts: (1) the glyph-less headers missing by name -- EXIT SIGNAL:, PAUSE SIGNAL:, VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS:, VEHICLE ON LEAD:, PRESENCE LANGUAGE:, CRM ENGAGEMENT RATING:, VEHICLE PIVOT DETECTED:, INVENTORY (, RULES FOR THIS RESPONSE:; (2) a STRUCTURAL family rule -- any Lead Pro directive glyph followed by an uppercase header -- which is what stops this being a list that silently rots every time a directive is added, which is precisely the failure this bug IS. 🔥 is deliberately matched only in the literal "🔥 LIVE CONVERSATION" rather than joining the glyph class, because customers genuinely text 🔥 in caps. SECOND DEFECT, FOUND BY THE NEW SUITE AND NOT BY INSPECTION: the glyph rule was first written as a CHARACTER CLASS. Most of those glyphs are astral-plane, so in a non-`u` regex a class matches a single surrogate HALF -- the cut landed one code unit late and welded a stray lone surrogate onto the note text. ⚠ and ☎ are BMP and worked correctly, which is exactly what made the broken version look right. Rewritten as an alternation, where each branch matches a complete pair. VERIFIED 19/19 against _scaffoldRe sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: both live leaks (Maci's genuine exit and Kahari's false one) retain only their real note text; the v9.7.529 original case still cuts; seven directive blocks added since that list was written are now caught, including 🚚 DELIVERY REQUESTED from v9.7.538 which the old list could never have known about; entries that are ENTIRELY scaffold are still dropped rather than truncated to nothing; and the must-not-touch set all pass -- an ordinary voicemail stub, a substantive call note full of capitals and colons, a customer using the same words in lowercase, a customer texting 🔥 IN CAPS, a customer texting ⚠ then lowercase, and Maci's own exit message. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a genuine CRM note that opens a clause with one of these eleven glyphs followed by two or more capitals would be truncated there. No human note in any dump reviewed does this, and the cut can only ever remove text from the END of an entry, never alter what precedes it. NOT ADDRESSED, flagged: the underlying reason the last arc entry absorbs trailing text at all is that ctxEntries splits on a LOOKAHEAD for the next dated header, so the final entry is unbounded (v9.7.529's own analysis). Every fix so far, including this one, cleans up AFTER that rather than bounding the split. Bounding it properly means giving the arc builder the transcript rather than the resolved leadContext, which is an architecture change and wants its own build and its own before/after evidence. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.542-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.542-dev (DEV. WRONGFUL CLOSE ON AN ACTIVELY BUYING CUSTOMER -- a do-not-contact instruction about a THIRD PARTY read as the lead declining. LIVE INCIDENT: Kahari Oford (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2065528863, 8/11). He is buying a surprise birthday car for his pregnant girlfriend, and his newest inbound is the most engaged message on the lead: "She has a 2024 Kia Forte from your car lot her birthday is in October and we recently found out she's pregnant so she sent me a tic tok of an SUV one day 2026 Kia sportage black with burgundy interior." LP replied "Kahari, I understand you're stepping back from the birthday vehicle plans. Thank you for your time, and we'll be here if you ever need us in the future." ROOT CAUSE, verified against the shipped bytes and the raw CRM dump: _declineRe's final alternative, do[\s-]not[\s-](?:contact|call|text|reach), was the ONLY subject-less member of an OR-chain whose own header comment promises "High precision: customer-ATTRIBUTED decline only". Every sibling requires an explicit subject; this one matched the bare phrase anywhere in the newest 10 notes. What tripped it was a routine coordination note: "GIRLFRIEND'S INFO BUT DO NOT CONTACT SINCE ITS A SURPRISE -- Jasmine Thompson, Cell: (346) 710-8939, jthompson100499@gmail.com" -- an instruction not to contact the GIFT RECIPIENT so the surprise holds, saying nothing whatever about Kahari. hasExitSignal went true ([LP EXIT SIGNAL DIAG] hasExitSignal:true, leadAgeDays:1) and the whole close scaffold fired on a lead mid-purchase. FIX, two parts: (1) the alternative now requires attribution in the same shape as its siblings, accepting BOTH subject-before ("customer said do not contact") and subject-after ("do not contact him / the customer"); (2) a SURPRISE-GIFT SCRUB, because attribution alone is not enough -- "do not contact her" where "her" is the recipient would still match. Birthday/anniversary/gift leads routinely log the recipient's contact details next to a do-not-reach instruction, and the recipient is by definition not the buyer. Any clause that ALREADY carries a do-not-contact instruction AND names gift/surprise/secret framing or a relationship word (girlfriend, wife, husband, daughter, mother...) is neutralized before matching -- the v9.7.536 neutralize-and-test-the-remainder pattern, so a genuine decline sharing the same note still fires. Scoped so ordinary notes mentioning a birthday or a spouse are untouched. VERIFIED 16/16 against _negInt, _declineRe and the scrub sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: Kahari's verbatim note goes silent, as do four more third-party shapes (anniversary/spouse, birthday/daughter, don't-tell/mother, straight-apostrophe variant); the target case "customer said do not contact her, not interested" still fires, as do both attribution directions and a genuine bare DNC with no gift context; all five pre-existing attributed alternatives are bit-identical; the narrow not-interested-IN cash-buyer carve-out still holds; and a note carrying BOTH a gift clause and a real decline still fires on the decline. ALSO CONFIRMED ON THIS LEAD, NOT FIXED HERE: [LP SCAFFOLD-LEAK DIAG] fired (868 chars truncated) and the delivered prompt still shows the full EXIT SIGNAL directive concatenated INSIDE a quoted CALL NOTE in the CONVERSATION ARC. The phrase "EXIT SIGNAL" appears ZERO times in the raw CRM dump, so this is Lead Pro's own scaffold re-entering the arc as though an agent had written it -- the same self-pollution class as v9.7.538/539, and the detector truncating is not the same as the leak being closed. Fixing the false positive removes the trigger for THIS lead but not the mechanism, which will recur on any lead where an exit is legitimately detected. It needs its own build. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.541-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.541-dev (DEV. NO DRAFT PRODUCED IS NOT A REJECTION -- correcting the v9.7.540 flush on its first live day. MEASURED, from the 8/11 daily report: 181 generations, 34 flushed as 'abandoned', and 28 of those 34 carried NO meta at all, surfacing as a phantom "unknown" store at 0% shipped / 0% first-try. CAUSE: _lpFeedbackCaptureMeta runs ONLY after a generation succeeds -- its own call site says "Capture feedback metadata now that generation succeeded" -- so an empty meta is positive evidence that the session never rendered a draft: a failed or aborted generate, or a second Generate click landing while the first was still in flight. v9.7.540 reported those identically to a real abandonment, which overstates rejection and drags the used-rate denominator by ~15% (28 of 181). FIX: the flush now distinguishes them -- meta present means a draft existed and was not used ('abandoned'), meta empty means none was ever produced ('incomplete'). Both are still reported, because client-side visibility into failed generates is worth having on its own, but only 'abandoned' belongs in the quality math. The send's flush parameter carries the fallback rating as a string instead of a bare boolean; the copy and thumb paths are untouched and still pass nothing. PAIRED WORKER BUILDS, both required for the numbers to read correctly: reporter v1.9 and proxy v7.50 exclude 'incomplete' from every quality denominator, add produced = total - incomplete, recompute usedRate as shipped/produced (the question it was meant to answer), and surface the incomplete count on its own line. Reporter v1.9 also stops rendering 0% for a store bucket with zero engaged sessions -- the phantom "unknown" row read as total failure when the truth was "nothing to measure"; it now renders an em dash. VERIFIED: 11/11 feedback-flush unchanged; reporter 28/28 and proxy 25/25 including the real 8/11 shape as a fixture -- explicit 42/1 preserved, total still 181, produced 153, engaged 147, shipped rate unchanged at 98%, usedRate corrected from 80% to 94%, and the phantom store proven to have zero engaged sessions. A bucket-reconciliation assertion caught a real gap while writing this: 'incomplete' was added to _newBucket and the tally but not to _bucketRates' returned object, so it vanished from every decorated per-store bucket. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: 'incomplete' is a new rating value that older renderers will not recognise; both paired worker builds handle it, but any other consumer reading these KV rows needs to know it exists. The 28-session-per-day incomplete volume is itself an unexplained finding, NOT addressed here -- it is 15% of generations never rendering a draft, and whether that is genuine generate failure, double-clicks, or something else needs the [LP FEEDBACK] trigger values from a live day to separate. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.540-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.540-dev (DEV. THE FEEDBACK LOOP COULD NOT RECORD A FAILURE -- and every quality change was being judged against a corpus that structurally excluded them. _lpFeedbackSend had exactly TWO call sites, the Copy handler and the thumb handler, and both imply the agent engaged with the draft. But the two negative legs of _lpFeedbackDeriveRating are guarded on !hasCopied -- "if (!hasCopied && regenCount > 0) return 'down'" and the chip equivalent -- so reached only from the copy path they were UNREACHABLE, along with the 'implicit_regen_no_copy' and 'implicit_chip_no_copy' signal types. The single most informative event in the product, the agent REGENERATING or CHIPPING a draft and then throwing it away, was never recorded; _lpFeedbackReset discarded the evidence on the next generate. THIS IS THE MEASURED SYMPTOM ALREADY ON RECORD: the v9.7.489 header reports the pairs export capturing 8 rows out of 239 signals on 7/25, 1 downvote out of 6, and attributes the loss to the Worker -- "that one is Worker-side and still needs the current Worker source". The Worker source (v7.48) is now in hand and that attribution is WRONG: POST /feedback validates only id+rating, accepts any truthy rating, and writes to KV with a 90-day TTL. Nothing was lost in transit because the rows were never sent. The corpus was biased to positives by construction -- copies and deliberate thumb clicks -- which is exactly the shape a 1-in-6 downvote rate produces. FIX: _lpFeedbackFlush(reason) reports the outgoing session before it is overwritten, called at the TOP of _lpFeedbackReset (verified ordering -- reset runs at the start of a generate, BEFORE any new draft is rendered, so the output fields still hold the abandoned draft the row is about) and on pagehide for the last session of a panel lifetime, which no later generate can supersede. It is guarded three ways against double-reporting: no generationId (the module-init call), hasCopied (the copy handler already sent), and explicitRating (the thumb handler already sent). A flushed session with NO interaction at all derives null and would still be dropped, so it reports as its own weak 'abandoned' rating -- that is the DENOMINATOR, the generate-and-move-on case, without which a downvote rate is meaningless. Drafts are still attached only on regen/chip/down per v9.7.309, so the weak abandons stay metadata-only and the PII surface does not grow. The send fetch gains keepalive:true so a teardown flush survives unload. VERIFIED 11/11 against the rating, signal and flush functions sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: all four previously-unreachable rejection shapes now land with the correct rating and signal type; a copied session, a thumbed-up session and a thumbed-down session each report exactly ONCE when subsequently superseded; the three positive paths (copied as-is, chip-then-copy, regen-then-copy) are bit-identical to before; and the flush is inert at module init. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: feedback ROW VOLUME rises substantially, because most generates are now reported rather than only the ones an agent copied or rated. That is the point -- the denominator was missing -- but KV write volume and the daily export both grow, and the new 'abandoned' rating must be filtered OUT of any positive/negative ratio or it will read as a collapse in quality on the first day. It is a distinct rating value specifically so it can be separated. NOT ADDRESSED, flagged and real: _lpFeedbackReset runs on every regen and zeroes regenCount before the call site increments it, so regenCount is only ever 0 or 1 and never counts consecutive regens. With this flush in place the true count is recoverable as consecutive superseded rows on one lead, so the field is misleading rather than load-bearing; correcting it changes signal semantics and wants its own build. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.539-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.539-dev (DEV. TRADE TRIGGER READ THE AGENT'S OFFER AS THE CUSTOMER'S REQUEST -- the third and last hop of the self-pollution chain, found by a Codex audit against the v9.7.538 builds and reproduced here against the shipped block in both. TWO PROVEN SHAPES: (a) a customer whose only words were "Yes, is it still available?" plus an agent note reading "we can get a Kelley Blue Book value and a trade offer today" matched "Kelley Blue" and armed the trade-value directive; (b) an agent note reading "I want to have your trade-in numbers ready before you arrive" matched "trade-in number" -- and that sentence is the DISTANCE BUYER block's OWN suggested wording, so Lead Pro's template goes out as agent copy, gets logged into the CRM as a note, and is read back one grab later as evidence the customer asked for a trade number. v9.7.538 closed the same-generation loop (LP directives in data.context); this closes the CRM round-trip loop, which is the exact mechanism the TRADE CONFLATION GUARD's own header describes for the Talia case (7/20 -> 7/27 -> today). WHY THE EXISTING GUARDS DID NOT COVER IT: v9.7.411 gated tradeValue on "the customer must have spoken" but left the SCAN on the full arc -- a boolean precondition with an unscoped search, the distinction v9.7.538's header already flagged as still open; and its `kelley`-must-be-followed-by-`blue` narrowing stopped a SURNAME (Roslynn Kelley) and was never capable of stopping agent copy that genuinely says "Kelley Blue Book". Both remain untouched and both still do their jobs. FIX: a trade REQUEST is by definition something the CUSTOMER asked for, so agent copy offering one can never be evidence of it. The trigger now reads _lpCustomerText (inbound + [CUSTOMER]-tagged lines) instead of the full arc. The v9.7.362 full-arc capability is deliberately PRESERVED for its real purpose -- a trade ask an agent transcribed into a call note -- by admitting only note lines that explicitly ATTRIBUTE the ask to the customer ("he asked", "customer wants", or a verb-initial "wants ..." after the note tag), rather than the whole arc. Deliberately NOT admitted: "I asked the customer about a trade", which is agent-initiated. VERIFIED 10/10 against the block sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: both Codex shapes go silent, as do an agent voicemail offering an appraisal and the Woodfork "Has trade-in: No" blob; while a customer asking in their own inbound, a [CUSTOMER]-tagged arc line, an attributed call note, a terse verb-initial note, a customer naming KBB, and a real ask sharing an arc with agent copy all still fire. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a genuine trade ask transcribed into a call note with NO attribution cue at all ("trade value on the truck", bare) no longer fires. That is the deliberate trade -- a missed trade directive leaves the model the full arc to read, whereas a fabricated one drafts a trade pitch at someone who never mentioned a trade -- and the attribution set can be widened from live [LP DEAL-TRIGGER DIAG] lines, which log the matched text. ALSO FROM THE SAME AUDIT, NOT CHANGED HERE AND FLAGGED FOR A HUMAN CALL: auth.js validateLicense fails OPEN on network error, on 404/501, and when no Worker base is configured, returning devMode:true. The 404/501 leg is documented as deliberate (v9.7.462: do not lock agents out before the Worker ships the route), but the catch-all network leg is broader than that rationale and the decision needs the CURRENT Worker source to settle -- specifically whether /generate enforces the license server-side, which cannot be determined from either extension archive. Not a unilateral change: failing closed without server-side enforcement in place could lock out the entire fleet during an outage. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.538-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.538-dev (DEV. TRADE FABRICATED ON A LEAD WHOSE CUSTOMER SAID THEY HAVE NO TRADE -- and LP's own directive was the evidence. LIVE INCIDENT: Manzel Woodfork (Audi Lafayette, lead 2065076548, 8/10). The delivered SMS read "I can help get a solid range for your trade ... send me its year, make, model, and mileage" on a lead whose CarGurus deal-builder line says "Has trade-in: No" and whose CRM Trades panel is empty. TWO DEFECTS IN SERIES, both measured against the shipped bytes. (1) A NEGATIVE TRADE ASSERTION READ AS A TRADE DISCUSSION: _tvMentioned tests /\btrade[- ]?in\b/ against the arc, which matches the FIELD NAME in "Has trade-in: No" and in the panel's own "Trade-in Info (none entered)" while ignoring the VALUE. The customer positively declaring NO trade is the strongest possible evidence against a trade, and it was being read as evidence for one. That armed the TRADE CONFLATION GUARD, which pushed ~1,100 chars of trade directive into vehicleExtras. (2) LP READ ITS OWN DIRECTIVE BACK AS LEAD EVIDENCE, INSIDE ONE GENERATION: populateFromData appends vehicleExtras to the module-level leadContext as a "VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS:" block, and leadContext BECOMES data.context downstream; the DEAL/INFO triggers then scan _ddRecent = lastInboundMsg + conversationBrief + context. The guard's own sentence contains the literal phrase "if they are asking for a trade number", so _ddTradeRx matched it -- the log names the culprit verbatim, [LP DEAL-TRIGGER DIAG] custSpoke:true | matched -> tradeValue:"trade number" -- and stamped the prescriptive "The customer wants to know what their TRADE is worth -- they asked for a NUMBER, not an appointment" onto the prompt. PROVEN, NOT INFERRED: the same regex over the real arc WITHOUT that block returns null and WITH it returns "trade number". This is the v9.7.411 / v9.7.400 agent-text-pollution class with US as the polluter, and it is worse than the CRM round-trip the guard's own header describes (7/20 -> 7/27 -> today), because it closes within a single generation and needs no agent to log anything. NOTE ON v9.7.411: that build gated tradeValue on "the customer must have spoken" but left the SCAN on the full arc -- a boolean precondition, not a scope. custSpoke was true here only because lastInboundMsg is the CarGurus deal-builder blob. FIXES: (1a) neutralize negative trade phrases and test what remains, the v9.7.536 on-premise pattern, so a real ask sharing a note with a negative field still counts; (1b) strip the LP-authored VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS block from the arc before ANY DEAL/INFO trigger reads it -- this protects deadline, dealCondition and distanceRemote too, not just trade. The full-arc scan is deliberately KEPT (v9.7.362) so a trade ask recorded in a call note still fires; only our own text is removed. (3) SEPARATE DEFECT, same lead, reported by Gil: DELIVERY VERBIAGE WAS UNREACHABLE. The approved wording -- we do not deliver, the customer arranges transport and we help coordinate with a carrier they provide -- existed ONLY inside the sc.isDistanceBuyer scenario branch. A deal-builder lead routes to the CarGurus scenario, so the prompt surfaced "Delivery requested: YES" and told the model to "address that directly" while handing it NO approved phrasing; the model invented store-provided delivery and the email said "finalize the delivery plan with your Audi Brand Specialist". The DISTANCE BUYER flag fired on this lead but the distance SCENARIO did not, and the verbiage was attached to the scenario -- confirmed by grep: zero occurrences of the transport wording in the delivered 39,286-char prompt. Verbiage is now attached to the FACT, so it travels with any delivery-requested lead on any branch, and it bans "we deliver" / "we ship it to you" / "the delivery plan" by name while keeping the late-stage timing rule. VERIFIED 14/14 against detectors sliced out of the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: both negative shapes go silent while "Has trade-in: Yes", a customer asking what their trade is worth, an appraisal note, and a real ask sharing an arc with a negative field all still fire; the Woodfork arc plus the guard directive yields null where it previously yielded "trade number", a call-note trade ask still fires with the LP block present, and the delivery detector fires on YES while staying silent on NO and on absent. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: a lead whose ONLY trade evidence sits inside the VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS block loses its trade directive -- that block is LP-authored by construction, so there is no customer evidence to lose. Fix 1a narrows the guard, so an arc carrying ONLY "Has trade-in: No" no longer gets the conflation protection; it also no longer gets the trade directive that made the protection necessary. NOT ADDRESSED, flagged and real: the [LP DEAL-TRIGGER DIAG] custSpoke gate still treats a canned third-party deal-builder blob as "the customer spoke", which is the same low-signal-text-as-intent assumption behind the v9.7.537 pivot incident; it wants its own build. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse. Builds on v9.7.537-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.537-dev (DEV. CROSS-BRAND PIVOT FIRED BACKWARDS AND DECLINED A SALE ON AN IN-STOCK UNIT. LIVE INCIDENT: Allen Billups (Audi Lafayette, lead 2064909098, 8/10). The delivered SMS read "I saw your CarGurus interest in the Honda Accord -- solid choice, but we're an Audi store and don't carry Honda inventory" and closed WITHOUT an ask, on a lead whose vehicle of interest was a 2014 Ford Mustang 2dr Cpe V6 Premium confirmed present in that day's live inventory load (stock P7455). ROOT CAUSE, MEASURED NOT INFERRED: the CarGurus submission was the canned "I'm interested in this 2015 Honda Accord and I'd like to know if it's still available"; the VinSolutions dump for this lead carries "Primary vehicle changed from 2015 Honda Accord Sedan EX-L to 2014 Ford Mustang 2dr Cpe V6 Premium." timestamped 08/09 9:50 PM, i.e. the store had ALREADY re-pointed the lead the night before. The arc held nothing else -- convState=first-touch, hasOutbound=false, hasCustomerReply=false, zero customer messages beyond the submission. The pivot detector matched "accord" inside that submission blob, found it differed from the panel VOI, and concluded the customer had moved TO a Honda. It had no way to tell a move TOWARD a model from the vehicle the store moved AWAY FROM -- direction is the entire question, and the same string match means "pivot" in one arc and "stale original inquiry" in the other. WORSE, AND THE PART THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN CAUGHT EARLIER: the very same prompt already carried the v9.7.409/407 VOI PANEL AUTHORITY directive ("Do NOT name, reference, or ask about the 2015 honda accord -- it is stale"), logged at [Lead Pro] VOI CONFLICT. Two directives gave the model opposite instructions about one vehicle in one prompt, and the CROSS-BRAND block -- being the more specific and more emphatic -- won. Same shape as v9.7.411 (agent-text pollution firing a trade directive on a lead with no trade) and v9.7.446 (a weak disposition flag overriding a canonical one): a string match was trusted over the arc. FIX -- three PIVOT DIRECTION GUARDS, each sufficient alone: (1) NOTHING TO PIVOT FROM -- with hasCustomerReply false anywhere in the arc, the only inbound is the lead submission itself, so the vehicle it names is the ORIGINAL inquiry and not a change of mind; this one alone catches Billups. (2) PANEL VOI SUPERSEDES THE MESSAGE VEHICLE -- mirrors the v9.7.409 condition, so the detector can no longer build a directive around the exact vehicle that guard is simultaneously telling the model to ignore; gated on the candidate belonging to the stale make so a genuine pivot to a third model is untouched. (3) EXPLICIT SUPERSEDE NOTE -- parses "Primary vehicle changed from X to Y", which names the direction outright; deliberately NOT the primary defence, since on this lead only 1 of 8 notes reached the scraped context and this was not among them. SECOND DEFECT FOUND BY THE NEW TESTS, not by inspection: the scan broke on the FIRST model matched, walking a fixed Toyota/Honda/Kia list, so rejecting a stale candidate discarded any genuine pivot sitting behind it in list order (VOI Mazda, stale inbound Honda CR-V, customer actually asking for a Telluride -> pivot lost entirely). The scan now collects ALL candidates and the per-candidate guards skip and keep looking. New always-on [LP PIVOT GUARD] logs both the SUPPRESSED and the ALLOWED path with the full candidate list and the deciding facts, so the real firing rate is measurable from live logs rather than assumed -- the instrument-first discipline already applied to phone and notes-load. VERIFIED 10/10 against the pivot block extracted from the SHIPPED file of both builds, dev===comm on every case: the incident shape suppresses; so do the supersede-note variant, the panel-authority variant with a customer reply present, a same-brand first-touch lead, and a model name appearing only in an agent note. Critically the must-still-fire set all pass -- the "Sequoia" blast reply on a Tundra lead, a genuine cross-brand pivot after a real customer reply, the Baytown sister-brand handoff, the third-model case above, and a supersede note pointing TOWARD the detected model. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: guard 1 makes a pivot unreachable on any lead where hasCustomerReply reads false, so a scrape gap that loses the customer's replies now also loses a real pivot note. That is the deliberate trade -- a missing pivot note leaves the model the full arc to read, whereas a false cross-brand refusal actively declines a live sale -- and the always-on diagnostic makes the case visible if it occurs. NOT ADDRESSED HERE, flagged and real: the pivot model lists hold only Toyota/Honda/Kia nameplates, so at an Audi or other non-listed rooftop every hit is structurally cross-brand; and the refusal copy asserts "we do NOT carry <Brand>", which is false of any store retailing off-brand pre-owned -- this very lead's Ford Mustang at an Audi store is the proof. Brand is being used as a proxy for inventory. That needs its own build and is not touched here. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.536-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.536-dev (DEV. TWO THINGS: (A) THE v9.7.534 SMS ROOT-CAUSE FIX IS RESTORED -- v9.7.535 WAS FORKED FROM THE SUPERSEDED 534 AND SHIPPED WITHOUT IT, RE-OPENING A LIVE COMPLIANCE FAILURE; and (B) THREE CORRECTIONS TO THE v9.7.535 ON-PREMISE CLAUSE RULE, which is the right idea and is kept. (A) THE REGRESSION, AND HOW IT HAPPENED: two v9.7.534-dev builds exist. The FIRST added _bareCarrierKeywordOptOut and was proven a NO-OP on the real page by log99 ("[LP OPT-OUT SCOPE DIAG] ... bareCarrierKeyword:false ... -> smsOptOutIsExit:true"). The SECOND, same version number, added the root-cause fix that actually makes it fire. v9.7.535 was built on the FIRST: it carries _bareCarrierKeywordOptOut (7 references) and ZERO references to _newestInboundBodyRaw or [LP INBOUND BODY DIAG]. Restored here verbatim. WHY IT MATTERS -- this is not cosmetic. _newestInboundBody strips note headers with /Received (from|by)[^\n]*\n?/, but it runs on a transcript entry whose body has already been through sanitize(), whose first action collapses every newline to a space; on one line [^\n]* runs greedily to end-of-line and DELETES THE MESSAGE BODY. Gerra's entry reduced to the literal string "inbound text message". Consequences, both verified against the pristine region: a bare STOP with no accompanying carrier system note is MISSED ENTIRELY (isSmsOptOut comes back FALSE), and the render-time suppression gate -- which tests /^stop[\s.!]*$/ against lastInboundMsg, carrying the identical header -- fails too, which is why log99 contains no "SMS suppressed deterministically" line and DOES contain a generated SMS draft sent to a number the CRM had just confirmed removed from text messages. Without this hunk v9.7.535 texts opted-out customers. RE-VERIFIED HERE ON THE 535 BASE: 21/21 both builds, and a 4,800-shape differential against the pristine region shows 0 cases where isSmsOptOut is turned off, 0 where SMS suppression is lost, 0 where a farewell is added. (B) ON-PREMISE. v9.7.535's same-clause requirement is correct and is KEPT unchanged -- Tiger's note (place-token "on the lot" from an inventory question colliding with subject-token "said" from a callback clause) stays silent. Three corrections, each found by running the REAL extracted detector block against a corpus, not by inspection: (1) AN EXCLUSION MUST NEUTRALIZE ITS OWN PHRASE, NOT VETO THE WHOLE CLAUSE. v9.7.535's three exclusions are clause-level NOTs, so an idiom anywhere in a clause discards every other fact in it. "He is outside and the vehicle is on the lot" and "Customer is out front, said the payment is outside of budget" are GENUINE presence observations that v9.7.535 silently drops and the pre-535 build caught -- two real regressions its 288-combination fuzz did not surface, because that fuzz paired presence fragments with DISTRACTOR fragments and never with an EXCLUDED IDIOM in the same clause. Now each idiom is replaced with a space and the place/subject test runs on what remains; suppression is unchanged when the idiom is all there is ("The vehicle is on the lot and ready to view" leaves nothing to match). (2) _opInventoryExcl WAS STILL TOO NARROW AND THE CLAUSE SPLIT ONLY HID IT. Tiger's note is defused by the clause rule only because the agent happened to type a PERIOD. Rewritten the way agents actually write -- "...any CR-V EX-L's in white on the lot, he said that he will talk it over" -- the tokens share one clause again and it FIRES; a semicolon does the same. Verified against the real block, both confirmed live before this fix. The v9.7.535 header itself observes the exclusion misses "have ANY" but left it narrow and leaned on clause-splitting instead; widened the quantifier set (any/some/more/an/N) and the gap (20 -> 40 chars -- the real note needs 22), and added ';' to the clause separators. Narrowing-only. Punctuation is no longer load-bearing. (3) SUBJECT/PLACE NOW TEST THE APOSTROPHE-NORMALIZED CLAUSE. v9.7.533 normalized only the two guards, deliberately, so it could "only ever SUPPRESS, never widen" -- but that leaves he's/she's/they're unmatchable when VinSolutions renders U+2019, which it demonstrably does (that is exactly how the Artemisa VM slipped through). CORRECTION TO THE v9.7.533 CHANGELOG, which lists a curly-apostrophe "She’s outside now" among its passing cases: that note fires in NEITHER the pre-535 build NOR v9.7.535 -- confirmed by running both real blocks. It only ever fired when a second, straight-quoted subject token happened to be present. THIS IS THE ONE WIDENING IN THIS BUILD and is labelled as such; it adds exactly the curly forms of he's/she's/they're/it's. NOT DONE, MEASURED AND FLAGGED RATHER THAN QUIETLY ACCEPTED: a genuine observation whose place-token and subject-token fall in DIFFERENT clauses ("Customer arrived. Out front by the blue Telluride.") is LOST by the same-clause rule -- 2 such shapes in the review corpus. The v9.7.535 header states all 51 of its narrowed cases were "audited as belonging to one of the three confirmed false-positive families -- none a genuine presence signal lost"; that holds for its own fuzz corpus but not in general, and the cross-clause family is the counterexample. The trade is still correct -- a false "we know you're here" costs far more than a missed one -- so the rule stands, but the loss is real and should be recorded rather than described as absent. The principled recovery is to allow an ADJACENT clause only when the verb is a STRONG arrival verb (arrived / pulled up / walked in), never the weak is/was/said that caused all three incidents; that is a detector change and wants its own build and verification pass, per this file's own standing rule. VERIFICATION, all against the REAL extracted blocks (string-sliced out of both shipped popup.js files and evaluated -- not reimplementations): a 25-case on-premise corpus at 25/25 on both builds, covering 11 genuine shapes (Armando, here-now + standing-out-front, curly apostrophe, walked-the-lot, showroom, pulled-up, left-message mid-text, inbound Received-from, arrived + showroom, and the two idiom-sharing clauses v9.7.535 dropped) and 11 false-positive shapes (Tiger with periods AND with a comma AND with a semicolon, Robin Newman, the real Artemisa VM, vehicle-is-on-the-lot, get-one-on-the-lot, outside-of-budget, outbound availability copy, no-presence language, inventory "any X on the lot") plus the 3 cross-clause gaps reported explicitly as gaps rather than hidden. A 2,592-note differential fuzz of v9.7.535 against this build -- presence and noise fragments joined by every separator agents actually use (period, comma, semicolon, newline, "and", dash) in both orders and with three author prefixes -- gives 96 newly-firing and 240 newly-silent, with EVERY newly-firing case carrying a genuine presence fragment and EVERY newly-silent case carrying a noise fragment: 0 widened on noise, 0 narrowed on a genuine signal. node --check clean on both builds; the on-premise block and the SMS-stop region are each byte-identical between DEV and COMMERCIAL. Worker untouched at v7.47; content.js untouched, scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. STILL OPEN, unchanged: lastInboundMsg carries the routing header for every popup-side consumer; m.isSmsOptOutOnly arrives popup-side as undefined; the TOP-PRIORITY SMS OPT-OUT directive still says "generate a very short opt-out confirmation" while the render layer blanks the field; the EXIT SIGNAL scaffold leak into the arc; and the first-person "I am outside" subject-token gap. Pairs: DEV v9.7.536-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.534. Builds on v9.7.535-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.535-dev (DEV. ON-PREMISE STRUCTURAL FIX -- place-token and subject-token must now fall in the SAME CLAUSE, closing the recurring shape named explicitly three times already (Robin Newman v9.7.523, Artemisa v9.7.532/533) and hit a THIRD time live. LIVE INCIDENT: Tiger Nguyen (Community Honda Baytown, lead, 8/7). A routine inbound call note -- Rotaxlyn logged "He asked if stock #P4837 has remote start... He asked if we have any CR-V EX-L's in white on the lot. I told him that we have a certified 2026 available, stock #P4848 for $35,093. He said that he will talk it over and will call back." -- contains NOTHING about Tiger's physical location. The place-token matched "on the lot" from Rotaxlyn describing INVENTORY availability; the subject-token matched "said" from an unrelated clause about him planning to call back later. [LP ON-PREMISE DIAG] fired "customer on the lot 2 min ago"; LP generated "Tiger, we know you're here... what are you standing in front of so we can find you?" sent to someone who was not there. THE v9.7.523 EXCLUSION MISSED THIS PHRASING: it requires the pronoun immediately after the verb -- (get|have|receive|see|got)+(one|it|a|another|the) -- but Rotaxlyn wrote "we have ANY CR-V EX-L's ... on the lot", so "have" is followed by "any", not "a/the/one/another", and the narrow exclusion never matches. ROOT CAUSE, UNCHANGED SINCE v9.7.523: two independent regexes matching ANYWHERE in a note, with nothing requiring them to describe the same fact. Every fix so far (Robin Newman's inventory exclusion, Artemisa's outbound-framing + apostrophe guards) has been a targeted exclusion for one specific colliding construction, explicitly flagged each time as leaving the shape intact. THIS BUILD implements the structural fix that was deferred twice: split note content into clauses on [.!?\n], and require BOTH the place-token and the subject-token to match WITHIN THE SAME CLAUSE. Tiger's "on the lot" (inventory clause) and "said" (callback clause) are now in different clauses and no longer collide; this also makes the narrow v9.7.523 exclusion mostly redundant going forward (kept anyway, defense in depth, for any future same-clause "get one on the lot" phrasing). WHILE BUILDING, FUZZ-TESTED 288 combinations of place/subject fragment pairs (same-clause and different-clause) against the pristine region to confirm the new logic is a STRICT NARROWING -- zero cases where the new logic fires and the old one didn't; found and closed two more collisions the same-clause requirement alone does NOT fix, because the tokens genuinely share one clause: "vehicle is on the lot" (subject is the VEHICLE, not the customer -- _opVehicleIsHere generalized to _opVehicleAvailable, now covering "here now" / "on the lot" / "in stock" / "available", the same family it already excluded for one phrasing) and "outside of budget" (bare word "outside" matching the place-token idiomatically -- new _opBudgetExcl). Neither of these was a live incident; both were found by fuzzing before shipping, which is the point of doing the structural fix now rather than after a fourth incident. VERIFIED 18/18 against a corpus covering all 9 previously-confirmed genuine on-premise shapes (Armando, Artemisa's abused-but-genuine "here now, standing out front", curly apostrophe, left-message-mid-text, Received-from inbound, walked/showroom/pulled-up variants) plus 9 false-positive shapes (Artemisa's real VM note, outbound text/email availability copy, Robin Newman, Tiger, vehicle-is-on-the-lot, get-one-on-the-lot, no-presence-language, outside-of-budget) -- all correct, dev===comm. 288-combination fuzz run: 0 widened, 51 narrowed, and every one of the 51 narrowed cases audited as belonging to one of the three confirmed false-positive families (inventory "on the lot", vehicle-availability idiom, budget idiom) -- none a genuine presence signal lost. node --check clean both builds; changed region byte-identical between DEV and COMMERCIAL. Worker untouched at v7.47; content.js untouched, scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Pairs: DEV v9.7.535-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.533. Builds on v9.7.534-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.534-dev (DEV. BARE CARRIER `STOP` NO LONGER ENDS A LIVE LEAD -- THIS DELIBERATELY REVERSES THE v9.7.518 CARVE-OUT. This is not a regression fix: the shipped behaviour was working exactly as specified, and the SPECIFICATION was the problem. Read this entry before "restoring" the old behaviour -- it was not lost by accident. LIVE INCIDENT: Gerra (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2061540346, 5 days old, 8/7). She had just COMPLETED A CREDIT APPLICATION through Click & Go; the outbound she was answering was "I saw you completed your application through Click & Go, but no vehicle is attached yet. What model are you shopping for?" Her reply, in full, was the single word STOP. LP generated: "Subject: We'll respect your request / Gerra, Understood, and thank you for letting us know. We'll respect your request and won't send further messages. If you ever need Community Kia Baytown in the future, we'll be here." The log named the path with no ambiguity -- rawStopSignal:true, smsOptOutIsExit:true, hasExitSignal:true, isSmsOptOutOnly(final):false, and [LP EXIT SIGNAL DIAG] hasExitSignal:true | hasRecentReactivation:false | leadAgeDays:5 -- and the page dump confirmed the STOP was an inbound text on THIS lead, not a prior one. WHAT v9.7.518 SAID, quoted so it is not re-litigated by accident: that build added !_optOutPriorLead to smsOptOutIsExit so the farewell is suppressed when the opt-out came from a PRIOR lead (Raul Meza), and stated the carve-out explicitly -- "a STOP on the CURRENT lead still escalates to exit (compliance case, unchanged)". Gerra's STOP is on the CURRENT lead, so the v9.7.518 guard correctly did NOT apply. That sentence is what is being withdrawn here. WHY THE CARVE-OUT IS WRONG: STOP is a CARRIER KEYWORD. It means "stop texting me" -- a channel preference. It does not mean "I am not interested", and conflating the two ends live leads. Two distinct harms in Gerra's message: (1) it CLOSED AN ACTIVE SHOPPER who had a completed credit application in flight, and (2) it OVER-PROMISED -- "we won't send further messages" commits to stopping ALL communication when she opted out of exactly ONE channel; that is a worse statement than the compliance rule requires, and one we cannot honour while continuing to work the lead. THE FIX reuses the mechanism v9.7.518 already built and proved for the prior-lead case rather than inventing a new one: new _bareCarrierKeywordOptOut joins smsOptOutIsExit as a fourth negated term, so isSmsOptOutOnly goes TRUE, SMS stays suppressed, and the draft goes out on email/voicemail as a real response to her application. THE DISTINCTION IS NOT FLATTENED -- bare "STOP" -> SMS suppressed, email proceeds normally (CHANGED); "stop contacting me" -> exit + farewell (UNCHANGED); "not interested" -> exit (UNCHANGED); "we bought elsewhere" -> exit (UNCHANGED). The written request survives on its own path: exitRaw matches stop.*contacting and remove.*from.*list INDEPENDENTLY of smsOptOutIsExit, VERIFIED by lifting the exitRaw regex literal out of the real source line with the smsOptOutIsExit term stripped off and testing it alone, rather than by assuming it. "Bare" is strict -- the keyword ALONE modulo trivial punctuation/whitespace, never a sentence containing it -- and the truth set is rawStopSignal's OWN bare-keyword clauses reused verbatim (same /^stop[\s.!]*$/ shape, same three sources: _freshStopOnThisLead, inboundMessageOnly, recentInbound), so NO second keyword list is introduced and this cannot drift from what the codebase already recognizes as a carrier keyword. rawStopSignal's remaining clauses -- the status-note phrases and hasSystemOptOutNote -- deliberately keep the old escalating behaviour, because those carry no customer wording at all. The rule is affirmative, not exclusive: a bare STOP that ALSO generated a carrier system note still qualifies, because that note is the SAME event recorded twice, not extra evidence of disinterest; what overrides it is a real exit signal, which routes through hasExitSignal on its own merits. COMPLIANCE, THE HARD BOUNDARY -- SMS IS NOT RE-ENABLED ON ANY PATH: isSmsOptOut is untouched, only the FRAMING and the CHANNEL change, and restoring SMS remains gated on hasRecentReoptIn. This is PROVED MECHANICALLY, not asserted. The change adds only a negated term, so smsOptOutIsExit is monotonically weaker, hasExitSignal can only go true->false, and isSmsOptOutOnly can only go false->true -- which means the render-time deterministic suppression gate (isSmsOptOutOnly || bare-STOP lastInboundMsg, v9.7.379 F3) can only get STRONGER, never weaker. A differential run of the pristine 9.7.533 region against the patched region across 3,360 generated lead shapes confirms it empirically: 0 cases of isSmsOptOut drift, 0 cases where SMS suppression was lost, 0 cases where a farewell was ADDED, and 128 shapes where the farewell was removed -- all 128 audited and every one a bare-carrier-keyword shape with zero independent exit evidence, landing on isSmsOptOutOnly:true. VERIFIED with 20 functional assertions against the REAL shipped region (the harness slices the source lines out of popup.js and evaluates those exact bytes -- it is not a reimplementation), every case asserting the SMS state explicitly: Gerra's exact shape, bare STOP with no other signal present, "stop." with trailing punctuation, and a bare STOP alongside a carrier system note all now yield isSmsOptOutOnly:true / hasExitSignal:false with SMS SUPPRESSED; "stop contacting me", "no longer interested", "we bought elsewhere", "we went with another dealer", "take me off your list", and each of the five hasExitSignal components firing on its own (exitRaw, boughtElsewhere, exitByRemoval, hasInternalSoldElsewhere, hasInternalNotInterested, plus hasBereavementSignal) all still exit UNCHANGED; and Raul Meza's prior-lead shape, a lead with no opt-out at all, a historical system-note opt-out, and the hasRecentReoptIn restore path are all bit-identical to before. END-TO-END on Gerra: convState goes exit -> active-follow-up and the emitted directive goes from the gracious-close farewell to the SMS-opt-out WORKED-LEAD email branch ("DO NOT mention the opt-out at all... CONTINUE that conversation: pick up the actual inquiry/vehicle thread where it left off"), with SMS suppressed on both sides of the change. SURPRISE, FLAGGED NOT FIXED (out of scope): the Wendy-Browne shape -- bare STOP as the newest message with re-engagement language behind it -- previously produced isSmsOptOut:true with BOTH isSmsOptOutOnly:false AND hasExitSignal:false, because recentCustomerActive cancelled the exit while smsOptOutIsExit still blocked the opt-out-only flag; SMS suppression then rested entirely on the render-time lastInboundMsg test, whose regex is narrower than the scraper's. This build closes that hole as a side effect. ALSO NOTED, UNCHANGED: the TOP-PRIORITY SMS OPT-OUT directive still opens with "SMS = generate a very short opt-out confirmation" while two other directives and the render layer both say the SMS field must be EMPTY -- a residual contradiction left over from the v9.7.379 F3 alignment. It is harmless because render discards any SMS draft deterministically, but it now fires on more leads and should be cleaned up in a separate build. node --check clean on both builds; the changed region is byte-identical between DEV and COMMERCIAL. Worker untouched at v7.47; scraperVersion stays v9.7.51, content.js untouched. Pairs: DEV v9.7.534-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.532. Builds on v9.7.533-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.533-dev (DEV. THE v9.7.532 _onPremise GUARDS NEVER FIRED IN PRODUCTION -- 11/11 offline, 0/1 on the real page. Artemisa Amaro (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2063516075) regenerated on 9.7.532-dev and STILL produced "Since you stopped by today"; log97 confirms the build loaded (9.7.532-dev) and [LP ON-PREMISE DIAG] customer visited 4h ago fired again from popup.js:2064. BOTH new guards missed, for two independent reasons, and BOTH were VERIFICATION errors on my part rather than logic errors in the regexes: (1) ANCHOR. VinSolutions renders the note author as its OWN <div> INSIDE the content cell -- <div class="notes-and-history-item-content"><div>By: Kristen Willis</div>Left message - ...</div> -- so .innerText begins "By: Kristen Willis\nLeft message - ..." and a ^-anchored test for "Left message" can never match. Confirmed against dump96: 14 of Artemisa\'s 17 note contents open with a "By: <name>" line, the other 3 with "Sent to:" or "Subject:". v9.7.532 was verified against note bodies I RECONSTRUCTED from the CRM\'s rendered display, which drop that prefix -- which is exactly why it passed 11/11 offline while being unable to fire on a single real note. (2) APOSTROPHE. The real text uses a CURLY apostrophe U+2019 -- "it\u2019s here now" -- while _opVehicleIsHere tested /it\'?s/ with a STRAIGHT quote. The VM note contains no straight apostrophe anywhere, so the second guard could not catch what the first one missed. THE LESSON, which this codebase has now logged repeatedly and which I repeated anyway: reproduce against the ACTUAL scraped artifact, not a reconstruction of it. The 11-case suite was the right shape and the wrong corpus. FIX: normalize ONCE, for these two guards ONLY -- fold curly apostrophes (U+2018/U+2019/U+02BC/U+00B4) to straight, and strip a leading "By: <author>" line -- then apply the same anchored tests to the normalized string. PLACE and SUBJECT keep testing the ORIGINAL content, so the existing detector\'s behaviour is unchanged and this normalization can only ever SUPPRESS, never widen. Stripping "By: <author>" cannot hide a genuine observation: a real on-premise note reads "By: <agent>\nCustomer is outside waiting", which normalizes to "Customer is outside waiting" -- no outbound framing, still fires. VERIFIED AGAINST THE REAL CORPUS THIS TIME: all 17 of Artemisa\'s note contents extracted from dump96 as .innerText (not reconstructed) -- v9.7.532 sets _onPremise on note [9], v9.7.533 sets it on ZERO, so the visit block cannot render. Plus 12/12 on shaped cases, dev===comm on every one: 8 genuine on-premise notes ALL still fire in the real "By: <agent>" DOM shape -- customer outside waiting, walked the lot, in the showroom, pulled up, a CURLY-apostrophe "She\u2019s outside now", "left message" appearing MID-text, a real inbound "Received from:" note, and critically "Customer is here now, standing out front" (the same "here now" token the incident abused); and 4 outbound shapes stay suppressed including the real VM note, outbound text/email availability copy, and the Robin Newman v9.7.523 case. THE RECURRING SHAPE IS STILL INTACT, unchanged from the v9.7.532 header: two independent regexes -- a place-token and a subject/verb-token -- each free to match ANYWHERE in a note with nothing requiring them to describe the same fact (Robin Newman: "on the lot" + "said"; Artemisa: "here now" + "is"). Every fix so far has been a targeted exclusion for the specific colliding construction. _opOurOwnOutbound remains the more durable guard because it keys on WHO WROTE THE NOTE rather than on wording; _opVehicleIsHere remains a phrase pattern with a finite lifespan. The structural fix -- requiring both tokens to fall in the SAME clause, or requiring the note to be agent-observation-shaped -- is still NOT implemented and still wants its own build and its own verification pass. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.532-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.532-dev (DEV. _onPremise FIRED ON OUR OWN OUTBOUND VOICEMAIL -- LP told a customer who never visited that she had. LIVE INCIDENT: Artemisa Amaro (Community Kia Baytown, lead 2063516075, 8/6, Facebook lead created 8/5 10:52p). Generated message opened "Artemisa, thanks for reaching out on Facebook. I know you already stopped by today, and the 2027 Kia Seltos EX is still here..." She never visited. Her record holds 17 notes -- outbound texts, voicemails, two lead-log reassignments, general notes -- no showroom visit, no appointment, no walk-in. ROOT CAUSE, REPRODUCED BYTE-FOR-BYTE: the prompt names its own source. The on-premise block quotes, as its evidence of a visit, OUR OWN OUTBOUND VOICEMAIL -- her own note 6, logged 8:44 AM as "Outbound phone call (Machine)": "Left message - Hi, this is Kristen with Community Kia Baytown. I saw your Facebook message about the 2027 Kia Seltos S, and it\'s here now if you\'d like to come take a look." Running the shipped condition against that exact text: the PLACE clause matched "here now" (from "it\'s here now" -- describing THE VEHICLE) and the SUBJECT clause matched "is" (from "this IS Kristen" / "That IS 281-837-3380"). Two clauses, two unrelated fragments of one sentence, neither describing where the customer was. _opAgeMin then landed in the 120-720 minute window and the "came to the store earlier today" directive fired -- confirmed live by [LP ON-PREMISE DIAG] customer visited 4h ago. THE RECURRING SHAPE, NAMED EXPLICITLY BECAUSE THIS IS ITS THIRD APPEARANCE AND A FOURTH WILL COME: two independent regexes -- a place-token and a subject/verb-token -- each free to match ANYWHERE in a note, with nothing requiring them to describe the same fact. Robin Newman (v9.7.523) matched "on the lot" from a vehicle-arrival clause plus "said" from an unrelated clause. Artemisa matched "here now" from "it\'s here now" plus "is" from "this is Kristen". Every fix so far, including this one, has been a targeted exclusion for the specific colliding construction: that stops the instance and LEAVES THE SHAPE INTACT. FIX -- two guards AND-ed into the existing condition, and they are NOT equally durable, which is worth stating plainly: (1) _opOurOwnOutbound skips notes whose body opens with outbound framing (Left message / Sent to: / Sent by: / Subject:). This is the MORE DURABLE of the two because it keys on WHO WROTE THE NOTE rather than on wording -- an on-premise fact is something an agent OBSERVES and writes down; it never comes from the body of a message we SENT. New outbound phrasing cannot evade a structural prefix. (2) _opVehicleIsHere excludes "<vehicle> is here now" availability copy. This is ANOTHER PHRASE PATTERN with exactly the same finite lifespan as its predecessors -- it buys one construction and nothing more, and should be expected to need widening when the next availability phrasing collides. NOTED FOR A FUTURE BUILD, DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED HERE: the structural fix is to make the discriminator carry the weight -- require the place-token and subject-token to fall within the SAME clause, or require the note to be agent-observation-shaped rather than outbound-message-shaped, instead of enumerating collisions forever. That is a detector rewrite needing its own verification pass; this build\'s 11-case suite covers the two guards, not a restructure. PRIOR DIAGNOSIS CORRECTED (investigated, did not hold): this incident was first attributed to cross-lead contamination via the sticky-true OR-merge whitelist. log96 contains THREE SEPARATE GRABS of three different leads -- 2063517752 (CR-V, Honda Baytown 6191, 9 notes), 2063310788 (Mercedes, Toyota Baytown 6189, 7 notes, isShowroomFollowUp:TRUE), 2063516075 (Seltos, Kia Baytown 6190, 17 notes, isShowroomFollowUp:UNDEFINED). Every frame WITHIN each grab agrees on its lead ID, which is why zero "Frame rejected" lines appear -- the mismatch gate had no work, it was not absent and not bypassed (on the Veronica lead, where foreign frames genuinely were co-resident, it fired three times). isShowroomFollowUp was NEVER true on Artemisa\'s lead. Separately, "Showroom follow-up: reference the visit" occurs only in the SYSTEM prompt\'s static SUBJECT LINE RULES menu, which ships on every lead and was not triggered by anything here. The sticky-true merge is a real latent hazard but is NOT this bug; it is untouched and stays queued with its own attribution window. VERIFIED 11/11 against the condition extracted from the SHIPPED files, dev===comm on every case, ZERO regressions: the incident note now silent; outbound text and outbound email availability copy silent; the Robin Newman case still silent; and every genuine on-premise shape still FIRES -- customer outside waiting, walked the lot, in the showroom, pulled up, "left message" appearing MID-text in a real agent observation (the ^-anchor boundary case), a real inbound "Received from:" note, and critically "Customer is here now, standing out front" -- the SAME "here now" token the incident abused, proving the discriminator is outbound framing and not the token. END-TO-END: all 17 of Artemisa\'s real note bodies re-run through the shipped condition produce _onPremise = null, so the block is gone from the delivered prompt. STATED BEHAVIOUR, asked at review: a note BEGINNING "Subject:" is suppressed. That is intended -- in the real VinSolutions DOM, "Subject:" prefixes belong to OUTBOUND "Email reply to prospect" / "Email auto response", while genuine inbound notes begin "Received from:" / "Received by:" and are unaffected. SURPRISE FOUND AND FLAGGED, NOT FIXED HERE: the subject-token list is THIRD-PERSON ONLY (is/was/he\'s/she\'s/they\'re/said/waiting/standing). First-person arrival language -- "I am outside", "I\'m here", a customer announcing their own arrival -- has no match and never fired, before this change or after. Pre-existing gap, unchanged by this fix, worth its own build. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.531-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.531-dev (DEV. NOTES-ONLY SCRAPE FAILURE REACHED THE MODEL AS A FIRST TOUCH -- and the one guard that blocks Generate could not fire on it. LIVE INCIDENT: Veronica Schaefer (Community Honda Baytown, lead 2062181217, 8/6). She came in and TEST DROVE; the generated message opened "I saw your online inquiry... We have not connected yet." The delivered prompt carried "Total CRM entries: 0" and a COMPLETELY EMPTY transcript block. ROOT CAUSE, MEASURED NOT INFERRED: the only frame carrying this lead rendered WITHOUT its Notes & History section. [LP DOM-VERIFY DIAG] put that frame at htmlLen 155,498; the fully-rendered dump of the SAME lead is 236,727 bytes carrying 64 notes-and-history-item-content elements, "Showroom" x11 and "test drove" -- roughly 81 KB of missing notes DOM. The notes were not missed by a selector and were not destroyed downstream; they had not rendered. Every notes-derived field came back empty, so convState defaulted to first-touch, hasCustomerReply to false, and the ZERO-CONTACT / STALLED branch fired on a customer with 63 notes. NOT A REGRESSION FROM v9.7.530: an earlier grab of this exact lead on this exact build succeeded (notes:63, convState:active-follow-up, hasApptSet:true, isShowroomFollowUp:true, "Scenario: SHOWROOM FOLLOW-UP active"), and the complete code-only diff v9.7.529 -> v9.7.530-dev is 5 lines removed / 33 added, all five of them the audit fixes (incentive expiry filter, sanitize Get/> patterns, the trade-example string, the phone-up marker) -- none of which touch scraping, frame selection, the merge, the rescue, classifyScenario or SCENARIO assembly. [LP QUOTE-STRIP DIAG], [LP INCENTIVE EXPIRY DIAG] and [LP PHONE MARKER FALLBACK DIAG] all logged ZERO occurrences on the failing grab, and the log contains no ReferenceError/TypeError/SCRAPER FATAL. This reproduces on 529 and earlier. WHY NOTHING STOPPED IT, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL DEFECT: the TRANSCRIPT-MISSING rescue fired correctly, waited its passive 7s and re-scraped -- the frame was STILL unrendered, so it recovered nothing ("TRANSCRIPT-RESCUE override applied -- totalNoteCount=0 hasCustomerReply=false convState=first-touch"). Its "Rescue succeeded" line only reports that the merge ran, not that notes came back. The v9.7.356 override block itself is correctly written -- every assignment is one-directional (d.totalNoteCount > 0, d.convState !== 'first-touch') so it can only upgrade, never downgrade -- it simply had nothing to work with. The ONLY mechanism that blocks Generate is _phantomScrape, and it requires !phone && !email && !emailRaw && !lastInboundMsg && !context, i.e. a TOTALLY empty scrape. Veronica has both a phone ((832) 714-2325) and an email on file, so that conjunction collapsed and the guard could not fire. It is STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE of catching a notes-only failure on any lead carrying contact information -- which is most worked leads. Same shape as the v9.7.519 finding where _lpInvConfirmedAvailable could not protect an in-transit unit: the single guard built for a failure could not reach the case that most needs it. FIX: a separate, narrower gate beside _phantomScrape rather than loosening it -- widening its !_eff.phone term would re-open the v9.7.468 false positive that blocked genuine email-only first-touch leads, a documented past regression. The condition is re-derived from _eff (the object that actually feeds the prompt, per the v9.7.394 PHANTOM-EFFECTIVE lesson) instead of reading the _transcriptMissing flag, so it carries no dependency on that variable's scope AND correctly reads false when the rescue genuinely recovered notes. Blocks with "Notes didn't load for this lead -- refresh the page and try GRAB LEAD again" and disables both Generate and Voicemail. New ALWAYS-ON [LP NOTES-LOAD DIAG] reports panelWorked / totalNoteCount / historyLen / isAILead / blocking on every grab, so the real frequency of this failure is measurable from live logs instead of assumed -- matching the [LP PHONE DIAG] always-log discipline this file already uses, and specifically so a future scope change here cannot hide behind "nothing to report". VERIFIED 9/9 against the shipped decision logic extracted from both files, dev===comm on every case: the incident shape BLOCKS; worked-lead-with-hasOutbound and worked-lead-with-only-a-history-stub BLOCK; and critically the must-not-block set all pass -- Veronica's own healthy 63-note grab, a rescue that DID recover notes, a genuine never-contacted first-touch lead, the v9.7.468 email-only no-phone first-touch case, an AI Buying Signal lead (exempt, no notes by design), and a worked lead whose arc lives in history rather than the note count. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, PLAINLY: this BLOCKS Generate. A lead the panel reports as worked but which genuinely has zero notes is now blocked instead of generating. The TRANSCRIPT-MISSING rescue's own entry condition already asserts that combination is a scrape failure rather than a real state, so the blast radius should be small -- but a false positive costs an agent a grab, which is why the diagnostic is always-on. NOT ADDRESSED HERE, flagged and real: on the EARLIER grab, frame 595 reported VOI "2025 Toyota Camry LE" for lead 2062181217, while the fully-rendered dump of that same lead contains ZERO occurrences of "Camry" and shows 2026 Honda Civic. A frame claiming this lead ID served a wrong vehicle, and [LP VOI DIAG][conflict] passed it as "clean -- 1 model family" because the detector needs two competing families within one grab to fire. Two leads were open in the tab (2062181217 and 2062181375) and the mismatch rejection worked correctly on the other one, so this is a WITHIN-lead-ID wrong-vehicle case, not cross-lead bleed. It deserves its own build and is not touched by this fix. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version and version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.530-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.530-dev (DEV. FOUR FIXES FROM THE EXTERNAL AUDIT, ONE PER FAILURE CLASS, PLUS A PARITY BREAK THAT WOULD HAVE DELETED A SHIPPED COMMERCIAL FIX. (G1) READ-TIME EXPIRY ENFORCEMENT -- the Data Tool drops expired incentive rows at PUBLISH time and its own UI calls expiry "the kill-switch," promising Lead Pro "stops using any line after its expires date." THE EXTENSION NEVER DID THAT. `expires` appeared in exactly THREE executable places in this whole file, all inside the _daysLeft urgency branch, where it only chose between "OFFER ENDING SOON" and normal framing: a NEGATIVE _daysLeft -- an offer that already ended -- fails the `>= 0` test, falls into the else, and is injected labelled "real & current". Nothing upstream filtered on it either; _lpYearFilterIncentives filters on model YEAR only. So once a store's KV snapshot aged past its dates, every line in it kept flowing into live prompts as a current, quotable fact -- no log, no warning, and nothing distinguishing a stale snapshot from a fresh one. Reproduced by execution: an incentive 181 days expired reached the "real & current" branch. Same failure family as the fabricated "0% APR for 36 months." FIX: new _lpExpiryFilterIncentives wraps the year filter at the single read site, so an expired line can no longer reach that branch AT ALL -- the branch itself is untouched and is correct as written once only current lines arrive. A missing expires is treated as NOT publishable, matching the Data Tool's own publish rule. Format is validated strictly (/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/) BEFORE the lexicographic compare, because that compare is only meaningful on zero-padded ISO -- "soon" and "2026-8-6" both sort ABOVE a real date and would otherwise survive as though current. New [LP INCENTIVE EXPIRY DIAG] logs store, dropped count, expired-vs-undated split, how many remain, and the most recent dropped expiry, with an explicit NO CURRENT LINES REMAIN warning -- a store going stale is now visible instead of silent. VERIFIED 9/9 mandated cases incl. expires-today surviving, yesterday/6-months dropped, null/empty/missing dropped, both malformed forms dropped, nothing thrown; plus a mixed store (2 future, 2 expired, 1 undated) correctly keeping exactly its 2 current lines -- the over-broad-filter failure mode is ruled out. WHAT THIS MIGHT BREAK, STATED PLAINLY: any store whose KV snapshot is ALREADY past its dates will have its STORE INCENTIVE line stop appearing entirely rather than quoting stale facts. That is the correct outcome, but it is immediate and customer-visible, so it needs a re-publish queued alongside the deploy. THE PRE-DEPLOY KV SURVEY COULD NOT BE RUN FROM THE BUILD ENVIRONMENT -- leadpro-proxy.gilsguzman.workers.dev is blocked by egress policy (403 on CONNECT); a ready-to-run survey script ships with this build and MUST be run before deploying so no store is surprised. (F4) TRADE-DIRECTIVE SELF-POISONING -- the TRADE-IN CONCERN string carried a hardcoded example, "Angel, still want to get that 2023 Tacoma appraised". That string is embedded into concernBlock -> conversationBrief -> d.conversationBrief, which the trade-conflation guard scans for a year+make. On a real captured prompt (Montea, Audi Lafayette, zero-contact, record says "Has trade-in: No") it found "2023 Tacoma", ruled it not the VOI, and told the model "the customer HAS described their trade in their own words... Use THAT vehicle" -- the only trade vehicle anywhere in that prompt being Lead Pro's own example, a Toyota, at an Audi store. Same shape as the v9.7.494 incident where this guard argued FOR the error it exists to prevent, reached by a new route: the VOI-exclusion check works correctly and is bypassed because the contaminating vehicle is neither the VOI nor real. FIX is the one-line example change (no parseable year+make); verified the chain now yields _tvNamed:false and takes the correct "no trade vehicle has been named" branch in both builds. NOT DONE HERE, still open: _tvHay is built from the ASSEMBLED BRIEF, so ANY directive text Lead Pro writes into conversationBrief is readable as evidence by every downstream detector that scans it -- concernBlock was one string, not the only one. That is a structural change with its own surface and wants its own build. (F1) sanitize() footer strip: `\s*(?:Sent from|Get|Downloaded from)` -- `\s*` matches ZERO-WIDTH, so under /i the bare literal "Get" matched the tail of "bud|get", "for|get", "tar|get", and `[^]*` deleted to end of note. IDENTICAL mechanism to the v9.7.528 "opti|on " incident, on the branch immediately above the one that fix touched. A \b alone is NOT sufficient here, unlike the On case: "Can you get Gmail to send me the quote?" is ordinary speech where "get" IS at a word boundary and it lost 73 chars. "Get" is therefore split out and bound to the only form Microsoft emits -- "Get Outlook for Android/iOS"; the other two openers keep their alternation and gain \b. MIGHT BREAK: a bare "Get Gmail" footer would no longer strip; Google does not emit that string, and silently deleting customer speech is the strictly worse failure. (F2) sanitize() quote strip: `.replace(/\s*>[^]*/g, '')` sat at the BOTTOM of the chain calling itself "strip any remaining '> quoted' email lines" -- it could never do that, because the FIRST link collapses every newline to a space, so no line starts remained and the pattern carried no anchor of its own. Net effect: a SINGLE ">" anywhere deleted everything to the end of the note. "Timeline: this week -> next week at the latest..." came out as "Timeline: this week -" (68 chars gone); arrows and comparisons are ordinary customer and agent shorthand, so this needed ONE character to fire -- strictly worse than the multi-token conditions the On patterns required. FIX: the strip moves ABOVE the newline collapse and requires a real line start -- (?:^|\n)[ \t]*> -- deliberately [ \t]* not \s*, since \s matches \n and would let the anchor drift. Old unanchored pattern DELETED, not kept alongside. New [LP QUOTE-STRIP DIAG] reports chars removed -- emitted via _lpD, NOT console.log, because sanitize() lives INSIDE inlineScraper (4267-8590; sanitize ~5771) and a console.log there prints to the PAGE console, the exact trap that has now silently hidden four diagnostics (v9.7.455, 505/506, 510). F1+F2 VERIFIED 12/12 against sanitize() extracted from the SHIPPED files, not a reimplementation: all five documented losses (71/89/73/66/68 chars) now lose ZERO; genuine "Sent from my iPhone", "Get Outlook for Android", a newline-anchored "> quoted" block, both v9.7.528 On-chains and the "------ Original Message ------" separator all still strip; the Megan Mckennie guard ("Are you open on Saturday?") is BYTE-IDENTICAL to its pre-fix output. (F6) PARITY: the v9.7.517 PHONE-UP MARKER FALLBACK shipped to COMMERCIAL but NEVER landed in DEV -- 0 occurrences here, 1 there. COMMERCIAL's own v9.7.517 header asserts "byte-identical between dev and comm (diff confirms)" and "Pairs: DEV v9.7.521-dev", but DEV's v9.7.521-dev is the cache-boundary sentinel, an entirely different change, and DEV's chain skips 522 (520->521->523). Without this, a phone-up lead with no "Inbound phone call" note gets NO current-lead marker, and every marker-dependent guard (opt-out scope, sold-news, exit-signal, self-consistency, stalled-lead) falls back to treating the whole unscoped transcript as current. The forward risk was the greater one: DEV becomes the next COMMERCIAL, so promoting DEV as-is would have DELETED a shipped fix. Ported verbatim in behaviour, with ONE deliberate deviation -- COMMERCIAL's line used console.log at a site INSIDE inlineScraper (comm 4038-8342, line sat at 6330), so that diagnostic has never once appeared in an exported log; switched to _lpD in BOTH builds so they stay byte-identical and the diagnostic is actually observable. Marker string count now 1 in both. NOT IN THIS BUILD, deliberately: audit findings 3/5/7/8/9/10, the section B structural bound, Data Tool G2/G3/G4, and the _lpVerifyStoreSignature merge. node --check clean both builds; both manifests parse; version + version_name both bumped. Builds on v9.7.529-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.529-dev (DEV. PROMPT SCAFFOLD LEAKING INTO THE CONVERSATION ARC AS IF IT WERE A CRM RECORD — found by reading a real _lpDumpPrompt() capture end-to-end rather than grepping it, the first time the full delivered prompt has been readable at all. The arc summary was presenting this to the model, quoted, labeled as note content: CALL NOTE: "By: System https://www.callmeasurement.com/... FOLLOW-UP: read the full transcript and write a response... Total CRM entries: 30 MOST RECENT CUSTOMER MESSAGE... CRITICAL: Your response MUST directly address what the customer said above... IDENTIFIED CUSTOMER CONCERNS - lead with these, do not bury them: COLOR PREFERENCE..." — i.e. Lead Pro\'s OWN directives dressed as something a human typed into VinSolutions, truncated mid-sentence. ROOT CAUSE: ctxEntries splits the resolved context with a LOOKAHEAD on the next entry\'s date header, so each entry is bounded by its successor — except the LAST entry, which has no successor and therefore absorbs every trailing character. The resolved context is history + conversationBrief concatenated (confirmed by [LP CTX-SCOPE DIAG]: notes 7553 + histCurrent 6231 = current 13785), so the oldest transcript entry swallowed the brief\'s entire header block. The pre-existing \' --- \' guard was the right instinct but only fires when that separator survives as a spaced token; here the brief follows without one. WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS: the model has to decide whether "CRITICAL: Your response MUST..." is an instruction or something an agent wrote in the CRM — and this codebase\'s own most-repeated lesson (Civic-trade, showroom-thin-notes, anti-restate, Gary Hudson) is that the thread\'s own record is a STRONGER signal than a directive stated elsewhere. Laundering a directive INTO the record is therefore the worst available arrangement: it both corrupts the record and weakens the directive. FIX: cut msgText at the first prompt-scaffold marker (Lead Pro\'s own section headers — strings a human-written CRM note never contains, so the cut can only ever remove scaffold, never customer or agent speech); an entry that is ENTIRELY scaffold is dropped rather than truncated to nothing. New [LP SCAFFOLD-LEAK DIAG] reports the character count removed whenever it fires, so recurrence and any future context-assembly change stay observable. VERIFIED 7/7 against the real leaked entry verbatim from the capture: it now retains only its genuine call-note content (the callmeasurement URL and By: line) with every directive removed; a normal agent call note is untouched; a customer message containing similar words ("I read the full transcript... a critical question") is untouched; lowercase "critical" in ordinary speech is untouched; a pure-scaffold entry is dropped. NOT ADDRESSED HERE, flagged for a later pass: the same capture shows the customer\'s key correction repeated 5 times across sections and the full conversation present in 3 forms (arc summary, numbered index, full transcript) at ~15.5k tokens per generation — data starvation is no longer the failure mode; dilution is. That is a prompt-architecture question deserving its own build and its own before/after evidence, not a drive-by trim. node --check clean both builds. Builds on v9.7.528-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.528-dev (DEV. ROOT CAUSE FOUND — WORD-BOUNDARY BUG IN sanitize(), and it invalidates the diagnosis behind BOTH prior fixes for this incident. Daniel Boyd (Community Kia Baytown, 8/5): his only explicit correction — "Pre-owned vehicles preferably" — never reached the model across three separate _lpDumpPrompt() captures, each showing the same transcript entry: "Subject: Re:A Telluride Hybrid opti", exactly 35 chars, cut mid-word. v9.7.526 blamed .innerText layout-dependency and added a .textContent fallback; v9.7.527 blamed lazy-rendering and added incomplete-note detection. NEITHER COULD EVER HAVE FIRED, because the content is not missing at scrape time — it arrives COMPLETE and is destroyed downstream. Proof that redirected the search: the truncated string "Telluride Hybrid opti" does not exist ANYWHERE in the page HTML (both occurrences in the dump read the full "option"), so the DOM was never the source of the truncation. THE ACTUAL BUG: sanitize()\'s three email-quote-chain strippers each opened with `\\s*On ` under the /i flag. `\\s*` can match ZERO-WIDTH, so "On" was free to match the "on" INSIDE a word — here the tail of "opti|on " in the subject line itself. From that mid-word anchor the pattern found a comma within 80 chars and a "wrote:" within the next 200 (both genuinely present further down the note), and `[^]*$` then deleted everything from mid-subject to the end of the note. Reproduced byte-for-byte offline against the real note text: output is exactly the observed 35-char string. FIX: `\\bOn\\s+` on all three patterns — a real word boundary, so "option"/"season"/"reason"/"soon" can never anchor a quote strip again. VERIFIED 14/14, including against the sanitize() extracted from the shipped file itself, not a reimplementation: the real note now keeps its full body AND still correctly strips its own genuine trailing "On Fri, ... wrote:" chain; day-name and numeric-date chains still strip; the documented Megan Mckennie guard ("Are you open on Saturday?") still holds; a mid-word "season" trap is no longer misread; "Sent from Yahoo Mail" and "------ Original Message ------" strippers untouched; and the sibling 7/27 note on this same lead is BYTE-IDENTICAL to its pre-fix output (zero regression on the working path). SCOPE NOTE: this ran on every note through sanitize(), on every lead, at all five rooftops, for as long as these patterns have existed — any customer message whose text contained a mid-word "on" followed later by a comma and a "wrote:" lost everything after that point, silently. The two superseded mitigations (v9.7.526 textContent fallback, v9.7.527 incomplete-note annotation) are LEFT IN PLACE deliberately: both are harmless, both address real adjacent failure modes that could still occur independently, and the incomplete-note annotation remains a correct safety net for genuinely unloaded notes. LESSON, the same one this codebase has now logged repeatedly: reproduce the exact observed output offline before theorizing about a mechanism — two builds were spent on plausible-but-wrong theories that a single offline run of the real function against the real string would have ruled out immediately. node --check clean both builds. Builds on v9.7.527-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.527-dev (DEV. INCOMPLETE-NOTE DETECTION — second attempt at the Daniel Boyd shape (Community Kia Baytown, 8/5), and the v9.7.526 fix confirmed insufficient by a second live regrab: a fresh _lpDumpPrompt() capture showed the SAME "Subject: Re:A Telluride Hybrid opti" truncation, and this time BOTH .innerText and .textContent read the identical 35 characters — disproving the innerText-layout theory for this specific note, since if both DOM-reading properties agree, the full text genuinely is not in the live DOM yet at read time, and no property choice can recover content that was never there. Two manual page saves (vinsolutions-dump 85 and 86, byte-identical, taken across two separate sessions) both have the FULL text present in their static HTML, while Lead Pro's automated GRAB LEAD gets the truncated version both times — consistent with VinSolutions lazy-rendering note bodies in a virtualized/scroll-triggered list that a human naturally triggers by browsing before saving, and an instant automated scrape does not. A real scroll-simulation fix needs live verification to get the timing right and is NOT shipped here — what ships now is detect-and-flag: an "Email reply" note with a Subject: line but no By: line anywhere is structurally, definitionally incomplete for this note type (every genuinely-loaded example in every log this session has "By: <name>" as its second field, working or broken). Annotates the transcript entry IN PLACE at the exact site it enters the transcript — same proven pattern as the repeated-choice-question fix (v9.7.507/508) — telling the model explicitly not to treat an incomplete note as the customer having said nothing, and to ask the customer to confirm/resend if the note looks recent and relevant. New [LP INCOMPLETE-NOTE DIAG] logs the note title/date/content whenever this fires, so future occurrences (and whether the root-cause scroll fix is still needed) stay observable. VERIFIED 5/5: the actual broken note detected, the actual working sibling note NOT flagged, an outbound email never flagged regardless of shape, a non-email note title never flagged, a normal note without a Subject: prefix never flagged. Root cause (lazy-rendering / virtualized notes list) remains UNCONFIRMED and UNFIXED — this is a mitigation, not a fix; flagged clearly to Gil as the next real step, pending a cheap manual test (scroll fully through the notes panel before hitting GRAB LEAD) to confirm the hypothesis before any scroll-simulation code gets written blind. node --check clean both builds. Builds on v9.7.526-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.526-dev (DEV. INNERTEXT LAYOUT-DEPENDENCY TRUNCATION — root cause found via the new _lpDumpPrompt() diagnostic (v9.7.525). Live incident, Daniel Boyd (Community Kia Baytown, 8/5): his one explicit correction — "Pre-owned vehicles preferably" — never reached the model at all. The real 14,204-char prompt showed this note's transcript entry as just "Subject: Re:A Telluride Hybrid opti" — cut off mid-word, before even "By:". Raw page HTML confirmed byte-for-byte: the full content IS present in the DOM, and structurally IDENTICAL in shape to a sibling note nine days earlier that came through completely intact — same customer, same email client, same <div class="...extra-buttons">Subject: ...</div><div>By: ...</div>[body] shape. Since DOM shape isn't the variable, rendering STATE is the likely one: the note-scraping loop reads .innerText, which requires the browser to have actually computed layout for an element and silently returns partial/empty text for anything not fully rendered at read time (collapsed, off-screen in a virtualized panel) — VinSolutions' Notes & History is exactly the kind of long scrollable list that plausibly virtualizes older entries. .textContent reads the raw DOM tree unconditionally, immune to this. FIX is self-healing, not a wholesale switch: read both .innerText and .textContent for each note's content element, use whichever is longer, but ONLY past a real threshold (20+ chars AND 30%+ longer) — a tiny gap is more likely hidden UI chrome textContent picks up that innerText correctly excludes on every ordinary note, and substituting on any difference would trade a rare severe-truncation bug for a constant low-grade one. New [LP CONTENT-TRUNCATION DIAG] logs every time the fallback actually fires, naming the note title/date and the character gap — this failure class is now OBSERVABLE going forward instead of silently shipping a gutted transcript entry. Verified the threshold correctly triggers on the real observed numbers (innerText 35 chars vs textContent 460 — both the absolute-gap and ratio checks clear by a wide margin) and would NOT trigger on a normal few-character UI-chrome gap. TRADE-OFF STATED PLAINLY: textContent doesn't insert the line-break separation innerText adds between sibling block elements, so a recovered note may read slightly more run-together — cosmetic cost, never a content-loss one. Applied at the PRIMARY note-scraping loop that feeds the actual conversation transcript (content.js untouched, scraperVersion unchanged); ~12 other .innerText reads of this same selector elsewhere in the file serve narrower diagnostic purposes (schedule detection, opt-out scanning) and carry the same latent risk, flagged but not touched in this pass — fixing the confirmed live incident first rather than a sweeping unverified change. node --check clean both builds. Builds on v9.7.525-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.525-dev (DEV. FULL PROMPT DUMP DIAGNOSTIC — every prior diagnostic question this file could answer about "was fact X actually in the prompt" required either re-deriving it from byte counts or a manual DevTools capture that STILL only recovers what was already logged — and nothing had ever logged the real, complete system+user prompt text, only a length plus a ~100-300 char preview. Confirmed live: Daniel Boyd's (Community Kia Baytown, 8/5) explicit correction couldn't be diagnosed at all without this — no amount of "Preserve log" could recover text the code never printed in the first place. Adds window._lpLastSystemPrompt / window._lpLastUserPrompt (overwritten each generation, bounded — not a growing log) and window._lpDumpPrompt(), a DevTools-callable helper that saves both to a downloadable .txt file on demand, same Blob/createObjectURL pattern as the existing backup-export feature. Deliberately NOT auto-downloaded on every generation (would spam a file per lead for every agent) — memory-only until explicitly called, wrapped in try/catch so a capture failure can never block a real generation. This diagnostic is what found the v9.7.526 bug directly above one build later. node --check clean both builds. Builds on v9.7.524-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.524-dev (DEV. NO VOI, CUSTOMER NAMED THE MODEL THEMSELVES. Live incident, Chris Faerber (Community Toyota Baytown, 8/5): his lead-received text reads "...What specials do yall have on new and used Limited Grand Highlanders" -- the model is right there in his own words -- but VinSolutions' own VOI field on this lead is empty ([LP VARIANT-MISMATCH DIAG] confirmed VOI:""), and the STORE INCENTIVE block's _lvInc was built ONLY from that structured field (d.vehicle || d.vehicleRaw). With no VOI, [LP INCENTIVE DIAG] logged "no lead vehicle known -- cannot match" and the whole matching gate never ran -- even after the SAME session's Toyota window.__SSR_STATE__ parser fix (see v9.7.523 below) put two genuinely correct Grand Highlander lines into the freshly-corrected incentive cache (4.99% APR/48mo, $469/mo 36mo lease). Same root shape as the original "0% APR for 36 months" fabrication one level up the chain: no real fact ever reaches the model, so it invents one. FIX: new _lpGuessModelFromCustomerText(text, vfc) derives a fallback model name from the customer's OWN inbound message, checked against THIS DEALER'S OWN currently-cached incentive model names -- deliberately not a separate static list to maintain, and structurally unable to ever match a name with no real current data behind it. Longest-name-first, same protection the Data Tool's own chunker already uses against the Highlander/Grand-Highlander collision; trailing s? handles ordinary pluralization ("Highlanders", "Camrys", "4Runners") -- confirmed necessary, since Chris's own message pluralizes the model and the fix does not fire without it. Wired in as the LAST fallback in the existing _lvInc assignment (d.vehicle || d.vehicleRaw || this || '') -- a real VOI on file always wins, this only ever fires when the CRM field is genuinely empty. CONFIRMED WORKING LIVE (log82): Gil manually set VOI to "2026 Toyota Grand Highlander" and regrabbed as a manual workaround before this fix shipped -- produced the correct "4.99% APR for 48 months or a $469/month 36-month lease with $3,999 due at signing" message, proving the mechanism; this change automates that same manual step for the next lead shaped like this one. VERIFIED 9/9 against the real dealer 6189 incentive cache shape and Chris's exact real message: correct resolution with empty VOI, a real VOI on file is never overridden, missing/absent incentive cache doesn't throw, an unrelated message produces no false positive, three separate plural forms (Camrys/4Runners/Tacomas) resolve to their singular base model without bleeding into a different real model (4Runner i-FORCE MAX), and the Grand Highlander/Grand Highlander Hybrid variant pair disambiguates correctly. node --check clean both builds; function body byte-identical between DEV and COMMERCIAL. Builds on v9.7.523-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.523-dev (DEV. ON-PREMISE FALSE POSITIVE — VEHICLE arriving "on the lot" read as the CUSTOMER being on the lot. Live incident, Robin Newman (Community Honda Lafayette, 8/5, log78): a call note reads "...The next one due in is a Meteorite Gray due to arrive around 8/11. She said to let her know when we get one on the lot." — the v9.7.493 on-premise detector's two independent regex checks (a place-token and a subject/verb-token, tested anywhere in the note with nothing requiring them to describe the same fact) both matched, but in two unrelated clauses — the place-token from the VEHICLE's own arrival, the verb-token from an entirely different clause about the customer's own request. Result: LP told her "I see you're here... what are you standing in front of?" while she was on the phone at home in Ville Platte. FIX: added a targeted exclusion for the specific construction this collided with — the standard agent shorthand for "notify when inventory arrives" (get/have/receive/see/got + one/it/a/another/the + "on the lot") — where the object of "on the lot" is the vehicle, not the customer. Proximity windowing was considered and rejected: the false clause and the true trigger sit in the SAME sentence here, so distance alone doesn't discriminate; the construction itself is the correct discriminator. VERIFIED 7/7: Robin's exact case now suppressed, Armando's original real on-premise case (the incident this mechanism was built for, v9.7.493) still fires, genuine presence phrasing ("pulled up and is on the lot," "walked the lot") still fires, a variant vehicle-arrival phrasing ("text him when we have one on the lot") also correctly suppressed, unrelated notes stay silent. node --check clean both builds. Builds on v9.7.521-dev — branches from the plain baseline, NOT from the separately-built-but-undeployed v9.7.522/520 wrong-rooftop-signature-guard work, which still needs to be merged in separately before either ships.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.521-dev (DEV. DELIVERED CACHE-BOUNDARY SENTINEL — pairs with Worker v7.42 to make every persona/agent/store share ONE cached prefix. WHY: Worker v7.41 added a GPT-5.6 explicit prompt_cache_breakpoint at the END of the system message. That worked, but it cached a SEPARATE prefix per persona/agent/store combination — live data showed sysChars varying 19,629-22,010 across requests, only 35% of requests hitting a warm prefix (43 of 123), and roughly 80 cache writes bought for ~43 reads. At GPT-5.6's 1.25x cache-write rate that ratio is only barely net-positive. The static head of buildSystemPrompt (~4,449 tokens / ~17,800 chars) is byte-identical for every persona, agent, store and day, and the file already marked exactly where it ends — but ONLY as a JS SOURCE COMMENT ("VARIABLE SUFFIX (changes per persona/agent/store — cache boundary here)"), which is never delivered, so the Worker had nothing to split on and could not see the boundary. THIS CHANGE emits a real sentinel line into the delivered string at that exact point. The Worker splits there, places the breakpoint after the static head, and STRIPS the sentinel so the model never sees it. SAFETY PROPERTIES, all verified rather than assumed: (1) the sentinel is stripped on the Luna path when it splits, AND separately stripped on the FALLBACK/EMERGENCY tiers, which do not take the splitting path — that leak was caught in testing before shipping and would have put literal marker text into the system prompt on exactly the degraded path where the prompt most needs to be clean; (2) deploy order does not matter — a Worker without the split still works with this build, and an extension without the sentinel still works with Worker v7.42 (it falls back to v7.41 end-of-system behaviour), so there is no broken window in either direction; (3) the sentinel is deliberately NOT phrased as a directive and sits immediately before a section header, so if it ever did survive it reads as an inert marker rather than an instruction; (4) the Worker guards the 1,024-token cacheable minimum and falls back to a single block rather than emitting a breakpoint the service would ignore. VERIFIED with 9 assertions on the real payload-construction logic: two different personas/stores produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL first block (the shared prefix — this is the whole point) while their variable blocks correctly differ; the sentinel never appears in any payload reaching the model; the regen path still shares the same static head AND retains its constraint text; an older extension without the sentinel falls back cleanly; a short static head falls back to a single block; and the fallback tier receives a plain-string system message with no cache params and no sentinel. EXPECTED EFFECT: the WARM RATE should rise well above 35%. cached_tokens as a share of total stays bounded by the same per-request ceiling (~22-49%, system tokens over total) because the per-lead prompt legitimately dominates — this improves how OFTEN the prefix is warm, not the ceiling itself. node --check clean both builds. Pairs: DEV v9.7.521-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.518 / Worker v7.42. Builds on v9.7.520-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.520-dev (DEV. [LP MERGE DIAG] FALSE POSITIVE CORRECTED — and the sold-signal correlated-merge hypothesis is now DISCONFIRMED by real data, not merely unproven. The observe-only diagnostic added in v9.7.516 fired sameLeadDisagreement:true on three separate grabs in log77 (Honda Accord lead 2018558062, Kia K4 2041029799, Toyota Tundra 2013954811) and appended its own verdict: "<<< CONFIRMS the correlated-merge hypothesis — bundle these atomically". It was wrong. All three grabs had the IDENTICAL shape: exactly ONE frame carrying the real vehicle panel with invWarn:true, alongside three same-autoLeadId frames with veh:"" and invWarn:false. Those are EMPTY SHELLS — they report false because they have no vehicle panel to warn about, not because they disagree about one. Absence of data is not conflict. The original comparison bucketed purely by autoLeadId with NO panel check, so any panel-bearing frame sitting next to its own shells tripped it — which is the normal frame shape on essentially every grab, meaning the alarm fired on healthy sessions. WHY THIS MATTERED MORE THAN A NOISY LOG LINE: the merge was already correct the whole time (inventoryWarning, inventoryWarningFromNotes, vehiclePendingSale and the sub-fields have been _voiFromPanel-gated in BOTH passes since v9.7.507/508, so shells cannot contribute either way and the panel frame's value wins). Acting on the embedded "CONFIRMS" verdict would have meant bundling working code and plausibly undermining that gating — a wrong fix shipped on autopilot, on the sold signal, which is the highest-consequence path in the system. FIX: the disagreement check now compares ONLY panel-bearing frames (r._voiFromPanel), which are the only ones whose vehicle-status reading is anchored to an actual vehicle and the only ones the merge will accept. Per-frame rows now include panel:true/false and the line reports panelBearingFrames so the frame shape is readable at a glance. VERDICT TEXT REMOVED ENTIRELY — a diagnostic that asserts its own conclusion is exactly how a wrong fix gets shipped without anyone re-deriving it; it now reports the observation and leaves the judgement to a human reading the shape. APPLIED TO BOTH BUILDS, deliberately: the misleading verdict was live in COMMERCIAL too, so every production grab was emitting a confident wrong recommendation into agent-visible logs. Parity is also the standing convention here, and this codebase has been bitten before by logic living in one build and not the other. VERIFIED 4 assertions: the exact log77 shape is now quiet (was a false positive); a GENUINE conflict — two panel-bearing frames on one lead with different invWarn — still fires; two panel frames agreeing stays quiet; an all-shells grab stays quiet. node --check clean both builds. Pairs: DEV v9.7.520-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.516 / Worker v7.40. Builds on v9.7.519-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.519-dev (DEV. IN-TRANSIT SUPPRESSES SOLD — the prompt was carrying two contradictory directives simultaneously. LIVE INCIDENT: Raul Meza (Community Toyota Baytown, 2026 RAV4, lead 2061535887) got "The original unit has sold, but I can help match you to a comparable RAV4, including an inbound unit that may be secured before arrival." The model did NOT hallucinate — the assembled prompt contained BOTH the correct scenario TASK ("This vehicle is IN TRANSIT — the VIN is confirmed, meaning it exists and is assigned") AND a false closeOverride ("SITUATION: The vehicle they inquired about has sold. Pivot to comparable alternatives"). It stitched them together, which is the only coherent thing to do with a contradiction. EVIDENCE THE SOLD CLAIM WAS FALSE: every merged sold flag was false (inventoryWarning, inventoryWarningFromNotes, vehiclePendingSale, _rgxInventoryWarning, _pdInventoryWarning), and dump 81 confirmed PageData Status:"A", InventoryType:"N", VIN assigned, and no "no longer in your active inventory" text anywhere on the page. CULPRIT: sc.vehicleSold — the FIFTH, independent ctx-regex sold scan first identified in v9.7.509, which re-tests a ~7,000-char assembled context with greedy alternatives (/sorry.*(?:vehicle|car|it).*sold/, /no longer available/). v9.7.509 added _lpInvConfirmedAvailable specifically to override this scan, but that override is STRUCTURALLY UNABLE to protect an in-transit unit: it works by matching the VOI's stock number against the live inventory feed, and an inbound vehicle has no stock number yet — logged here as stockNum:"(none)", confirmedAvailable:false. So the single guard built for this failure could never fire on the case that most needs it. FIX: a vehicle whose context explicitly carries "vehicle status: in transit" cannot simultaneously be sold-and-gone — an assigned VIN is positive existence evidence, the same class of proof as an inventory match. Deliberately narrow: suppression requires the explicit in-transit status, so a genuinely sold vehicle with no in-transit marker still reports sold unchanged. Inlined rather than referencing s.vehicleInTransit because that field is assigned on the NEXT line (order dependency). Diagnostic logs when the suppression is active and why the inventory override could not apply. VERIFIED 4 cases: Raul's shape (in-transit + spurious regex match) now false where it was true; a genuinely sold vehicle with no transit marker stays true; in-transit with no sold text stays false; sold-plus-inventory-confirmed stays false. ALSO CONFIRMED THIS ROUND, NOT YET FIXED — conversationBrief is built from GENERAL/AGENT notes only. Raul had 31 notes, all texts and system logs, so brief was NONE and notes:0; Gil added ONE general note and it jumped to notes:1327. That matters because v9.7.516 repointed the context guards (_lpCtxNotes) at conversationBrief, so on any fresh web lead with no agent notes — common — those guards read an empty string. Same dead-read shape as the original M1 bug, reached structurally instead of by a naming error. Deliberately NOT fixed here: the correct source (notes-only vs notes+transcript) changes what sellerIntent, the vehicle check and the totaled-trade guard each scan, and after the v9.7.516 inverted-slice lesson that wants its own pass rather than riding along with a customer-facing fix. IMPORTANT READ ON THE TEST: adding the note made the OUTPUT correct but did NOT fix the cause — the false SITUATION line was still present in the good generation too; the model simply had enough real context (notes:1327 vs notes:0) to weight the correct TASK over it. On a thin-notes lead it would have resurfaced. node --check clean both builds. Pairs: DEV v9.7.519-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.515 / Worker v7.40. Builds on v9.7.518-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.518-dev (DEV. A NEW LEAD IS A NEW OPT-IN OPPORTUNITY — stale opt-out no longer produces a farewell on a fresh inquiry. LIVE INCIDENT: Raul Meza (Community Toyota Baytown, 2026 RAV4) submitted a NEW lead and got "thanks for considering Community Toyota Baytown. I understand, and I'll leave it there." MY FIRST HYPOTHESIS WAS WRONG and is recorded so it is not re-tried: I assumed the exit scan's time-filtered filteredTranscript.slice(0,5) was picking up old history, and was about to bound it at the CURRENT LEAD marker. The relayed [LP EXIT DIAG] — visible for the first time BECAUSE of the v9.7.516 diagnostic relay that un-stranded it from the page console — named the actual path outright: "hasExitSignal fired via smsOptOutIsExit (SMS STOP treated as full exit)", with isSmsOptOut:true rawStopSignal:true. Raul texted STOP on a PRIOR lead 600+ days ago; the exit scan was never involved. TWO ROOT CAUSES, both fixed. (1) AGE SCRAPE FAILURE: the guard that should have caught this is hasNewLeadToday = _leadAgeScraped && leadAgeDays === 0, but [LP AGE DIAG] showed leadAgeDays NOT scraped (Created text: "Status:") — labelValue('Created') grabbed an adjacent DOM cell, the "(Nd)" regex was the ONLY parse path, and age fell back to UNKNOWN, silently switching OFF every fresh-lead branch. Same failure observed on Chris Faerber's grab, so this is not a one-off. Added a PageData fallback: _pdCreatedH (LeadCreatedUTC) is already parsed upstream and is not DOM-adjacency dependent, so when the regex misses, age is recovered from it and logged as RECOVERED. (2) "CREATED TODAY" IS TOO NARROW anyway — a lead submitted two days ago is still a new inquiry relative to an opt-out from 600 days back. Rather than widen to an arbitrary date window, used the CURRENT LEAD marker, whose partitioning is now VERIFIED working in both directions by real grabs: Raul's bounded 5,630 chars of history down to 23 for the current lead, and Chris Faerber correctly had NO marker because his record holds exactly one lead (dump 79 confirmed a single AutoLeadID and zero lead-received notes). New rule: if the opt-out evidence appears ONLY below the marker it belongs to a prior lead and the new inquiry supersedes it; if it appears in the current lead's own segment it is live and still escalates, unchanged. COMPLIANCE, DELIBERATELY PRESERVED: this suppresses only the EXIT/farewell FRAMING, it does NOT re-enable the SMS channel. isSmsOptOutOnly becomes TRUE in this case, so SMS stays suppressed and the draft goes out on email/voicemail — the customer gets a real response to their new inquiry instead of a goodbye, and we still do not text an opted-out number. Restoring SMS itself remains gated on an actual opt-in record (hasRecentReoptIn). Added [LP OPT-OUT SCOPE DIAG] (relayed, popup-visible) logging isSmsOptOut, marker presence, optOutFromPriorLeadOnly, hasNewLeadToday, leadAgeDays and whether it was scraped, and the resulting smsOptOutIsExit. VERIFIED with 5 functional assertions on the real logic: Raul's exact shape yields no farewell with SMS still suppressed; a STOP on the CURRENT lead still escalates to exit (compliance case, unchanged); the Faerber shape with no marker behaves exactly as before; a lead created today is still handled by the existing guard; and a lead with no opt-out is untouched. node --check clean both builds. Pairs: DEV v9.7.518-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.514 / Worker v7.40. Builds on v9.7.517-dev.)
