// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.503-dev (DEV. ANTI-RESTATE WAS HANDING THE MODEL ITS OWN PRIOR MISTAKE AS THE THING TO VARY FROM. Gil: "updated notes should have steered the messaging but it still looks lost" -- on the SAME David Matthies lead the v9.7.501 THIN VISIT DATA fix was built for. That fix is correct and untouched by this build; the failure is a SEPARATE, unrelated mechanism (ANTI-RESTATE, v9.7.279/435) that independently surfaces "YOUR LAST SUBSTANTIVE MESSAGE TO THIS CUSTOMER (do not restate it)" to stop the model from literally repeating a prior pitch. On this lead, the message being shown back as that anchor WAS the pre-fix voicemail script -- "on your visit with Sean Bailey, the credit/kiosk step was started but it didn\'t finish" -- because it had already gone out and gotten logged as a real outbound note BEFORE v9.7.501 landed. The anti-restate instruction only bans literal repetition ("same pitch, same options, same ask IS a restate") -- nothing told the model the THEME of that prior message was itself wrong, so it produced a reworded VARIATION that kept the same subject: "the credit step started, but wasn\'t completed... come in tomorrow (9:15 AM or 10:30 AM)." Confirmed via [LP ANTI-RESTATE DIAG] hasSubstantiveOutbound:true WILL FIRE:true and 3 consecutive outbound touches in the same log. Exactly the Civic/trade error-propagation shape from earlier this week, wearing different clothes: LP\'s own prior output, once logged, becomes "the record" the next generation builds from -- except this time the propagation vector is the anti-restate mechanism, not the raw transcript note, so the v9.7.501 fix (which only addresses the raw Showroom Visit note) never touched it. FIX: right where the anti-restate anchor gets pushed, check whether that prior message itself contains the same banned mechanical/kiosk language ("kiosk", "credit app/application step", "visit not completed", "credit report ... step"); if so, add an explicit correction -- that message was itself a mistake, built on CRM tracking noise, do not vary or continue that theme, move on entirely to a general how-did-it-go check-in. ALSO NOTED: like the SOLD-pivot fallback fixed in v9.7.502, this whole ANTI-RESTATE block had NO diagnostic printing the actual anchor text, so the exact propagation could not be confirmed from the log alone -- worth a future pass adding one, flagged rather than built here since this fix does not depend on it. VERIFIED 7/7: fires on David\'s exact real prior message and on the exact bad text that was actually generated; stays silent on five genuine substantive-message shapes (pricing, appointment, trade-value, unrelated credit-union mention, unrelated "application" mention) so normal anti-restate behavior for every other lead is unaffected. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.502-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.502-dev (DEV. NO-INCENTIVE COMPARABLE FALLBACK: LOG IT, AND STOP THE VAGUE-PROMISE ESCAPE. Prompted by Gil asking whether LP can search inventory and make suggestions in these situations -- answer: yes, several real mechanisms exist (SOLD-pivot comparables with optional incentive pairing, alternate-condition CPO/new swap, off-franchise stock check, a standalone similar-vehicles fact) -- but tracing a specific live case (Keijo Armstrong, Honda Lafayette, Sorento LX sold) found the capability worked and the output still failed to use it. The SOLD-pivot search found one real match, a 2022 Kia Sorento SX-Prestige (Sparkling Silver), correctly declined to pair it with an incentive (used/CPO, incentive pairing is new-car-only), and per the code should have fallen into the no-price PIVOT TO THESE branch -- which names the real unit directly. Yet the generated SMS read "I\'ll line up a couple comparable Sorento options that match that so you can review what\'s actually available" -- a vague future-tense promise instead of naming the unit already in hand. UNLIKE its sibling incentive-paired branch (which logs "leading with incentive on..."), this fallback had NO diagnostic of its own, so there was no way to confirm from a log whether the named unit actually reached the prompt -- a real gap in observability, not just a hunch. FIX: (1) added a matching [LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG] line logging exactly what this branch names, closing that gap for future sessions; (2) the directive itself now explicitly forbids the vague-promise pattern observed -- "I\'ll line up some options," "let me check what we have," "I\'ll put together a list" -- and requires naming at least one real unit specifically, since the model has the confirmed unit in hand, not something to go check on. Price framing (do not quote/lead with price) is unchanged. VERIFIED 7/7: all three banned phrasings present verbatim, the naming requirement is explicit, price guidance intact, the new diagnostic is distinct from and does not disturb the existing incentive-paired diagnostic. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.501-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.501-dev (DEV. THIN/NO SHOWROOM-VISIT-NOTES FALLBACK. Live: David Matthies (Toyota Baytown, Walk-in, 5 notes total). The generated message read "Sean Bailey started the credit/kiosk step on your visit but it didn't finish. We can wrap it up by phone, or you can come in" -- Gil: the notes are thin to nonexistent, this message is not relevant and does not move the deal forward, and with little to no notes to work with the model should keep it general -- how did the visit go, any questions. ROOT CAUSE: David's only showroom-visit record is pure CRM system tracking metadata -- "By: Sean Bailey Showroom visit started at 07/25/2026 7:58 PM lasting 2 days Visit not completed! End Visit Credit App Kiosk Credit Report" -- a visit timer and kiosk-workflow state, not a manager's account of what happened. decodeShowroomVisit() correctly finds none of its known process codes or longhand phrases in that text (no NA/WA/DM/MT/FT/DE, no "needs assessment", "test drive", etc.) and returns an empty decoded summary -- verified against the ACTUAL content-element text pulled directly from the dump's DOM (not a title+content merge, which was double-checked and corrected during testing). But the RAW note still reaches the model verbatim in the transcript, because its presence is required to satisfy the existing "only reference a visit if a [SHOWROOM VISIT] note confirms it" rule -- and with nothing telling the model that raw text is mechanical noise, it extracted the only concrete-sounding fragments available ("credit/kiosk step", "didn't finish") and built the entire message around resuming a backend workflow step. FIX: when decodeShowroomVisit() returns nothing, the model is now told so explicitly and redirected -- THIN VISIT DATA: the record is CRM tracking metadata with no manager narrative (no vehicle discussed, no objection, no next step); do not build the message around backend/system language ("kiosk", "credit app step", "visit not completed", timestamps); keep it general -- acknowledge the visit, ask how it went, invite questions. This is the honest low-pressure default Gil described for exactly this data shape. VERIFIED 4/4 against the real content-element text extracted directly from David's dump (not a reconstructed approximation): the fallback fires on his exact note; a genuine narrative note (codes + free text) still decodes and surfaces normally; a second thin-timer variant without kiosk/credit wording also correctly triggers the fallback; and a code-free but genuinely substantive manager note ("wants to talk to her credit union first") still surfaces via the existing freeText path, confirming the fix only fires on true information-empty cases, never on real signal. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.500-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.500-dev (DEV. NAME-FIELD PLACEHOLDER STRIP + CHAT-TRANSCRIPT NAME GUARD. Live: Riley (Kia Baytown, Gubagoo m-chat, 7/27) was greeted "Amelia" in both SMS and email. Gate eval showed the scraped name was "Riley n/a" for the ENTIRE session -- the scraper was correct throughout. VinSolutions had the literal string "n/a" as the last name (an agent's own placeholder entry), and the existing _lpLooksLikeName guard (v9.7.236, built to catch a NOTE accidentally left in the name field, e.g. "dont call after 5pm") only screens for instruction-phrases -- it has no concept of placeholder tokens, so "Riley n/a" (2 words, no digits, no instruction phrasing) sailed through untouched and reached the model as the customer's name. The model, given a name containing "n/a", apparently distrusted it and went hunting the transcript instead -- landing on "Amelia", the dealership's OWN GUBAGOO CHAT WIDGET introducing itself inside the embedded transcript in the lead-received note ("Guest: Looking for something / Amelia: Hi there! My name is Amelia, and I'm here to help..."). Gil's manual fix -- deleting "n/a" from the last name in VinSolutions and re-grabbing -- confirmed the mechanism exactly: clean name in, correct name out ("Riley"). FIX 1: rather than depend on a human catching every future instance of this in the CRM, placeholder tokens (n/a in all its punctuation variants, TBD, unknown, none, null, test, noname/no name) are now stripped from the name field as WHOLE WORDS before the existing looks-like-a-name check runs, so "Riley n/a" -> "Riley" automatically -- a real, usable name -- rather than falling through to the existing _rejectedName path, which discards the whole name down to a generic greeting; that path remains fully intact and unchanged for its original purpose (an actual instruction like "dont call after 5pm" still gets rejected exactly as before). If stripping placeholder tokens leaves nothing (a name that WAS purely "N/A N/A"), the cleaned empty string correctly falls through to the existing reject path rather than being treated as valid. FIX 2, a general-purpose companion for any FUTURE lead where the name field is empty or unusable: a new directive tells the model to use ONLY the customer name supplied in this prompt and never a name found inside an embedded chat transcript in the notes -- explicitly naming the "Guest: ... / [Name]: Hi there! My name is [Name]" shape as the WIDGET/BOT introducing itself, with the customer's own lines always labeled "Guest" in that format -- and to use a generic greeting rather than adopt a chat agent's self-introduction as the customer's name. VERIFIED 18/18: the real "Riley n/a" case cleans correctly and the result passes the existing name validator; every placeholder variant (N/A, spaced "n / a", NA, Unknown, TBD, None, Test, and a leading-placeholder form) strips correctly; an all-placeholder name reduces to empty and correctly falls through to the existing reject path; and -- the tests that mattered most -- real names that CONTAIN placeholder-like substrings (Nadia, Natasha, Ana Garcia, Nathan Cole, John Testerman) are confirmed UNTOUCHED, since the strip matches whole words only. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.499-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.499-dev (DEV. REVERT + CORRECT FIX: v9.7.498's zip directive contradicted the existing DISTANCE HARD RULE. Gil caught it immediately: "I don't want the model reverting to naming the distance from the dealerships again. We had adjusted that thinking." He was right -- the new "CUSTOMER ZIP ON FILE" line told the model to "acknowledge the drive and make the visit worth it", which is the exact behavior the DISTANCE HARD RULE block (in-state, non-remote buyers: NEVER state miles or drive, use the fact SILENTLY, forbidden-phrasing list, strip any prior agent message that named the distance) was built to prevent. Two directives touching the same fact, one silent-only and one "acknowledge it" -- a live contradiction the model would have to arbitrate on every generation. THE STANDALONE DIRECTIVE IS REMOVED ENTIRELY. What replaces it is not a new prompt line at all: `customerZip` -- which already feeds `_isLocalByZip` -> `isDistanceBuyer` -> the correct silent-use system two thousand lines downstream -- was being extracted by a bare regex matching "State, ZIP" in page text, the same fragile shape as every other scrape bug fixed this week. It is now a hybrid: PageData Buyer.PostalCode primary (confirmed populated correctly back in Stage 2), the regex kept as fallback for leads where PageData is absent. Zero new prompt text, zero contradiction risk -- PageData just makes the EXISTING, ALREADY-CORRECT distance system more reliable. The now-redundant `buyerZip` field is removed from the scraper's return object; `isHotLead` and the VOI-count line from v9.7.498 are untouched, since neither touches distance framing. VERIFIED: the literal string "acknowledge the drive" no longer appears in any live directive (only in this changelog's own description of what was removed); the DISTANCE HARD RULE block's forbidden-phrasing list is byte-identical to before; customerZip is confirmed PD-primary with the regex fallback intact. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.498-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.498-dev (DEV. SELF-CONSISTENCY PASS + THREE GRADUATED FACTS. THE BIG ONE: LP had no memory of what it had already told a customer. Steph Montoya (Honda Baytown) is the clearest case of the week -- LP said the 2023 K5 was "here and ready for you to see" on 6/24, said it "has sold" on 6/29, said "already sold" on 7/2, and on 7/27 asked whether she is "still focused on the K5 GT". Three separate patches this week -- the trade/VOI conflation guard, the arc-aware SOLD pivot, and the in-place record correction -- were all the same underlying problem treated locally: every generation reads the thread fresh with no notion of what LP has ALREADY ASSERTED, so it contradicts or re-announces its own claims. Rather than a fourth local rule, the claims themselves are now extracted and put in front of the model as its own commitments. During note iteration, OUTBOUND messages are scanned for three claim types -- availability, sold, and appointment confirmation -- keeping the newest of each with its date. Popup-side they render as a WHAT WE HAVE ALREADY TOLD THIS CUSTOMER block, oldest first, with the rule that the MOST RECENT claim may not be silently contradicted: if the newest thing we told them was that the vehicle sold, the draft may not now treat it as available, ask whether they are "still interested", or invite them to come see it. Genuine changes are allowed but must be SAID -- "it actually came back available" -- so a reversal is visible and honest rather than accidental. When an availability claim is followed by a sold claim, an extra line names the sold message as the standing position, which is exactly Steph's shape. OUTBOUND DISCRIMINATION MATTERS AND WAS CAUGHT IN TEST: the first title filter accepted anything containing "email", which made "Email reply from prospect" -- the CUSTOMER asking "is it still available?" -- register as OUR claim; the shipped filter requires an outbound marker AND rejects inbound/from-prospect/received/reply-from titles, verified 7/7 across real title shapes. Claim extraction verified against Steph's actual notes: both claims captured, the 7/2 sold correctly identified as newest, the available-then-sold conflict detected, and a bare check-in note producing nothing. THREE SMALLER GRADUATIONS, all previously captured-but-unused: HotLead (the CRM's own priority scoring) now tells the model to match that energy without ever telling the customer they are "flagged"; VehiclesOfInterest is used when POPULATED rather than only as an authoritative empty -- more than one means the customer is comparing, and writing as though there is a single unit is its own form of not listening; and Buyer.PostalCode is supplied as a plain fact to support the hand-set Distance Buyer flag, explicitly without inventing a mileage or drive time. DoNotCall was surveyed and deliberately NOT built at Gil's direction. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.497-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.497-dev (DEV. TWO FIXES FROM THREE REPORTED ISSUES -- the third turned out not to be an LP error. (1) SOONEST-FIRST APPOINTMENTS: Travis Hartman (Honda Baytown, Monday 7/27 11:11 AM, store open another 8h49m) was offered "Saturday (8/1) or Monday (8/3)?" and "what day works best this week?" across several drafts, on a lead that could have walked in the same afternoon. Nothing violated the existing rules -- they bar CLOSED days and times already past, nothing more -- and the CALENDAR REFERENCE lists options out to next Tuesday with no preference among them, so the model chose freely and drifted a week out. The open-store status line now states the preference positively: there is usable time TODAY, so today is the default; lead with a today time or today-plus-tomorrow, and do not reach for later-week framing unless the customer asked to push it out, named a later day, or the arc shows they cannot come sooner. Stated as a preference rather than a prohibition so it cannot fight the closed-day rule. (2) NEWER LEAD EXISTS: David Cornell (Kia Baytown) has an ACTIVE 6/25 KMF LUV Program lead with no trade, while a NEWER 7/24 Tradepending lead on the same customer sits in the selector marked Duplicate -- and Tradepending is a trade submission by definition, so the trade information LP most needs is on the lead LP does not read. Gil asked whether the newer duplicate could be checked for information the active lead lacks. DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: reading the sibling lead's data. Its panel is not rendered on this page, and scraping sibling frames is precisely the cross-lead contamination the v9.7.480 ID-CONFLICT rejection exists to prevent -- that guard should not be undermined to save a click. What the LeadSelector row already gives for free is enough to be useful: status, created date and source. When a sibling lead post-dates the active one, a directive now states that a newer lead exists, names its date/source/status, forbids inventing what it contains, and -- when the source is trade-shaped (Tradepending/KBB/ICO/appraisal) -- warns that any "no trade on file" state here is INCOMPLETE rather than settled, so the message never tells a customer we have nothing on their trade when a newer lead says otherwise. It also steers the wording to stay true regardless of what the unseen lead holds. VERIFIED 8/8 against the REAL selector rows from both dumps: David's 7/24 Tradepending Duplicate is detected with status and source, his older Sold lead is ignored, and Travis -- whose siblings are all older -- correctly produces nothing; absent siblings, older-only siblings, and a missing active-created-date all resolve to silence rather than a guess. THIRD ISSUE, INVESTIGATED AND NOT A BUG: Claudelle Anderle was reported as LP wrongly saying she is awaiting an insurance settlement on the VOI. LP wrote "insurance QUOTE", not settlement, and the link to the HR-V was not LP's invention -- her 7/18 inbound says only "Waiting on insurance quote" with no vehicle attached, and it was the 7/22 agent text from Bennett Johnson that established the linkage: "Were you able to get insurance quotes for the vehicle you are inquiring about?" LP is faithfully continuing the agent's own framing. If that framing is wrong the correction has to start with a human note on the lead; no code change is warranted on the evidence. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.496-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.496-dev (DEV. STOP ARGUING WITH THE TRANSCRIPT AND CORRECT IT IN PLACE. This is the fifth build against the Civic-trade error and the first that changes approach rather than wording. v9.7.491 through v9.7.495 each added a DIRECTIVE telling the model not to call the vehicle of interest a trade, and each diagnosis was correct -- v9.7.492 fixed a branch that matched the VOI itself and argued FOR the error, v9.7.495 closed a nickname loophole where the ban named the full "2024 Honda Civic Sedan Sport" while the model wrote "the Civic" -- yet the error survived every one. The reason is structural: Talia's notes say "trade value/number for the Civic" repeatedly, and the model is doing precisely what it should, continuing the conversation as recorded. A directive sitting elsewhere in the prompt is a weaker signal than the thread\'s own repeated wording, so no amount of rephrasing the directive was ever going to win. FIX: annotate the offending note IN PLACE, where the model actually reads. Nothing is deleted and nothing is rewritten -- the original sentence stays intact and auditable -- a bracketed [RECORD CORRECTION: ...] is appended immediately after it, stating that the named vehicle is the VEHICLE OF INTEREST the customer wants to BUY, that the CRM holds no trade vehicle for them, that the note is factually wrong on that point, and that the correct phrasing is "your trade" with no vehicle name. The contradiction now travels with the line it corrects instead of competing with it from across the prompt. GATED HARD so it can never touch a legitimate note: PageData must be present AND positively report zero trade vehicles, AND the note must pair trade language with the VOI\'s own model name inside a short window (40 characters forward, 30 back) in either order. VERIFIED 9/9 against Talia\'s REAL notes -- the 7/27 voicemail is annotated; her out-the-door note (no trade word) and the generic "your trade and your numbers" note (no vehicle name) are both untouched; the original sentence is preserved verbatim; a real trade on file or absent PageData disables it entirely; Jayson\'s genuine "Trade in: 2019 Dodge Challenger R/T" is never corrected; reverse word order is caught; and a Civic-and-trade pairing separated by a long clause is deliberately left alone. Ships alongside the v9.7.495 nickname ban rather than replacing it -- the directive and the in-place correction now reinforce each other. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.495-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.495-dev (DEV. THE CIVIC-TRADE BAN HAD A NICKNAME LOOPHOLE. Gil: "still missing with the trade angle to Armando fix is good now" -- confirming the on-premise work is done ("Hey Armando, I see you are on our lot right now. I am sending someone out to you -- what spot are you by?") while the trade error survived a FOURTH build. v9.7.494 was loaded and the guard fired, and this time the branch selection was correct too. The failure is in the wording of the ban itself: it forbade writing "trade value for the <FULL VOI STRING>" -- i.e. "the 2024 Honda Civic Sedan Sport" -- while the model wrote "a trade value/number on the Civic" and "verify the Civic in person". Literal compliance with the prohibition as written, same factual error twice in one message, and the short name is precisely the form the corrupted prior notes use, so transcript momentum pushed straight through the gap. FIX: derive the SHORT model name from the VOI (year + make + model token, allowing a leading digit so 4Runner and 3-Series survive while requiring at least one letter so a stray number cannot become the banned nickname) and forbid it explicitly by example -- "a trade value on the Civic", "your Civic trade", "verify the Civic" are named as THE SAME ERROR as the full name. The directive also stops being a pure list of prohibitions and states the one approved construction: "your trade" or "your trade-in" with NO vehicle name attached at all, and "verify it in person" / "get eyes on it" when appraisal needs mentioning. Finally the transcript warning is hardened from a conditional to a statement of fact -- earlier notes in this thread ARE known to contain the error, they are WRONG, and continuing the conversation does not mean inheriting it. VERIFIED 10/10 on nickname extraction across every real VOI shape seen in the dumps: Civic, Sorento, EV6, Accord, F-150, 4Runner, Colorado, Maxima, plus safe degradation to empty when the VOI has no year, in which case the directive simply omits the nickname clause rather than banning a garbage token. NOTE FOR THE NEXT PASS: if this still leaks, the remaining lever is the transcript itself rather than the directive -- the corrupted notes would need neutralizing before the model reads them, which is a heavier change and should only be made on evidence that the wording fix was insufficient. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.494-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.494-dev (DEV. THE TRADE GUARD WAS ARGUING FOR THE BUG IT EXISTS TO PREVENT. Gil: "still missed and stuck on the Civic trade angle." The v9.7.493 build WAS loaded and all three new guards DID fire -- [LP ON-PREMISE DIAG], [LP INTAKE REQ DIAG] and [LP TRADE CONFLATION GUARD] are all present in the log -- yet Talia's message still read "the trade-value number for the Civic". ROOT CAUSE, and it is mine from v9.7.492: the _tvNamed test asked whether a year+make appears anywhere in the arc, to decide between "the customer already told us their trade, use it" and "nothing is named, ask what they are trading". Talia's arc contains "2024 Honda Civic Sedan Sport" -- because that IS her vehicle of interest. The bare test matched the VOI, the guard concluded she had described a trade, and the directive instructed the model to USE THAT VEHICLE. The guard was pointing at the Civic and calling it the trade, which is precisely why the phrasing survived both v9.7.491 and v9.7.492. FIX: collect every year+make candidate in the arc and require at least one the VOI does not account for, prefix-matched in both directions so "2024 Honda" and "2024 Honda Civic Sedan Sport Sedan" both resolve to the VOI rather than a phantom trade. Verified on both live leads: Talia now takes the ASK branch, Jayson (whose lead note really does name a 2019 Dodge Challenger R/T alongside a 2026 Kia EV6 VOI) still takes the USE-IT branch. ALSO: the unnamed branch now names the transcript itself as a source of contamination -- earlier notes and voicemails in the thread may already contain "trade value for the <VOI>", those are wrong, and continuing the thread does not mean repeating them; write "your trade" with no vehicle attached. This is the v9.7.491 error-propagation problem again, and it needed saying explicitly because the model was faithfully continuing a corrupted thread. SECOND FIX, from the same log: Armando's on-premise message came out correct in substance -- "I see you are on the lot now with the 2026 Kia Sorento EX, someone can come grab you right away, what are you standing by?" -- but opened "Thanks for reaching out on Facebook" to a man standing in the lot, because the Facebook scenario's REQUIRED OPENING ("this is not optional") competed with the live on-premise directive. The live tier now explicitly overrides any required-opening or source-acknowledgment instruction: where they came from stopped mattering the moment they arrived. VERIFIED 5/5 on branch selection across both live arcs plus a VOI-mentioned-twice case and a both-vehicles-present case. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.493-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.493-dev (DEV. CUSTOMER-ON-THE-LOT NOW OUTRANKS EVERYTHING -- the single most time-critical state a lead can be in was arriving as ordinary call-note text and being missed repeatedly. Live incident, Armando (Kia Baytown, 7/27): at 9:38 AM Kaylee logged an outbound call reading "said he was outside right now looking at the vehicles", and four minutes later the lead was reassigned from her to salesperson Robert Staten -- the store plainly knew he had walked on. LP still generated "We tried reaching you by phone -- would you like to stop by today around 12:00 PM or 12:45 PM?" while he was standing in the lot, and Gil reports this note gets missed every time. WHY IT SLIPS: the fact lives in the CONTENT of an OUTBOUND call note. Every signal on the wrapper says agent activity -- data-direction Outbound, title "Outbound phone call (Contacted)" -- so an arrival reads as "we attempted contact", which is exactly how the generated message treated it ("we tried reaching you by phone"). Nothing in the prompt elevated it, and a customer physically on the lot outranks every other consideration. FIX: during note iteration the scraper captures any note reporting physical presence, WITH its timestamp -- requiring both a place token (outside / out front / in the lot / on the lot / in the showroom / here now / out here / walk-or-tour the lot) AND a subject-or-verb token (is / was / he is / she is / said / waiting / standing / looking / pulled up / arrived / walked), so "Left message", "No Answer", "outside of budget", "here to help" and "a lot of interest" cannot trip it. The newest qualifying note wins. Popup-side the directive is TIERED, because the right message differs sharply by age: within 2 hours it is treated as LIVE -- placed first in the extras, stating the customer may still be on the lot, explicitly forbidding appointment invitations and "when would you like to stop by" (asking that of someone standing outside announces that nobody is paying attention), and instructing a one-or-two-sentence reply that acknowledges they are here, says someone is coming right out, and asks where on the lot they are so we can reach them. Between 2 and 12 hours it is treated as a COMPLETED VISIT -- do not write as though they have never been here, do not open by inviting them in, acknowledge the visit and continue from what happened during it. Beyond 12 hours it says nothing and judgment is unchanged. HONEST NOTE ON DIAGNOSIS: the log prints only the first 300 characters of context, so it cannot be proven from the log alone whether the note text reached the prompt and was under-weighted or never arrived -- what is certain is that the output treated an arrival as a failed contact attempt, and either way an explicit elevated directive is the fix. VERIFIED 11/11 on detection including Armando's exact note text plus walked/touring-the-lot, here-now, pulled-up-out-front and arrived/showroom variants, with all five false-positive probes silent; tiering confirmed at 22 minutes (urgent), 4 hours (completed visit) and 2 days (silent). scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.492-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.492-dev (DEV. THE LEAD-RECEIVED NOTE'S CUSTOMER REQUIREMENTS WERE BEING THROWN AWAY -- and they are the richest thing a lead carries. Live incident, Jayson (Kia Baytown, 7/27). His Gubagoo m-chat lead-received note contained a complete spec in the customer's own terms: a 2019 Dodge Challenger R/T trade with ~75,200 miles and a ~$24,000 payoff, a request for the absolute best 36-month / 7,500-mile lease on a 2026 EV6 Light Long Range RWD with the highest possible trade allowance, a demand for a complete lease worksheet itemizing selling price / dealer discount / Kia rebates and lease cash / trade allowance / negative equity rolled into the payment, and an offer to come in 7/27 at 9a. LP answered by asking "Do you have a trade-in (year, make, model)?" ROOT CAUSE: the transcript builder strips EVERY lead-received note on `isLeadReceived` alone. That blanket strip is correct for what it was written against -- TradePending/KBB valuation dumps, market data and credit figures the model misreads as customer concerns -- but it also discards the customer's own submission text. The existing extractedCustQ rescue cannot recover this shape: its freeform path caps at 300 chars, its PPC path requires a "Click Path:" marker and caps at 600, and this note is ~600 chars of Gubagoo m-chat formatting matching neither. FIX 1: before the strip (which is left exactly as it was -- the note still leaves the transcript), the customer's words are pulled out and only the vendor chrome removed: tracking URL, Lead Type, Department, Visitor Score, Related Vehicles, Show Less/More. Substance is gated on length, real words, and at least one concrete-intent token, so "Lead received with no comments", bare tracking URLs and metadata-only notes can never qualify. The result is injected as its own prompt block -- WHAT THE CUSTOMER ACTUALLY ASKED FOR, verbatim -- with three rules: never re-ask anything they already stated, answer what they actually asked rather than substituting a generic pitch (and if figures cannot be put in writing, say plainly how they get them), and treat any day/time they offered as their proposed appointment. FIX 2, and this one corrects v9.7.491 shipped hours earlier: the trade/VOI conflation guard had a single branch telling the model to "ask what they are trading". On Jayson that would have been actively harmful -- his CRM Trades[] is empty, yet he had described his trade in exhaustive detail, so the guard would have re-asked the very question that started this complaint. Empty Trades[] means NOT IN THE STRUCTURED RECORD, not NEVER MENTIONED. The guard now splits: when a trade vehicle is named anywhere in the intake or transcript it says use THAT vehicle and do not make them repeat it; only when nothing is named anywhere does it ask. Both branches keep the v9.7.491 protections -- the VOI is never called the trade, and an earlier note already containing that mistake is not to be repeated. VERIFIED 24/24 against Jayson's REAL note text: all eight substantive details survive (trade vehicle, mileage, payoff, term, mileage allowance, worksheet request, negative equity, the 7/27 9a offer) while all five chrome markers are stripped; boilerplate, bare-URL and metadata-only notes are rejected; a genuinely short intake still passes; and branch selection is confirmed correct on BOTH live cases -- Jayson takes the do-not-re-ask branch, Talia takes the ask branch. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.491-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.491-dev (DEV. TRADE/VOI CONFLATION GUARD -- and the root cause is ERROR PROPAGATION THROUGH LP'S OWN NOTES, which is the more important finding. Live incident, Talia (Audi Lafayette, 7/27): the generated message read "you were asking for a specific trade value on the Civic" -- but the 2024 Honda Civic Sedan Sport is her VEHICLE OF INTEREST, the car she wants to BUY, and PageData reports Trades: [] with the panel reading "(none entered)". There is no trade vehicle on this lead at all. IMPORTANTLY THIS WAS NOT A SCRAPE BUG AND NOT THE STAGE-2 TRADE GAP-FILL: that feature requires pdTradeCount > 0 and correctly never fired. Tracing her 44 notes shows the error was authored by LP itself, one message at a time -- 7/15 an agent email says "your trade and your numbers" (correct, trade unnamed); 7/20 an LP voicemail says "you asked for a specific trade value/number" (still correctly unnamed); 7/27 an LP voicemail says "a specific trade value/number FOR THE CIVIC" -- the conflation is introduced here, because the model held a trade-value request with no trade vehicle named and the only vehicle in context was the VOI; the agent logged that voicemail as a call note, and today LP read its OWN prior output back as established fact and repeated it. That is the failure mode worth naming: once LP output is logged into the CRM it becomes "the record", and every later generation inherits the mistake and compounds it. FIX: Trades: [] is an AUTHORITATIVE empty (Stage 2), so "the arc discusses a trade AND the CRM holds zero trade vehicles AND a VOI exists" is precisely detectable rather than guessed. When that fires, a directive states plainly that the VOI is the car they want to BUY and is NOT their trade, forbids attaching the VOI name to the trade in any form, and -- the part that breaks the propagation chain -- instructs the model that IF AN EARLIER NOTE IN THE THREAD ALREADY MAKES THAT MISTAKE IT MUST NOT BE REPEATED, correcting course silently by simply not naming a trade vehicle; if a trade number is what they want, ask what they are trading or offer the appraisal, and never name a vehicle we do not have on file. Gated to require PageData present, so it cannot assert an authoritative empty it has not actually confirmed. VERIFIED 11/11 against Talia's REAL note text: all three genuine trade references detected including the exact 7/27 sentence that started it; the guard fires on her actual field values; and it stays silent when a real trade IS on file, when no trade is discussed, when PageData is absent, and when there is no VOI -- with "trade show" and "tradition" confirmed as non-matches while "appraisal" matches by intent. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.490-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.490-dev (DEV. THREE MORE PAGEDATA FACTS GRADUATE, all chosen from a 14-dump survey where every candidate was checked for VALUES rather than mere presence. (1) SOLD DETECTION IS NOW A HYBRID -- and this is the highest-consequence change here. Unavailable/sold has been detected by ONE regex against page text, /no longer in your active inventory/, the exact fragile shape as the VIN and condition scrapes that died silently when Cox reworded things -- except this one drives the SOLD pivot, where being wrong means telling a customer a car is available when it is not, or the reverse. PageData carries the typed twin: LeadVehicle.Status 'A' active / 'I' inactive, which agreed with the warning text on 6/6 unit-bearing dumps. Wired as an OR rather than a replacement -- either signal alone marks the unit unavailable -- so a Cox wording change can no longer silence it and a PageData gap cannot either, and a new [LP SOLD SIGNAL DIAG] warn fires on any disagreement so drift surfaces instead of hiding. (2) FALSE-URGENCY GUARD from LeadVehicle.InventoryDate, which yields real days-on-lot (sample ran 16/37/37/45/80/135). The point is NOT to discount harder: scarcity language is a CHECKABLE claim, and "these move fast" on a unit that has sat four months is a lie the customer can verify from the public listing date, costing more trust than the urgency was ever worth. At 60+ days the model is told not to use scarcity or urgency language at all -- and explicitly never to state or hint at the age to the customer, since that is context for the writer, not the reader. At 14 days or fewer, "just arrived" is genuinely true and is offered as usable. Between those the block says NOTHING and judgment is unchanged -- so on the observed sample, 80 and 135 are guarded, 16/37/45 stay silent, and 16 is deliberately NOT overclaimed as fresh. Days-on-lot is sanity-bounded: future-dated and absurd/garbage dates resolve to null rather than handing the model a number nobody can stand behind. (3) PHONE-LEAD TONE from Lead.LeadTypeName, populated 14/14 (Internet 12, Phone 2) and the only always-present signal separating a customer who CALLED from one who submitted a form. Opening a phone lead with "thanks for your inquiry" reads as though nobody knows how they reached us; the directive bans that and the other web-form phrasings by name, points at "thanks for calling" / continuing from the call, and tells the model to answer what was actually discussed on the call when a call note exists. Match is exact ("Phone"), so a hypothetical "Phone Up" source does not trip it. All three ride the existing LP_PD_PRIMARY kill switch. VERIFIED 27/27 against the REAL observed values: hybrid sold logic across all four agree/disagree combinations plus kill-switch-off; days-on-lot reproducing 45/135/16 exactly from the actual InventoryDate strings in the dumps, with future, stale and garbage dates rejected; threshold banding checked against every observed value; phone matching exact-only; and the shipped directive text asserted to contain each ban and each offered alternative. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.489-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.489-dev (DEV. FEEDBACK RECORD LOCATOR -- autoLeadId, customerId, stockNum and vin added to the /feedback meta, with NO customer contact data. THE PROBLEM: a feedback row carried everything ABOUT a lead (dealerId, store, agent, persona, leadSource, convState, leadAgeDays, hasCustomerReply, hasApptSet) and nothing saying WHICH lead, so Gil could see a bad session in the daily report -- e.g. the 12:34 Honda Baytown voicemail that opened "this is Elsa, your Audi Concierge at Audi Lafayette" on a Honda lead -- and have no way to open it. His first instinct was to capture phone or email, since those are the fields VinSolutions actually lets you search on, and he asked whether that could be done without too much PII exposure. IT TURNS OUT NOT TO BE NEEDED: VinSolutions deep-links by ID. Evidence came out of Gil's OWN saved dumps, which contain ID-bearing app URLs (logcall.aspx?AutoLeadID=&GlobalCustomerID=, LetterPrintNow.aspx?GCID=&ALID=, frameploader.aspx?TargetControl=LeadManagement/AddNote.ascx&AutoLeadID=&mode=dashboard, and rims2 texting URLs carrying dealerId+customerId+autoLeadId), and Gil then confirmed live that BOTH the direct path and the /vinconnect/#/ SPA-shell form land on the correct lead -- the landing page shows the customer, the vehicle, and the Notes & History panel, which is enough to diagnose without touching anything else. The SPA-shell form is documented in-file as preferred since it routes the way the app itself does, preserving session and nav. Note his own address bar reads ActiveLeadsLayout.aspx with leftpaneframe/rightpaneframe and NO lead id -- the URL never changes when a lead opens, because the dashboard renders in nested frames under a hash route, the same architecture behind every frame-identity issue chased this week. WHAT THIS BUYS: a bad session in the export becomes one click away from the actual lead, and stockNum/vin let vehicle-side questions ("was this the sold-unit problem again?") be answered from the export without opening anything. PRIVACY POSTURE, deliberate: both IDs are internal CRM record locators that identify nobody outside a system Gil already owns and already has full access to -- data minimization, the least identifying thing that does the job. No phone, no email, no customer name is stored anywhere; the /feedback payload remains free of contact data and message content. Ranked at the time as deep link (no PII) > name + lead id (low) > email (higher) > phone (highest); the deep link won, so none of the fallbacks were needed. VERIFIED 11/11: all four locator fields present and sourced from lastScrapedData, existing meta fields intact, NO phone/email/name field introduced, the deep-link recipe recorded in-file for future reference, and a link built from a real dump ID pair has the correct shape. REMAINING GAP, unchanged by this build: the pairs export captured only 8 rows of 239 signals on 7/25 (1 of 6 downvotes), so this fixes "found the record but cannot tell which lead" and NOT "the record was never stored" -- that one is Worker-side and still needs the current Worker source. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- telemetry metadata only, no scrape or prompt logic touched. Builds on v9.7.488-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.488-dev (DEV. DOCUMENTATION-ONLY -- NO BEHAVIOR CHANGE. v9.7.487 shipped the two VinSolutions name-drift aliases with a comment saying to DELETE them once Gil confirmed VinSolutions had been updated. Gil's answer was the opposite: "Patricia Galvan is Patricia Serna and Kimberly is now Jolette Aguilar. Keep Galvan and Jolette." Both names for each agent are live and will stay live -- the contact sheet carries one name for each person and VinSolutions emits the other, and the numbers already proved they are the same human (all three store lines identical in both cases). The old comment was therefore a landmine: anyone acting on it later, including a future pass over this file, would delete two keys and silently drop Patricia and Jolette to the store general number on every lead arriving under the other name. The comment is rewritten to state the aliases are PERMANENT and confirmed, names the pairing in both directions, notes that Jolette is the current name while the sheet still says Kimberly, and explicitly retracts the earlier delete instruction. NOTHING ELSE CHANGED: the PHONE_DIR entries, all 19 keys, every number, and the five store fallbacks are byte-identical to v9.7.487, so v9.7.487 was already functionally correct and installing this build is optional -- it exists so the file cannot mislead a future edit. node --check clean both builds; DEV/COMM parity at the 472-line baseline. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.487-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.487-dev (DEV. PHONE_DIR AND STORE FALLBACKS REBUILT FROM GIL'S AUTHORITATIVE BDC AGENT CONTACT SHEET (7/25). The sheet is now the single source of truth for all three store columns. MOBILE numbers were deliberately EXCLUDED per Gil and are verified absent from the entire file. Cross-referencing the old table against the sheet found more than the requested cleanup: (1) TWO AGENTS WERE SIGNING WITH WRONG NUMBERS IN PRODUCTION -- Kristen Willis's Honda Lafayette line was 337-446-2432 (sheet says 337-443-4448), and Tania Gonzalez's Honda Lafayette line was 337-205-8301, which is ROTAXLYN HUDSON's number, meaning Tania has been signing customer emails with a colleague's direct dial. Both corrected. (2) THREE STALE PEOPLE REMOVED as instructed -- carly osuna, danay rodriguez (whose entry held 281-837-3624, the number that actually belongs to Elsa McHaney and caused the v9.7.486 collision), and veronica aguilar (a duplicate of veronica villanueva's three numbers). Zoey Zenker required no action: she was never in this table, surviving only in a v9.7.474 changelog note. (3) FOUR AGENTS ADDED who were missing entirely -- roslynn kelley, patricia serna, kimberly aguilar, samantha gonzalez. Note roslynn kelley's baytown line 281-837-3626 was previously sitting inside carly osuna's stale entry. (4) ELSA MCHANEY COMPLETED -- v9.7.486 added her from a verbally-supplied baytown number only; the sheet supplies her Audi (337-483-1427) and Honda Lafayette (337-889-2169) lines. (5) ALL FIVE STORE FALLBACKS ARE NOW REAL STORE LINES, ending the recurring bug behind v9.7.474 and v9.7.486 where personal direct dials sat in the fallback table: 6189/6190/6191 all become the general Baytown number 281-837-3687 (was Gil's, Noelia Diaz's, and Veronica Villanueva's personal lines respectively -- one number for all three Baytown rooftops, matching the sheet's single "Baytown" column), 24399 becomes 337-326-4484, and 21135 gets 337-252-0822 where it was previously OMITTED ENTIRELY and silently fell through to whatever the agent's own number was. Every fallback was checked against all 51 agent direct dials on the sheet and appears in none of them. TWO ALIASES KEPT DELIBERATELY AND FLAGGED FOR GIL'S CALL: 'patricia galvan' and 'jolette aguilar' are NOT on the sheet, but VinSolutions is still emitting both names as the agent on live leads TODAY, and each old entry's three numbers match a sheet person EXACTLY -- Patricia Galvan/Patricia Serna and Jolette Aguilar/Kimberly Aguilar are the same humans under different names, not extra staff. Deleting them satisfies "remove anyone not on this list" but causes a live regression: every lead where VinSolutions shows the old name loses the direct line and falls through to the store general number. They are retained pointing at the SHEET's numbers until Gil confirms VinSolutions has been updated, at which point deleting those two lines and nothing else completes the cleanup. VERIFIED 16/16 by machine-diffing the shipped table against the sheet transcription: all 17 roster agents match on all three columns, all three removals confirmed absent, both aliases confirmed to carry their sheet-person's exact numbers, entry count is exactly 17+2, all five fallbacks correct, no fallback collides with any agent direct dial, Tania no longer shares Rotaxlyn's line, and NONE of the 17 mobile numbers appears anywhere in the file. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- data-table only, no logic touched. Builds on v9.7.486-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.486-dev (DEV. THREE FIXES FROM THE DAVID MENDEZ REGENERATION. The v9.7.485 appointment-rules and chat-naming fixes both LANDED -- the regenerated message led with "are you coming by today at 1:30 PM" and deferred the trade appraisal to the visit, and neither SMS nor email said the vendor name -- which exposed three smaller defects in the same output. (1) CLOSED-DAY OFFER: the message asked "today at 1:30 PM, or tomorrow afternoon?" on a SATURDAY at a store closed Sundays. Every fact was already in the prompt -- CALENDAR REFERENCE read "TOMORROW = Sunday 7/26 (CLOSED)" and STORE HOURS read "Sunday: CLOSED" -- so this is a missing RULE, not a missing fact: the governing line covers appointment TIMES falling within store hours, and a bare relative day word carrying no clock time slips straight past that test. A CLOSED DAYS ARE NEVER OFFERED rule now sits with the store-hours block, banning closed days as dates, day names, AND relative words ("tomorrow", "this weekend", "over the weekend"), requiring every day reference to be resolved against the CALENDAR REFERENCE and checked for a (CLOSED) marker before it is written, falling through to the next OPEN day -- and it applies to alternatives the model volunteers itself, not just to days the customer asked about. (2) DUPLICATE PHONE IN EMAIL: the body carried a bare "281-837-3382" line, a blank line, then the signature block with the same number again. New hygiene pass strips a line that is ONLY a phone number when that same number recurs later in the email, so a phone appearing exactly once -- in prose or in the signature -- is never touched. (3) WRONG PHONE, and it is the v9.7.474 bug class again: Elsa McHaney had NO PHONE_DIR entry, so every message she signed fell through to STORE_PHONE_FALLBACK['6189'] = 281-837-3382, which is GIL'S OWN Baytown line (it is literally his PHONE_DIR entry) -- the same defect as Patricia Galvan's personal extension sitting in the 6191 slot. Elsa is added to PHONE_DIR at 281-837-3624 (Baytown only; no Audi/Honda-Lafayette line was supplied and the resolver falls through rather than inventing one) and the 6189 fallback becomes the general Baytown number 281-837-3687, both supplied directly by Gil; 281-837-3687 was verified absent from PHONE_DIR, confirming it is a true store line rather than another agent's direct dial. TWO ITEMS DELIBERATELY LEFT FOR GIL rather than guessed: (a) 281-837-3624 is ALSO currently listed under 'danay rodriguez' in PHONE_DIR -- if that entry is stale it should be removed, otherwise two agents will sign the same number; (b) the SAME defect exists in the other two Baytown slots, '6190' = 281-837-3683 (Noelia Diaz's personal line) and '6191' = 281-837-3682 (Veronica Villanueva/Aguilar's), but whether all three Baytown rooftops share one general number is a business fact, not something to infer. VERIFIED: closed-day rule 8/8 (bans the exact failing word, requires CALENDAR-REFERENCE resolution and the (CLOSED) check, falls through to the next open day, covers self-volunteered alternatives, sits with the store-hours rule); email dedup 8/8 against David's REAL email body (stray line removed, signature copy retained, prose untouched, no leftover blank runs) plus negatives -- a phone appearing once, a phone inside prose, a bare year line, and a bare phone with no later duplicate are all left alone. node --check clean both builds; DEV/COMM parity at the 472-line baseline. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.485-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.485-dev (DEV. APPOINTMENT RULES + CHAT VENDOR-NAME SUPPRESSION -- both from the David Mendez lead (Gubagoo - Chat, Toyota Baytown, 7/25), both per Gil's explicit call. THE LEAD: customer replied "1:30" the night before to set an appointment, the agent texted a reminder at 9:07 AM, Gil sent the invite at 9:09, and the grab happened that morning with the appointment due at 1:30 PM the SAME DAY. hasApptSet was true and the APPOINTMENT HISTORY block sat at the very top of the prompt -- yet the generated SMS and email never mentioned the appointment, opened a NEW information-gathering thread ("before we go any further, I need two quick details: current mileage and whether you have any accidents"), asked for mileage that was sitting in lastInboundMsg ("Subject: Re:Sell your car (16,939 miles)"), and closed with "when you\'re ready to move." NOTHING WAS MISSING FROM THE PROMPT -- the appointment fact was present but delivered NON-prescriptively ("read the arc... do not apply a fixed rule -- use judgment"), while the competing DEAL/INFO tradeValue trigger and the auto-switched sales persona were prescriptive; the soft directive lost to the hard ones. FIX 1 (APPOINTMENT RULES): when hasApptSet is true and the lead is not sold/delivered, a dominant directive is prepended with the appointment history, ahead of the transcript, stating that the live appointment OUTRANKS the TASK/scenario directive and every other angle, and that other angles (trade numbers, incentives, comparables, financing) are permitted only in SUPPORT of it -- never as a replacement and never as a reason to push it later. The model KEEPS its judgment about what the appointment state actually is (upcoming, today, already passed, needs rescheduling) -- the timeline is still read as an arc -- what changed is PRIORITY, not interpretation. Four failure shapes observed in the live message are banned by name: the "before we go any further" precondition thread, open-ended "when you\'re ready to move" framing that implies no appointment exists, re-asking facts already in the thread (mileage explicitly), and first-touch source re-acknowledgment on a lead with a time on the books. FIX 2 (CHAT NAMING): the same message said "Gubagoo" to the customer in BOTH the SMS and the email. Mechanism confirmed from the log -- SOURCE ACKNOWLEDGMENT appears ZERO times in that prompt, because v9.7.462 correctly made dedicated-scenario sources skip the generic block (they contradicted each other) and that block was the ONLY carrier of the "paraphrase internal-sounding source names" rule, whose own worked example is Gubagoo. Per Gil, the source is referred to as "chat": a NAMING rule is added to BOTH chat scenario branches (the aged chat-evolved-into-a-thread branch and the fresh live-chat branch) instructing "the chat" / "our website chat" and never the vendor name -- customers used a chat box on the dealership website and the vendor name is internal jargon to them. The consumer-recognizable dedicated sources (CarGurus, Cars.com, AutoTrader, KBB, Costco, TrueCar, Facebook, CarPro) are deliberately untouched -- naming those is correct and desirable. VERIFIED 15/15 against the shipped file: gate fires on David\'s exact conditions and stays silent on sold/delivered and on no-appointment leads; the directive bans each of the four observed failure shapes by name including his verbatim "when you\'re ready to move" close; it asserts precedence over the scenario directive while preserving upcoming-vs-passed judgment; the naming rule is present on both chat branches with the approved wording and the explicit vendor-name prohibition; and the appointment directive is confirmed to sit ahead of the transcript in the prompt. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- prompt-side only. Builds on v9.7.484-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.484-dev (DEV. CPO ALTERNATE-CONDITION ANGLE WAS DARK -- SETTLED EMPIRICALLY, NOT HYPOTHESIZED. Gil asked whether the inventory feed sees CPO as a category to offer, then supplied the Data Tool's normalized vAuto export (946 units, all 5 rooftops, 7/25) which answers it definitively. The v9.7.414/412 ALTERNATE CONDITION angle (a GSM ask: surface the same nameplate in the opposite condition as a value-conscious secondary option) decided a unit's condition by REGEX ON THE UNIT'S `vehicle` STRING -- a deliberate v9.7.414 choice made when the feed's schema fields "had never been confirmed to carry" condition. The export settles both halves of that premise, and both were wrong: (a) every unit DOES carry condition in {new, used, cpo} AND a certified boolean, and the two agree on 946/946; (b) ZERO of 946 `vehicle` strings contain any condition word (they read "2026 Toyota 4Runner SR5"). So the regex matched nothing, _condAlts was always empty, and THE ENTIRE ANGLE HAS NEVER FIRED SINCE IT SHIPPED -- 46 CPO units group-wide (Toyota Baytown 10, Kia Baytown 9, Honda Baytown 11, Audi Lafayette 2, Honda Lafayette 14) plus 247 used units invisible to it. Same silently-dark shape as the VIN and condition bugs earlier this week. FIX: (1) new _lpUnitCondition(u) reads the REAL fields (certified boolean first, then condition, with the old string sniff demoted to a last-resort fallback for any future feed shape); the alternate-condition block now uses it, and its stock-exclusion checks BOTH `stock` and `stockNum` since the raw export carries `stock` and the fetch boundary adds `stockNum`. (2) CPO is now surfaced AS CPO rather than lumped into "used": alternates are individually labeled "-- Certified Pre-Owned" / "-- used", the header label reads "Certified Pre-Owned / used" when a certified unit is present, and a certified alternate adds one sentence framing the certified warranty/inspection/program benefits as real selling points (value option, not a downgrade) with an explicit do-not-invent-terms guard -- the same value Stage 2 unlocked for the lead's OWN unit, now available for what we offer alongside it. (3) _lpComparables labels certified units too, so SIMILAR VEHICLES IN STOCK and the SOLD-pivot comparable list stop presenting a CPO unit as an anonymous used car. (4) SEPARATE BUG FOUND IN THE SAME EXPORT, fixed: 53 of 946 units carry a raw factory color CODE instead of a name (49 of them Toyota Baytown -- "01l7", "08x8", "04z2"), which was rendering into prompts as "2026 Toyota 4Runner SR5 (01l7)" -- gibberish one step from a customer-facing message; new _lpCleanColor drops code-shaped values (requires BOTH short-alphanumeric shape AND a digit) while keeping all 881 real names untouched. (5) [LP ALT-CONDITION DIAG] added -- this angle was invisible precisely because nothing logged it; it now reports what it found or why it found nothing, both branches. NOT CHANGED, verified correct already: the SOLD-pivot incentive-pairing gate at ~1985 reads `condition + vehicle` so it already consulted the real field; the chat-VOI promotion already reads the certified boolean and correctly refuses to call an off-brand unit CPO (a Honda store cannot certify a Chevy); _lpUnitInTransit already reads u.condition. VERIFIED 24/24 against the REAL 946-unit export using the helper functions extracted from the shipped file: resolver produces new 653 / used 247 / cpo 46 exactly matching the feed's own counts with zero disagreements against the raw fields; the OLD string regex reproduced at 0 matches; color scrubbing hits exactly the 53 codes and keeps exactly the 881 names with no real name dropped; and an end-to-end check on a real store+nameplate that carries both conditions (Toyota Baytown / Corolla) now surfaces "2026 Toyota Corolla LE (Blueprint) -- Certified Pre-Owned" where the old code could surface nothing at all. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.483-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.483-dev (DEV. OFF-FRANCHISE VEHICLE REQUEST -- live incident, Halie Bott (Honda Lafayette, 7/25). Her own inbound text: "we are planning to purchase a new chevy silverado in a couple weeks. Do you have any in dark red?" -- and LP answered "I can absolutely look up whether we have any dark red Silverados that match what you are looking for, but I will need the year and trim." Community Honda Lafayette is a HONDA franchise: it cannot sell, order, locate, or dealer-trade for a new Chevrolet, so that was a promise the store structurally cannot keep -- and it burned the two plays that ARE available (per Gil): buy the customer's vehicle outright even though they are buying the truck elsewhere, and/or offer the house-brand equivalent. Nothing in the prompt had ever told the model which brands a rooftop actually sells. FIX: (1) the rooftop->franchise map, previously a local _cvBrandMap buried inside the chat-VOI promotion, is promoted to ONE shared _LP_STORE_BRAND definition (matching this file's existing single-definition discipline) with a _lpNormMake alias normalizer (chevy->chevrolet, vw->volkswagen, benz->mercedes); the chat-VOI site now reads the shared map. (2) A new guard in populateFromData injects an OFF-FRANCHISE REQUEST directive carrying the structural fact (this is a <brand> store; we cannot sell/order/locate a new <make>), an explicit ban on the exact failure phrasing ("check what we have" / "look it up" / "find one" / asking year+trim as if we could source it), the live-inventory reality, and Gil's two plays -- buy their vehicle outright (naming the actual trade when known, e.g. "their 2019 Dodge Durango GT Plus, 87,360 mi" via the Stage-2 typed Trades record) and offer the house-brand equivalent in the same segment, with model choice left to the model rather than a rot-prone cross-brand equivalence table, and no specific unit/stock named unless confirmed elsewhere in the prompt. PRECISION GUARDS, because off-brand text is everywhere on a normal lead: customer's OWN words only (_lpCustomerText -- agent notes and panel text cannot trigger it); the customer's own vehicle is excluded, since trade/owned/prior-sold are almost ALWAYS off-brand (Halie's own trade is a Dodge Durango and correctly does not trip it); live inventory is consulted FIRST, so a used off-brand unit genuinely on the lot stays silent unless they explicitly said NEW (the codebase's own chat-VOI comment cites "a used Lexus on the Honda lot" -- off-brand used stock is real and must remain sellable); and with no inventory cache and no explicit "new" it stays silent rather than guess. VERIFIED 21/21 using the REAL helper functions extracted from the shipped file (not reimplementations): the verbatim Halie inbound fires with make chevy->chevrolet, saidNew true, zero Chevrolet in stock, and her Durango correctly NOT selected as the hit; own-trade-only and owned-vehicle-only stay silent; an off-brand unit IN stock stays silent; off-brand not in stock fires on inventory evidence; NEW-off-brand-with-a-used-one-on-the-lot fires AND surfaces the real used unit; house brand at Honda and Toyota rooftops stay silent; no-cache-no-"new" stays silent while no-cache-plus-explicit-NEW fires on structural certainty; Audi/Kia rooftops fire correctly; agent-note-only off-brand mentions stay silent. KNOWN RECALL LIMIT, deliberate and tested: a bare MODEL with no make ("do you have any f-150s?") is not detected -- requiring the make token is what keeps precision high (a bare "any Accords?" is house brand), and a model->make table across 30+ makes is exactly the rot-prone artifact this codebase avoids; revisit only if fleet traffic shows bare-model requests are common. ALSO NOTED, not built: the same log shows Monique Meaux told "ready to move on a 2022+ Ford F-150 XLT" at the same Honda rooftop -- if that unit is not real used stock, this guard now covers it; if it IS real stock, the guard correctly stays silent. [LP OFF-FRANCHISE DIAG] logs the phrase, make, store brand, saidNew, and in-stock count on every firing. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.482-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.482-dev (DEV. ARC-AWARE SOLD PIVOT -- live incident, Moranda Moren (Dealers WebSite, Toyota Baytown, 7/25): TWO consecutive drafts on her lead each LED with "The 2018 Camry LE you asked about has sold" + XSE pivot + appointment times, while a more recent manager exchange in the thread went unengaged -- the v9.7.227-era 🔴 SOLD directive fired per-generation with zero awareness of whether the arc had already delivered the sold-news, and its mandatory announce-and-pivot language outweighed the generic read-the-thread principle. The sold announcement is ONE-TIME NEWS, not a per-message opener. FIX: (1) _soldNewsInArc -- popup-side scan of the current-lead thread (context sliced at the CURRENT LEAD marker, same slicing the vehicle-mention check uses, plus history) for sold-communication language (has sold / it sold / already sold / that unit sold / no longer available / sold over-the-weekend-style datings); 8/8 in the detection harness incl. the critical negatives: a customer selling THEIR OWN car ("we sold our old Corolla"), bought-elsewhere exit notes, and "these sell fast" urgency language all stay clean. (2) When the news is already in the arc, the directive FLIPS: do not re-announce (re-announcing news the thread contains reads as not listening), anchor on the MOST RECENT exchange -- especially the latest agent or manager message -- and move that thread forward, referencing the sold unit only if asked; a [LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG] line logs the suppression. (3) Even in the announce path, the directive now says tell them ONCE and explicitly yields to any more recent exchange that has moved past the unit -- the sold notice supports the message, it does not replace the conversation. CAVEAT logged honestly: dump 28 captured the outer tab-host shell rather than the rims2 content frame, so Moranda's actual transcript was not readable this session -- the mechanism (unconditional per-generation announce) is proven from the two drafts alone, but a proper content-frame dump of her lead would confirm the manager-text engagement; the Audi all-available policy branch, the pendingSale branch, the hasApptSet-neutral branch, and the isSoldDelivered suppression are all untouched. node --check clean both builds; DEV/COMM parity at the 472-line baseline. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.481-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.481-dev (DEV. BEREAVEMENT GUARD -- live incident, Robert (AI Buying Signals - Previously Sold Customer, Toyota Baytown, 7/25): the most recent inbound was a DEATH NOTIFICATION -- a reply to a birthday email beginning "Sorry to inform..." -- and NO signal in the detection set matched it, so convState stayed call-followup, the 513-day-stalled framing pushed the RAV4 hook, the appointment engine ran normally, and the generation paired a condolence with Friday time slots in a single message ("I'm really sorry for your loss... I can get everything lined up for Friday at 10:00 AM or 2:30 PM"). Reputation-damaging if ever sent. FIX, riding the proven exit rails: (1) hasBereavementSignal, detected frame-side against fullScanText with deliberately TIGHT, automotive-safe patterns -- unambiguous vocabulary (passed away / deceased / funeral / obituary / death in the family / no longer with us / lost-my-<family-member> / possessive "X's passing") plus subject-anchored died/passed forms where the subject list and a negative lookahead block the domain's famous false-positive idioms: "the car died", "battery died", "engine died", "the deal died", "she passed ON the offer", "passed BY the dealership", "passed UP the warranty" all stay clean while "she passed", "my husband died", "Robert's passing" all fire -- 16/16 in the detection harness. (2) The flag joins hasExitSignal UNGATED by !recentCustomerActive -- the death notice IS the recent customer activity, so the guard protecting the other exit components would have suppressed exactly the case that must fire; keepingTrade is equally irrelevant to it. Riding the exit chain buys the ENTIRE existing suppression stack for free: convState exit, appointment machinery off, incentive gating adversarial, director exit routing. (3) A dominant popup-side directive supplies the tone the generic exit close doesn't: 2-3 sentences of sincere condolence, offer to close the file / stop outreach, confirm anything the sender asked for -- and ABSOLUTELY NO vehicle mentions, appointments, incentives, questions, or "door open for the future / if you're still shopping" language, because the generic exit close's door-open line is itself wrong for a death notice. (4) The v9.7.440 [LP EXIT DIAG] source naming now identifies bereavement firings explicitly, and the diag gate widened so bereavement-only firings log their source. ALSO IN THIS LOG, NO CODE CHANGE NEEDED: the missed-appointment shape (Dena Howard, Honda Lafayette, 2027 HR-V LX) handled correctly end-to-end -- no-show + "was at work" reply -> appointment history injected, persona auto-switched bdc->manager, empathetic recovery offer with a concrete time and flexibility; her grab is also the first FULL Stage-1 sweep (src vin:pd stock:pd cond:pd, all four matches true -- lead 15, still zero mismatches). node --check clean both builds; DEV/COMM parity at the 472-line baseline. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.480-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.480-dev (DEV. PAGEDATA HYBRID -- STAGE 2. The three graduated behaviors that turn observed PageData into acted-on data, shipped on a Stage-1 record of 14 leads / zero mismatches / zero ID-CONFLICT events. (1) ID-CONFLICT NOW REJECTS THE FRAME: a frame whose vindebug and PageData identities name DIFFERENT leads (the mid-transition stale-frame signature) is discarded from the merge -- with a safety valve: if the conflicted frame is the ONLY lead frame available it is RETAINED with a loud verify-before-sending warn rather than producing an empty grab. Wrong-customer contamination from a leftover frame becomes structurally impossible whenever any clean frame exists. (2) TRADES[]/VEHICLESOFINTEREST[] FEED THE ABSTAIN LOGIC: (a) authoritative empty -- PageData present with NO LeadVehicle and a zero-length VOI array now adds a CRM CONFIRMS line telling the model the blank panel is REAL (ask confidently what they are shopping for; no hedging about "the vehicle you asked about"); correct even on KBB ICO trade leads, where no-VOI is the true state of a selling customer; (b) trade gap-fill -- when the trade panel didn't render but the CRM's TYPED Trades[] record exists (YearName/Make/Model/ModelTrim/Mileage -- confirmed shape on the Christina Fregia dump: "2023 Kia Telluride S, 52,000 mi"), hasTrade + tradeDescription are backfilled from PageData so EVERY downstream trade consumer (trade hook, decline detection, VR trade gating) works uniformly; the panel STAYS PRIMARY whenever it renders (fresher, agent-entered detail). (3) CERTIFIED SURFACED AS ITS OWN FACT: InventoryType C keeps mapping to condition "Pre-Owned" (zero downstream equality-check changes) but now also sets isCertifiedUnit, which adds one prompt line giving the model the CPO selling points (certified warranty/inspection/program benefits, with an explicit do-not-invent-specific-terms guard) it was previously blind to. All three ride the LP_PD_PRIMARY kill switch: OFF disables the trade gap-fill and the PD-sourced facts along with the Stage-1 adoptions. VERIFIED 15/15: real dumps (Christina trade shape + panel-primacy, Heather authoritative-empty with no trade fabricated, Betty/Anthony non-certified negative cases) + synthetic paths no dump exercises (InventoryType C certified flag, panel-beats-PD trade primacy, kill-switch-off gap-fill disable, and all three ID-CONFLICT scenarios: rejected-when-clean-exists, retained-when-only-frame, normal-frames-unaffected). node --check clean both builds; DEV/COMM parity at the 472-line baseline, zero new divergence. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.479-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.479-dev (DEV. PAGEDATA HYBRID CUTOVER, STAGE 1 -- the first PageData fields graduate from diagnostic to PRIMARY data source with the full regex pipeline retained as fallback and referee, on the strength of the completed validation record: 11/11 distinct leads across all 5 stores, statuses NEW/ACTIVE/WAITING/DUPLICATE-BAD/LOST, invType N/U/C, leadIdMatch true on every diag line, ZERO mismatches vs the (fixed) regex scraper. GRADUATING FIELDS: (1) VIN -- pd.LeadVehicle.VIN primary, full regex resolution still runs and is retained as _rgxVin; (2) stockNum -- pd DealerStockNum/StockNumber primary, adopted BEFORE VIN resolution so the stock-suffix validation runs against the authoritative stock, _rgxStock retained; (3) condition -- pd InventoryType primary mapped N->New, U/C->Pre-Owned (C is Certified, mapped to preserve downstream semantics; raw granularity stays visible in the diag invType field), _rgxCondition retained; (4) velocity-governor created-time -- pd LeadCreatedUTC parsed as UTC (verified against page-rendered CDT: Betty Langley 8:42p CDT = 01:42Z), text parse remains fallback; (5) AutoLeadID as frame-identity FALLBACK when vindebug is absent, and when BOTH are present and DISAGREE (the stale-frame signature, unobserved in 11 leads) an ID-CONFLICT warn logs -- logged only, NOT auto-rejected in stage 1, so fleet traffic shows whether it ever fires before rejection gets wired to it. EXPLICITLY NOT GRADUATING: SmsOptinStatus (proven dead -- null even on Heather Ockman's confirmed opt-out with the CRM's own "manually changed to: Opt-Out" note rendered on the same page; notes-based opt-out detection, which already matches that note form incl. re-opt-in, remains the compliance mechanism permanently), DoNotCall/DoNotEmail (semantics unvalidated), buyer fields, Trades/VehiclesOfInterest arrays (diag-only). KILL SWITCH: LP_PD_PRIMARY=false at the top of the hybrid block reverts the entire stage to pure v9.7.478 regex behavior, PD back to diagnostic-only. ONGOING AUDIT: the PAGEDATA DIAG stays on permanently, its cmp fields now compare PD against the retained _rgx* values (comparing against the finals would be PD-vs-PD), the log line adds src markers (src vin:pd/rgx/-) showing which source produced each final field on every grab, and MISMATCH/STALE-NOTES/ID-CONFLICT warns all remain armed. VERIFIED: 36/36 in a harness running the shipped hybrid logic against every locally-available real dump -- PD-primary finals correct with src:pd markers on all unit-bearing leads, no-unit leads (Jessica KBB, Heather LOST) correctly empty on both sides with src:- , kill-switch-OFF finals byte-equal to pure regex output on every dump, PD-vs-rgx cmp never false, and the UTC velocity parse lands on the exact known instant. node --check clean both builds; DEV/COMM parity at exactly the 472-line baseline, zero new divergence. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.478-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.478-dev (DEV. MATURE-LEAD BATCH RESULTS + CONFLICT-DETECTOR SCOPING -- the 5-lead mature batch (7/24 late: Jonathan Felix 5-lead-history Cargurus Reengagement/Audi Lafayette, Anthony Barragan DUPLICATE_LEAD/BAD Referral, Abigail Rodriguez 90-day Showroom CPO, Jessica Royal KBB ICO trade lead, Brionna Benoit Gubagoo DRS CPO/Honda Lafayette) CONFIRMS the v9.7.476/471 fixes live in fleet: vinMatch/stockMatch/condMatch/leadIdMatch ALL TRUE on every unit-bearing lead, rgx side fully populated (vin:Y stock:Y cond:Pre-Owned), the unit-less KBB lead correctly all-N on BOTH sides, lastMsgSeenInNotes:true on all five mature leads, zero MISMATCH and zero STALE-NOTES warns -- PageData is now 10/10 distinct leads across all 5 stores, statuses NEW/ACTIVE/WAITING/DUPLICATE-BAD, invType N/U/C(+empty), with leadIdMatch true on every single line; and the frame-rejection guard correctly bounced Anthony's duplicate-pair sibling frame (2007337966) a dozen times. THIS BUILD fixes what the batch surfaced: the VOI-conflict detector's REMAINING false-positive classes, which are input-scope, not regex -- v9.7.455 feeds it context + pageSnippet, and pageSnippet is whole-page TEXT (first 8000 chars) carrying (a) OTHER LEADS' vehicles from the LeadSelector history rows (Jonathan flagged 2017-vs-2018 where the "2018 Lincoln Continental" belongs to a Lost 2/4/25 lead -- ZERO of his 57 notes mention 2018) and (b) TRADE-IN vehicles (Brionna flagged 2025-vs-2012 where "2012 Honda Accord" is her trade). FIX 1: both call sites (~14483 raw-diag, ~14853 VM) now pass d.context ONLY -- the agent-context notes (general/call/showroom) that the v9.7.456 either/or rule is actually written against; pageSnippet dropped. FIX 2: inside the detector, trade-in phrase windows are stripped before the year scan ("trade-in"/"trade in vehicle" forms only -- "trade FOR a 2023 Telluride" is a TARGET vehicle and deliberately still matches). Verified 6/6 against the batch's real cases: Jonathan clean, Brionna trade-in clean, trade-FOR-target detected, Paige-class general note detected, KMF current-vehicle detected, timestamps-only clean (476 regression check). Known recall trade-off, accepted: a conflicting year embedded ONLY in lead-received text (not in agent notes) is no longer scanned -- for KMF leads the v9.7.222/470 banner override already handles that class upstream, and the detector is DIAG-ONLY. NOTE: Abigail's 2023-vs-2026 flag was the one arguably-REAL case (her VehiclesOfInterest array -- the batch's first vois:1 -- holds a sold used 2023 HR-V alongside the 2026 CPO panel unit); under context-only scoping it flags only if agents discussed it, which is the correct bar for the either/or rule. FIX 3 (visibility): populateFromData log line now prints engagementStrength -- the CRM ENGAGEMENT RATING prompt line sits past the 300-char context excerpt, so tonight's logs could not confirm it was flowing; next batch settles it from the log line directly. OPEN QUESTION from the batch, no code change: SmsOptinStatus is null on all 10 leads incl. heavily-texted mature ones -- it may simply never populate in this CRM config; an actual opted-out lead's dump would settle whether it can ever serve as the authoritative compliance source. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.477-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.477-dev (DEV. GENERIC ROUTING NOTE EXCLUDED FROM TRANSCRIPT -- Gil confirmed the untitled By:System note "Customer requested to work with Community Kia (If available)" (Sue Hallman dump, Kia Digital 3rd Party Lead, 7/24) is the lead form's generic preferred-dealer routing field, NOT customer speech -- it names no salesperson and appears regardless of what the customer typed. It was entering the transcript because it has content but no title (the !title && !content gate only drops fully-empty items), letting boilerplate read as a customer request. FIX: one skip in the transcript builder, next to the existing lead-log/system skips, matching the generic form ONLY -- "customer requested to work with" plus either the "(If available)" suffix or a store-name target (community/brand/dealership/store). A note naming an actual person ("requested to work with Patricia Galvan") matches neither signal and STAYS in the transcript, per Gil's own distinction: naming a salesperson is meaningful routing context, a store-target entry is form boilerplate. Verified against 5 cases: Sue's exact note (skipped), store-target-without-suffix (skipped), salesperson-named (kept), the "requests to be contacted by text" consumer note (kept -- different phrase, untouched extractor), unrelated customer note (kept). BUILD NOTE: the first v9.7.477 packaging attempt truncated both popup.js files to the header comment via a write-before-read scripting bug -- caught by the DEV/COMM parity check reading an impossible 0-divergence, restored from the v9.7.476/471 archives, and every content check below re-run on the restored files. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.476-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.476-dev (DEV. FIRST-NIGHT PAGEDATA-DIAG PAYOFF -- the v9.7.475 diagnostic's first fleet batch (5 leads, 5 logs, 6 matching dumps, 7/24 evening) exposed THREE real regressions of the v9.7.465 "Cox updated the site" class, all fixed here and every fix verified against the REAL derivation pipeline running on all 7 dumps (39/39 -- the previous harness's hardcoded stand-ins masked the condition bug; this one runs the actual selector->canonicalize->derive chain). (1) VIN EXTRACTION WAS DEAD FLEET-WIDE: the scan requires a literal "VIN:" label but the current UI renders the VIN as a BARE 17-char line under "Stock #:" -- confirmed on all 6 dumps and by vinMatch:null with pd.VIN populated on every diag line. FIX: bare-VIN capture scoped STRICTLY to the active unit's own VehicleInfo panel chain (m_VehicleDetails / __VehicleInfoPanel / #vehicleinfo -- verified on the Patricia Santos dump that her trade VIN, living in a Tradepending NOTE, stays outside this scope), digit-required, with the stock-suffix validation still the primary gate; PLUS a sole-panel-VIN acceptance branch for USED units, whose sequential lot-style stock numbers (P7372) have no VIN-suffix relationship (the suffix rule is new-units-only per the code's own comment) and whose vicinity fallback also demanded the dead label. (2) CONDITION WAS EMPTY ON EVERY FULL PANEL -- OUR OWN v9.7.81 BUG, surfaced by condMatch:null on 5/5 live leads: the canonicalization REASSIGNS vehicleRaw to bare Y/M/M/Trim, stripping the "(New)"/"(Used)" token before `const condition` ever reads it; condition only worked on condition-ONLY panels (the VOI-4 layout) where canonicalization doesn't match -- inverted coverage, confirmed by Gary Barto's Ram passing coincidentally (digit-leading "1500" defeats canonicalization). FIX: condition now reads the preserved pre-canonicalization raw (_vehicleRawFull); same test, correct input. WAKE-UPS (dormant designed behavior now live again, named per the v9.7.429 discipline): isInTransit is detectable again (it requires vin AND condition, both previously empty -- the entire in-transit feature set incl. the v9.7.426 incentive/comparables disclosure has been unreachable); the VOI "(New)"/"(Pre-Owned)" prompt suffix returns; used-vehicle incentive suppression (_incUsed) actually suppresses; the v9.7.426 generic-VOI first-touch incentive stops mis-firing on leads that DO have a VIN on the page; noSpecificVehicle stops over-firing; and both v9.7.467 DATAISLAND DIAG lines become reachable for the first time (each was gated on one of the two dead fields -- why they never fired in any fleet log). (3) VOI-CONFLICT DETECTOR FALSE-POSITIVE CLASS: the bare \b20\d{2}\b note scan read CALENDAR DATES as vehicle years -- every note leads with "07/24/2026 8:42 PM", so hasConflict:true fired on effectively every fresh lead (confirmed: Sue Hallman's "noteYear 2026" was nothing but timestamps; zero vehicle years in her notes), grading correct VOI_ONLY outputs as violations and poisoning the exact adherence stats the v9.7.456 VM fix is judged by. FIX (diag-only, zero generation change -- the detector feeds ONLY the adherence logs at ~14397/14765): date-shaped tokens stripped first, then years must be VEHICLE-ADJACENT (followed by a 2+-letter word) -- the KMF "Current Vehicle: 2021 SORENTO" and Paige "2020 or newer" real-conflict shapes both still detected, dates-only notes now clean. ALSO: the PAGEDATA DIAG line now prints the rgx-side values (vin Y/N, stock Y/N, cond) next to the pd side -- the first-night nulls could not say WHICH side was empty and cost real analysis time. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.475-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.475-dev (DEV. VIN-DUMP FINDINGS BATCH -- three fixes + the PageData validation diagnostic, all derived from a fresh full rims2 dump (Jace Savage, Audi Lafayette dealer 21135, Cargurus - Appt - Digital Deal, 7/24) reviewed against the live selector set. (1) VELOCITY GOVERNOR WAS DEAD: the Created-stamp regex captures VinSolutions' actual rendered format "7/24/26 11:41a" -- and new Date("7/24/26 11:41a") is Invalid Date in V8 (verified NaN in a live runtime; "11:41 AM" parses fine), so createdMs was NaN, `createdMs > 0` was ALWAYS false, and isVelocityResponse could never fire -- the sub-90-second first-touch suppression directive has been unreachable since it shipped. FIX: normalize the bare a/p suffix to " AM"/" PM" before parsing; verified against the dump's exact string (NaN before, valid ms after, round-trips to the correct minute). (2) VOI-4 ROW-DISCOVERY HARDENING: the same LeadSelector rows carry data-row-key="<leadId>" (confirmed on the dump: data-row-key="2057482372"); rows are now discovered by class OR data-row-key, additively, scoped strictly to the LeadSelector container -- if Cox drops the LeadSelector_LeadId_* class the way they dropped ant-table-row-selected (v9.7.465), row discovery survives. Union can only ADD rows, never remove one; verified identical row set on the current markup. (3) ENGAGEMENT STRENGTH FACT: VinSolutions' own engagement rating (#engagement-strength .engagement-strength-label, "High" on this dump; svg prediction-* class as fallback) is now scraped -- whitelist-guarded to Very High/High/Medium/Low/Very Low/None so DOM junk can never reach a prompt -- and passed as ONE plain context line (CRM ENGAGEMENT RATING), no prescriptive handling attached, per the missing-FACT-not-missing-RULE philosophy. Merges via the standard first-non-empty scalar path. (4) PAGEDATA DIAGNOSTIC (LOGGING ONLY, ZERO BEHAVIOR CHANGE): the validation gate for the span#PageData cutover flagged at v9.7.465 -- frame-side, self-contained (v9.7.455 trap respected: no popup helpers, results travel back in _pdDiag and are logged POPUP-side), it records PageData presence/coverage (SmsOptinStatus, DoNotCall/DoNotEmail, HotLead, InventoryType/InventoryDate, Trades[]/VehiclesOfInterest[] lengths, LeadCreatedUTC, Buyer zip), agreement vs the regex scraper (vinMatch/stockMatch/condMatch/leadIdMatch -- a MISMATCH warn names the exact disagreeing field), the four __dataIsland attributes the v9.7.467 diag never read (data-leadid/-inventoryid/-leadstatus/-leadstatustype), and a STALE-NOTES probe: whether Lead.LastAutoLeadMessageID appears among the rendered note items' data-unique-identifier-value (verified true on the dump: 27599420112 present) -- a per-frame freshness signal aimed straight at the open SPA iframe-race issue. Run it a week of fleet traffic; if coverage and agreement hold across lead types, the cutover ships data-backed. VERIFIED: node --check clean; all four blocks exercised against the REAL dump in a jsdom harness (velocity fires inside the 90s window and not outside it; engagementStrength="High"; _pdDiag: present, all four cmp fields true, trades 0, vois 0, smsOptin null, lastMsgSeenInNotes true; VOI-4 union returns the identical single row). scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- content.js untouched; every change is in the popup-injected inlineScraper + popup-side prompt/log code, always version-consistent with this file. Builds on v9.7.474-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.474-dev (DEV. WRONG AGENT PHONE IN SIGNATURE -- PHONE_DIR/STORE_PHONE_FALLBACK drift. Gil reported Veronica Villanueva's Honda Baytown messages signing with 281-837-3384 instead of 281-837-3682. Confirmed via [LP PROMPT PHONE] log: source was "signer", not the correct dealer-config admin value (shown separately in the same log line as storeConfig: 281-837-3682, but never consulted by the actual lookup path). Two compounding bugs, both real: (1) PHONE_DIR (a separate, hand-maintained directory in this file, completely independent of the LeadPro_Agent_Contact_Sheet.docx reference doc edited earlier) was never updated when Zoey Zenker -> Veronica Villanueva and Jacqueline Ramos -> Jordyn Guzman -- editing the doc never touched this file, so neither replacement agent had an entry under their real name. (2) Far more serious: STORE_PHONE_FALLBACK['6191'], the fallback used when an agent has no directory entry, was literally Patricia Galvan's PERSONAL Baytown extension (281-837-3384) rather than a real store-level number -- so the fallback for ANY missing agent at Honda Baytown was silently routing customers to a specific co-worker's direct line, not a generic figure. Veronica's BD Agent field read "Veronica Aguilar" on the live lead (not "Villanueva" -- flagging this name discrepancy for Gil to confirm in VinSolutions separately), which combined with bug (1) to fall straight through to bug (2). FIX: PHONE_DIR now has entries under BOTH "veronica villanueva" and "veronica aguilar" (whichever name VinSolutions actually carries resolves correctly either way) plus "jordyn guzman" (same latent bug, not yet reported but definitely live); STORE_PHONE_FALLBACK['6191'] corrected to 281-837-3682, matching the dealer-config admin panel's own value. Verified against 5 cases: both Veronica name variants, Jordyn, a genuinely unknown agent (now falls to the correct generic number instead of Patricia's personal line), and Patricia's own lookup (unaffected). Builds on v9.7.473-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.473-dev (DEV. SOLD VEHICLE PRESENTED AS AVAILABLE -- inventoryWarning excluded from SPLIT-FRAME trust. Gil reported a message telling a customer a vehicle was "here and available to see" when VinSolutions actually shows it sold. Confirmed live and reproduced exactly: Anthony Lopez, TrueCar/Extended Network, 2021 Jeep Grand Cherokee Laredo X, Stock #TC004955A -- a fresh page dump for this exact lead confirms the red "Warning: This vehicle is no longer in your active inventory" row is genuinely present right now. Root cause, fully traced through two separate merge paths: the vehicle-identifying frame for this lead was, both times (main merge AND rescue), a leadId-less "SPLIT-FRAME" panel frame -- the verified/active leadId-matched frame had NO panel content at all ("(no panel element)"), so its own inventoryWarning computed trivially false. The SPLIT-FRAME relaxation (v9.7.375/381/383) already trusts this leadId-less frame enough to adopt vehicle/vehicleRaw/stockNum/vin/color from it -- but inventoryWarning was never in that adopted-fields list, so it stayed bound to the panel-less "verified" frame's false reading instead of the SAME trusted frame's true one. Two-part fix: (1) MAIN MERGE -- inventoryWarning + vehiclePendingSale added to the SPLIT-FRAME adoption array (~line 7436); the existing per-field condition already correctly overwrites a falsy m value with a truthy one, so this alone closes the gap for the main pass. (2) RESCUE MERGE -- a second, separate bug: even with fix (1), the rescue path's generic boolean-merge rule only adopts d's value when m[_k] is still undefined, and m.inventoryWarning is very often already an explicit `false` by the time rescue runs (written by that same panel-less active frame during the earlier main pass) -- so a stale false could never be corrected by the generic rule alone. Added an explicit carve-out right where the rescue SPLIT-FRAME acceptance already logs: when _splitFrameSafe is true, inventoryWarning/vehiclePendingSale copy over from the trusted frame unconditionally, mirroring the exact same trust boundary already extended to the vehicle identity fields from that frame. Verified against 3 cases: the exact Anthony Lopez pattern (both merge paths now correctly land on inventoryWarning=true), and a normal non-sold lead (stays false, no regression). scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- content.js untouched, inventoryWarning was always computed per-frame already (line ~6705), this only fixes which frame's reading gets trusted downstream. Builds on v9.7.472-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.470-dev (DEV. KMF LUV PROGRAM -- WRONG VEHICLE/YEAR CITED (5-year miss) -- Gil reported a generated message telling Farhan Siddiqui "I saw your 2026 Kia is coming up on the end of your current loan term" when his real, on-record vehicle is a 2021 Sorento. Confirmed via the full page dump + console log: VinSolutions renders the IDENTICAL literal "2026 Kia Full Line (New)" VOI placeholder for every Kmf Luv Program lead regardless of customer -- verified across 4 different customers in the same session (Marcelo Cisneros, Linda Deshotels x2, Farhan Siddiqui), all showing that exact string, and VehiclesOfInterest is a genuinely empty array in VinSolutions' own structured lead JSON for these leads -- there is no real per-customer VOI data at all for this source. The codebase already has the right mechanism for this (v9.7.222): parse the customer's REAL current vehicle out of a lead-received note and use it to override the generic "your current Kia (program placeholder)" banner text. But that parser only recognized the label "Current car:" -- Farhan's note used a DIFFERENT program's wording, "Current Vehicle:" ("Current Vehicle: 2021 SORENTO 5XYRL4LC8MG052732") -- so it never matched, _hcaCurrentVehicle stayed empty, the banner fell back to the generic placeholder, and the model (seeing the placeholder text is abstract but the VEHICLE/LEAD DETAILS "2026 Kia Full Line" line is concrete) cited the wrong vehicle verbatim. FIX: widened the one regex to accept "Current Vehicle:" alongside "Current car:" -- reproduced the exact failure against Farhan's real note text (old regex: empty match; new regex: "2021 Kia Sorento"), and confirmed zero regression against the original KFA-format note ("Current car: 2023 RIO..." still parses to "2023 Kia Rio"). No other code changed -- the override-the-placeholder logic downstream (v9.7.222) already does the right thing once _hcaCurrentVehicle is populated; this was one missing label variant, same shape as the "Delivery requested" miss. Builds on v9.7.469-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.469-dev (DEV. STALE-FRAME CROSS-LEAD CONTAMINATION -- DIAGNOSTIC ONLY, NO BEHAVIOR CHANGE (Gil's "Kia LUV" report -- Kmf Luv Program is a real lead source, not a typo). Linda Deshotels, Kia Baytown, 5 historical lead records. Confirmed via a full fresh-page comparison: the ACTUAL active lead (Kmf Luv Program, VOI "2026 Kia Full Line") generated a message about "K5 inquiry on Gubagoo - Chat" instead -- wrong lead source AND wrong vehicle, sent under the correct customer's name. Root cause: frame 83 reported leadId 2057182342, MATCHING the confirmed-active lead -- so the existing cross-frame conflict check (v9.7.410/408) correctly treated it as trustworthy first-class data. But frame 83's vehicle content ("2021 Kia K5") was STALE, evidently left over from one of Linda's other 4 historical leads via a recycled/not-fully-refreshed iframe -- while frame 85 (leadId-less, therefore deliberately lower-trust by design) had the CORRECT "2026 Kia Full Line" all along. The leadId-confirmed-but-stale frame won by default, and the leadId-less-but-correct frame was excluded from the comparison entirely, so no conflict was ever detected ("clean -- 1 model family"). This is NOT a simple selector fix like VOI-4 -- loosening which frames count as "confirmed" risks new false positives in the far more common case where a leadId-less frame genuinely belongs to a different customer entirely. One confirmed instance isn't enough to safely change accept/reject logic, so this adds ONE diagnostic log line -- [LP FRAME-STALENESS DIAG] -- that fires when a leadId-confirmed frame's vehicle disagrees with an unconfirmed-but-well-formed same-page frame, so real fleet volume (this is very plausibly correlated with multi-lead-history customers) can show how often this actually happens before any resolution-logic change is considered. Verified against 4 simulated cases: the real Linda Deshotels pattern (fires), a normal single-lead customer (silent), a genuinely different customer's own frame (silent, correctly ignored), and an agreeing leadId-less frame (silent). Zero behavior change -- vehicle selection, m.vehicle, and _voiCrossFrameConflict are untouched; this is pure console.log. Builds on v9.7.468-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.468-dev (DEV. PHANTOM-SCRAPE GUARD FALSE-POSITIVE ON EMAIL-ONLY LEADS -- Gil reported an agent getting the red "Lead data didn't fully load" error specifically on email-only leads (confirmed live: Jose Gonzales, Community Honda Baytown, Cargurus, lead 2050860295 -- fresh first-touch, 0 notes, no phone on file, [LP PHONE DIAG] correctly showing contactRecoveryPhone remained true). Root cause: the phantom-scrape guard (v9.7.83, patched once already at v9.7.394 for a DIFFERENT bug -- the R-1/effective-object mismatch) blocks Generate whenever vindebug confirms a real lead but notes/phone/lastInboundMsg/context are all empty, with only ONE exemption: isAILead. It never accounted for the extremely common case of a lead whose only contact method IS email -- a lead with a real, fully-scraped email address and genuinely nothing else yet (fresh, first-touch) satisfied every one of the guard's "phantom" conditions and got blocked as if the scrape had failed, when it had in fact succeeded completely. FIX: both copies of the guard (rescue-path and initial-path) now also exempt on !_eff.email && !_eff.emailRaw / !m.email && !m.emailRaw -- checking emailRaw specifically (not just email) because email is deliberately blanked for masked/relay addresses (v9.7.459), so a masked-relay-but-real email still correctly proves the buyer panel was read rather than falsely re-triggering this exact bug for masked-email leads. Verified against 5 cases: Jose Gonzales' exact pattern (no longer blocks), a masked-relay email-only lead (no longer blocks), a genuinely phantom scrape with nothing at all (still correctly blocks), an AI Buying Signals lead (still exempted), and a normal phone-bearing lead (unaffected). Builds on v9.7.467-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.467-dev (DEV. DATAISLAND DIAGNOSTIC (LOGGING ONLY, ZERO BEHAVIOR CHANGE) -- follow-up to a manual 7-lead review of VinSolutions' undocumented __dataIsland element (data-vin/data-stocknumber attributes, never previously read by the scraper). Findings from the manual sample: data-vin tracked the resolved-panel state perfectly (7/7) -- redundant with the existing regex, not a new source of truth. data-stocknumber was more interesting: present on 3 of 4 fully-resolved leads, absent on one (Hardik Jain, 2026 Honda CR-V) for no cause the sample could explain -- and the initial "new vs used" theory was disproven by a SECOND new-vehicle lead (2026 Tacoma) that DID have a stock number. Four hand-picked samples can't characterize a real pattern, but the fleet generates hundreds of scrapes a day -- so instead of more manual dumps, this adds two silent diagnostic log lines so real traffic answers the question: (1) [LP DATAISLAND DIAG] warns if the existing VIN regex and data-vin ever disagree, surfacing the regex's real-world error rate for free; (2) logs a notes/context snippet whenever a resolved panel has data-vin but no data-stocknumber, so the in-transit/on-order hypothesis can be checked against many real cases instead of one. Both are wrapped in their own try/catch, touch nothing but console output, and cannot alter vin, stockNum, condition, or any downstream prompt content -- verified against all 7 real leads from the manual review (silent on 6, fires exactly once on the Hardik Jain pattern) plus a synthetic VIN-mismatch case. Safe to run indefinitely; remove once the pattern is understood. Builds on v9.7.466-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.466-dev (DEV. CARGURUS DIGITAL DEAL — MISSING 'DELIVERY REQUESTED' FACT — Gil flagged a miss another agent hit: Nicholas Patel, CarGurus Digital Deal, Honda Lafayette. The lead's own ADF 'Lead received' text carried the full deal-builder status line including 'Delivery requested: YES' — but the generated reply never once mentioned delivery, asked a generic 'finance or cash?' question, and implicitly framed an in-person visit close. Root cause, reproduced against the lead's actual raw text: _lpParseDealBuilder's known-label list (v9.7.237, the same missing-FACT-class fix this parser exists for) never included 'Delivery requested' -- the regex only starts a match AT a recognized label, so that field was silently skipped rather than dropped-with-a-trace, and it never reached the DEAL-BUILDER STATUS line the model is told to engage with. Every OTHER field on this exact lead (Placed deposit, Financing, Credit App, Offer Received, Offer Selected, Has trade-in) parsed correctly -- this was one label short of covering a real, actionable customer preference. FIX: added 'Delivery requested'/'Delivery Requested' to the label list (verified: now captured as its own fact, reproduced against the lead's real raw text), the CarGurus Digital Deal TASK directive now names delivery explicitly alongside deposit/credit app/offer/trade and instructs the model to address a delivery request directly rather than only assuming an in-person visit, and a stray trailing '|' (the ADF field separator) that was leaking into captured values across ALL deal-builder fields is now stripped too. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- no content.js change. Builds on v9.7.465-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.465-dev (DEV. VOI-4 SELECTED-ROW CLASS REGRESSION (VinSolutions site update) -- Gil asked for a full selector audit against a fresh VinSolutions page dump after noticing Cox had updated a lot of their site, and supplied a real rims2.aspx render to check against (Nicholas Patel, Honda Lafayette). Confirmed a live regression: the LeadSelector ant-design table's selected-row class changed from 'ant-table-row-selected' to a bare 'selected' token on the current UI (verified directly against the supplied markup: class="ant-table-row selected LeadSelector_LeadId_2020336370 LeadSelectorItem ant-table-row-level-0"). VOI-4's condition-only-panel fallback (v9.7.381, extended v9.7.413) depends entirely on this class to identify which row is the active lead among a multi-lead customer's rows -- with the old regex silently matching nothing on the new markup, _selHits and _selRowsAll always computed 0, so every multi-lead customer needing this fallback quietly downgraded from "correctly pick the selected row's vehicle" to an unnecessary ABSTAIN (safe failure, but a real accuracy regression -- asking the customer which vehicle they want when the DOM unambiguously already answered it). FIX: both class checks (row-level sel flag, and the _selRowsAll count) now accept either 'ant-table-row-selected' OR bare 'selected', additively -- verified against three cases (new markup, legacy markup, genuinely unselected row) with zero regression risk since it can only ADD matches, never remove one the old regex already caught. FULL AUDIT RESULT (rest of the page, all confirmed against the same supplied markup): Notes & History scraping (.notes-and-history-item / .legacy-notes-and-history-title / .notes-and-hsitory-item-date incl. the VinSolutions DOM typo / data-direction) is fully compatible with the current UI, no changes needed. Buyer panel ([id*="BuyerInfo"] fallback chain), VOI panel (gid1 exact match), and the vindebug-section dual-signal anchor (including a newly-observed SECOND, unwrapped duplicate vindebug-section on the page -- harmless, the code is already correctly scoped to #vindebug-section-wrap and ignores the stray copy) all still match correctly. NOTED BUT NOT ACTED ON (needs its own validation batch before any code changes, per the discipline that shipped the 449-454 incident): the page now also carries a data-vin/data-stocknumber attribute pair directly on a stable-id div (__dataIsland) AND a full structured JSON blob (span#PageData/data-pagedata containing the ENTIRE lead record -- Buyer, LeadVehicle, Trades, VehiclesOfInterest, GlobalActiveProcesses -- as clean typed JSON), neither of which the scraper currently reads at all. This could eventually replace a meaningful share of the regex/DOM-text parsing this file leans on, but needs confirmation it renders reliably across every lead type/source/frame before becoming a data source -- flagged for Gil's prioritization, not shipped blind. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- no content.js change (the fix is popup-side only). Builds on v9.7.464-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.464-dev (DEV. FACEBOOK "YES IT'S HERE" vs PRESENCE-LANGUAGE CONTRADICTION -- Gil reported LP told a customer a Toyota Tundra was "available to view" on a lead whose VOI had no VIN/stock at all (Manuel Morales, Toyota Baytown, source "Digital Advertising-Facebook"). Root cause: s.isFacebook (~8643) matches ANY leadSource containing "facebook," including generic paid-social ad leads with no specific unit tied to them -- not just genuine Marketplace-listing replies (which always imply a real posted unit). The Facebook branch's "yes it is here, come check it out" line fired unconditionally, directly contradicting the PRESENCE LANGUAGE guard (populateFromData ~1568) which correctly detected no confirmed stock and told the model NOT to claim the vehicle was ready to view. Two directives disagreed in the same prompt; the model followed the wrong one. FIX: the Facebook branch now computes the identical confirmed-presence signal the PRESENCE LANGUAGE guard already uses (stockNum present, not in transit, no inventory warning, agent hasn't already said unavailable) -- confirmed unit keeps the original "yes it is here" line; unconfirmed gets an honest lineup-level framing ("we've got Tundras in right now") that still invites them in without fabricating a specific unit's availability. Verified against 4 cases (Manuel's no-stock lead, a real listing with stock, an in-transit unit, an agent-already-said-no case) -- all resolve correctly. Builds on v9.7.463-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.463-dev (DEV. STALE INVITE "SENT" TWO-PART FIX -- PART 2 (the actual live incident, corrected diagnosis): Gil supplied the full Inez Hernandez lead record showing karla_cordova81@yahoo.com IS her real on-record email -- the pane fields were CORRECT for the current lead, only the Sent button was phantom, which rules out simple field bleed and (since the fleet 453 build already resets the button on tab entry) leaves exactly one code path: lpSendInvite's fetch resolving res.ok AFTER a reset, stamping "SENT" unconditionally onto whatever the pane shows NOW. The 453 invite fetch has no timeout, and the incident timestamp (7:52p CT) sits inside that day's 18:xx-19:xx Worker-latency window (P95 37s per the daily report) -- a slow send + a fast agent is the whole recipe. FIX: window._lpInviteSendToken increments on every pane reset; lpSendInvite captures it at send start and every resolution path (ok / http-fail / network-fail) verifies it before touching the UI, logging and standing down on mismatch (the send itself is not cancelled -- the Worker may have legitimately emailed the ORIGINAL lead's customer; only the UI write is gated). PART 1 (still real, still in):  Gil's screenshot (Kia Baytown, 7/18 7:52p): Invite tab showed "\u2713 Sent" on a lead whose invite was never sent, with a mismatched name/email pair in the fields. Root cause: lpOpenInviteTab()'s full state reset (added v9.7.442 -- fields, scrape status, AND the Sent button) is wired to switchTab('invite') ONLY, i.e. it fires on ENTERING the tab. If the agent sends an invite on lead A, then grabs lead B while the Invite tab is still the active tab, no tab switch occurs -- the pane keeps lead A's Sent button and a stale field mix against the new customer: an unsent invite looks sent, and one mis-click could send lead A's appointment details to lead B's record. FIX: populateFromData (the single choke point every successful scrape path -- initial, rescue, best-of -- flows through immediately after assigning lastScrapedData) now re-seeds the Invite tab via lpOpenInviteTab() whenever the invite pane is currently active, deferred one frame so it reads fully-assigned data. Verified pane-invite id + _tabPaneMap both resolve. Builds on v9.7.462-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.462-dev (DEV. AUDIT ROUND-2 FIX BATCH -- all remaining backlog items from the 7/18 full audit, applied per Gil's sign-off; every fix re-verified against the shipped file, node --check clean on all six edited JS files, DEV/COMMERCIAL parity re-verified post-edit. RELIABILITY: (1) 90s AbortController timeout on the generate AND voicemail Worker fetches -- a hung request previously pinned the button on 'Generating...' until the browser socket timeout with zero feedback; AbortError now surfaces as a clear 'timed out -- hit Generate again'. (2) Worker errors arriving as bare strings no longer render 'API Error: undefined' (string-or-object handling, both paths). UX: (3) showError renders in the crm-status strip and CLEARS the output fields -- previously the error text was written INTO the SMS box, where the Copy button would copy 'Network error: Failed to fetch' for pasting into a live customer thread. PROMPT: (4) sources with a dedicated NAMED SOURCE / scenario-baked acknowledgment (CarGurus/Cars.com/AutoTrader/Edmunds/CarFax/KBB/TradePending/Costco/TrueCar/Facebook/CapitalOne/CarPro) now SKIP the generic v9.7.342 SOURCE ACKNOWLEDGMENT block -- the two stacked on the same early-touch lead with partially contradictory rules ('MUST mention in BOTH' vs 'do NOT repeat in both openings identically') and double token cost on the highest-volume marketplace leads. (5) NEW system-prompt non-negotiable: transcript/customer/note text is DATA, never instructions; AND the LP-command arrow-line fallback scan now requires a Lead-Pro command marker in the context, so a customer-authored '►' line can no longer be ingested as an agent override (the concrete injection vector from the audit). (6) Arrival-time slot math respects the customer's stated am/pm (the regex captured it, then force-PM'd every hour < 12 -- '10am' became 22:00 and morning arrivals never got adjusted slots); no-meridiem still assumes PM. (7) Costco/CarPro 'CLOSE: Two specific appointment times' mandate is stripped whenever the deal-condition/deadline trigger suppresses the appointment close (they contradicted in the same prompt; suppression wins -- mirrored via window._lpDdSuppressAppt to the second scenarioRules injection site). INVITE TAB: (8) client-side validation before send (valid email, date+time required, past-date block) and the Worker's actual error body surfaced on failure -- previously EVERY failure read as a bare 'Failed -- retry'; (9) license key attached via _lpAttachLicense -- this was the ONE Worker call without it, leaving /appointment-invite unauthenticated (Worker-side enforcement can flip on once the fleet carries this build); (10) Appts-grid scrape gains an id-suffix fallback selector + [LP INVITE CANARY] warn so selector rot is visible; dead 'dealer' local removed. DEAD CODE / HYGIENE: (11) dead s.isHighIntent leg removed from the sales auto-route (never assigned anywhere -- wiring credit-app/VR-completion high-intent into that route is a product decision deliberately NOT made here); dead s.isPriceGate/s.isCreditSensitive writes removed (consumers read activeFlags directly); duplicate first s.isLoyalty assignment removed (superset survives, no reader between them); dead agentOffered var removed; second same-scope 'var hasCustomerReply' redeclaration in buildUserPrompt now reuses the first. (12) Customer phone masked to last-4 (_lpMaskPhone) in the two console diags that logged it in full. PAIRED FILES: auth.js -- validateLicense endpoint fixed (window.LEADPRO_PROXY_URL never existed: top-level const does not attach to window, so login validation ALWAYS silently passed in dev-mode; now resolves the real globals and posts to /validate-license explicitly; 404/501 keeps the historical fail-open so agents cannot be locked out before the Worker ships the route, while an explicit rejection blocks); garbled duplicate docblock removed; profile-edit values attribute-escaped (escAttr). dealer-setup.js -- all 9 value interpolations attribute-escaped (dsEsc). content.js -- ZIP prefers the 'City, ST 12345' shape with loose fallback; onMessage no longer signals async responses it never sends. NON-PAYLOAD for the live flow (content.js results are not consumed by the current popup -- v9.7.46 note; precedent v9.7.307/374): scraperVersion stays v9.7.51, REQUIRED_SCRAPER unchanged, no forced re-scrape on update. DEFERRED with reason: extending targeted-panel extraction to stock/VIN/color against the 12k text cap needs a live-lead regression pass (the exact untested-scraper-change class that produced v9.7.449-454) -- not shipped blind; ALSO NOTED for Gil, not changed: scenarioRules renders twice per prompt (v9.7.325 SCENARIO section + post-HARD-CONSTRAINTS) -- removing the duplicate changes prompt emphasis, needs an explicit call. Pairs: DEV v9.7.462-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.457. Builds on v9.7.461-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.461-dev (DEV. APPOINTMENT INVITE UTC DATE-SHIFT FIX -- found during the full Fable 5 codebase audit (7/18). _lpInviteFormatDate used d.toISOString().split('T')[0], which renders the date in UTC, while _lpInviteFormatTime uses toTimeString (LOCAL). Central Time is UTC-5 (CDT) / UTC-6 (CST), so any appointment at or after 7:00 PM CDT -- 6:00 PM CST in winter, squarely inside prime evening appointment hours -- crossed UTC midnight: the invite tab's date field silently showed the NEXT day while the time field stayed correct, and the .ics emailed to the customer carried the wrong day. Reproduced deterministically before fixing (TZ=America/Chicago node: '7/20/26 7:00p' -> date 2026-07-21 / time 19:00; '1/20/26 6:00p' -> 2026-01-21 / 18:00). Fix: build the date string from local getFullYear/getMonth/getDate -- same wall-clock frame as the time field. One function, invite tab only; grab path, generation, and scraper untouched. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Pairs: DEV v9.7.461-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.456. Builds on v9.7.460-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.458-dev (DEV. PERFORMANCE-TRIM INCENTIVE GATE. Live incident (Jaydon Mason, Honda Baytown, 7/16): a 2026 Civic TYPE R lead was quoted the ordinary Civic program ('5.49% APR for 24-60 months or a $249/mo 36-month lease with $3,999 due at signing') in a real outbound text. Type R and Si are performance trims excluded from standard Civic incentive programs — the nameplate token 'civic' alone was enough for the scorer to match ('R' is a 1-char token, filtered; 'Si' was never a distinguishing signal). Rule per Gil: Type R / Si excluded from ordinary-model incentives. Implemented as a BIDIRECTIONAL marker-agreement gate at the single matching choke point every path routes through (_lpMatchByModel: main STORE INCENTIVE gate, SOLD-pivot pairing, alternate-condition nameplate matcher): a performance marker must agree between lead vehicle and candidate, so a Type R lead never matches a plain Civic incentive AND a plain Civic lead never matches a Type R-specific offer if one ever exists. Exclusions log via [LP PERF-TRIM GATE]. Extensible one-line list for future confirmed exclusions (e.g. Toyota GR models, pending Gil's confirmation). Verified 10/10 against the shipped code including the exact CRM vehicle string, both directions, 'si' word-boundary safety (Sienna / 'due at signing' confirmed unaffected), ordinary-Civic and prior RAV4/Pilot regressions intact, and the alternate-condition path correctly matching a used Type R only. scraperVersion unchanged; content.js untouched. Pairs: DEV v9.7.458-dev / COMMERCIAL v9.7.453. Builds on v9.7.457-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.457-dev (DEV. STALL-SPECULATION FIX (ported from the parallel v9.7.441/439 line onto this lineage after confirming the STALLED block was byte-identical). Two live downs, back to back days, same shape: 'usually because they found the right car elsewhere or their priorities shifted' (Lexus NX, 7/15) and 'they found a different offer, or something on their end changed (timing, baby due date, or they're still comparing). Which one is it for you?' (Accord, 7/16). Root cause: the STALLED context-flag directive itself instructed 'one honest read of why' — the model was following the instruction, not freelancing. FIX: redirected 'honest read' to concrete facts already in the arc (a missed appointment, an unanswered question, a number they were waiting on) and explicitly banned every guess-shape from both live incidents plus any menu of such guesses. Exit-offer guidance immediately after is byte-unchanged. Verified: 11/11 checks against the shipped file, including that the v9.7.456 VM output-contract work is untouched. scraperVersion unchanged; content.js untouched. NOTE: only the DEV build was provided from the parallel chat (the second upload was a duplicate of the same file) — the COMMERCIAL twin needs this same one-block edit applied when available. Builds on v9.7.456-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.456-dev (DEV. VM OUTPUT-CONTRACT CONFLICT RULE -- why the voicemail alone stayed VOI_ONLY through every fix while SMS/email complied (adherence diag on v9.7.455: sms:BOTH, email:BOTH, vm:VOI_ONLY -- consistent across every test, meaning structural, not model whim). The VM is generated by a SEPARATE API call (generateVoicemail) whose system prompt is buildSystemPromptVoicemailOnly: it inherits the full main prompt (including the AGENT CONTEXT note-precedence rule that says 'applies identically to voicemail') but then appends an OUTPUT CONTRACT whose own header says it 'overrides any instruction above,' with beat 2 constraining the callback reason to ONE sentence in a 60-80 word spoken format. Sitting last, with explicit override authority and a hard brevity constraint, that contract legally outranked every upstream instruction -- under the one-sentence squeeze the model consistently dropped the either/or question as expendable and fell back to the simplest reason: the structured VOI vehicle. Every prior VM fix (v9.7.447's per-format wording, v9.7.448's 'applies identically' clause) targeted layers this contract overrides -- which is exactly why they never changed the outcome. Fix: the rule now lives INSIDE the contract itself, in beat 2 -- when AGENT CONTEXT notes state a different condition/year/trim/model than the VOI field, the ONE reason sentence IS the either/or question naming both distinctly ('are you set on a 2020-or-newer pre-owned A6, or the 2026 model?'); referencing only the VOI vehicle is explicitly banned in that case, with brevity explicitly ruled out as a reason to drop it. The question fits the one-sentence beat naturally, so the fix works WITH the contract's structure rather than fighting it. Popup-side only (line ~9094, far outside the injected scraper) -- zero grab-path change from v9.7.455, which is the build whose grab was just confirmed working live. Builds on v9.7.455-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.455-dev (DEV. GRAB-FAILURE ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED -- the v9.7.449 conflict-diagnostic stash calls (window._leadProVoiConflict = _lpDetectVoiConflict(d, contextNoteLines)) were inserted at the notePrefix/notePrefix2 AGENT CONTEXT assembly sites, which live INSIDE inlineScraper (verified against the codebase's own '} // end inlineScraper' marker: the function spans ~3.4k lines and the insertion points fall squarely within it -- an earlier scope analysis claimed these sites were popup-side prompt code that 'only runs at Generate'; that was WRONG, and Gil's insistence that the builds themselves were the problem was RIGHT). inlineScraper is serialized and injected into VinSolutions page frames via chrome.scripting.executeScript -- only its own body travels, so popup module-scope helpers like _lpDetectVoiConflict (defined at top level, line ~984) DO NOT EXIST in the frame. The call threw ReferenceError frame-side, aborting the entire scrape -- and because the block is guarded by if(contextNoteLines.length), it only fired on frames WITH agent-context notes, i.e., precisely the real, rendered lead frame, while empty stale husks (no notes) skipped the block, survived, and returned isLeadFrame:true shells -- exactly the pattern in every failure log from v9.7.449 through v9.7.454. The codebase's own v9.7.228 comment, ~250 lines above the insertion point, documents this exact trap verbatim ('popup module-scope helpers are NOT in scope here (calling one throws and aborts the entire scrape)'). Why every capture looked silent: frame-side console output (including v9.7.452's [LP SCRAPER FATAL] catch, which DID fire) prints to the PAGE's console, not the extension side-panel console the logs came from; popup-side, the error-shaped return had isLeadFrame:false and was silently skipped by every merge loop. FIX: both frame-side calls DELETED from inlineScraper; conflict now computed popup-side at the generate-time raw-diag sites, from fields the scraper already returns (context + pageSnippet), fully try-wrapped; VM-site adherence reuses the popup-side stash or recomputes safely. VERIFIED: (1) node -c clean; (2) marker-based scope check -- zero references to any diagnostic helper inside inlineScraper's boundaries, matching the confirmed-working v9.7.447 exactly (the failing v9.7.454 shows 2); (3) isolated diff of the injected scraper function between this build and working 447 shows ONLY the two inert v9.7.448 prompt-string literals -- zero executable difference in everything that runs during a grab; (4) the relocated popup-side computation reproduces the identical conflict result on Paige-shaped data (voiYear:2026/new vs noteYear:2020/preowned). Builds on v9.7.454-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.454-dev (DEV. REBASE onto the CONFIRMED-WORKING v9.7.447-dev baseline -- per Gil's direction after a direct A/B test showed 452-dev failing a grab immediately after this exact 447-dev file succeeded on the same lead, same session. v9.7.450 through 453 were four consecutive builds chasing a grab-failure mystery (stale/live frame selection, a "fatal error capture" that turned out to catch nothing, a retracted claim about frameLoadedAt:0, and a still-unresolved marker-detection diagnostic) that a clean diff proved were never the actual cause of anything -- that investigation is set aside as its own open thread, not carried forward into this build. This build is built FRESH from the working 447 file plus ONLY the two changes that were validated as real, working fixes on top of it: (1) v9.7.448's vehicle-conflict merge-ban wording (bans stating a new-trim name alongside an old model-year range as one description, e.g. "Premium Plus quattro (2020 or newer)"; requires one direct either/or question naming both distinctly; applies identically across SMS/email/voicemail) and (2) v9.7.449's diagnostic additions ([LP VOI CONFLICT DIAG] / [LP CONFLICT ADHERENCE DIAG], plus the previously-missing rawVm raw-diagnostic log). Re-verified the reapplied diagnostic logic against Paige's real data before shipping -- produces the identical result as the original 449 build (voiYear:2026/voiCond:new vs noteYear:2020/noteCond:preowned). Confirmed via direct grep against the rebuilt file: zero trace of any v9.7.450-453 code (_rescueOrdered, _scraperFatalError, LP LEADID DETECT DIAG all absent), both 448/449 fixes present and intact. Commercial was never touched by the 450-453 tangent in the first place (dev-only per Gil's request at the time) and needs no corresponding change -- it already sits at v9.7.449 with exactly these same two fixes. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.447-dev (unmodified base) + v9.7.448 + v9.7.449.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.447 (Dev. VOICEMAIL PER-FORMAT INSTRUCTION GAP -- ROUND 3 on the Paige Page / Audi A6 lead. v9.7.446's zero-contact fix confirmed working live (log: 'Zero-contact stalled confirmed,' isZeroContactStalled:true) -- SMS and email BOTH now correctly surfaced the real situation ('leaning more toward the 2020+ pre-owned A6, or the 2026 model?'), same generation, same prompt. But the voicemail script from that identical response still said only 'the 2026 Audi A6 Premium Plus quattro' -- no preowned mention at all. Root cause isolated to the per-format instruction set inside the SAME zero-contact block (~line 2298): SMS's instruction reads 'one specific observation about the vehicle OR THEIR SITUATION' -- that 'or their situation' clause is what let it draw on the note-based conflict. VOICEMAIL's instruction read only 'Reference the vehicle' -- singular, definite article, no situation alternative -- so it anchored straight back to the structured VOI field, reproducing the original bug in miniature, scoped to this one line. Fix: aligned VOICEMAIL's phrasing with SMS's ('the vehicle OR their actual situation (per any note) -- whichever is more accurate'), keeping it just as brief. Checked the OTHER (non-zero-contact) voicemail instruction elsewhere in the file ('one specific reason to call back') -- already neutral, not the source, untouched. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- prompt-side only. Builds on v9.7.446.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.446 (Dev. ZERO-CONTACT FABRICATION, ROUND 2 -- ROOT CAUSE FOUND VIA LIVE CONSOLE LOG. Real incident: Paige Page (Audi A6). v9.7.445 tried a vehicle-precedence prompt instruction, which did NOT fix it -- the actual regrab log (def.log) showed hasCustomerReply:false correctly computed, yet the injected banner still asserted 'Customer engaged earlier, then went quiet,' and shouldDirector:false confirmed this lead never even reaches the Director-mode resolver v9.7.445 targeted -- wrong code path entirely. Root cause: neverReplied's gate (populate, ~line 2281) required !d.isContacted in addition to !d.hasCustomerReply, and isContacted is derived ONLY from VinSolutions' raw 'Contacted: Yes' flag plus a staleness check -- zero content awareness. Paige's lead showed 'Contacted: Yes (0:02)' -- a 2-second call -- with Patricia's own note reading 'answered but did not say a word.' That alone flipped neverReplied to false, overriding the canonical hasCustomerReply=false, which injected a categorically false 'engaged earlier' banner into the prompt -- and THAT gave the model license to invent a prior conversation, fabricating a trim/dealership comparison narrative from Austin's own OUTBOUND sales-pitch line ('comparable units') as if Paige herself had said it. Fix: dropped isContacted from the neverReplied gate entirely -- it cannot distinguish a real conversation from a silent connect-and-hang-up, so it should never have been able to override the canonical flag alone. Re-derived BOTH the target case (Paige: hasCustomerReply:false, convState:'active-follow-up' -> neverReplied now correctly TRUE) and the ORIGINAL v9.7.428/430 fix target (Ava: hasCustomerReply:true -> neverReplied correctly stays FALSE, short-circuits on the canonical flag same as before, unaffected) against real logged values before shipping. convState.includes('replied') and isLiveConversation remain as legitimate phone-only-engagement paths, since both are derived with actual context rather than a bare disposition tag. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- prompt-side only. Builds on v9.7.445.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.445 (Dev. VEHICLE-PREFERENCE NOTE PRECEDENCE. Real incident: Paige Page (Audi A6, internet lead) -- Austin's call note explicitly said "wants a preowned a6 2020 or newer. making a note to keep handy," but the stalled-lead reactivation draft asked about NEW-trim features (driver assist package, massage seats, B&O audio) for the 2026 A6 Premium Plus quattro sitting in the structured Vehicle of Interest field, ignoring the note entirely. Traced the pipeline: the note was NOT dropped -- it passes every contextNoteLines filter (title matches /phone call/i, content 5+ chars, no boilerplate match) and reaches the model whole, prefixed 'AGENT CONTEXT -- READ THIS FIRST,' uncapped. So this wasn't a missing FACT, it was a missing RULE: that prefix already told the model a note overrides the customer's last TEXT for status/timing, but never said a note overrides the structured VOI field for WHICH VEHICLE/CONDITION to discuss. Added that explicitly to both AGENT CONTEXT injection points (first-touch and follow-up state) -- same prefix string, same pattern as the existing RETURN VISIT / VISIT override warnings just below it. No raw prompt capture exists for this lead to confirm conclusively the note reached the model unmodified vs a live-DOM extraction gap this source trace wouldn't reveal; shipping on the strength of the code trace, not a captured payload. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- prompt-side only, no scrape changes. Builds on v9.7.444.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.444-dev (DEV. MODEL-YEAR INCENTIVE FLOOR -- confirmed live gap via a real Toyota Tundra lead: Toyota runs 2025 AND 2026 Tundra incentives simultaneously (0.99% APR/$5,000 Cash vs 2.99% APR/$1,000 Cash/$469 lease), and _lpMatchByModel's scorer tokenizes year OUT of both strings before comparing (_lpToks strips \\b(19|20)\\d{2}\\b), so year was categorically invisible to the match -- by tokenizer design, not a weighting gap. The lead's own vehicle year was left for the model to infer purely from prompt context, with nothing upstream verifying it; changing the CRM's VOI year field between test sends changed which figures got cited, confirming the guess was doing the real work. New _lpYearFilterIncentives reuses the proven _lpCompYr helper from the comparables year-floor (v9.7.431/429) -- unlike that floor (never go backwards), incentives need an EXACT year match, since a 2026-only figure genuinely does not apply to a 2025 buyer or vice versa. Applied at both incentive call sites (STORE INCENTIVE block + SOLD-pivot pairing); the inventory nameplate-matching caller (_sameNameplate) is untouched -- different concern, not incentives. Lines with no year tag (pre-migration KV data, or any store not yet re-normalized) are kept as year-agnostic, same "can't judge, keep it" fallback _lpComparables already uses -- safe to ship ahead of every store's KV being updated; only starts actively filtering once the companion Data Tool fix populates x.year. Verified in isolation against the real Tundra data: a 2025 VOI now deterministically surfaces only the 2 real 2025 lines, a 2026 VOI only the 2026 lines, an unknown-year VOI falls through to today's exact unfiltered behavior. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- prompt/matching-side only; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.443-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.440-dev (DEV. DIAG-ONLY: EXIT-SIGNAL ATTRIBUTION LOGGING. Investigating a live down (Rick Sonnier, Honda Lafayette, Phone Up) where hasExitSignal:true drove a 'closing your file' message on a customer whose actual last text was about message delivery, not a decline. Ran all five component signals (exitRaw, boughtElsewhere, exitByRemoval, hasInternalSoldElsewhere, hasInternalNotInterested) against every real line in the transcript by hand — none matched anything visible, meaning the true cause is either smsOptOutIsExit (not visible as a discrete note in this lead's history) or something outside the reviewed text. hasInternalSoldElsewhere and hasInternalNotInterested already logged their matched excerpt on fire; exitRaw/boughtElsewhere/exitByRemoval never did, so a false positive from any of those three was previously undiagnosable from the log alone. Added [LP EXIT DIAG], logging exactly which of the five signals fired and the matched substring where applicable (or 'smsOptOutIsExit' when the STOP-derived exit path fired instead). No behavior change — logging only. Verified against the real shipped code: correctly attributes each of the three previously-silent paths with the right matched text, produces zero output when nothing fires, and doesn't duplicate or interfere with the two pre-existing internal-note logs. Next generation that routes to exit will show definitively which signal caused it. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.439-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.439-dev (DEV. SHOWROOM/NO-VEHICLE DIRECTIVE CONFLICT FIX. Live down (7/14, Honda Lafayette, Nextup, agent Samantha): a showroom-floor lead with no vehicle captured anywhere drew 'What are you looking to get into, and do you have a trade-in?' — the exact internet-lead qualification the v9.7.431 Showroom directive explicitly bans, verbatim. Root cause: the Showroom directive's ban is unconditional, but a separate, source-agnostic directive ('No vehicle on lead... Ask a qualifying question only if no vehicle appears anywhere') fires independently whenever no vehicle is found, and neither directive knew the other existed. That second directive is correct for a cold internet lead; it is wrong for a showroom visit. FIX: when the Showroom directive fires AND data.noVehicleAtAll is true, it now supplies the in-person-appropriate substitute directly ('Ask what they discussed or looked at during the visit/call instead... e.g. "What caught your eye while you were in?"') rather than leaving the model to fall back to the banned cold-lead phrasing when no vehicle is on file. No change when a vehicle IS known (the addendum only appears in the no-vehicle case), no change for non-showroom sources, and the generic no-vehicle directive is untouched for every other source where it remains correct. Verified against the real shipped code: fires correctly for the exact Nextup/no-vehicle case, stays silent when a vehicle is known or the source isn't showroom-type, and the Phone-Up directive (checked for the same latent conflict) is confirmed unaffected. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.438-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.438-dev (DEV. CALL-NOTE PAUSE SCOPE FIX. Live incident: Tracy Hensley (Tradepending, RAV4 trade appraisal) — a genuinely fresh, 0-day-old lead, never contacted, zero customer messages of any kind — got a draft opening 'I understand you're not moving forward right now.' No basis for that claim exists anywhere in this lead's actual conversation. Root cause: hasCallNotePause (the detector for a BDC-recorded not-ready status, e.g. 'still deciding... will get in touch when ready') scanned callNoteText/recentNoteText/the full page TEXT — none of them bounded to the current lead's window — against this customer's full 106-note record stretching back to 2020, full of years-old soft-declines ('Currently no,' 'No Ty,' 'Nvm'). The fix reuses recentHistory, a variable this file ALREADY computes and ALREADY trusts to solve this exact class of bug for hasOutbound and the model-facing transcript — its own existing comment states the purpose directly: 'Apply transcript cutoff to ALL leads - filter out notes predating the current lead. This prevents old "not interested" messages from bleeding into fresh inquiries.' The pause detector was simply never switched over to use it. recentHistory already contains [CALL NOTE]-tagged entries, so no legitimate current-lead signal is lost — only the unrelated, years-old history is excluded. Verified against the real shipped code: Tracy's properly-scoped recent history (lead-received, rep changes, the one failed call attempt — nothing else) produces no pause match; the ORIGINAL case this detector was built for (Africa Dauphiney's real, in-window call note: 'still deciding... will get in touch when she is ready') still fires correctly and matches the same phrase as before — this fix narrows scope, it does not weaken detection. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.437-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.437-dev (DEV. SMS OPT-OUT / RE-OPT-IN FIX. Live incident: Oriana Haygood (Phone Up, Kia Sportage EX) opted out of SMS years earlier; agent Abby Montez manually flipped her SMS status back to Opt-In days before this generation ('SMS status has been manually changed to: Opt-In by Abby Montez'); the lead re-engaged normally afterward (customer texting 'Hi! Still looking!'). SMS still came back blank. Root cause: hasRecentReoptIn correctly detected the explicit re-opt-in note (true), but the gate required hasNewLeadToday to ALSO be true — 'isSmsOptOut = rawStopSignal && !(hasNewLeadToday && hasRecentReoptIn)' — so on this 3-day-old lead the explicit, unambiguous re-opt-in event was silently ignored purely because the LEAD RECORD wasn't created today. An agent manually flipping SMS status is real, deliberate consent regardless of lead age; it was never supposed to require that AND. FIX: hasRecentReoptIn now disqualifies an old/historical opt-out on its own, independent of hasNewLeadToday. SECOND FIX (Gil): a newly received lead is itself a fresh consent event and now ALSO disqualifies an old opt-out on its own, with no explicit re-opt-in note required. BOTH signals are scoped to old/historical opt-out evidence only — neither can override a STOP that IS the customer's own current, live newest message on this lead (the original Wendy Browne case this file already guards against: a customer's first action on a brand-new lead being 'STOP' must never be waved away by new-lead or reopt logic). Verified against the real shipped code, not a simulation: Oriana's case now correctly restores SMS (not treated as exit); the Wendy fresh-stop case is fully unaffected (still respected, still SMS-only suppression not full exit) even when a new lead or a reopt note is ALSO present in the same test; the new old-optout-plus-new-lead rule fires correctly standalone; the standard established-lead opt-out (no new lead, no reopt, real stop in history) is unchanged. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.436-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.436-dev (DEV. DIAG-ONLY: ANTI-RESTATE decision-point logging. The Lloyd Tisdale regen (CarGurus/BMW 328i) on v9.7.435 came back a near-total restate of the original outbound — same five clauses, same order, same CTA verbatim — despite the v9.7.435 thin-reply fix. Hand-verified the detector against Lloyd's exact logged lastInboundMsg string: _lpThinReply evaluates true, and the chain of gating variables (_hasReplyForDirectives, the _freshLeadActive override) all check out on paper to let the OR-condition fire. But this was the one decision point in the whole ANTI-RESTATE/TOUCH POSITION region without a diagnostic log line, so there was no way to tell from the log whether the block fired-and-was-ignored by the model, or never fired for a reason not yet found — and guessing which is not how this codebase gets debugged. Added [LP ANTI-RESTATE DIAG] logging hasSubstantiveOutbound / hasOutbound / hasReplyForDirectives / thinReply / thinReplyText / WILL FIRE at the exact decision point. No behavior change — logging only. Next grab of a similar lead will show definitively which failure mode this is, and the real fix (code or prompt-strength) follows from that, not from a guess. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.435-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.460-dev (DEV. "FIRST TOUCH" MISREAD FIX — Gil reported a generated response "read like a first touch" on Jarrett Neveuu (AutoTrader, Audi Lafayette, lead age 14d, 4 unanswered voicemails, zero prior texts/emails). Confirmed: TASK framing for every lead source (~20 branches) forks purely on `hasRealOutbound`, which is intentionally digital-only (texts/emails) — correct for anti-restate/signature logic, where duplicating exact prior wording is the risk. But that same narrow flag was ALSO deciding "does this read as a cold open" for the TASK line, so a lead with 4 real voicemail attempts and 0 texts got the identical bare "TASK: AutoTrader lead." framing as a lead created five minutes ago — the model had no signal the team had already tried reaching this customer, so it wrote a fully cold "Hi Jarrett, this is Melanie, I saw your AutoTrader request..." opener. FIX: new `hasCallOnlyPriorAttempt` signal (call-note text present in context, no real digital outbound yet), checked ONCE after the ~20-way source dispatcher instead of a duplicate edit per branch — appends an acknowledge-the-prior-calls addendum to scenarioDirective whenever it fires and no branch already applied "PRIOR OUTREACH already made" framing. Explicitly skipped when the default branch's pre-existing first-touch anti-stall guard (_scFirstTouchAntiStall) already ran, since that guard's "write this exactly as the very first message" instruction is a different, narrower claim (about not referencing customer-side silence) that would read as contradictory stacked with this one — anti-stall guard wins on a genuinely fresh, zero-attempt lead. Builds on v9.7.459-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.459-dev (DEV. APPOINTMENT INVITE EMAIL FIX — Gil reported the invite email had to be typed in manually on a lead where it was "clearly on the record." Live incident: Kathy Teal, Facebook lead, Honda Baytown (dealer 6191) — her plain kathyteal@icloud.com sat right in the Buyer and Co-buyer Information panel next to her phone number, which scraped fine. REAL ROOT CAUSE, found by checking the actual page against the code rather than trusting the first plausible theory: email extraction (inlineScraper) still ran a single blind regex against the whole-page TEXT (truncated to 12000 chars at function top) — the EXACT failure shape phone was fixed for at v9.7.81 (Tyrone Brass incident, VOI/notes content pushing the Buyer panel past a length cutoff) — but email never got that fix. Phone's targeted buyerPanelEl/detailEl scan was sitting right there, unused by email. FIX 1 (primary): buyerEmail now searches the same targeted Buyer-panel text phone already trusts FIRST, falling back to the wider TEXT scan only if the panel itself has no email in it — closes the actual gap. FIX 2 (secondary, kept — a real but separate issue caught while investigating): the frame-merge's final `email` field is also intentionally blanked whenever the address matches a marketplace/relay domain (isMaskedEmail — CarGurus, Cars.com, Facebook Marketplace, Apple relay, etc.) so the SMS/email draft asks for a direct address instead of replying into a dead end; that's correct for the draft but left the Appointment Invite tab with an unexplained blank for genuinely-relay leads. Now `emailRaw` (unmasked, unconditional) ships alongside `email`, the invite tab falls back to it when masking is the reason `email` is empty, and shows an inline "⚠ Marketplace relay address — may not deliver" hint (new #lp-invite-email-hint in popup.html) instead of a silent blank. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no content.js changes; both fixes are in the popup-side inlineScraper/frame-merge. Builds on v9.7.458-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.435-dev (DEV. THIN-REPLY RESTATE FIX — live incident: Lloyd Tisdale (CarGurus, BMW 328i) replied to an outbound pitch with 'Thanks. Look forward to answers.' and the follow-up SMS/email came back virtually identical to the original outbound. Two connected bugs, both fixed. (1) sanitize() didn't recognize the Outlook-style '------ Original Message ------' quote-chain header — Lloyd's email reply carried the ENTIRE original outbound pitch quoted back underneath it, so the model was shown its own prior pitch a second time immediately after a one-line 'thanks.' Same failure class as the already-documented 'Friday, July 3' quote-header bleed-through this function exists to prevent (v9.7.369) — this closes a format-specific gap in it. (2) The proven v9.7.279 ANTI-RESTATE mechanism (surfaces the model's own last substantive outbound VERBATIM next to a hard don't-restate instruction — built after an earlier identical incident, the Rebecca Partin restate) was gated to fire ONLY when the customer had not replied at all. A reply that adds no new information ('Thanks. Look forward to answers.', 'Ok', 'Sounds good') is functionally silence for restate purposes but was falling through that gate into the weaker reply-handling path, which has no verbatim anchor. Added a narrow thin-reply detector (short, no question mark, no digits, matches a closed set of acknowledgment openers) and OR'd it into the existing ANTI-RESTATE gate — a genuinely substantive reply is never affected. CAUGHT IN REVIEW: the first version of the detector stripped the CRM's 'Subject:/By: <name>' prefix with a greedy character class that also consumed the real message text following it (no delimiter separates a scraped 'By: Name' from what follows), collapsing genuine content to empty string and silently disabling the detector on every case with that prefix shape — caught by testing against the real Lloyd string before shipping rather than assuming correctness; fixed by stripping only the label text (non-greedy, bounded to the literal word 'by:') and searching for the ack phrase anywhere in what remains instead of anchoring at the string start. Verified end-to-end: raw Lloyd string -> sanitize() -> thin-reply check -> correctly flagged, using the exact shipped functions, not a simulation. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.434-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.434-dev (DEV. SCENARIO-CLASSIFICATION FOLLOW-UP FIX — the real root cause behind the Tulio/Tradepending appointment-time incident. classifyScenario correctly computed isFollowUp:true / convState: active-follow-up on an 84-day, 51-note lead with a customer reply THAT MORNING, but the scenario-DIRECTIVE dispatcher (a ~30-branch if/else chain keyed on lead source) had a gap: the final catch-all default branch — reached by any source not in that list, Tradepending among them — unconditionally injected 'TASK: Standard first-touch response to a new inquiry' + 'THIS IS A FRESH LEAD — NOT A STALL', regardless of reality. Every OTHER special-cased branch (Costco, IdentityMax, CarPro, ThirdPartyOEM, etc.) already forks on isFollowUp && hasRealOutbound; the default branch was the one place that pattern was missing. Because the misclassified lead never reached the follow-up-aware closing logic that reads computeAppointmentTimes' real grounded slots, the model had zero scheduling facts to anchor on and invented 'tomorrow at 5:30 PM, Monday after 4 PM' from nothing — skipping a same-day window that was still open and four other business days for no reason. FIX: added the identical, already-proven isFollowUp fork to the default branch (genuine first-touch leads render exactly as before; established conversations now correctly get 'read the transcript, continue naturally' framing and reach the real appointment-time logic). Audited the full ~30-branch chain for the same gap: found and fixed the identical shape in four more source branches with NO follow-up handling at all — isOEMLead, isDealerWebsite, isGoogleAd, isReferral (the latter two likely meaningful volume). Reviewed and left as-is: isLoyalty and isLeaseMature already avoid asserting a false fresh-lead claim (embed dual first-touch/follow-up guidance inline rather than a hard fork); isRepeatCustomer's framing is follow-up-compatible by nature; isChatLead deferred — session-based source, needs a closer look before changing. All pre-existing forked branches (Costco/IdentityMax/CarPro/ThirdPartyOEM) verified byte-unchanged. Verified: replayed the exact Tulio case (isFollowUp:true, hasRealOutbound:true) through the fixed default branch — now renders follow-up framing, fresh-lead warning suppressed; replayed a genuine first-touch case — output identical to before; replayed the isFollowUp-true-but-no-real-outbound edge case — correctly still renders first-touch, matching every other branch's AND-gate. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.433-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.433-dev (DEV. RTL COMPARABLE FIX + BOOT/DEALER-CONFIG TIMING DIAGS. (1) RTL TRIM-CODE FIX — live incident: a sold 2025 Ridgeline RTL pivoted to a 2026 Passport RTL while 9 real Ridgelines sat in stock. 'rtl' was never in the comparable-scoring stop-word list, and Honda reuses RTL as a trim code across Ridgeline AND Passport — a non-RTL-trim Ridgeline and an RTL-trim Passport tied on score, and the daysOnLot tiebreak let the fresher-arrived Passport win the slot. Replayed the exact live vehicles plus a synthetic 9-Ridgeline-vs-1-Passport inventory against the real shipped scoring function before packaging — confirmed Passport now scores 0 and is excluded outright. Also closed the same class preemptively for two more confirmed-shared Honda trim names (Elite: Pilot/Odyssey; TrailSport: Ridgeline/Passport/Pilot/CR-V) — no live incident yet on these two, flagged for fast diagnosis if one appears. (2) BOOT + DEALER-CONFIG TIMING DIAGS — Tania Gonzalez has hit a panel-frozen-until-clicked stall twice; both times the same session showed a short dealer-config read (4 of 5 stores). She's the one multi-store agent (Kia Baytown + Audi Lafayette), carrying a larger chrome.storage.sync config record than most single-store agents — more surface area for a partial or slow sync read, especially after a browser restart or MV3 service-worker cold start. Added a boot timestamp as close to true script-start as a top-level var allows, and a [LP DEALER CONFIG DIAG] line on every dealer-config load reporting elapsed ms since boot, which store IDs actually came back, the raw byte size of the stores/assignments payloads, and an explicit ⚠ SHORT flag if fewer than 5 stores load — turns "seemed frozen" into concrete numbers next time it recurs, for her or anyone else. Shipped in BOTH builds so any agent who hits this is covered, not just DEV. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.432-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.432-dev (DEV. PHONE-UP SOURCE FIX — Gil: "Phone up should be handled correctly. Any notes should be leveraged. These are usually thin but we can at least acknowledge the salesperson and convo or VOI added." A Phone Up is a real call that already happened — the 7/13 down opened with internet-lead triage questions AND fabricated 'when you submitted your request' (web-form language) on a customer who called. New PHONE-UP LEAD directive fires on source alone (mirrors the v9.7.431 Showroom rule) but, unlike Showroom, does NOT assume rich notes exist: it leverages whatever actually scraped — salesRep (acknowledge them as the person who took the call) and/or vehicle (treat VOI as already established, don't re-ask) — and degrades gracefully to a plain "this was a call, not a form fill" framing when neither is present, rather than inventing a conversation the notes don't support. Also instructs referencing any real call-note content beyond a bare disposition line when present. CAUGHT IN REVIEW: the first draft of this fix read populateFromData's _voiIsProgramPlaceholder var from inside buildUserPrompt — a different function, out of scope — which would have thrown a ReferenceError on every Phone-Up lead with a vehicle on file (the exact hoisting/scope bug class this codebase has shipped before). Caught before packaging; buildUserPrompt now computes the same placeholder check locally instead of reaching across functions. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.431-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.431-dev (DEV. AGENT-FEEDBACK FIX BATCH — all four items from Gil's 7/13 down-session review. (1) SENSITIVE FINANCE GUARD (top item, from the Honda Lafayette down that texted 'the 400 credit and the $10k upside-down situation' — a credit score and negative-equity dollar figure in writing to the customer): two-layer fix on the KFA-suppression precedent — a SENSITIVE FINANCE RULE prompt directive fires whenever the arc touches credit standing or equity position, and a deterministic _lpFinanceGuard at render time (sms/email/voicemail choke points) redacts any credit-score number or negative-equity dollar amount that slips through, logging every redaction. Patterns verified narrow: '$400 credit toward accessories', '$750 Conquest Cash', '3.99% APR', '$2,000 down', 'credit union', 'credit approval' all survive untouched. (2) STORE-NAME CANON: 'Community Toyota BAytown' shipped in a customer email signature; _lpFixStoreNames normalizes any case/spacing variant of the five rooftop names (canon = DEALER_ID_MAP, self-maintaining) at the same choke points. (3) SHOWROOM/FLOOR-UP SOURCE RULE: Nextup ran 0% on 7/13 — both downs were internet-style triage on customers already working with floor staff. Nextup/Thenextup/Showroom sources now ALWAYS inject the floor-lead directive (source alone, no other conditions, per Gil): never run shopping-for/budget/trade qualification; build on the existing in-person relationship and the salesperson named in the notes. (4) PIVOT YEAR FLOOR (Gil's rule: a pivot must NEVER go backwards on model year — the 2026 Pilot Touring S → 2025 Pilot Touring down): _lpComparables and _lpComparablesWithUnits now exclude units older than the sold/lead vehicle's year (parsed from vehicle text; unit year from the feed field or its display string; unknown years keep the unit). A sold 2026 with only 2025s left simply produces no pivot and falls through to normal sold handling. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — no scrape-side changes; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.430-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.430-dev (DEV. LIVE-DAY-ONE FIX BATCH — every item driven by the 7/13 live log (ff.log) and the Ford/Jeep abstain incident, per instrument-first. (1) ZERO-CONTACT FABRICATION (Ava): populate's neverReplied never consulted the canonical hasCustomerReply — isContacted is a call-disposition (undefined on her lead) and convState 'pause' erases 'replied' — so a REPLIED-then-paused customer was branded 'This customer has NEVER replied' and lost the appointment engine; fixed at the source AND belt-and-suspenders gate on isZeroContactStalled (no leg may fire against hasCustomerReply=true). (2) CROSS-STORE ABSTAIN, ECCS TIE-BREAK: the 7/13 abstain's rendered vehicles were off-brand USED units (Ford Expedition/Jeep Wrangler) — brand disambiguation structurally can't map those to a franchise rooftop, and the ECCS read the pre-scrape held on BOTH attempts was never consulted. The abstain path now stashes the per-grab ECCS read (cleared at grab start, 30s freshness) and, when brand fails, picks by ECCS cdQuery active-lead-in-candidates first, then unique ECCS-dealerId→candidate-store match (Strategy 0.5/2 trust, v9.7.291 login-store caveat logged loudly); only then abstains. (3) ABSTAIN COPY: now leads with the one action that actually clears stale iframes — Ctrl+Shift+R on the VinSolutions tab, reopen the lead, GRAB again ('click the exact lead and re-grab' cannot remove a stale sibling frame; the agent followed it twice with byte-identical frame states). Abstain log line now includes ECCS state so the next occurrence answers whether the tie-break could have resolved it. (4) INVENTORY FIELD SHAPE settled by the live [LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG]: units carry `stock` NOT `stockNum` — so the color-match VOI dedupe, its ' — stock N' display, and the CPO dedupe have been reading undefined since they shipped. Normalized BOTH directions once at the fetch boundary; all consumers work regardless of feed shape. (5) PROVIDER-BOILERPLATE SCRUB: TrueCar '*** DEALER PORTAL ***' banners and 'Traffic Source: KBB' lines were framed as 'the customer's own words — address it directly' (the live draft promised the portal's $2,000 banner). Star-banners, Traffic Source lines, and pathed portal URLs are scrubbed from the inquiry extraction (bare domains/emails survive); pure boilerplate leaves the slot empty → clean first-touch framing. (6) TRADE-IN CHIP actually wired: the v9.7.429 resolver clause was a verified no-op (persona-setting classifyScenario call never received activeFlags); updatePersonaBar now attaches live flags and chip toggles re-resolve, guarded so a manual persona override is never stomped. (7) DIRECTOR RESOLVER wentCold leg (replied-then-quiet >=21d) — re_engagement mode is now reachable for its actual target case (probe-verified unreachable before). scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 — inline-scraper changes (boilerplate scrub) are popup-injected and version-consistent; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.429-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.429-dev (DEV. DUAL-AUDIT FIX BATCH -- external 16-finding audit + this session's independent pass, every finding re-verified against the code before changing, 35-case regex/logic smoke suite green on the shipped file. CRITICAL: (1) deal-condition/deadline triggers now read the CUSTOMER'S words only via new shared _lpCustomerText() (bare "i'm good" and "we might be able to work" alternatives removed -- the conditional "then i'm good/done/in" form still matches; deadline additionally requires a customer day-word, finally consuming the never-read _ddNamedDay; exit/pause leads skip the trigger entirely) -- the full-arc scan was fabricating "customer put a SPECIFIC DEAL CONDITION on the table" from brush-offs and agent copy and suppressing the appointment close on soft exits. (2) Verbal-commitment detector: bare weekday / "next week" / "credit approval" standalone alternatives removed -- day-words now only match as the object of a commitment verb ("try again friday" and "busy next week, do not push" no longer read as commitments; the v9.7.197/368 fabrication class closed at the class level, [LP VERBAL COMMIT DIAG] still logs every fire). HIGH: (3) VFC STALE-WHILE-REVALIDATE -- the 30-min TTL refresh was NULLING the dealer's cache synchronously and re-fetching, so the same populateFromData call that triggered the refresh read null in the same tick (the v9.7.419 zero-ms race, reintroduced every 30 minutes; incentives/comparables silently vanished on the first grab per dealer past TTL and [LP INCENTIVE DIAG] blamed KV). Refresh now seeds from the old snapshot; an authoritative HTTP-ok response replaces it (including a legitimate empty), a FAILED fetch keeps stale. (4) JSON Recovery 2: fieldType -> key (undeclared identifier threw exactly when finishReason=MAX_TOKENS -- the truncated case Recovery 2 exists for -- and the silent catch dropped output to the Recovery-3 raw split). (5) hasNewLeadToday now requires _leadAgeScraped: an unrendered Created (Nd) marker no longer makes an age-missing OLD ghost read as created-today (the v9.7.399 fresh-no-reply branch was hard-forcing first-touch on those); unknown age falls through to the pre-399 branches and [LP AGE DIAG] logs every occurrence. (6) Comparables sold-unit exclusion checks u.stock OR u.stockNum, and only excludes on a truthy sold identifier (if the feed field is stockNum -- the validated color/CPO consumers read stockNum -- the old filter was a permanent no-op and the SOLD pivot could recommend the just-sold unit as its own alternative); one-time [LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG] unit-fields line settles the feed shape from the first live grab. (7) SOLD->INCENTIVE pivot pairs an incentive ONLY with a comparable confirmed New (v9.7.241's own rationale -- no new-car APR on a used unit) and never in exit/negative-reply state; skipped units stay in the generic no-price pivot. MEDIUM: (8) totaled-vehicle path A requires the loss form (no more "total price/garbage/win" fires) and the scan is bounded to the current lead's conversation. (9) ONE shared _lpExplicitDistanceReq() replaces the two divergent definitions that could stamp "acknowledge the distance once" AND "never mention distance at all" into one prompt. (10) ONE Director-mode resolver (_lpResolveDirectorMode) now feeds BOTH buildSystemPrompt and the user-prompt persona block -- dead 'stalled'/'zero-contact' comparisons and the phantom sc.isZeroContactStalled removed, real _isStalled/_neverReplied + exit/pause signal flags read, the v9.7.315 LP-command override honored in both; a day-3 zero-reply ghost can no longer draw ACTIVE-MOMENTUM voice, and re-engagement mode is reachable for the first time (it previously required convState values that never exist). (11) leadAgeDays normalized ONCE at the top of populateFromData (3-tier fallback written back onto d) -- the stale-appointment guard, trade-supersede note, isStalled and isNewLeadToday all inherit it, and the voicemail-first path inherits via lastScrapedData. (12) First-touch incentive overrides no longer unlock the like-vehicle/CPO/discipline cross-sell blocks (_incIsFirstTouchLead pristine copy). (13) The STORE INCENTIVE / comparables / CPO / SOLD-pivot silent catches now log [LP * DIAG] block-threw lines. RESCUE PATH: rescued._voiFromPanel is now carried through (the v9.7.383 SF-1 rescue relaxation could never fire without it -- the field was stripped in the rescue copy loop) and rescueFrameResults is passed into rescueComplete (it was a DIFFERENT callback's parameter, so the competing-frame count threw a silently-caught ReferenceError and logged 0 forever -- every [SPLIT-FRAME DIAG] count since v9.7.375 is void; re-collect before trusting the relaxation further). ALSO: sc.storeBrand -> aiBSStoreBrand/storeBrandLabel (four dead/degraded AI-matrix sites); day-off override reads customer text only, drops the loose bare-"off" pattern ("dropped it off on Friday"), and pivots CLOSED days per the v9.7.245 rule instead of commanding "Sunday ONLY" against a (CLOSED) calendar; sbContacted no longer counts "Not Contacted"/"Contacted: No" toward "customer was reached N time(s)"; the JSON parse-failure diag indexes the cleaned text it actually parsed; rejected-name leads can Generate (their generic-greeting directive was unreachable behind the name gate); manual Trade-In chip now reaches the sales persona route; duplicate leadSource fill removed. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 -- the one inline-scraper change (_leadAgeScraped flag + AGE DIAG log) is popup-injected and always version-consistent with this file; content.js untouched. Builds on v9.7.428-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.428-dev (DEV. GENERIC-VOI FRESHNESS METRIC FIX -- Gil's own pushback caught a real bug: he hand-picked leads he KNEW were fresh with no VIN/stock and a qualifying model, and the override still didn't fire. Checked the exact leads and confirmed it directly -- both showed ageDays:0 (created TODAY) yet carried 5 and 66 'notes' respectively, which is impossible as genuine conversation history in under a day. totalNoteCount counts EVERY log line indiscriminately (agent-reassignment routing, auto-reply texts, system events), none of which reflect whether the customer's inquiry is genuinely still unpinned to a specific unit -- a lead can rack up dozens of routing entries in minutes with zero real human engagement. Replaced with leadAgeDays directly (<=1 day), hoisting the same proven fallback already built for the re-engagement-hook day check (v9.7.420) so both share one robust computation instead of two -- a lead created today is unambiguously fresh regardless of how much routing noise piled up around it. Locked in as a permanent regression test using the exact real-world shape Gil's log exposed (ageDays:0, totalNoteCount:66, must still qualify). scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.427-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.427-dev (DEV. INCENTIVE FIRING-REASON DIAG + FIRST REAL-WORLD CONFIRMATION -- inspected a real 10-lead batch log for v9.7.426's generic-VOI/in-transit first-touch overrides. Zero Toyota leads in the batch, so in-transit correctly never fired -- not a bug, just no eligible leads this round. The generic override DID fire correctly on one real lead (Roder Russo, Thirdparty Honda source, noteCount:2, first-touch) -- confirmed via the actual generated message: the incentive appears naturally after the greeting ('for qualified buyers there's a current CR-V finance incentive'), not leading with price, exactly as designed, and the message still closes with a real appointment ask. The other first-touch leads in the batch stayed correctly suppressed for real reasons (66 notes, 5 notes just over the tight <=3 freshness bar) -- the tight gates working as intended, not a gap. One real diagnostic improvement found while verifying this: the 'gate passed' log line never reported WHICH override reason fired (asked/generic/in_transit/plain non-first-touch), requiring manual cross-referencing to confirm what actually happened -- now tagged directly ('| first-touch override: generic') so this is never ambiguous from the log alone again. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.426-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.426-dev (DEV. GENERIC/IN-TRANSIT FIRST-TOUCH INCENTIVE + IN-TRANSIT COMPARABLES DISCLOSURE -- three pieces from one conversation with Gil. (1) GENERIC-VOI FIRST-TOUCH: a lead with no VIN and no stockNum on its VOI hasn't locked onto a specific unit -- model-level shopping, not a listing click -- so a real incentive is a legitimate first-touch hook, framed as a natural, non-pushy mention (not a hard sell). Deliberately TIGHT per Gil's own follow-up call after discussion: two independent secondary checks now gate it, both required -- (a) excludes known VDP/listing-tied aggregator sources (TrueCar, Cars.com, Autotrader, CarGurus, Credit Karma, Edmunds, KBB), whose entire business model IS a specific unit, so a blank VIN/stock there is more likely a scraper gap than genuine model-level interest; (b) requires totalNoteCount <= 3 (freshness) -- a lead that's been through several touches and still shows blank VIN/stock is more likely a persistent data gap than a fresh vague inquiry. (2) IN-TRANSIT VOI FIRST-TOUCH: Gil's own follow-up -- a Toyota in-transit vehicle (VIN allocated, no stockNum yet, the SAME isInTransit definition already used elsewhere) is a third, distinct, MORE reliable case (real allocated VIN, not absent data) -- naturally disjoint from the generic case. The codebase already treats in-transit as 'a strong selling moment' worth urgency on its own; this pairs a real incentive with that existing framing rather than inventing new behavior. (3) IN-TRANSIT COMPARABLES: Gil's third follow-up -- the SOLD-pivot's inventory lookup already includes in-transit Toyota units in its comparable pool, undisclosed. New _lpComparablesWithUnits variant (parallel to _lpComparables, doesn't touch its other callers) carries the raw unit through so in-transit status (Toyota-only per confirmed-with-Gil scope, NOT extended to Honda/Kia/Audi without separate evidence) can be honestly disclosed on both the incentive-led pivot and the generic fallback -- the fallback's explanatory clause is conditional on at least one comparable actually being in transit, found via my own test catching it always appearing otherwise. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.425-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.423-dev (DEV. DOUBLED MODEL-NAME FIX — a real [LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG] log (v9.7.421) caught it immediately: "Accord Hybrid — Accord Hybrid — 3.99% APR for 24-60 mos". Root cause: the Data Tool's line-builder (this afternoon's work) bakes the model name INTO the line field by design ("the exact fact, the way a customer should hear it", a complete sentence), but every consumer here -- the SOLD-pivot directive AND its diagnostic, OFFER ENDING SOON, and the general STORE INCENTIVE line (4 places total) -- unconditionally prepended "model + ' \u2014 ' + line" too, assuming line was just the bare offer detail. Fixed with a new shared _lpModelLine(model, line) helper that DETECTS whether line already starts with the model rather than assuming either shape, since not every incentive is guaranteed to come from the Data Tool pipeline (older manually-typed sheet data may genuinely lack the model prefix) -- webpage-parsed lines stay clean, manually-typed ones still get the model prepended for clarity. Verified against both shapes directly. Also fixed test_v415.js, whose isolated Function scope didn't have access to the new helper (a separate function defined elsewhere in the file), which silently zeroed out 10 of its 19 cases until caught. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.422-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.422-dev (DEV. SOLD PIVOT VARIABLE-HOISTING FIX — the v9.7.421 diagnostics paid off immediately: a real log showed dealer 6191's valuefact genuinely had 39 lines loaded and cached WELL BEFORE the SOLD-pivot block ran (confirmed via [LP VFC DIAG] timing), yet the pivot always reported 'no incentive data cached.' Not a race at all this time -- a plain code-ordering bug. Root cause: the SOLD-pivot block (introduced in v9.7.418) referenced the shared `_vfc` variable, which is declared and assigned much LATER in the same populateFromData function (the separate STORE INCENTIVE section) -- due to `var` hoisting, `_vfc` exists as `undefined` for the entire function until execution reaches that later assignment, so the SOLD-pivot's check was structurally guaranteed to always read `undefined`, independent of whether real data was cached. Fixed by reading `_lpValueFactCache[d.dealerId].vf` directly and independently (`_soldVfc`) instead of depending on a variable this block runs before its own assignment -- found and fixed a second stray `_vfc` reference in the block's own diagnostic log that the first pass missed. Also fixed the test harness (test_v418.js), which had been passing `_vfc` as an artificial parameter -- that convenience was exactly what let this bug ship untested; the harness now exercises the real dependency instead of a shortcut. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.421-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.421-dev (DEV. LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG — a real log confirmed the leadAgeDays fallback fix (v9.7.420) fully working end to end: the Charles Underwood/Sorento lead now shows RE-ENGAGEMENT HOOK firing AND the actual generated email leading with 'Sorento customer cash available for qualified buyers' — the whole chain from cache fix through to the final message, confirmed. Same log showed a SOLD lead (Gerry, Accord Hybrid Touring) NOT leading with an incentive despite dealer 6191's 39 lines loading successfully (no VFC race this time) — but the SOLD -> INCENTIVE PIVOT block (v9.7.418) had zero diagnostic visibility of its own, unlike the general STORE INCENTIVE block, so there was no way to tell whether it never ran, found no comparable units, or found comparables that matched nothing. Added [LP SOLD PIVOT DIAG] logging at every decision point: no inventory cache, no comparables scored, comparables found but none matched an incentive (names them), or a real match leading the pivot. Pure diagnostics, no behavior change. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.420-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.420-dev (DEV. LEADAGEDAYS FALLBACK ORDERING FIX — the v9.7.419 load-time warm fix confirmed working via a real log: the same Charles Underwood/Kia Sorento lead that failed twice before now correctly shows [LP INCENTIVE DIAG] gate passed. But the RE-ENGAGEMENT HOOK's day-based leg (leadAgeDays >= 31) silently never fired for this exact lead despite it being 73 days old — traced precisely: 'leadAgeDays normalized via fallback: 73' logged SEVERAL LINES AFTER the incentive block ran, meaning d.leadAgeDays was still 0/unset at check time, so (d.leadAgeDays || 0) >= 31 evaluated against 0. This exact class of bug was already fixed once elsewhere in the file (a comment nearby: 'Fix: run the resolver's same fallback BEFORE any prompt-building code reads from lastScrapedData') but that fix runs even later than populateFromData, so it never covered this consumer. Mirrors that same proven 3-tier fallback (raw leadAgeDays -> "Created (Nd)" pattern -> earliest bracketed transcript date) locally, inline, so the stall-phase check doesn't depend on the later global normalization having already run. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.419-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.419-dev (DEV. PROACTIVE LOAD-TIME CACHE WARM + DUPLICATE-FETCH FIX — Gil pasted a real log showing v9.7.418's merge-time trigger STILL lost the race on both leads shown, and worse, was firing a redundant duplicate fetch. Root cause traced precisely: there's a populateFromData call path (on partial/interim data) that starts the fetch and runs its own synchronous incentive check in the SAME tick — a genuine zero-millisecond race that no 'trigger a little earlier' fix can close, since there's no meaningful gap to win. Real fix: there are only 5 known dealers total (DEALER_ID_MAP) — warm all five caches proactively at extension load, long before any lead is ever grabbed, giving the fetch seconds of head start instead of microseconds. Also fixed the duplicate-fetch bug this surfaced: the staleness guard couldn't distinguish 'still pending' from 'genuinely failed' (both look like vf:null/inv:null), so a second trigger call would re-fetch a request that was already in flight — added an explicit pending flag, cleared only once BOTH feeds (valuefact + inventory) have settled, confirmed via direct simulation (back-to-back calls now fetch once, not twice; a real failure is still retryable). The v9.7.418 merge-time trigger is retired as the primary mechanism (confirmed too late for the racing call path) but left in place as a harmless, pending-guarded defense-in-depth call. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.418-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.418-dev (DEV. SOLD -> INCENTIVE PIVOT + EARLY VFC PREFETCH TRIGGER — Gil's read on a real generated message (Dzung Mai, CR-V sold): the sold-pivot directive produced a soft 'is it still something you're considering?' check-in that dwelled on the loss instead of moving forward. (1) SOLD -> INCENTIVE PIVOT: the sold-unit comparable pivot now cross-references each candidate alternative against the incentive cache (same _lpMatchByModel used everywhere else); when a real, current incentive exists on one of the comparables, the pivot leads with it explicitly ('the one you had your eye on sold, but we've got [X] on [alternative]') instead of the generic no-price 'similar vehicle' framing — this is the one case where quoting a real number on the pivot is the right call, deliberately overriding the existing no-price default. Falls back to today's unchanged generic pivot when no comparable has a matching incentive. A new _soldPivotIncentiveUsed flag suppresses the general STORE INCENTIVE block from also firing (avoids two separate incentive mentions for the same moment) and is logged in [LP INCENTIVE DIAG] so the suppression reason is visible, not silent. (2) EARLY VFC PREFETCH TRIGGER: dealerId is known during the merge step, well before populateFromData runs later — starting the incentive/inventory fetch there instead of at populate-time gives it a real head start before Generate is ever clicked, directly addressing a confirmed race (a live log showed the incentive gate running synchronously before the async fetch had resolved). The existing TTL guard makes the later populateFromData-time call a harmless no-op once this one has already started. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.417-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.417-dev (DEV. LP INCENTIVE DIAG — Gil pasted a real log for a stall-phase, model-matching lead (CR-V Hybrid, convState:pause, 37 days) where the final message carried no incentive, asking whether the cadence logic judged it unworthy. Hand-verified the actual scoring math against real 39-line Honda data: CR-V Hybrid correctly outscores bare CR-V for this lead (3 vs 2, no tie) — a match SHOULD have fired. Most likely explanation: the lead's SCENARIO was Credit Karma/stepping-back with an explicit 'avoid payment or financing pressure, reassuring tone' directive, and the model reasonably deprioritized the incentive fact in favor of that more specific instruction — but the log had NO way to confirm whether the fact was even injected into context vs. never reaching it, which are two different situations needing different fixes. The entire gate chain (adversarial/first-touch/used/sold, then the match itself) had zero logging on which check decided the outcome. Now every path logs to [LP INCENTIVE DIAG]: which gate suppressed it (if any), or how many lines matched and were injected, plus whether the stall-phase RE-ENGAGEMENT HOOK fired. No behavior change — pure diagnostics, same instrument-first discipline as v9.7.416's VFC DIAG. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.416-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.416-dev (DEV. VALUEFACT/INVENTORY PREFETCH DIAGNOSTICS — Gil pasted a real 6-lead log asking why none of them got incentive messaging, including a textbook customer-asked case (Alex Devoe, CR-V, lastInboundMsg literally says "best price"). The log contained ZERO occurrences of "valuefact" or "_vfc" anywhere — the prefetch that feeds STORE INCENTIVE, comparables, and CPO/used-alternate facts (v9.7.232/414/415) had no logging on ANY path: not on success, not on an empty KV response, not on a hard failure. Two layers of silent failure were stacked (the outer call-site try{}catch(e){}, and an empty .catch(function(){}) on the fetch itself), so a real gap could run an entire session with zero trace — exactly the failure class this project has hit before. Confirmed the GET /valuefact route itself is correctly wired server-side (right query param, right KV key, right response shape), so the gap is somewhere on the extension side: wrong base URL, an empty KV entry, or a network/CORS failure — genuinely unknown without the log this ships. Every path (no base URL, HTTP non-ok, empty valuefact, fetch exception) now logs to [LP VFC DIAG], matching the instrument-first discipline used everywhere else in this codebase. No behavior change — pure diagnostics. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.415-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.415-dev (DEV. INCENTIVE CADENCE LOGIC — GSM follow-up (7/11): 'not on first reach-out, but when should they be introduced?' Four additions to the existing STORE INCENTIVE gate (v9.7.235/241/296), all additive — no existing gate removed, only refined. (1) CUSTOMER-ASKED OVERRIDE: a conservative regex over d.lastInboundMsg (deals/discount/rebate/incentive/promo/financing/lease-special/cash-back/best-price/OTD/payment-or-APR asks) overrides the first-touch suppression — a question the customer actually asked isn't unprompted pricing exposure, it's an answer. Low-risk on false positive (worst case: surfaces one touch early). (2) EXPIRING-SOON URGENCY: when a matched incentive's real expires date is within 5 days, it gets dedicated '⏰ OFFER ENDING SOON' framing instead of the generic STORE INCENTIVE line — genuine, dated urgency is categorically different from manufactured pressure and the model is told explicitly to treat it as real. (3) RE-ENGAGEMENT HOOK: pause/stalled convState OR day-based reactivation/long-dormant phase (leadAgeDays >= 31) now gets an explicit note that the incentive is a legitimate, specific reason to restart a quiet conversation — stronger than a generic check-in — and should be favored among the engagement-angle-discipline options (v9.7.414/412) for that touch. (4) Long-dormant reactivation folded into the same _incStallPhase flag rather than built as separate offer-freshness-diff infrastructure that doesn't exist yet — the day-based phase check is a reasonable proxy without fabricating a 'is this offer new since they went quiet' comparison the codebase has no data for. All four still respect the existing adversarial/used-VOI/sold gates unchanged. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.414-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.414-dev (DEV. ENGAGEMENT ENHANCEMENTS — GSM ask (7/10): improve customer engagement via secondary follow-ups offering like-vehicle options, used/CPO alternates on the same nameplate, and tighter incentive cadence. Store incentives were already built (v9.7.235/241/296 — model-keyed, non-first-touch, non-used-VOI gated); the two new pieces: (1) LIKE-VEHICLE COMPARABLES — reuses the existing SOLD-pivot comparables engine (_lpComparables, real inventory only) as a general secondary-touch fact, not just a sold-unit fallback. (2) ALTERNATE CONDITION (CPO/Used) — same-nameplate strict match (shared scorer with incentives) filtered to the opposite condition bucket, condition read via the same regex pattern already used for the VOI's own condition (no assumed inventory schema field — real data only). Both gated non-first-touch/non-adversarial/non-sold per Gil's call (model's judgment, no hard arc-trigger required), and both stand down via a new _voiCrossSellSuppressed flag when the color-mismatch or SOLD pivot already surfaced comparable units, so the prompt never carries two competing alternative-vehicle lists. (3) ENGAGEMENT-ANGLE DISCIPLINE — a new directive covering all three secondary-touch angles (comparables, alternate condition, incentive): pick at most ONE per message; stacking them reads as an info-dump, which undercuts the engagement goal this whole feature exists for. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 (all inventory reads are through the existing prefetch cache). Builds on v9.7.413-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.413-dev (DEV. VOI-5 LOST-LEAD VEHICLE HARVEST + IN-STATE DISTANCE DIRECTIVE CONTRADICTION — Katrina Ware (Kia Baytown, 7/10, fresh VOI-less Gubagoo Dynamic Credit App). (1) VOI-5: the v9.7.381 VOI-4 LeadSelector fallback's 'only year+make row' guard counted vehicle-BEARING rows, not TABLE rows — the active lead's VOI-less row was filtered out before the count, so the dead Sept-2025 lead's '2025 Kia Telluride' row became the sole candidate and was harvested onto the new lead ([finder] named the exact tr.LeadSelector_LeadId_1890867873 source — the v380 instrumentation working as designed). Tightened, abstain-don't-guess: selected status is computed over ALL rows and a selected-but-vehicle-less row now ABSTAINS (the active lead genuinely has no VOI; other rows are other leads by definition); an unselected row is accepted only when the table has exactly ONE lead row. Multi-lead customers without a vehicle-bearing selected row always abstain loudly. VOI-less-and-asking is the safe failure; a dead lead's vehicle named confidently is the costly one. lsFallback diag now records WHICH acceptance/abstain path ran. (2) DISTANCE: the _inStateFar DISTANCE BUYER branch instructed 'Acknowledge the drive directly and make the trip feel worth it' — a direct contradiction of the v9.7.405 unconditional wall (never characterize the trip for drivable in-state), and Katrina's draft surfaced it ('your drive will be worth it', alongside the branch's own pre-fill-paperwork example line). The branch is rewritten wall-compliant: distance stays an INTERNAL fact, the visit ask survives, the justification names what is READY for them, never the distance; the 'reason the drive is worth it' REQUIRED bullet likewise. One directive voice on distance now — the wall. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.412-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.412-dev (DEV. CALL-NOTE PAUSE PHRASE GAP + DELETED-SHOWROOM-VISIT FACT — second half of the Africa Dauphiney lead (the v9.7.411 trade-trigger fix handled the first): with the fabricated trade ask gone, the draft still pushed same-day appointment times at a walk-in customer whose call note said 'still deciding... will get in touch with Madison when she is ready.' Two independent misses. (1) PAUSE: hasCallNotePause — the exact detector built for a recorded not-ready status (v9.7.189) — knew 'will reach out/will contact' but not 'will get in touch', 'still deciding', or 'when she is ready'; convState stayed first-touch, so the FRESH-LEAD banner commanded first-impression framing and the appointment scaffolding stayed live, overpowering the judgment line the model also had. Added the missing agent-note-shaped phrasings ('will let you know' requires the WILL so agent-outbound 'let me know what works best' can never match) and [LP PAUSE DIAG] now logs the matched phrase — the gap survived because the detector failed silently. (2) VISIT: the v9.7.228 deleted-visit rule (correctly) keeps isShowroomFollowUp false, but it silently erased the one fact separating post-visit follow-up from cold outreach — Madison's visit record was deleted 2h later as bookkeeping while the lead itself was created as a walk-in one minute after it. Deleted visits within the 30-day window are now captured as deletedShowroomVisit/-Date (never confirming, PASS-2 gated) and injected as an explicitly UNCERTAIN fact: corroborated by other notes -> post-visit treatment; uncorroborated -> normal outreach, never claim a visit. Arc-first: the fact plus its uncertainty, the model decides. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 (inline scraper only). Builds on v9.7.411-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.411-dev (DEV. TRADE-VALUE TRIGGER SURNAME FALSE-FIRE — Africa Dauphiney lead (Honda Lafayette walk-in, 7/10): the v9.7.364 trade-value trigger's bare `kelley` token (intended for Kelley Blue Book) matched BD agent Roslynn KELLEY's name in note bylines and reassignment log lines on the full-arc scan, firing tradeValue:true on a lead with no trade, no customer reply, and an empty lastInboundMsg. The injected directive fabricated 'the customer asked what their TRADE is worth' and the model obeyed — drafting a trade-appraisal push woven around the CR-V VOI ('once we see your vehicle') for a customer who never messaged. Agent-text-pollution class, v9.7.400 precedent. FIX: (1) tradeValue now requires a customer voice — hasCustomerReply or non-empty inbound; a customer who never messaged cannot have asked for a number. (2) `kelley` only matches followed by 'blue' (Kelley Blue Book); a surname can never trip it again; bare `kbb` stays (not a plausible name, and behind gate 1). (3) [LP DEAL-TRIGGER DIAG] now logs the exact matched text for every fired sub-trigger — this false-fire lived undetected because the log said tradeValue:true without saying WHY; instrument-first. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51. Builds on v9.7.410-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.410-dev (DEV. LP VOI DECLARATION + CROSS-FRAME VOI CONFLICT + VOI-CONFLICT CHIP + FLAG-CHIP VISUAL SYNC. The duplicate-lead vehicle problem (Rose Warren class: N unmerged leads, N vehicles, no single correct VOI to recover) gets the ask-don't-assert treatment plus a deterministic agent override, per the 7/10 design. (1) AGENT LP VOI DECLARATION: an agent note '[LP: 2025 Honda CR-V is the VOI]' (also 'VOI is/=/: X', 'focus vehicle is X', 'customer wants the <year> ...') is parsed from agentLPCommands and promoted to the authoritative d.vehicle — wins over panel, message, and merge sources. If the declared vehicle is a DIFFERENT model family than the scraped one, stale unit identifiers (stock/VIN/color/condition) are dropped so they can't misbind. Newest-note-wins inherited from the scraper. If SUPERSEDED (customer replied after the note), it is NOT hard-pinned — the model gets a soft 'the agent earlier marked X; follow the arc' line instead, and a customer's own later vehicle mention always outranks the note (arc-first: the note is a snapshot, not a pin). (2) CROSS-FRAME VOI CONFLICT DETECTION (merge-time): two or more DISTINCT model families across frames CONFIRMED same-customer (active leadId match or customerId==_activeCustomerId) sets m._voiCrossFrameConflict. Distinctness is model-family level (year/trim-insensitive: 2025 vs 2026 CR-V is NOT a conflict), service frames/sources and unverified frames never count — conservative by construction so ordinary dupes cannot fire it (the false-alert concern). (3) ASK-DON'T-ASSERT DIRECTIVE: on unresolved conflict the model is told not to confidently assert a vehicle, to use the arc if the focus is obvious, and otherwise to clarify naturally in one line ('are you still focused on the RAV4, or the CX-5?') — never mentioning duplicates/records/systems. An LP VOI note suppresses the entire path (authoritative directive replaces it); the v9.7.409/407 panel-authority conflict block is also skipped when an LP VOI is declared. (4) VOI CONFLICT CHIP: new context-flag button (data-flag=voi_conflict) auto-lit on detection; agent can also set it manually (generation-time injection mirrors the distance-chip pattern, double-injection guarded). Cleared on lead change like distance. (5) FLAG-CHIP VISUAL SYNC: fixes the long-standing Distance Buyer highlight bug — paths that mutate activeFlags directly (lead-change clear, regen-chip transient adds) desynced the Set from the buttons' .on class; populateFromData now reconciles all chips to the Set unconditionally, and the lead-change clear drops the .on class explicitly. [LP VOI DIAG][lp-note]/[conflict] fire-and-forget lines log which path won on every grab: lp-note / clean / conflict. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 (no content.js change). Builds on v9.7.409-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.409-dev (DEV. VOI PANEL AUTHORITY over a stale message vehicle. Henalyn Velasco lead (7/9): the agent had CHANGED the VOI on the lead to "2020 Mazda CX-5 Sport", but the model generated about a "2023 Honda CR-V" — which came from the customer's ORIGINAL CarGurus inbound ("I'm interested in this 2023 Honda CR-V…") sitting in lastInboundMsg. The panel VOI (agent-corrected) and the message vehicle (customer's superseded original inquiry) conflicted, and nothing told the model which wins. Fix: when the panel carries a vehicle AND the inbound message names a DIFFERENT year+make, emit an explicit VOI-CONFLICT directive — the panel is authoritative (an agent changing the VOI is a deliberate correction), use only the panel vehicle, never name the stale message vehicle. Conflict logged. Only fires on a genuine make mismatch, so normal leads (message vehicle == panel, or no message vehicle) are unaffected. Builds on v9.7.408-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.408-dev (DEV. BRAND-DISAMBIGUATION replaces the flawed v9.7.407. Gil's correction on the Elouise Williams lead: she is at HONDA LAFAYETTE (24399), and agent Jordyn was SITTING in the Toyota #6189 session tab while working her. So the vote winner 2029123486→24399 (Honda Laf) was CORRECT all along — the abstain killed the right answer. v9.7.407 (exclude content-less frames) was WRONG: both leadId frames were content-less, so it would have thrown away her correct Honda Lafayette signal too, AND the stale Audi frame actually had MORE findable content (a 2019 Dodge Challenger) than her real frame — "content" is the wrong discriminator, and dropping the leadId risks the store falling back to the Toyota session chrome. REVERTED 407. New fix: when the active-lead vote spans stores, use the freshly-RENDERED active vehicle's brand to pick the matching store before abstaining. Elouise's rendered vehicle was "2025 Honda Accord Hybrid" (Honda) → uniquely matches Honda Lafayette among the candidates → resolves to her real lead, no abstain, correct store, immune to the session-chrome the agent sits in. Abstain only remains when the brand can't uniquely disambiguate. Builds on v9.7.407-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.407-dev (DEV. STALE-FRAME VOTE EXCLUSION — fixes a FALSE multi-store abstain. Elouise Williams (ONE lead, Community Toyota #6189, 2025 Honda Accord Hybrid) got "Multiple leads open on this customer (different stores) — click the exact lead..." on 9.7.406. The log shows why: on Jordyn's heavy session, rims2 count=0 (real markers unrendered, so ECCS Strategy 0.5 couldn't fire), and the vote fell through to STALE frames lingering in the DOM from prior grabs — 2029123486 (Honda Lafayette 24399) and 2048394808 (Audi Lafayette 21135), neither of which is Elouise's lead. Those frames had isLeadFrame set but were empty shells (panelText "(no panel element)", rawLen 0), while her REAL Accord frame had the rendered panel but no leadId yet (vindebug race). The v9.7.391 multi-store abstain then saw two different rooftops among the stale leads and refused. Fix: a frame only votes if it has actually RENDERED content (notes, a vehicle panel with rawLen>0, or customer data) — a lingering leadId on an empty shell no longer counts as a lead "open on this customer", so it can't trigger the abstain. Excluded stale frames are logged. The abstain remains for GENUINE co-resident multi-store leads (all with real content). Builds on v9.7.406-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.406-dev (DEV. CRASH FIX — v9.7.405 introduced a hang. The unconditional distance block referenced `sc.mayStateDistance`, but `sc` does not exist in the data-prep scope (everything there is `d`/`data`), so it threw "ReferenceError: sc is not defined" — which propagated up and HUNG the grab on "Reading frames" (Johnny King lead, err.log line 20). mayStateDistance is computed on the scenario object downstream and never copied onto d, so this build computes it INLINE from data (out-of-state via dealerId→home-state map + customer state, or explicit remote request in customer text). Distance rule behavior is unchanged from the 405 intent; this only fixes the undefined-variable crash. My regression, caught same session by the hang. Builds on v9.7.405-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.405-dev (DEV. DISTANCE-RULE UNCONDITIONAL — the distance directive was only added to the prompt inside the `if (d.isDistanceBuyer)` block, so on leads where the distance FLAG didn't fire, the model got NO distance instruction and stated the miles anyway. Michelle Lacour (Alexandria LA → Lafayette, ~80mi but IN-STATE, mayStateDistance=false, isDistanceBuyer not set): the email said "Since you're driving about 80+ miles" — echoed straight from the AGENT'S OWN prior email in the notes (agent-text pollution). SMS softer but still "with your drive." Fix: the distance directive now runs UNCONDITIONALLY on every generation (skipped only for Audi/showroom), so whether or not the flag fired, the model is always told: do not state the miles unless truly out-of-state/remote, and explicitly DO NOT echo a prior agent message's distance mention forward. True remote buyers still get the acknowledge-once directive. Builds on v9.7.404-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.404-dev (DEV. CROSS-BRAND FINAL SAFETY — closes the residual risk Gil flagged in the walk-in guard: because a salesperson can label a walk-in as plain "AI Buying Signals" with NO "(Walk-in)" suffix, the v9.7.403 source-string guard will eventually miss, and if the showroom note is not loaded at classify time the AI-signal cross-brand matrix can still run. The real protection cannot depend on the source string. Added an absolute final check: if the target brand literally appears in the STORE NAME, aiBSIsCrossBrand is forced false — a Toyota at "Community Toyota Baytown" is in-brand, full stop, independent of source/label/showroom-timing/config. This makes the "we don't carry Toyota" contradiction at a Toyota store structurally impossible. The v9.7.403 walk-in source guard remains as a minor secondary (harmless, not load-bearing); this store-name check is the guarantee. Builds on v9.7.403-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.403-dev (DEV. WALK-IN GUARD + cross-brand backstop. Reinaldo's lead produced "we don't carry Toyota here" signed "Community Toyota Baytown" (a Toyota store) on a customer who WALKED IN and looked at a Tundra. Two causes, both fixed. (1) The salesperson manually assigned source "AI Buying Signals - Not Previously Sold (Walk-in)" — the "(Walk-in)" means a physical visit, but isAISignalLead fired on the "AI Buying Signals" prefix and ran the cross-brand prospecting matrix. Now any source containing walk-in/drive-by/showroom is NOT treated as a true AI-signal lead (matrix suppressed); the real showroom-follow-up path is driven independently by the Showroom Visit CRM note (recentShowroomVisit), which is source-agnostic — so a genuine showroom lead is still detected whether or not "walk-in" is in the source. (2) Defensive backstop: aiBSIsCrossBrand now requires BOTH store and target brand positively known — an empty aiBSStoreBrand (dealer-config lookup miss) no longer counts as "different from Toyota", and store brand is recovered from the store NAME when config misses. Either fix alone prevents the contradiction; together they close the walk-in mislabel AND any future config-miss. Builds on v9.7.402-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.402-dev (DEV. DISTANCE-SILENCE hardened — per Gil's standing instruction: the "I see you're N miles away / since it's a drive" line should NEVER appear for a normal drivable lead, only for a true out-of-state / explicit-remote buyer. The rule already existed (v9.7.398, gated on mayStateDistance) and already produced the correct "DO NOT STATE THE MILES" directive for in-state leads like James Bellard (70mi, LA→LA, mayStateDistance=false, verified) — the distance line in his draft actually came from the separate _ddDistanceRemote DEAL/INFO trigger, which v9.7.401 fixed by scoping to customer text. This build hardens the suppression directive itself: explicit that it applies on the FIRST reach-out, an expanded forbidden-phrasing list ("before you make the trip", "since it is a drive", "it is a haul"), and a blanket ban on mentioning miles/distance/driving/travel/"the trip" for non-remote leads. Two distance sources now both closed (401 = the remote-escalation trigger, 402 = the mileage-mention rule). Builds on v9.7.401-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.401-dev (DEV. DISTANCE-REMOTE = CUSTOMER-TEXT-ONLY — the distance wall on the James Bellard lead had a SECOND source I missed: the DEAL/INFO trigger's _ddDistanceRemote flag, which fired distanceRemote:true and emitted the full remote-buyer directive ("send a video walkaround," "don't push a visit"). Root cause is agent-text pollution, same class as the old lastInboundMsg leaks: _ddDistanceRemote scanned the FULL arc (_ddRecent) with a bare "\d{2,3} miles" pattern, which matched BERENICE'S OWN opening outbound ("because you're about 70 miles out" — which the distance flag itself told her to write). Self-reinforcing: the distance flag makes the agent state the mileage, then that agent sentence re-triggers the remote directive. Fix: _ddDistanceRemote now reads CUSTOMER inbound text only (data.lastInboundMsg), and the mileage pattern requires customer framing ("I'm/I live/we're NN miles", "NN miles away/out/from") instead of a naked number that appears in agent copy. James — who asked for nothing remote — no longer trips it. The v9.7.398 distance-once guard (on the toggleFlag path) and this fix are two different distance sources; both needed handling. Builds on v9.7.400-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.400-dev (DEV. ISFOLLOWUP-TRUST-CONVSTATE — the SECOND half of the James Bellard fix. v9.7.399 correctly made convState=first-touch on the fresh no-reply lead (confirmed in the 399 log), but the "it's been quiet since you requested the Civic" framing PERSISTED because a SEPARATE flag, s.isFollowUp, was still true via its `|| !!data.hasOutbound` clause — Berenice's opening text/email counts as outbound, so isFollowUp stayed true and kept selecting the Thirdparty Honda FOLLOW-UP branch (tpoFollowUp = isFollowUp && hasRealOutbound). Two independent flags encoded "this is an ongoing conversation"; fixing only convState left the other leaking. Fix: isFollowUp now TRUSTS convState (isFollowUp iff convState !== first-touch) instead of re-deriving from raw hasOutbound — one source of truth. A genuine follow-up (real customer reply, or aged lead) still has convState !== first-touch and behaves exactly as before; a fresh no-reply lead is now first-touch on BOTH flags. Also removes the distance-wall on this lead as a side effect, since first-touch + the v9.7.398 distance-once guard now govern. Builds on v9.7.399-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.399-dev (DEV. FRESH-NO-REPLY = FIRST-TOUCH — the ACTUAL fix for the "it's been quiet since your Civic request" framing on the 13-minute-old James Bellard lead. Gil's screenshot correctly showed persona = BDC Agent, NOT Director — so the v9.7.398 ghost-floor (a good change) was aimed at the wrong mechanism; Director never fired here. The 397 log revealed the real cause: classifyScenario logged convState 'active-follow-up' on a lead created 12 min earlier, because the convState assignment hit `totalNoteCount > 4 && hasOutbound → active-follow-up` — and the 11 notes were the agent's own opening text+email burst plus reassignment lead-logs (System→Melanie→Berenice→Jacob), not a conversation. active-follow-up carries "continue the conversation / it's been quiet" framing. The existing first-touch guard missed it because contactedAgeDays was defined+recent (contacted today), failing its undefined/>90 condition. Fix at the source: a lead created TODAY with NO real customer reply is first-touch regardless of note count — outbound today with no reply is the FIRST outreach, not a stalled follow-up. A genuine customer reply flips hasRealCustomerReply and the normal follow-up path resumes. This is the missing-fact discipline: the model was reasoning fine, it was handed the wrong convState. Builds on v9.7.398-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.398-dev (DEV. TWO fixes from the James Bellard lead review (7/8). (1) FRESH-LEAD GHOST FLOOR — the lead was created 3:35p and grabbed ~3:50p (13 min old, ageDays 0) but got flagged isHeavyAttemptGhost and routed to Director ghost-reactivation framing ("I know it's been quiet since your Civic request") on a lead whose first outbound went out minutes ago. Cause: _staleNoteCount counted 11 notes that were almost entirely REASSIGNMENT lead-logs (System→Melanie→Berenice→Jacob), not outreach. A lead cannot ghost on day 0. All three ghost signals (isHeavyAttemptGhost, isLongSilenceGhost, wentCold) now gated behind a real age floor (<2 days = too fresh to ghost), so the fresh-lead anti-stall handling applies as intended regardless of scenario branch. (2) DISTANCE-ONCE — agents flagged that the distance flag opens EVERY touch with how far away the customer is (text, email, follow-ups), building a "wall of distance" that gives customers a reason not to drive in. Distance now shapes HOW we handle (stage the visit, verify the car first) without being announced every message; state it at most once, early, never restate mileage in a follow-up. Builds on v9.7.397-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.397-dev (DEV. TOTALED-TRADE path-A tightening. Nicole's exact CRM message — "the car is at the ford dealership in Carencro... so they are deeming it as totaled just waiting on insurance" — DID fire the detector on 396, but via the situational path (C: elsewhere + insurance-pending), not the clinical word. The clinical path (A) was missing "deeming it as totaled" because deemed? didn't match "deeming" and the "it as" object ordering didn't fit. Rewrote A's verb group to (declar|deem|call|rul|consider|mark|writ)(e|es|ed|ing)? + optional object (it / the <noun> / this) + optional (as|a) + total(ed|loss)?, so the clinical phrase now matches directly — two independent nets (clinical A + situational C) instead of relying on C alone. Verified: "deeming it as totaled", "declared a total loss", "marked as total loss", "ruled it a total" all fire; "deeming the appointment important", "call it a day" do not. Builds on v9.7.396-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.396-dev (DEV. TOTALED-TRADE situational detection — the v9.7.395 fix didn't fire on Nicole's actual grab because the WORD "totaled" was never in her message or the scraped context; it lived only in agent notes and the model's own output. Her real words: "the car is at the ford dealer... waiting on insurance." Customers describe the SITUATION, not the clinical term. Added detection path (C): a "vehicle is elsewhere / at another dealer / body shop / in the shop / being assessed" phrase co-occurring with an "insurance pending / waiting on insurance / claim open / adjuster" phrase now triggers the totaled-trade handling — that combination IS a total-loss-in-progress in plain language. Patterns (A) explicit total-loss language and (B) wreck+insurance co-occurrence unchanged. Verified against Nicole's exact wording plus false-positive traps (normal "at the dealer" without insurance, GAP mentions on a healthy trade). Builds on v9.7.395-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.395-dev (DEV. TOTALED-TRADE — missing-fact fix for the totaled-vehicle miss Tania caught: on Nicole's lead (Pathfinder being totaled at the Ford store, insurance pending) the model applied normal trade-appraisal reasoning — "send damage photos and I'll get Trent to give you the real number" — which is factually impossible. A totaled vehicle can't be appraised or taken as a trade; the INSURANCE SETTLEMENT is the customer's equity (paid to lienholder/customer), and the dealer's role is helping with the REPLACEMENT once the claim settles. Per fix philosophy this is a missing FACT, not a missing rule: the prompt's only "total loss" handling was a KMF loyalty-source regex, nothing told the model a totaled trade isn't a trade. Added _lpVehicleTotaledNow (explicit total-loss phrasing, or wreck/accident + insurance-claim co-occurrence; guarded against bare "insurance" mentions) scanned over the full conversation context, and a totaled-trade branch that takes priority over the declined/active trade branches: don't appraise, don't ask for damage photos, don't push a same-day appraisal appointment; acknowledge the loss, position the settlement as replacement funds, help once the claim settles. Builds on v9.7.394-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.394-dev (DEV. TWO FIXES. (1) PHANTOM-EFFECTIVE — the "couldn't grab / won't generate" report on the email-only Kia lead 2025538199 was a FALSE phantom-scrape block on a SUCCESSFUL rescue. R-1 (v9.7.383) folds a richer re-scrape into lastScrapedData and leaves m sparse by design; the phantom guard was still reading m, so on R-1-rescued leads it saw 0 notes and blocked Generate even though 18 notes were populated and on screen (log: "Re-scrape succeeded — notes: 18" immediately followed by "PHANTOM SCRAPE detected ... Blocking Generate"). Guard now evaluates the EFFECTIVE object (richer of m / lastScrapedData). This is why it failed on BOTH builds — the guard predates both. (2) SMS-BLANK NOTICE now yellow (crm-status.notice: amber, bold) instead of dim gray — agents reported the opt-out/blank-SMS message looked disabled. Builds on v9.7.393-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.393-dev (DEV. DUAL-SIGNAL — strengthens v9.7.392's ECCS active-lead strategy with a cross-check against the vindebug [data-autoleadid] div (the element Gil surfaced: <div class="vindebug-section" data-globalcustomerid data-autoleadid data-dealerid>). Key insight: the two signals fail in DIFFERENT conditions — the vindebug div RACES on SPA navigation (not rendered when pre-scrape runs → null → the old vote, exactly the v9.7.354 count=0 case), while the ECCS cdQuery lives in the frame URL and is present immediately. So ECCS stays primary (it sidesteps the render race), but when a loaded rims2 frame's leadId (sourced from that same data-autoleadid attr) matches the ECCS-named lead, the grab is dual-confirmed and logged as such. On the Sonia Canizales 3-lead record (Active 2027 Telluride Hybrid 2048174241 + Lost + Duplicate 2025 Telluride, all Kia/6190), both signals point to 2048174241 — bulletproof. No behavior change beyond the log detail + confidence flag; the anchor was already correct in 392. Builds on v9.7.392-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.392-dev (DEV. ECCS-ACTIVELEAD — the REAL fix for the multi-lead-customer wrong-lead bug, replacing the v9.7.391 abstain's premise. Gil's point: the agent is already correctly parked on the right lead (clicked in, scrolled to notes per SOP), so asking them to re-choose was solving the wrong problem — the active lead IS determinate, the extension just wasn't reading it. The 7/8 dashboard console proved the signal: with SIX co-resident rims2 lead frames and no sole winner, only the ACTIVE lead's ECCS frame carried AutoLeadID in its cdQuery (cdQuery=?AutoLeadID=2048174241), while inactive-customer ECCS frames carry GlobalCustomerID (no lead). The pre-scrape was parsing ECCS for dealerId ONLY and discarding cdQuery, then voting across all six frames to guess what ECCS already stated. Fix: extract AutoLeadID from the (URL-encoded) ECCS cdQuery and use it as Strategy 0.5 — above every frame-count heuristic, below only frameploader. Guarded to require the ECCS-named lead actually match a loaded rims2 frame (else the ECCS frame is stale and we fall through). This resolves the wrong-lead case on the FIRST grab with no re-grab — the agent's SOP navigation is exactly what sets the cdQuery. The v9.7.391 cross-store abstain remains as a backstop for the rare case ECCS also fails, but should now almost never fire. This is the 'newest-rendered detection, not voting' the notes called for — turns out VinSolutions hands us the active lead ID directly. Builds on v9.7.391-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.391-dev (DEV. MULTI-STORE-ABSTAIN — root fix for the 7/8 Edge cross-lead bleed (Kia lead came up Toyota). Diagnosis from paired logs: GIL's grab took the "Pre-scrape winner (sole rims2)" fast path → correct Kia; JORDYN's grab had NO sole rims2 winner (her chronic slow-hydration, same root as the adaptive-wait fix) so it fell to the voting fallback, which on a 3-lead customer (Kia + Toyota + Honda all co-resident, none cleanly active) voted onto the Toyota lead 1944999008 → Toyota store/panel on a Kia lead. So "correct for you, wrong for her, same lead" = different render state → different resolution PATH (certain vs voting), not different code. Fix: when the active-lead VOTE (last-resort path only) spans candidates from DIFFERENT rooftops, that's a cross-store coin flip, not a pick — ABSTAIN and tell the agent to click the specific lead and re-grab (same refuse-don't-guess stance as SF-1/F5). Captures dealerId per candidate lead in the vote loop; abstains only when distinct stores appear among candidates. The sole-rims2 fast path is never touched, so clean grabs (the common case, and every grab that already works) are 100% unaffected. Does NOT make VinSolutions render faster — it converts a silent wrong-store ship into a visible re-grab prompt. Builds on v9.7.390-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.390-dev (DEV. ADAPTIVE-WAIT — self-righting fix for slow-hydration sessions (Jordyn's 7/7 session: 55/55 first frame-reads empty, passive-wait rescue succeeded 8/10, so every grab burned the full 2×2.5s auto-retry ladder + 7s wait ≈ 14.5s to populate, ~5s of it doomed retries that never work on that session's load). Root: the retry ladder is climbed every grab even after the session has proven rims2 won't render in the retry window. Fix: when the 7s passive-wait rescue SUCCEEDS, set a session-scoped flag (sessionStorage 'lp_sessionNeedsPassiveWait'); subsequent grabs in that session skip the auto-retry ladder and go straight to the rescue wait, saving ~5s/grab. Fast sessions never set the flag and are 100% unaffected. Session-scoped so it self-resets on a fresh side-panel and re-tunes to current tab load rather than sticking. Note: this makes the WAITING smarter, not VinSolutions faster — the floor is still however long that session takes to hydrate rims2 (2.5–7s here); this just stops adding ~5s on top of the floor. Builds on v9.7.389-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.389-dev (DEV. SMS-SUPPRESS-UX follow-up — per request, the SMS pane itself now shows an explicit opt-out notice when F3 blanks the SMS, not just a status line. On an opt-out lead (customer texted STOP) the SMS tab's empty-state now reads "🚫 Customer opted out of texts — do not send SMS. Use the Email tab." (or a generic "no SMS generated" notice if the blank wasn't opt-out driven), so whichever tab the agent lands on is self-explanatory. Also resets the SMS overlay caption back to default on the next normal lead, since the panel persists between grabs. Builds on the v9.7.388 tab-switch fix. No logic change to generation/suppression. Builds on v9.7.388-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.388-dev (DEV. SMS-SUPPRESS-UX — the "failed to generate" report on the Cherry Jefferson (Kia Baytown) lead was NOT a failure: the customer texted STOP, so F3 deterministic SMS suppression (v9.7.379) correctly returned an empty SMS with a full, substantive email (trade-appraisal message). Generation succeeded — but the panel opened to the empty SMS tab showing "SMS WILL APPEAR HERE", which looked identical to a total failure and caused repeated re-grabs. Root cause was UX, not logic: post-generation always switched to the SMS tab even when SMS was intentionally blank. Fix: when SMS is empty but email has content, open to the Email tab and show a status line explaining the SMS is blank because the customer opted out (or, if not an opt-out, that SMS came back empty). Normal leads open to SMS as before. No change to generation or suppression logic — this only fixes which tab is shown and adds the explanatory status. Builds on v9.7.387-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.387-dev (DEV. STORE-DIAG-MERGE — noise fix for the 386 diagnostic, no behavior change to store resolution. The 386 log confirmed STORE-FROM-DEALERID works: merged store correct on all 7 grabs, the [Lead Pro] Store override line GONE entirely (store is now right at merge time, nothing to correct). But [LP STORE DIAG] fired 2/grab — structurally, because only the rims2/vindebug frame of the 7 carries a dealerId, so the ECCS/dashboard/loader frames always lack it and fall to chrome. That per-frame flag was noise, not signal. Moved the diagnostic to the MERGE point: it now fires ONLY when the merged (shipping) store is NOT dealerId-backed — the one grab shape where a wrong store can actually reach a draft. Expected frequency on healthy grabs: zero. Builds on v9.7.386-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.386-dev (DEV. STORE-FROM-DEALERID — completes the 385 store fix. The 385 log showed the reorder wasn't enough: on the live VinSolutions layouts there is NO lead-scoped store text (no breadcrumb / page-title / header match), so resolution still fell to session chrome on 4 grabs — correct only because the popup-side dealerId override caught it afterward. Root realization: store was being read as TEXT and then corrected from dealerId, when dealerId — already resolved in-frame — IS the source of truth. Now store is derived from dealerId FIRST (via a locally-inlined map; DEALER_ID_MAP is module-scope and would throw inside the injected scraper, the v9.7.228/268 scope trap), with the text scrape and session chrome demoted to fallbacks for the genuinely dealerId-less case only. _storeFromChrome now fires ONLY when dealerId is absent AND no lead-scoped source resolved — the one case a wrong store can actually ship — so the diagnostic is now a precise signal instead of firing on every dealerId-corrected grab. Net: the pre-override store is now RIGHT at scrape time, not corrected after. Builds on v9.7.385-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.385-dev (DEV. STORE-SCOPE — fix for the session-chrome store leak found in the 7/7/26 384-dev log: two Kia Baytown leads (dealerId 6190) text-scraped as "Audi Lafayette" because the agent had the Audi tab selected in her VinSolutions session chrome. Root cause: the store firstOf() chain put parent-frame chrome sources FIRST — storeFromUrl (reads window.parent.document's selected customer-dashboard tab) and the in-frame read of that same tab element — ahead of every lead-scoped source. dealerId overrode both correctly (10/10 this session), but the text scrape is the ONLY store fallback when dealerId can't resolve (rescue/split-frame grabs), and there it would ship a Kia lead as Audi — wrong persona/store voice all session. Same class as every fallback-asserts-instead-of-abstains bug this week. Fix mirrors v9.7.83 labelValue scoping: lead-scoped DOM + page-header text now resolve FIRST, session chrome only as true last resort. _storeFromChrome flag + [LP STORE DIAG] logs (frame + popup) mark when the chrome source was used, so a chrome-store on a dealerId-less grab is now visible. LS-DIAG result from same log: ZERO SOURCE DIAG hits across 9 grabs — leadSource fallback is far rarer than the dashboard suggested; downgraded to watch-for-it. Builds on v9.7.384-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.384-dev (DEV. LS-DIAG — diagnostic for the leadSource contamination found in the 7/7/26 feedback review: dashboard "By Lead Source" table showed free-text disposition fragments ("brought at different dealership", "coming in at 3pm", "left showroom without buying") instead of real source values. Root suspect from code review: labelValue('Source') does a SUBSTRING match on row labels ("source" matches ANY label containing it, not just the true Lead Source row) and runs as the last-resort fallback after the specific selectors and the KMF/TFS keyword block both fail. Two log points added, no behavior change: (1) inside labelValue() itself — logs the exact label text matched, the value returned, and the nearest ID'd ancestor, so the next live hit names its own wrong row; (2) at the call site — tracks whether the specific selectors already found something BEFORE labelValue runs, exposed as _lsViaLabelFallback on the scrape result, so frequency of fallback use is visible even on grabs that don't hit the bug. Builds on v9.7.383-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.383-dev (DEV. SPLIT-FRAME RELAXATION SHIPPED + RESCUE NO-DOWNGRADE — from the Hector Solis live capture (Cars.Com Finance Intent, Honda Baytown, 7/7/26), where TWO bugs compounded into a cross-brand pivot ("we don't have Fords on our lot") about an IN-STOCK 2014 F-150. SF-1: the split-frame vehicle relaxation pre-registered in the v9.7.375 changelog is now built, in BOTH the rescue gate and (new) the main-merge PASS-2 path: a leadId-less PANEL-sourced vehicle is accepted when there is no positive lead-ID or dealer mismatch AND zero competing lead-like frames AND (main merge) it is the only such candidate. Live diag counts justifying it: Bruce Richerson 0, Hector Solis 0; stale old-lead frames retain their autoLeadId markers (every DOM-VERIFY datapoint) so they disqualify the relaxation and the Ronnie Alls cross-store protection is untouched. Loud ACCEPTED/ABSTAINED logs on every use. R-1: the passive-wait re-scrape and the broad-merge rescue are independent async stages and the sparse broad-merge populate was LAST-WRITE-WINNING over the rich re-scrape (21 notes + active-follow-up downgraded to 0 notes + first-touch in the same capture — generation lost the entire transcript). The rescue populate now folds its fields into richer already-populated data instead of replacing it. Builds on v9.7.382-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.382-dev (DEV. Beacon version-mismatch warning deduped to once per popup session (console.log thereafter) — Chrome's extension dashboard collects console.warn from extension pages as error entries, so the per-grab warn stacked identical dashboard "errors" all day while a tab ran stale content.js. Behavior unchanged: beacon still rejected, pre-scrape still resolves. Builds on v9.7.381-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.381-dev (DEV. VOI-4 — the finder's first live batch (7/7/26) NAMED the missing-VOI layout: VehicleInfo panel renders ONLY the condition token ("(New)"/"(Used)") while the actual YMM lives in the LeadSelector ant-table row (tr.LeadSelector_LeadId_*). Two captures in one session: Amber Gardner (Facebook/Kia, panel "(Used)", row "2024 Kia Telluride" — generation asked "what vehicle are you looking for" with the Telluride on the lead) and Megan Smith (Gubagoo VR, panel "(New)", row "2017 Toyota Corolla"). New frame-side fallback fires ONLY on a bare-condition panel (an EMPTY panel means genuinely no VOI — the Chanelle Jackson trade lead stayed correctly VOI-less), prefers the selected ant row, accepts an unselected row only when it is the ONLY year+make row (multi-lead customers → loud ABSTAIN, never guess: LeadSelector_LeadId_* is a different ID namespace than AutoLeadID), and rejects any row vehicle that also appears in the TradeInfoPanel. Diag gains condOnlyPanel + lsFallback fields. VOI-1/2/3 (finder print, year ranges) confirmed working in the same log. Builds on v9.7.380-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.380-dev (DEV. VOI EXTRACTION — three fixes for the open missing-VOI bug (Arnulfo Reta class: vehicle on the CRM lead that no frame scrape extracted). VOI-1: the v9.7.375 FINDER was collected in _voiDiag but NEVER PRINTED — both DIAG log lines (main + rescue) omitted vd.finder, so every live failure since v9.7.375 captured the selector-naming data and discarded it; the instrument ran blind, which is why the bug outlived the diagnostic build. Both prints now emit [LP VOI DIAG][finder] with element paths (or proof the text is absent → hydration, not selector). VOI-2: the active-panel text-scan fallback matched 202[0-9] ONLY — a 2018 preowned unit could never match (verified by test), and preowned skews pre-2020; broadened to (19|20)\d{2}, junk guards and panel scoping unchanged. VOI-3: canonicalization and the panel rescan matched 20[12][0-9] (2010–2029) — 2009-and-older units silently failed and the pattern breaks in 2030; both now 1980–2039. NEXT LIVE FAILURE: read the [finder] line — element paths → add that selector; 'no year+make text found' → hydration timing, re-grab confirms. Builds on v9.7.379-dev.)
// Lead Pro -- popup.js  v9.7.379-dev (DEV. FINAL-AUDIT FIX BATCH — extension side of the Fable 5 last-chance audit, findings F1-F9 + client halves of worker findings W3/W4. F1-complete: PASS-2 lead-state gate (hasTrade, tradeDescription, inventoryWarning, isInTransit, isShowroomFollowUp, buyingSignals, leadAgeDays, leadStatus, ownedVehicle) added alongside the v9.7.365 conversation gate — same rims2 count=0 trigger, remaining field surface; also fixes the leadAgeDays falsy-zero inheritance that broke hasNewLeadToday. F2: REQUIRED_SCRAPER finally ENFORCED — the beacon now carries scraperVersion (content.js) and the popup rejects a mismatch (MV3 doesn't reload content scripts in open tabs after auto-update). F3: STOP-lead directive contradiction resolved — the v9.7.62 stateLabel and the LEAD-section SMS STATUS line are aligned to the v9.7.294 override (SMS field = empty string, email never references the opt-out) PLUS deterministic SMS suppression at render (the long-parked item, now shipped — model output discarded if it drafts SMS for an opted-out customer). F4: hidden frames no longer write the storage beacon (cross-tab/cross-window hijack inside the 10s trust window; the beacon short-circuited Strategy 1.5 before it could correct). F5: ANCHOR-MISMATCH HARD STOP — anchor set but zero PASS-1 matches while ID-bearing frames exist now aborts with a visible re-grab message instead of silently merging PASS-2 leftovers (stale deep-link tab.url case). F6: the trailing-period JSON repair regexes stripped the final period from 100% of well-formed responses (verified by simulation); relocated to Recovery 0 in the catch path. F7: value-fact/inventory cache gains a 30-min TTL and retries after a both-null failed fetch (side panel lives all day; fetchedAt was recorded but never checked). F8: selector canary on VinSolutions' '.notes-and-hsitory-item-date' DOM typo — if Cox ever fixes it, every date-gated filter opens silently; now logged loudly frame- and popup-side. F9: customerState cross-frame rescue no longer accepts an unverified frame's state when the active customer is known (prior-customer stale dashboard fed Distance Buyer). W3: license key attached to all worker calls (generate, voicemail, translate, feedback) so worker v7.30 REQUIRE_LICENSE can be enabled once the fleet updates — DEPLOY EXTENSION FIRST. W4: worker SAFE_FALLBACK (generic appointment push) detected via candidates[0]._fallback and never rendered as a draft on any path (main/VM/translate). VALIDATION: F1/F3/F5 ride the planned 15-20 real-lead before/after batch before fleet rollout. scraperVersion stays v9.7.51 (beacon field addition + canary flag are non-payload). Builds on v9.7.378-dev.)
