    // An "Email reply from prospect" note is NOT customer-authored text. It is the customer's
    // words, then their mail client's signature, then a quoted-reply header, then the ENTIRE
    // PRIOR AGENT EMAIL. Every detector below was reading the whole blob.
    //
    // Jeri Mayes (Community Honda Lafayette, lead 2065165925, 8/21) is what that costs. Her only
    // inbound email reads, in full:
    //   "Thank you.Sent from Yahoo Mail for iPhoneOn Monday, August 10, 2026, 3:00 PM, Kaylee
    //    Guzman kguzman@... wrote: Jeri,Your CarGurus inquiry is for the 2026 Honda Odyssey..."
    // The day detector's `(?:until|till|on|this|next)\s+(monday|...)` alternative matched the
    // "On Monday" of a YAHOO QUOTED-REPLY HEADER — a timestamp the mail client generated, naming
    // when OUR OWN email was sent — and emitted "Customer said Monday is when they are available.
    // LOCK IN Monday". Jeri never wrote the word Monday. She wrote "Thank you." She was offered
    // times three days out on a day nobody chose, while the store was open with four hours left.
    //
    // The cut is at the FIRST marker of any kind, because everything after the first marker is
    // someone else's text by construction. Enumerating client signatures alone would be the trap
    // this file keeps falling into, so the two SHAPE rules carry the weight: "On … wrote:" and
    // "<an email address> … wrote:" cover every mail client without naming one.
    //
    // MUST live inside inlineScraper — same scope trap as _isShowroomVisitDeleted above. It is
